Abstract N°: 91
Effectiveness of Upadacitinib in moderate vs. severe atopic dermatitis: interim results
Lauffer Felix*
1
, Ralph von Kiedrowski
2
, Sven Quist
3
, Dariusch Mortazawi
4
, Melanie Huber
5, 6
, Fatima Abousamra
6
,
Knut Schäkel
7
, Stephan Weidinger
8
1
Ludwig-Maximilians University Hospital, Department of Dermatology, Munich, Germany
2
Dermatological Practice Selters, Selters, Germany
3
Dermatology Clinic, Helix Medical Excellence Center Mainz, Mainz, Germany
4
Dermatology Practice, Remscheid, Germany
5
Private University in the Principality of Liechtenstein 
(
UFL
)
, Triesen, Liechtenstein
6
AbbVie Deutschland GmbH & Co. KG, Wiesbaden, Germany
7
Heidelberg University Hospital,, Department of Dermatology,, Heidelberg, Germany
8
University Hospital Schleswig-Holstein, Department of Dermatology and Allergy, Kiel, Germany
Objectives:
Approximately 60% of real-world Upadacitinib patients have moderate atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
. However, there is
no analysis comparing the effectiveness of Upadacitinib in moderate versus severe AD patients. This analysis aims
to compare effectiveness rates in both patient populations.
Methods:
UP-TAINED is an ongoing observational study for atopic dermatitis patients treated with Upadacitinib in Germany.
Moderate and severe patients were stratified based on baseline validated Investigator Global Assessment 
(
vIGA
)
,
where vIGA = 3 is considered moderate and vIGA = 4 is severe AD. Patient demographics and disease parameters
at baseline were analyzed. Treatment outcomes included the Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
, Worst
Pruritus Numeric Rating Scale 
(
WP-NRS
)
, and Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
. All patients with at least one
follow-up visit were included in this analysis. Results are reported as observed for up to two years of observation.
Results:
A total of 441 patients were included, of which 287 had moderate 
(
vIGA = 3
)
 and 154 had severe AD 
(
vIGA = 4
)
.
Despite disease severity being the main factor for dose choice, no difference in prescribed dosage between both
severity groups was observed. No statistically significant differences were found in age, gender, disease duration,
or BMI categories between the two groups.
The mean EASI at baseline was 18.0 for moderate and 33.3 for severe AD patients. Differences in EASI were
present until month 6, after which both groups had comparable mean EASI scores. 61.1% of moderate patients
achieved EASI
≤
3 at month 1 and 77.7% at month 3. EASI
≤
3 response rates in severe patients were 46.9% and
63.5% at month 1 and 3, respectively. No differences in EASI75/90/100 response rates were observed.
The WP-NRS score was significantly higher at baseline in patients with severe AD 
(
7.8 vs. 6.7 for patients with
moderate AD
)
. At month 1, the proportion of patients achieving WP-NRS0/1 was higher in patients with moderate
AD. At later follow-up visits, itch response rates were similar.
Mean DLQI scores were 13.3 for moderate and 15.5 for severe AD patients. 60.3% and 78% of the patients with
moderate and severe AD, respectively, reported that AD had a very to extremely large impact on their quality of

life 
(
DLQI >10
)
.
Some differences were observed in affected body regions. For example, patients with severe AD more likely had
active AD lesions on the feet 
(
48.7% vs. 25.1%
)
, hands 
(
73.4% vs. 58.2%
)
, and genital area 
(
29.9% vs. 16.7%
)
compared to patients with moderate AD. Although the proportion of patients with facial AD was similar in both
groups 
(
76.3% in moderate AD and 79.9% in severe AD
)
, severe AD patients had more moderate-to-severe facial
AD 
(
86.2% vs. 66.7%
)
 compared to moderate AD patients.
During up to two-years of follow-up, 725 adverse events 
(
AEs
)
 were reported in 231 
(
52.4%
)
 patients. The most
frequent AEs were dermatitis atopic 92 
(
20.9%
)
, nasopharyngitis 25
(
5.7%
)
, and acne 23
(
5.2%
)
. 18 
(
4.1%
)
 patients
reported serious AE.
Conclusion:
Regardless of investigator-assessed disease severity and other observed differences at baseline response rates
were similar in both severity groups after 1 to 3 months of Upadacitinib treatment. This suggests that Upadacitinib
is a suitable treatment option for diverse AD patients, aligning with its indication for moderate to severe AD.
This analysis also highlights the significant negative impact on the quality of life among moderate AD patients,
emphasizing the need for treatment.
