Abstract N°: 294
Patient-reported outcomes evaluations of 12-months tralokinumab treatment in 824 adults with atopic
dermatitis: Real-world data from the prospective, non-interventional, international, single-cohort TRACE
study
Michael Cork* , Ahmed Ameen
1
, Pierre-Andre Becherel
2
, Cory Rubin
3
, Abel Jarell
4
, Alessandra Narcisi
5, 6
, Teodora
Festini
7
, Ida Vittrup
7
, Ulla Ivens
7
, Michael Cork
8, 9
1
NMC Specialty Hospital, Abu Dhabi, United Arab Emirates
2
Dermatology and Clinical Immunology Unit, Antony Hospital, Antony, France
3
Michigan Dermatology Institute, Waterford, Michigan, United States
4
allCUTIS Research, Inc., Portsmouth, New Hampshire, United States
5
IRCCS Humanitas Research Hospital, Dermatology Unit, Rozzano, Milan, Italy
6
Humanitas University, Department of Biomedical Sciences, Pieve Emanuele, Milan, Italy
7
LEO Pharma A/S, Ballerup, Denmark
8
Sheffield Children
’
s Hospital, Sheffield, United Kingdom
9
Sheffield Dermatology Research, Department of Infection, Immunity and Cardiovascular Disease, University of
Sheffield, Sheffield, United Kingdom
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic, burdensome skin disease significantly affecting patients
’
 quality of life 
(
QoL
)
,
sleep, and self-confidence. Tralokinumab is a monoclonal antibody targeting interleukin-13 indicated for the
treatment of moderate-to-severe AD and was effective and well-tolerated in Phase 3 clinical trials. Here, we
present data on the impact of 12-months real-world tralokinumab use on patient-reported outcomes 
(
PROs
)
including QoL measures.
Materials & Methods:
TRACE is a prospective, non-interventional, international, single-cohort study of adults with AD who were
prescribed tralokinumab according to national approved label at treating physician
’
s discretion. Concomitant
medications were allowed. Of the enrolled 835 patients 
(
Nov-2021 to Jun-2023
)
, 824 comprised the full analysis
set. Assessments included dermatology life quality index 
(
DLQI
)
, peak pruritus numeric rating scale 
(
PP-NRS
)
,
sleep-numeric rating scale 
(
Sleep-NRS
)
, atopic dermatitis control tool 
(
ADCT
)
, recap of atopic eczema 
(
RECAP
)
,
and/or work productivity and activity impairment questionnaire-general health 
(
WPAI-GH
)
 as per individual
clinical practice. Data are presented as observed.
Results:
At baseline, patients were 47.6% female, 52.4% male and 75.7% White, with a mean age of 44.1 years and mean
AD duration of 18.8 years. All PRO evaluations showed AD improvement over time 
(
Table 1
)
. The mean DLQI score
(
0
–
30; higher score means worse QoL
)
 improved from 12.8 at baseline 
(
n=452
)
 to 7.0 
(
n=253
)
 and 4.9 
(
n=192
)
 at
months 3 and 12, while the proportion of patients with DLQI 
≤
5 
(‘
no
’
 to 
‘
small
’
 QoL impairment
)
 increased from
20% at baseline to 68% at month 12. The mean PP-NRS score 
(
0
–
10; 
‘
no itch
’
 to 
‘
worst itch imaginable
’)
improved from 6.4 at baseline 
(
n=490
)
 to 4.3 
(
n=267
)
 and 3.3 
(
n=192
)
 at months 3 and 12, while the proportion
of patients with PP-NRS 
≤
4 increased from 22% at baseline to 70% at month 12. Mean Sleep NRS score 
(
0
–
10; 
‘
no
sleep loss
’
 to 
‘
I did not sleep at all
’)
 improved from 5.1 at baseline 
(
n=377
)
 to 2.8 
(
n=212
)
 and 2.3 
(
n=149
)
 at

months 3 and 12. Among patients with Sleep NRS 
≥
2 at baseline, a 
≥
2 points improvement was achieved by 67%
(
n=148
)
 and 70% 
(
n=105
)
 of patients at months 3 and 12. AD control improved over time 
(
lower score means
better control
)
 from ADCT mean score 
(
0
–
24
)
 of 13.9 
(
n=280
)
 at baseline to 7.9 
(
n=166
)
 and 6.4 
(
n=115
)
 at
months 3 and 12, and from RECAP mean score 
(
0
–
28
)
 of 16.5 
(
n=239
)
 at baseline to 10.0 
(
n=145
)
 and 7.7 
(
n=93
)
at months 3 and 12. All health-related work ability measures 
(
WPAI-GH
)
 improved over time 
(
Table 1
)
.
Conclusion:
12-months treatment with tralokinumab in adults with AD in a real-world setting provided early improvements
across all endpoints including itch, QoL, sleep, AD control, and health-related work ability.
