Abstract N°: 434
Abrocitinib for Cyclosporine-Resistant Moderate-to-Severe Atopic Dermatitis: A Real-World Case Series
Sk Shahriar Ahmed*
1
, Abhishek De
1
, Disha Chakraborty
2
1
Calcutta National Medical College & Hospital, Dermatology, Kolkata, India
2
VA Medical Center, Dermatology, California, United States
Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disease characterized by
pruritus and eczematous lesions. Abrocitinib, a Janus kinase 1 
(
JAK1
)
 inhibitor, has recently been recently
approved for moderate-to-severe AD in adults. This case series evaluates the effectiveness and safety of
abrocitinib in patients with moderate-to-severe AD who had inadequate response to cyclosporine and/or required
a rapid response. Our objectuives were to evaluate the effectiveness of abrocitinib in the treatment of moderate-
to-severe atopic dermatitis in patients with inadequate response to cyclosporine and to assess the safety and
tolerability of it.
Materials & Methods:
 This was a real-world retrospective case series evaluating the use of abrocitinib in patients
with moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
. Patients included had an inadequate response to cyclosporine
(
3
–
5 mg/kg body weight
)
 and/or required a faster therapeutic response due to significant life events such as job
interviews or marriage.
A total of nine patients 
(
five females, four males; mean age: 19.44 ± 7.68 years, range: 10
–
34 years
)
 with
moderate-to-severe AD were treated with abrocitinib. Among them, three were adolescents or children, for whom
the drug was used off-label. The mean disease duration was 13.33 ± 8.06 years 
(
range: 6
–
29 years
)
, and eight
patients had a positive family history of AD.
Prior treatments included cyclosporine, methotrexate, and systemic corticosteroids. Abrocitinib was prescribed at
a mean dose of 177.77 ± 44.09 mg, with seven patients receiving 200 mg and two receiving 100 mg daily.
Treatment effectiveness was assessed at baseline and at weeks 2, 4, 8, and 12 using the Eczema Area and Severity
Index 
(
EASI
)
, Investigator
’
s Global Assessment 
(
IGA
)
, and Peak Pruritus Numerical Rating Scale 
(
PP-NRS
)
.
Results:
 Significant reductions were observed in EASI, IGA, and PP-NRS scores over the 12-week treatment period.
Mean EASI score was decreased from 48.22 ± 7.88 at baseline to 35 ± 8.81 at 2 weeks, 19.44 ± 8.29 at 4 weeks,
12.44 ± 6.67 at 8 weeks, and 6.33 ± 3.35 at 12 weeks 
(
Baseline vs. 2 weeks, P = 0.0003; Baseline vs. 12 weeks, P <
0.0001
)
. Mean IGA score was decreased from 4 ± 0 at baseline to 3.44 ± 0.72 at 2 weeks, 2.33 ± 0.71 at 4 weeks,
1.77 ± 0.44 at 8 weeks, and 1.22 ± 0.44 at 12 weeks 
(
Baseline vs. 4 weeks, P = 0.001; Baseline vs. 12 weeks, P <
0.0001
)
. Mean PP-NRS score was decreased from 8.44 ± 0.52 at baseline to 4.55 ± 0.88 at 2 weeks, 3.22 ± 0.97 at
4 weeks, 2.33 ± 1.11 at 8 weeks, and 2 ± 0.86 at 12 weeks 
(
Baseline vs. 2 weeks, P < 0.0001; Baseline vs. 12 weeks,
P < 0.0001
)
.  
Most patients demonstrated excellent treatment adherence. Some patients required dose adjustments during the
study period. One patient experienced mild thrombocytopenia.
Conclusion:
 Abrocitinib demonstrated rapid and significant improvement in clinical outcomes for patients with
moderate-to-severe AD, including those with prior inadequate response to cyclosporine. The treatment was
generally well-tolerated. These findings suggest that abrocitinib is an effective therapeutic option for moderate-
to-severe AD for Indian patients, particularly in cases requiring a fast response or with prior inadequate response
to cyclosporine

