Abstract N°: 485
Cumulative Life Course Impairment in Moderate-to-Severe Atopic Dermatitis: A Novel Cross-Sectional
Study in Singapore
Meng Jie Ho*
1
, Rehena Sultana
1
, Haur Yueh Lee
2
1
Duke-NUS Medical School, Singapore, Singapore
2
Singapore General Hospital, Dermatology, Singapore, Singapore
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 significantly impacts patients
’
 quality of life over their lifespan. Traditional measures such
as Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
 and Patient-Oriented Eczema Measure 
(
POEM
)
 primarily assess short-
term effects at a single time point, potentially overlooking cumulative impairment. The novel Cumulative Life
Course Impairment 
(
CLCI
)
 model uniquely evaluates both retrospective 
(
CLCI R
)
 and prospective 
(
CLCI P
)
 disease
burdens. This study, one of the first globally, aims to extensively assess CLCI in moderate-to-severe AD patients
and its association with disease severity and quality-of-life metrics. Understanding the long-term implications of
AD through CLCI assessments can significantly enhance patient care and inform targeted interventions.
Materials & Methods:
A cross-sectional study was conducted involving 82 adult patients diagnosed with moderate-to-severe AD.
Participants completed CLCI R and CLCI P questionnaires. Clinical assessments included evaluations of disease
severity using Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
 and POEM, quality of life through DLQI and symptom-
specific assessments including ITCH and SLEEP scores. Demographic and clinical data, such as age, gender,
duration of disease was systematically collected. Statistical analyses include Spearman
’
s correlation coefficients to
assess associations, Mann-Whitney U tests for group comparisons, and multiple regression analyses to determine
independent predictors of cumulative impairment. Further exploratory subgroup analyses were conducted to
assess the potential influence of demographic and psychosocial factors.
Results:
The mean CLCI R score was 29.4 ± 1.88, with no significant difference between moderate and severe AD groups
(
p=0.907
)
, indicating substantial retrospective impairment in both severity groups. However, prospective
impairment 
(
CLCI P
)
 scores were significantly higher in severe AD patients 
(
Mean: 28.4 ± 5.50 vs. 15.9 ± 1.96;
p=0.007
)
, indicating increased anticipated future impairment in more severe disease. Statistically significant
correlations were observed between CLCI P and DLQI 
(
r=0.611, p<0.01
)
 and disease severity 
(
r=0.489, p<0.01
)
.
Multiple regression analysis identified DLQI 
(β
=0.07 
(
0.04,0.10
)
, p<0.001
)
, CLCI-R 
(β
=0.02 
(
0.01,0.03
)
, p<0.001
)
,
disease duration 
≥
21 years 
(β
=0.33 
(
0.05,0.60
)
, p=0.019
)
, and age >34 years 
(β
= -0.38 
(
-0.66,-0.10
)
, p=0.007
)
 as
significant predictors of prospective impairment. Notably, age >34 years exhibited a protective effect against
future impairment, possibly due to enhanced coping strategies or better access to resources, warranting further
investigation.
Conclusion:
This study highlights significant cumulative impairment in moderate-to-severe AD patients, supporting the novel
use of the CLCI model in routine dermatology practice. Implementing CLCI assessments could identify high-risk
individuals earlier, enabling personalized care and proactive interventions. Findings encourage integrating AD
care into broader chronic disease frameworks, emphasizing early intervention, mental health support and

innovative approaches such as teledermatology. Future research should validate these results longitudinally and
explore targeted interventions to reduce cumulative disease burden.
