Abstract N°: 530
Lebrikizumab provides high levels of effectiveness in patients with moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis in
routine practice: interim analysis of the non-interventional study AD-LIFE
Felix Lauffer*
1
, Ralf Gutzmer
2
, Franca Wiemers
3
, Tobias Hagemann
4
, Laura Susok
5
, Afra Kempf
6
, Sebastian
Diemert
6
, Sven Silberborth
6
, Diamant Thaçi
7
1
Department of Dermatology and Allergy, LMU Hospital, Munich, Germany
2
Johannes Wesling University Medical Center, Minden, Germany
3
Dermatology Practice of Dr. med. Wiemers, Dr. med. Franca Wiemers, Leipzig, Germany
4
Private Dermatological Practice of Dr. med. Tobias Hagemann, Bad Sassendorf, Germany
5
Klinikum Dortmund, Hautklinik, Dortmund, Germany
6
Almirall Hermal GmbH, Hamburg, Germany
7
Institute and Comprehensive Center for Inflammatory Medicine at the University of Lübeck, Lübeck, Germany
Introduction & Objectives:
 Efficacy and safety of lebrikizumab 
(
LEB
)
 for the treatment of moderate-to-severe
atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 was demonstrated throughout its extensive clinical development program. However, real-
world data on LEB in AD remains scarce. Here, we report interim effectiveness and safety data from the non-
interventional study 
(
ADlife
)
.
Materials & Methods:
 This is an open, prospective, multi-center, observational cohort study to evaluate AD
control at week 
(
W
)
 52. Patients were included if they were 
≥
18 years old, had moderate-to-severe AD requiring
systemic therapy as per label, received LEB treatment according to physician
’
s decision, and provided informed
consent. Effectiveness outcomes reported here were percent change from baseline 
(
CFB
)
 of EASI, percentage of
patients with EASI50, EASI75, EASI90 and IGA 0/1, percent CFB of 24h-peak pruritus numerical rate scale 
(
peak-
pruritus NRS
)
, and percentage of patients with peak-pruritus NRS 
≥
4-point improvement from baseline 
(
W4 and
W16
)
. Safety outcomes reported were adverse drug reactions 
(
ADRs
)
 and serious ADRs. This interim analysis
covered patients completing 16W of treatment. Data are reported as observed. T-Test was performed for CFB
analyses.
Results:
 A total of 100 patients were included 
(
8 patients dropped out of the study until W16
)
. Baseline
characteristics: mean [SD] age of 41.3 [15.0] years and 57% were female; baseline severity: mean scores of EASI
21.6 [11.5], IGA 3.4 [0.6], peak-pruritus NRS 7.2 [2.1] and DLQI 17.1 [7.2]
)
. The most common atopic comorbidities:
63% of patients presented allergic rhinitis /hay fever, 40% asthma, 27% food allergy and 60% other allergies.
Thirty percent 
(
30/100
)
 of patients have been previously treated with advanced systemic treatments 
(
of which
76.7% [23/30] of patients had been pre-treated with dupilumab
)
 and 86% 
(
86/100
)
 with TCS. At
baseline/W4/W16, 48/36/26 patients received TCS as concomitant topical prescription. Effectiveness outcomes
(
W4 and W16
)
: percent CFB of EASI was -53.8% and -70.1% 
(
p<0.0001 both
)
; % of patients achieving EASI50:
66.0% and 85.9%; % of patients achieving EASI75: 30.9% and 63.0% and % of patients achieving EASI90: 11.7% and
32.6%, respectively; % of patients with IGA 0/1 was 10.4% and 31.9%; percent CFB of peak-pruritus NRS was -
44.0% and -52.5% 
(
p<0.0001 both
)
; and 48.9% and 60.5% of patients achieved peak-pruritus NRS 
≥
4-points
improvement from baseline, respectively. In this preliminary analysis, ADRs under lebrikizumab were consistent
with those in clinical trials. No new safety signals were observed.
Conclusion:
 The results of this interim analysis, the first data reporting real-world evidence on LEB for moderate-
to-severe AD, demonstrated high levels of effectiveness with relevant improvements of signs and symptoms, seen
as early as W4, and a favorable safety profile. These data suggest that efficacy seen in randomized clinical trials

translates well into the real-world setting even with a significant share of patients being pre-treated with
advanced systemics and patients suffering from comorbid conditions.
