Optimizing the Management of Atopic Dermatitis with JAK Inhibitors: Real-World Evidence from a 36-
week Multicenter Study 
–
 Italian Landscape AD
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic, relapsing inflammatory skin condition that often requires personalized
treatment strategies. Janus kinase inhibitors 
(
JAKi
)
 represent a novel class of targeted therapies with rapid onset
of action and potential to address both objective signs and subjective symptoms of AD. While randomized clinical
trials have demonstrated their efficacy, real-world data are essential to better understand their performance in
routine clinical practice. This study aimed to evaluate the real-world effectiveness of JAK inhibitors in the
treatment of moderate-to-severe AD across a large cohort of Italian patients, using optimal treatment targets
recently recommended by Silverberg and colleagues.
Materials & Methods:
This observational, multicenter study involved 30 Italian dermatology centers and included 388 adult patients
initiating treatment with a JAK inhibitor. Patients were evaluated at baseline and at Weeks 4, 16, and 36. The
primary objective was to assess the proportion of patients achieving the combined endpoint of Eczema Area and
Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
 
≤
 3 and itch-Numerical Rating Scale 
(
NRS
)
 0/1. These targets were selected in accordance
with the consensus proposed by 
Silverberg et al.,
 which emphasizes the need for both objective and patient-
reported outcomes to define optimal response in AD. No direct comparisons were made between different JAK
inhibitors.
Results:
At baseline, the mean age of participants was 40.1 years 
(
SD 15.9
)
, with 54.6% being male. The mean disease
duration was 24.2 years 
(
SD 20.3
)
. Notably, 70.9% of patients were bio-experienced, with dupilumab being the
most frequently used prior treatment.
At Week 4, 30.1% of patients achieved the combined endpoint of EASI 
≤
 3 and itch-NRS 0/1. This proportion
increased to 55.2% at Week 16 and reached 59.2% at Week 36 
(
Figure 1
)
. These results demonstrate a progressive
and sustained clinical response over time. As expected, response rates followed a time-dependent pattern,

reflecting the therapeutic trajectory of JAKi in routine practice. Data for baricitinib at Week 36 were not shown due
to very limited sample size.
Conclusion:
This large, real-world study confirms the effectiveness of JAK inhibitors in managing moderate-to-severe atopic
dermatitis across a heterogeneous patient population. The proportion of patients achieving optimal disease
control increased steadily over time, supporting the role of JAKi as a valuable therapeutic option in daily clinical
care. The use of combined, patient-centered treatment targets
—
aligned with recent international
recommendations
—
offers a practical and meaningful approach to evaluating treatment success. These findings
provide real-world support for integrating JAK inhibitors into optimized AD management strategies, while
highlighting the need for continued research into long-term outcomes and treatment sequencing.
Figure 1. Proportion of patients achieving the combined endpoint response during the 36 weeks of observation.
