Abstract N°: 974
When living in the countryside is not that peaceful
Safa Gueroum
1
, el fetoiki fatima zahra
1
, hali fouzia
1
, chiheb soumiya
1
1
University Hospital Center Ibn Rochd - Casablanca, CASABLANCA
Introduction & Objectives:
Compositae dermatitis or specifically parthenium dermatitis is a term commonly used to indicate a specific form
of chronic allergic contact dermatitis, called weed dermatitis by many physicians. It is a chronic disorder whose
management is extremely difficult, since the allergen avoidance is almost impossible, the topical corticosteroid
therapy provides only partial relief and a prolonged systemic corticosteroid therapy has unacceptable side-effects.
We report a case of an old farmer with a severe parthenium dermatitis treated with azathioprine.
Materials & Methods:
A 60-year-old male farmer was referred to our hospital due to a rash on his face, hands, and forearms. This rash
occurred each spring but had become more frequent and nearly constant in recent years. The patient reported
that the itching was significantly impacting his quality of life.
Upon physical examination, we observed erythematous, lichenified plaques with mild scaling on his bilateral upper
and lower eyelids, brow bones, and forehead, sparing the nasal bridge and extending to the chin, zygomatic
prominence, and nasolabial folds on both sides. Additionally, erythematous patches and scaling were affecting his
forearms and hands. The patient appeared depressed.
Results:
We considered a diagnosis of airborne contact dermatitis. The patient underwent patch testing with the European
Comprehensive Baseline Series. Results read on day 2 showed a positive reaction to the sesquiterpene lactones
mix 
(
+3
)
. He was started on topical corticosteroids, which yielded mild improvement.
Since the patient had no plans to relocate to an urban environment, and given the widespread presence of the
allergen and the persistence of flare-ups due to climate change, we decided to initiate a steroid-sparing
immunosuppressive treatment with 100 mg of azathioprine per day. This treatment provided significant
improvement.
Conclusion:
The Compositae family of plants is extensive, ranging in type from sunflower and chrysanthemum, thistle to
ragweed and cocklebur. Its members can cause mechanical, irritant, and allergic contact dermatitis or phototoxic
and photoallergic reactions.
The typical clinical picture is that of an elderly male farmer with a chronic, widespread eczematous eruption in
exposed areas, reminiscent of airborne contact dermatitis, photocontact dermatitis, or atopic eczema. Involved
skin is erythematous, thickened, and often lichenified.
Though it has a very low rate of mortality, the disease usually persists with variable remissions and relapses,
causing great distress and morbidity. The management of the dermatitis is very difficult because avoidance of
Parthenium is almost impossible owing to its ubiquitous distribution. Treatment is mostly symptomatic and

topical, and systemic corticosteroids are the mainstay of therapy. However, to overcome the side-effects since the
disease runs a chronic course with frequent relapses, azathioprine has been extensively studied as a steroid-
sparing drugs; it is considered as an effective and safe alternative to corticosteroid therapy in the treatment of
chronic Parthenium dermatitis
