Abstract N°: 978
Exercise may improve atopic dermatitis by modulating the gut microbiota
Wanchen Zhao
1
, Peng Ge
1
, Abudouwanli Alafate
1
, Quan Sun
1
, Mengyao Yang
1, 2
, Wang Shan
1, 3
, Yi Tan
1
, Shigaku
Ikeda
1
, Hideoki Ogawa
1
, Ko Okumura
1
, François Niyonsaba
1, 4
1
Atopy 
(
Allergy
)
 Research Center, Juntendo University Graduate School of Medicine, Tokyo, Japan
2
Department of Dermatology, the First Affiliated Hospital of China Medical University, Liaoning, China
3
Department of Dermatology, Beijing Children
’
s Hospital, Capital Medical University, Beijing, China
4
Faculty of International Liberal Arts, Juntendo University, Tokyo, Japan
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
, a prevalent chronic inflammatory skin disorder, arises from complex interactions between
genetic predisposition, environmental triggers, and immune dysregulation. While epidemiological studies indicate
an inverse correlation between moderate-to-vigorous physical activity and AD incidence, the underlying
mechanisms remain elusive. This study investigates the therapeutic effects of voluntary exercise on AD-like
inflammation in mice and elucidates its gut microbiota-dependent mechanisms.
Materials & Methods:
AD-like inflammation was induced in mice via topical application of the vitamin D3 analog MC903. Mice
underwent voluntary wheel running for 4 weeks. To dissect microbiota involvement, a tripartite experimental
design was implemented: 1
)
 Gut microbiota depletion via oral broad-spectrum antibiotics; 2
)
 Fecal microbiota
transplantation 
(
FMT
)
 from exercised/sedentary donors; 3
)
 Multidimensional assessment including dermatitis
severity 
(
SCORAD-like scoring
)
, transepidermal water loss 
(
TEWL
)
, serum IgE quantification, Th2 cytokine profiling
(
IL-4/IL-13
)
, and histopathological analysis of CD4
⁺
 T-cell infiltration.
Results:
Voluntary exercise markedly alleviated AD-like pathology in mice, as evidenced by significant reduction in
epidermal hyperplasia, TEWL, and ear swelling. Exercised mice demonstrated suppressed systemic inflammation
characterized by decreased serum IgE levels and downregulated cutaneous expression of Th2-associated
cytokines 
(
IL-4/IL-13
)
. Furthermore, histopathological analysis revealed substantially diminished CD4
⁺
 T-cell
infiltration in lesional skin. Importantly, improvement of AD-like inflammation persisted beyond the exercise
intervention period.
Notably, sedentary AD mice co-housed with exercised counterparts exhibited parallel improvements in ear
swelling and epidermal thickness. Antibiotic-mediated gut microbiota depletion abrogated the therapeutic
benefits of exercise, whereas FMT from exercised mice effectively recapitulated the anti-inflammatory phenotype,
restoring improvements in TEWL, epidermal architecture, and inflammatory cytokine expression.
Conclusion:
This study demonstrates that voluntary exercise confers durable AD protection through gut microbiota
remodeling, potentially via microbiota-immune crosstalk. Our findings validate exercise as a viable adjunct therapy
for AD and propose microbiome-targeted strategies as novel therapeutic frontiers.
