Abstract N°: 1042
Joint Pain and Inflammatory Arthropathy After Dupilumab Use in Atopic Dermatitis: A Systematic Review
and Meta-Analysis
Hui Xin See Tow
1
, Yik Weng Yew
2
1
National University of Singapore, Singapore, Singapore
2
National Skin Centre, Singapore, Singapore
Introduction & Objectives:
Dupilumab has emerged as an important treatment for inadequately controlled moderate-to-severe atopic
dermatitis 
(
AD
)
. Dupilumab is a human monoclonal antibody which works by inhibiting the interleukin 
(
IL
)
-4
receptor subunit 
α
. However, adverse events from dupilumab use has been reported, including ocular
complications, nasopharyngitis and injection site reactions. Additionally, rarer adverse events like joint pain,
alopecia areata, psoriasis, lymphoma, red face, skin peeling have also been reported. Notably, complaints
regarding joint pain has prompted the inclusion of arthralgia to the Summary of Product Characteristics of
dupilumab. Joint pain included conditions like osteoarthritis, arthralgia, myalgia, musculoskeletal symptoms, back
pain, and inflammatory conditions, such as arthritis, enthesitis or enthesopathies, tendinitis or tenosynovitis,
rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, inflammatory joint pain, infrapatellar fat pad inflammation and
psoriatic arthritis. In this paper, we reviewed arthritis and inflammatory arthropathy as an adverse effect of
dupilumab treatment for AD.
Materials & Methods:
A comprehensive search was performed for randomized controlled trials, observational studies, case series and
case reports analysing joint pain prevalence among AD patients treated with dupilumab. This study was
performed according to the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic reviews and Meta-Analyses 2020 checklist
and quality was assessed via the Newcastle-Ottawa Quality Assessment Scale 
(
NOS
)
 
(
Figure 1
)
.
Results:
43 studies were included in the systematic review, and 34 studies 
(
15101 patients
)
 were included for the meta-
analysis. In the systematic review, seven case reports and two case series have reported patients with joint pain
after dupilumab use, in addition to the 27 cohort studies and 7 RCTs showing reports of joint pain following
dupilumab use. Some studies showed investigations being conducted for joint pain, which included elevated
inflammatory markers, such as CRP and ESR, ultrasound and MRI confirmed inflammatory arthropathy or
enthesitis
Among the 34 studies, the pooled prevalence of joint pain among dupilumab patients was 1.74% 
(
95%CI:1.12
–
2.68
)
 
(
Figure 2
)
. Among the eight studies that reported inflammatory arthropathy prevalence among dupilumab
patients, the pooled prevalence of inflammatory arthropathy among dupilumab patients was 2.62% 
(
95%CI: 1.44
–
4.70
)
 
(
Figure 3
)
. There were 34 cases of arthritis, 79 cases of enthesitis or enthesopathies, 40 cases of tendinitis or
tenosynovitis 
(
or 60 were enthesitis or enthesopathies or tendinitis or tenosynovitis
)
, one case of rheumatoid
arthritis, one mild ankylosing spondylitis, four cases of inflammatory back pain, one infrapatellar fat pad
inflammation and one case of psoriatic arthritis. Some patients had multiple symptoms, such as arthritis and
tenosynovitis concurrently. Among the 8 studies with a control group, the overall relative risk of developing joint
pain after dupilumab use for AD was not significantly higher at 0.68 
(
95%CI: 0.29
–
1.58, p=0.31
)
, compared to the
control group 
(
Figure 4
)
.

Conclusion:
Although our findings found that there is insufficient evidence to suggest that dupilumab use was associated with
joint pain, the prevalence of joint pain observed among patients treated with dupilumab may warrant a proactive
surveillance in clinical practice.




