Abstract N°: 1045
Repercussions of Atopic Dermatitis on Patients
’
 Quality of Life: An Impact Not to Be Overlooked
ouissal hormi
1
, zerrouki nassiba
1, 2
, zizi nada
1, 2
1
Department of Dermatology Venereology and Allergology, CHU Mohammed VI, Oujda Morocco., Oujda,
Morocco
2
Laboratory of Epidemiology, Clinical Research, and Public Health, Faculty of Medicine and Pharmacy, Mohammed
First University, Oujda, Morocco, Oujda, Morocco
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic and recurrent skin condition characterized by skin inflammation, intense
pruritus, and an impaired skin barrier. Beyond physical manifestations, AD has profound repercussions on
patients
’
 quality of life, affecting their psychological well-being, sleep, and social life. This study aims to evaluate
the impact of AD on the quality of life of patients followed in our department.
Materials & Methods:
This is a retrospective descriptive study involving children with atopic dermatitis treated in the dermatology
department, conducted between January 2016 and January 2025. Quality of life was assessed using the CHILD-
DLQI.
Results:
We included 120 children aged 4 to 16 years with atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
. The mean age of patients was 7.4 ± 3.2
years. A slight male predominance was noted 
(
55% boys
)
. The severity of AD varied, with 45% of cases classified
as mild, 37% as moderate, and 18% as severe. The impact on quality of life, measured by the CHILD-DLQI score,
had a mean of 10.6 ± 4.3, indicating a moderate to severe impact. Regarding score distribution, 5% of children
reported no impact, 28% reported a mild impact, 42% a moderate impact, 18% a severe impact, and 7% a very
severe impact. Pruritus and skin discomfort were reported by 85% of patients, significantly affecting sleep in 65%
of them. Additionally, 50% of children experienced difficulties in engaging in physical activities, 40% suffered
psychological repercussions such as frustration or depression, and 30% faced social interaction issues, with cases
of isolation reported in 15% of them. Regarding treatment, 70% of children were on topical corticosteroids, while
25% received systemic treatments. A significant improvement in CHILD-DLQI scores was observed in 60% of
patients after six months of follow-up. These results highlight the considerable impact of atopic dermatitis on
children
’
s quality of life, necessitating a comprehensive therapeutic approach.
Conclusion:
Atopic dermatitis has a significant impact on children
’
s quality of life, affecting not only their physical well-being
but also their psychological and social health. The use of tools such as the CHILD-DLQI is essential to assess the
extent of this impact and guide therapeutic management in a holistic manner.
