Introduction & Objectives:
 Dupilumab, an anti-interleukin 
(
IL
)
-4R
α
 antibody, has showed efficacy and
effectiveness for moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
. However, some patients experience persistent lesions
on the head and neck despite lesions improving on the limbs and trunk after long-term treatment with dupilumab,
which is one of the unmet needs of dupilumab. Although some patients switch to tralokinumab or lebrikizumab
(
anti-IL-13 antibodies
)
 as a result, evidence showing the effectiveness of these treatments is limited. Herein, we
investigated the effectiveness of anti-IL-13 antibodies in patients who switched to one to these IL-13 antibody
treatments due to persistent dermatitis in the head and neck after long-term dupilumab treatment.
Materials & Methods:
 This study included all AD patients who had been treated with dupilumab for more than 1
year at our department and subsequently switched to tralokinumab or lebrikizumab by December 2024 due to
persistent facial and neck dermatitis. The total Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
 and head and neck EASI 3
months after switching to tralokinumab or lebrikizumab were evaluated. Patient data were retrospectively
collected from medical records. The patients with insufficient data were excluded. Assessments were conducted by
well-trained dermatologists in our department. This study was approved by the Teikyo University Institutional
Review Board and was conducted in accordance with the principles of the Declaration of Helsinki. Informed
consent was obtained by the opt-out method on the website.
Results:
 Twelve patients 
(
female, 1; male, 11
)
 were analyzed. The median age of the patients was 43.5 years 
(
1st
quartile, 33.25 years; 3rd quartile, 50 years
)
. All patients directly switched from dupilumab to tralokinumab 
(
7
patients
)
 or lebrikizumab 
(
5 patients
)
. All patients except for one showed a decrease in head and neck EASI, with a
significant difference from 0.9 
(
0.55, 1.65
)
 to 0.3 
(
0.175, 0.9
)
 in total 
(
Wilcoxon matched-pairs signed-rank test,
p=0.0088
)
 and from 1.5 
(
0.9, 2.1
)
 to 0.4 
(
0.2, 0.9
)
 in patients switching to lebrikizumab 
(
paired t-test, p=0.0224
)
. A
numerical difference from 0.8 
(
0.5, 1.05
)
 to 0.2 
(
0.15, 0.8
)
 was observed in patients switching to tralokinumab
(
Wilcoxon matched-pairs signed-rank test, p=0.1563
)
.
Conclusion:
 Head and neck EASI scores significantly decreased 3 months after switching from dupilumab to
tralokinumab or lebrikizumab. Switching to tralokinumab or lebrikizumab could be considered for AD patients
with persistent head and neck lesions during dupilumab treatment.
