Abstract N°: 1264
Atopic eczema is associated with herpes zoster in adults
Anna Schober*
1
, Sinead Maire Langan
1
, Hywel Williams
2
, Harriet Forbes
1
, Julian Matthewman
1
1
London School of Hygiene & Tropical Medicine, Department of Non-Communicable Disease Epidemiology,
London, United Kingdom
2
University of Nottingham, Centre of Evidence Based Dermatology, Lifespan and Population Health, School of
Medicine, Nottingham, United Kingdom
Introduction & Objectives:
Eczema is associated with an altered immune response and a heightened susceptibility to certain
infections. Systemic eczema treatments including oral corticosteroids, methotrexate, and more recently JAK
inhibitors - a subclass of newly developed small-molecule treatments - have been linked to an increased risk of
herpes zoster. However, some limited evidence suggests an independent association between eczema and herpes
zoster.
The aim of the present study was to investigate the association between eczema* and herpes zoster in the UK and
explore the role of eczema treatments in this relationship. 
Materials & Methods:
A matched 
(
on age, sex, clinical practice
)
 cohort study was conducted using routinely collected primary care data
from the UK
’
s Clinical Practice Research Datalink Aurum 
(
CPRD
)
 over a period from 1997 to 2023. A total of
2,445,090 adults with incident or prevalent eczema were identified using a previously validated algorithm and
compared with 11,636,684 observations from adults without eczema. Cox regression models were used to
estimate the univariable and adjusted association between eczema and herpes zoster. Covariates included age at
baseline, behavioural factors, and multiple comorbidities. Various definitions of immunosuppressant treatment
and oral corticosteroid exposure were employed to examine their influence on the association. To assess the
impact of age on effect estimates, an interaction term between eczema and age was incorporated into the
analysis.
Results:
Individuals with eczema had an unadjusted hazard ratio of 1.33 for herpes zoster 
(
95%-CI: 1.31
–
1.34
)
, which
decreased to 1.28 
(
95%-CI: 1.27
–
1.29
)
 after adjusting for covariates. The risk increased with greater eczema
severity 
(
mild: aHR: 1.22 [95%-CI: 1.20-1.23]; moderate: aHR: 1.28 [95%-CI: 1.27-1.30]; severe: aHR: 2.33 [95%-CI:
2.22-2.45]
)
. Adjustments for different definitions of oral corticosteroid exposure led to slight further
reductions 
(
aHRs: 1.24 to 1.27 with confidence intervals from 1.23
–
1.25 and 1.26
–
1.28, for exposure at baseline
and ever-exposure respectively
)
. There was strong evidence for effect modification by age, with younger eczema
patients experiencing a comparatively higher relative excess zoster risk 
(
group of 30- to 40-year-olds: aHR 1.51
[95%-CI: 1.47-1.56], followed by 40- to 50-year-olds: aHR 1.42 [95%-CI: 1.39-1.46]
)
.
Conclusion:
Our findings indicate that individuals with eczema have an increased risk of herpes zoster, regardless of
comorbidities or oral corticosteroids. With herpes zoster increasingly emerging as a potential adverse effect of
newer eczema treatments, recognising this risk is especially important for guiding clinical practice and vaccination

strategies to improve care and prevent herpes zoster in individuals with eczema. Further research is required to
investigate the underlying biological mechanisms and to assess additional factors such as vaccinations.
