Abstract N°: 1284
Online Education Significantly Improved Dermatologists
’
 Knowledge and Competence in Diagnosing and
Managing Chronic Hand Eczema
Elaine Bell*
1
, Marinella Calle
1
, Sonja Molin
2, 3
1
Medscape Global Education, LONDON, United Kingdom
2
Department of Dermatology, Venerology and Allergy, Charité Universitätsmedizin Berlin, Berlin, Germany
3
Department of Medicine, Queen
’
s University, Kingston, Canada
Introduction & Objectives:
Chronic hand eczema 
(
CHE
)
 is defined as hand eczema 
(
HE
)
 that lasts for more than three months or relapses
twice or more within a year. Key symptoms include itch and pain. CHE can also negatively impact patients
’
 quality
of life, psychological well-being, physical functioning, and ability to work.
We assessed whether online accredited education could improve dermatologists
’
 understanding of the impact of
CHE, differential diagnosis of CHE and emerging therapies to treat CHE, and could also enhance their confidence
in communicating with patients about this condition.
Materials & Methods:
Dermatologists participated in a 30-minute, online video activity entitled 
‘
Managing Chronic Hand Eczema: How
Can We Do Better for Our Patients?
’
. Responses to 3 multiple-choice, knowledge questions and 1 self-efficacy, 5-
point Likert scale confidence question were analyzed. Educational effect was assessed using a repeated-pair
design, pre-/post-assessment. A paired samples t-test was conducted for significance testing on overall average
number of correct responses and for confidence rating. A series of McNemar
’
s tests were conducted at the
question level 
(
5% significance level, P <.05
)
. Cohen
’
s d with correction for paired samples estimated the effect
size of the education on number of correct responses 
(
<.20 modest, .20-.49 small, .59-.79 moderate, 
≥
.80 large
)
.
The activity launched on 26 September 2024 with data collection through 9 November 2024.
Results:
Dermatologists 
(
n=68
)
 significantly improved their knowledge of:
The mode of action of emerging therapies for CHE 
(
46% correct responses at baseline rising to 71%
post-activity; 
P
 <.001
)
Differential diagnosis of CHE 
(
63% correct responses at baseline rising to 79% post-activity; 
P
 <.01
)
Dermatologists 
(
n=68
)
 also demonstrated significant competence gains in:
Selecting an appropriate questionnaire to assess the impact of CHE on patients
’
 lives as the basis for
patient communication 
(
57% correct responses at baseline rising to 82% post-activity; 
P
 <.001
)
The average percentage of correct responses increased from 55% at baseline to 77% post-activity 
(
P
 <.001
)
,
and there was a large educational impact 
(
Cohen
’
s d=0.84
)
The proportion of dermatologists answering all 3 questions correctly rose from 21% at baseline to 59% post-
activity
After completing the activity, 51% of dermatologists reported improved confidence in communicating with
patients about the impact of CHE on their lives, resulting in a notable confidence shift of 51%
Conclusion:

This online activity significantly improved dermatologists
’
 knowledge and competence regarding the impact,
diagnosis and management of CHE. Given the evolving treatment landscape for this condition, dermatologists
would benefit from additional education to improve their knowledge of recently approved and emerging
therapies and translate this knowledge into clinical practice for improved patient outcomes.
