Abstract N°: 1285
Online Education Significantly Improved Dermatologists
’
 Knowledge Of The Mode Of Action And Key
Clinical Data On Emerging Therapies For Atopic Dermatitis That Target The OX40-OX40 Ligand Pathway.
Elaine Bell*
1
, Marinella Calle
1
, Stephan Weidinger
2
1
Medscape Global Education, LONDON, United Kingdom
2
Dept of Dermatology & Allergy, Kiel, Germany
Introduction & Objectives:
Moderate to severe atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic, heterogeneous condition with unpredictable disease
course that requires effective, long-term disease control. Several new therapies have reached the clinic recently
with more in the pipeline. Dermatologists need to stay abreast of emerging therapies in order to be fully informed
about how the treatment landscape continues to evolve. We assessed whether online accredited education could
improve dermatologists
’
 understanding of the mode of action and clinical data on emerging therapies for AD that
target the OX40-OX40L pathway.
Materials & Methods:
This online educational video activity entitled 
‘
What
’
s on the horizon in atopic dermatitis? A focus on OX40-OX40
ligand-targeted therapies
’
 was a 30-minute discussion with 3 panelists. Responses to 3 multiple-choice knowledge
questions and 1 self-efficacy, 5-point Likert scale confidence question were analyzed. Educational effect was
assessed using a repeated-pair design, pre-/post-assessment. A paired samples t-test was conducted for
significance testing on overall average number of correct responses and for confidence rating. A series of
McNemar
’
s tests were conducted at the question level 
(
5% significance level, P <.05
)
. Cohen
’
s d with correction
for paired samples estimated the effect size of the education on number of correct responses 
(
<.20 modest, .20-
.49 small, .59-.79 moderate, 
≥
.80 large
)
. The activity launched 26 June 2024 with data collection through 9
October 2024.
Results:
1,479 dermatologists viewed the education in this time period; 145 completed all pre- and post-assessment
questions. The average percentage of correct responses across all questions increased from 31% at baseline to
57% post-activity 
(
P
 <.001
)
, with a large educational impact 
(
Cohen
’
s d=0.94
)
. The proportion of dermatologists
answering all 3 questions correctly rose from 2% at baseline to 20% post-activity.
Dermatologists significantly improved their knowledge of survival of pathogenic T cells as a key consequence of
OX40-OX40L interaction in the pathogenesis of AD 
(
17% pre- vs 43% post-activity; 
P
 <.001
)
, the mode of action
of amlitelimab and rocatinlimab 
(
21% pre- vs 55% post-activity; 
P
 <.001
)
 and clinical data for these novel
therapies 
(
54% pre- vs 74% post-activity; 
P
 <.001
)
.
Post activity, 62% of dermatologists reported improved confidence in understanding the role of the OX40-OX40L
pathway in AD, resulting in a notable confidence shift of 109%.
Conclusion:
This online activity significantly improved dermatologists
’
 understanding of the role of the OX40-OX40L pathway
in the pathogenesis of AD and of emerging biologic therapies targeting this pathway. Although there was
significant knowledge improvement for all questions, the post test scores of 43% to 74% indicate that

dermatologists would benefit from additional education to embed their knowledge of emerging biologic
therapies targeting this pathway in AD.
