Abstract N°: 1321
Unravelling the Mechanism of Action of Rilzabrutinib in Atopic Dermatitis: An Attractive Therapeutic
Agent for the Treatment of Itch Related Conditions
Vincent Mikol*
1
, Ester Del Duca
2
, Megan Lau
2
, Joel Correa Da Rosa
2
, Yeriel D Estrada
2
, Lydie Baret Cormel
1
, Leda
Mannent
1
, Cintia Palu
1
, Vinh Truong
1, 3
, Nils Ternes
1
, Emma Guttman-Yassky
2
1
Sanofi, R&D, Paris, France
2
Icahn School of Medicine at Mount Sinai, New York, United States
3
Ividata Life Sciences, Levallois-Perret, France
Introduction & Objectives:
 Rilzabrutinib, a selective oral Bruton
’
s tyrosine kinase 
(
BTK
)
 inhibitor, was
investigated in a placebo-controlled phase-2 trial 
(
NCT05018806
)
 in adult patients with moderate-to-severe
atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
. A rapid improvement in absolute and relative change in weekly average of daily Peak
Pruritus Numerical Rating Scale 
(
PP-NRS
)
 was demonstrated with rilzabrutinib.
Materials & Methods:
 We used a minimally invasive, tape-stripping approach, to evaluate the effect of
rilzabrutinib on the cutaneous transcriptomic and proteomic profile of AD patients. We performed transcriptomic
and Olink proteomic analysis on tape-strip skin samples from patients with AD 
(
n=27 patients for transcriptomics
and n=24 for proteomics
)
 treated with rilzabrutinib 800 mg/d 
(
n=8/5
)
, 1200 mg/d 
(
n=10/10
)
 and placebo 
(
n=2
BID-7 TID/9
)
. Tape-strips were obtained from lesional 
(
LS
)
 and non-lesional 
(
NL
)
 skin at baseline and week 16.
Differentially expressed genes and proteins were defined as fold changes 
(
FCH
)
>1.5/1.3, and p-value<0.05. RNA
sequencing 
(
RNA-seq
)
, followed by gene set enrichment analysis, was carried out on the skin tape strips to
identify pathways of genes altered with rilzabrutinib.
Results:
 At week 16, we observed in LS samples downregulation of BTK related pathways, in the 1200mg/d cohort
including MHC Class1 presentation, FcgR signaling, FceR signaling, DAP12 interactions, Myd88 deficiency
(
TLR2/4
)
, Irak4 deficiency 
(
TLR2/4
)
 and G-protein beta signaling. These pathways are associated with B-cell,
granulocytes and monocytes activation. In addition, downregulation of several immune related pathways
associated with Th1, innate immunity and Th17 as well as various itch-related markers including NGF, serotonin
biosynthesis, IL-31, and NMBR pathways in LS skin were seen at week 16 after rilzabrutinib treatment, with a dose
effect. Similar downregulation of immune products was also observed in the proteomic data. Unsupervised RNA-
seq analysis showed that improvement in the PP-NRS 
(
placebo corrected
)
 was associated with the
downregulation of 31 pathways in LS skin, 6 of which are directly related to BTK. This is in line with the proteomics
data which indicates that the lesional proteome of patients receiving rilzabrutinib 1200 mg/d showed 100%
improvement from baseline towards a non-lesional profile compared to 40% in the placebo respectively
(
p<0.001
)
, whereas no significant improvement was observed in the 800mg/d group. At baseline, responders
showed downregulation in keratinization pathways, increased sensory perception and IL-12 signaling.
Conclusion:
 These mechanistic insights suggest that rilzabrutinib is an attractive therapeutic agent for the
treatment of conditions associated with a high itch burden.
