Abstract N°: 1361
Optimising consultations for patients with atopic dermatitis- a clinical controlled trial
Anna Sophie Krontoft
1
, Camilla Dohn
1
, jesper elberling
1
, Marie Johansen
1
, Maria Frahm Pors
1
, Stine Simonsen
1
,
Anne Schwartzbach
1
, Nanna Aaen
1
, Kirsten Lomborg
2
, Lone Skov
1
1
Gentofte hospital, Hellerup
2
Steno Diabetes Center Copenhagen, Herlev, Denmark
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disease that requires ongoing self-management. Managing
symptoms, treatments, and the psychological and social impact of the disease is part of everyday life for patients
with AD. However, self-management can be complex, and patients request support from healthcare professionals
(
HCPs
)
 with knowledge and experience in AD, preferably the same HCP.
Integrating agenda-setting into consultations using Conversation Cards 
(
CCs
)
 may enhance self-management. A
set of CCs has been developed for patients with AD, empowering them to actively contribute to the focus of the
consultation and receive tailored support.
The objective of this study was to investigate whether using CCs developed for patients with AD, in combination
with a designated contact nurse offering additional self-management support when needed, could positively
impact patient well-being and self-management.
Materials & Methods:
The study was designed as a prospective, clinical, non-randomised controlled trial involving 200 patients of all
ages with AD. In the intervention group, CCs were used in all consultations, and patients were assigned to two
designated contact nurses. One of the contact nurses, alongside a physician, participated in consultations with
patients. Patients could contact the nurses between consultations if they needed further support with self-
management. The primary outcome was the Well-being Index 
(
WHO-5
)
, and secondary outcomes included
measurements of quality of life, symptoms, and collaboration. All participants answered questionnaires at
baseline, six months, and 12 months.
Results:
We report the preliminary results from baseline to six months, as 12-month data is not yet available. At 6 months,
we have data from n=61 and n=74 in the control and intervention groups, respectively. The median age is 36
years 
(
range: 1-76
)
 in the control group and 27 years 
(
range: 0-76
)
 in the intervention group, with 64% of women
in the control group versus 49% in the intervention group.
We find that from baseline to six months, patients in the intervention group have a mean change in the WHO-5
score that is 6.6 points higher than that of the control group 
(
P=0.01
)
. For patients 
≥
 15 years, the WHO-5 further
increases to 7.37 points 
(
P=0.03
)
. Additionally, patients 
≥
 15 years have a higher mean increase in the
collaboration score of 6.13 points 
(
P=0.00
)
. In measuring quality of life, we found an improvement of 2.47 points
(
P=0.02
)
 in patients < 15 years.
Conclusion:
We find that using CCs in consultation, while simultaneously adding two designated contact nurses whom

patients will meet in every consultation and who can provide extra self-management support if needed, affects
patients
’
 feelings of well-being, as well as their feelings of collaboration with HCPs, particularly those 
≥
 15 years
of age. For patients < 15 years, the intervention positively affects their quality of life measurements.
