Abstract N°: 1367
Preferences for therapy and treatment outcomes in atopic dermatitis: A systematic review
Emilie Hageman Pinborg*
1
, Signe Bjørn Laursen
1
, David Thein
1
, Ida Vittrup
1
, Christoph Schlapbach
2
, Julia-Tatjana
Maul
3
, Berenice Degboe
4
, Alexander Egeberg
1
, Simon Francis Thomsen
1
, Maria Oberländer Christensen
1
1
Bispebjerg Hospital , Department of Dermatology, Copenhagen, Denmark
2
Bern University Hospital, Department of Dermatology, Bern, Switzerland
3
University Hospital of Zürich , Department of Dermatology, Zürich , Switzerland
4
National University Hospital Center Hubert Koutoukou Maga, University Clinic of Dermatology-Venerology,
Abomey-Calavi, Benin
Introduction & Objectives:
The preferences and treatment goals of AD patients, caregivers, and healthcare providers have been a research
area of increasing interest. Several different new treatments for atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 have been approved in
recent years, but the potential influence of industry funding on published patient, caregiver, and healthcare
provider preferences remains uncertain. This systematic review aimed to investigate if patient, caregiver, and
healthcare provider preferences in relation to different properties of therapies and treatment outcomes of AD
might be subject to funding bias.
Materials & Methods:
This descriptive systematic review was performed following the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews
and Meta-Analyses Guidelines 
(
PRISMA
)
. PubMed and EMBASE were searched. Studies were included if they
presented data on therapy and/or treatment outcome preferences of AD patients, caregivers, and/or healthcare
providers. The risk of bias of each study was assessed by using the Newcastle-Ottawa-Scale 
(
NOS
)
 and identifying
funding sources of the included studies. Treatments developed and supported by pharma-sponsors were
assessed to identify if review findings favored certain therapies, advocated by sponsors.
Results:
A total of 8,346 studies were identified, and 42 were included for data analysis 
(
participants, 19,839; females,
62.2%
)
. Nineteen and twenty-three studies were pharma-sponsored and non-pharma-sponsored, respectively. The
pharma-sponsored studies were of recent dates, had larger patient populations, and included discrete choice
experiments 
(
DCE
)
. Three studies used validated surveys to investigate treatment preferences. Efficacy, especially
itch relief, was preferred by patients, caregivers, and healthcare providers, and most studies supporting this
finding were pharma-sponsored. Moreover, patients were willing to deprioritize safety to achieve other treatment
goals in many pharma-sponsored studies. Studies funded by Janus kinase inhibitors 
(
JAKi
)
 producing companies
showed that patients preferred treatment by oral route rather than injection despite increased administration
frequency. Studies evaluating preferred characteristics of topical treatment were often non-pharma-sponsored.
Conclusion:
The findings of this study indicate that preference research might be biased. Therefore, healthcare providers
should be careful when using preference research to guide decision-making. More independent research is
needed, and surveys should be standardized and validated before use to provide a more complex view of research
regarding preferences and treatment goals, ensuring that findings are impartial to the researchers
’
 affiliations and

the populations studied.
Figure 1.
PRISMA flow diagram.
Figure 2.


a
. 
Preferences regarding efficacy and safety categorized into pharma-sponsored and non-pharma-sponsored
studies. 
(
b
)
 Preferences regarding efficacy and safety categorized into the number of pharma-sponsored
studies being JAKi producing and non-JAKi producing. 
(
c
)
 Preferences regarding administration of treatments
categorized into pharma-sponsored and non-pharma-sponsored studies. 
(
d
)
 Preferences regarding
administration of treatments categorized into the number of pharma-sponsored studies being JAKi
producing and non-JAKi producing.
