Gachon University, Gil Medical Center , Dermatology, Incheon, Korea, Rep. of South
A Novel Topical JAK1 Inhibitor for Atopic Dermatitis: Safety and Early Efficacy of IN-115314
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disease characterized by pruritus and recurrent eczematous
lesions. Dysregulation of the JAK/STAT signaling pathway, particularly JAK1, plays a central role in mediating type
2 cytokines such as IL-4, IL-13, IL-22, and IL-31, which contribute to the pathogenesis of AD. IN-115314 is a novel
topical selective JAK1 inhibitor, exhibiting 10
–
36-fold higher selectivity for JAK1 over other JAK family kinases in
vitro. This first-in-human clinical study aimed to evaluate the safety, pharmacokinetics 
(
PK
)
 including systemic
absorption, and preliminary efficacy of IN-115314 ointment in healthy subjects and patients with AD.
Materials & Methods:
The study included both healthy subjects and patients with mild to moderate atopic dermatitis. In the healthy
cohort, a single-dose application of 0.3% and 0.5% IN-115314 ointment was administered in groups of four
participants 
(
3 active:1 placebo
)
 per concentration, whereas the 1%, 3%, and 5% formulations were evaluated in
groups of eight participants 
(
6 active:2 placebo
)
 per concentration. For multiple-dose administration, 1%, 3%, or
5% ointment was applied twice daily for 14 days in groups of eight participants 
(
6 active:2 placebo
)
 per dose. In
the patient cohort, individuals with 3
–
30% body surface area 
(
BSA
)
 involvement and mild to moderate AD 
(
EASI
score < 23
)
 received either 1% or 3% IN-115314 ointment, or pimecrolimus cream, applied twice daily for 28 days,
with twelve participants 
(
8 active:4 comparator
)
 in each group. PK endpoints included Cmax, AUC, Tmax, and t1/2.
Efficacy assessments included changes from baseline in the Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
, Investigator
’
s
Global Assessment 
(
IGA
)
, and Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
. Safety was evaluated throughout the study,
including assessments of both systemic and local adverse events 
(
AEs
)
.
Results:
Minimal systemic absorption was observed in both cohorts. In healthy subjects, plasma concentrations were
mostly below the limit of quantification 
(
LLOQ
)
 even at the 5% group. In AD patients, steady-state plasma
concentrations above the LLOQ were observed in only 2 of 8 participants in the 1% group and 5 of 8 participants
in 3% group. Statistically significant improvements were noted in EASI, IGA, and DLQI scores from baseline
(
p<0.05
)
, as well as in the pruritus numeric rating scale 
(
NRS
)
, indicating a rapid reduction in itch. The incidence of
AEs was low, with no clinically significant systemic AEs reported. Local application site reacions were mild and

comparable to those observed with the placebo or comparator 
(
pimecrolimus
)
 treatment.
Conclusion:
IN-115314 showed favorable safety, low systemic exposure, and early signs of clinical efficacy when applied
topically to both healthy individuals and patients with AD. These findings support its further development as a
topical selective JAK1 inhibitor for the treatment of atopic dermatitis.
