Abstract N°: 1458
Improving paediatric experience in connection with injection treatment to Atopic dermatitis
Stine Halgren Pedersen*
1
, Christina Nielsen
1
, Jette Marcussen
1, 2
1
Zealand University Hospital, Roskilde, Denmark , Department of Dermatology, Roskilde
2
University of Southern Denmark, Research Unit OPEN, Odense, Denmark
Introduction & Objectives:
More and younger children with atopic eczema need injection treatment. This places different demands on us. It
can be challenging for both the family and us as staff.
Medical procedures can induce pain and anxiety in children, potentially leading to long-term psychological effects,
including fear of future treatment, needle anxiety and reduced engagement in healthcare. This study presents a
structured, ethically sound approach to injection procedures ensuring that no child undergoes an injection
without proper local anesthesia, in accordance with national and international ethical guidelines.
Materials & Methods:
A structured protocol was implemented, emphasizing patient/ families-centered care. Key components included:
•
 Plan A: Pre-procedure counseling between nurse, child, and parents to assess prior experiences and discuss pain
management preferences.
•
 Five mandatory principles: application of topical anesthetic cream, use of sucrose solution or breastfeeding for
infants, proper patient positioning, age-appropriate distraction techniques, and effective communication
strategies.
•
 Plan B: Referral to the pediatric department for nitrous oxide sedation when necessary.
Results:
The implementation of these principles improved the pediatric experience during injection treatments. Children
demonstrated reduced anxiety, improved pain management, and increased cooperation. The occurrence of
unintentional restraint decreased, mitigating procedure-related trauma.
Conclusion:
A structured, child-centered approach to pediatric injection procedures enhances both ethical and clinical
outcomes. By integrating pain management strategies and communication techniques, children receiving
injections for atopic dermatitis experienced a less distressing, more positive healthcare encounter. This model
aligns with ethical mandates prioritizing child welfare, setting a standard for best practices in dermatological care.
