Post Graduate Institute of Medical Education and Research, Dermatology, Venereology and Leprology,
Chandigarh, India
Introduction & objectives:
Atopic Dermatitis
(
AD
)
 is not limited to childhood; many patients first experience symptoms in adulthood, and a
significant portion of childhood cases persist into adulthood. This study characterises adult atopic dermatitis in the
Indian population, addressing the scarcity of specific data. The aim of the present study was to study the
clinicodemographic patterns and characteristics of adult patients having AD.
Materials & methods:
This cross-sectional observational study recruited adult AD patients aged 18 and above, diagnosed using the
Hanifin and Rajka criteria. This study investigated the clinico-demographic features of adult AD, with a focus on
disease onset, prevalence, minor clinical features, site involvement, morphological patterns, seasonal variation,
and gender differences.
Results:
We enrolled 150 patients 
(
76 males, 74 females
)
. The median age of onset of AD was 22 years 
(
IQR: 12.25 to 34
)
,
with 68% 
(
102
)
 having disease onset in adulthood. The median duration of illness was 7.5 years 
(
IQR: 4
–
15.75
years
)
.
The median IgE, AEC, and SCORAD values were 1010 IU 
(
IQR: 213.25
–
3192.00
)
, 0.33 
(
IQR: 0.20
–
0.60
)
, and 30 
(
IQR:
22
–
40.88
)
, respectively.
Disease commonly began in the head and neck 
(
32%
)
 and upper limb flexors 
(
25.33%
)
. Flexor involvement
(
elbow: 63.3%, popliteal fossa: 52%
)
 and extensor patterns 
(
elbow: 54.7%, knee: 26.7%
)
 were noted, along with
nasal sparing 
(
43.33%
)
.
Morphologies included papular 
(
40.7%
)
, follicular 
(
40%
)
, nummular 
(
34.7%
)
, lichenification 
(
38.6%
)
, and prurigo
(
20.7%
)
, with prurigo more common in older age groups 
(
p = 0.041
)
.
Two-thirds of cases had adult-onset AD; one-third had childhood-onset persisting/recurring in adulthood.
Prevalence was equal among genders, peaking in the 18
–
30 age group 
(
46.7%
)
 and lowest in the 61
–
70 years
group 
(
6%
)
.
Elevated IgE levels 
(
84%
)
 indicated extrinsic AD; 31.7% had elevated AEC.
Key features included xerosis 
(
96.7%
)
, keratosis pilaris 
(
80%
)
, intolerance to wool/solvents 
(
76%
)
, and seasonal
triggers 
(
winter: 35.3%, summer: 30.7%
)
. Family history of atopy was found in 51%, with allergic rhinitis 
(
79.3%
)
 as
the most common comorbidity.
Childhood-onset disease persisting into adulthood was associated with more itching when sweating 
(
83.8% vs
36.4%, p = 0.007
)
, cheilitis 
(
81.1% vs 52%, p = 0.008
)
, perifollicular accentuation 
(
83.8% vs 58.8%, p = 0.020
)
,

orbital darkening 
(
81.1% vs 58.8%, p = 0.044
)
, and Dennie
–
Morgan folds 
(
73% vs 44.1%, p = 0.010
)
.
Males showed more severe disease 
(
23.6% vs 9.5%, p = 0.026
)
, involving the head and neck 
(
86.8% vs 73%, p =
0.034
)
 and extensors 
(
63.2% vs 45.9%, p = 0.034
)
, as well as early-onset disease 
(
15.8% vs 5.4%, p = 0.039
)
.
Females had higher rates of non-specific hand/foot dermatitis 
(
86.8% vs 73%, p = 0.034
)
 and prurigo 
(
25.5% vs
10.8%, p = 0.103
)
.
Conclusion:
Adult atopic dermatitis 
(
ad
)
 demonstrates significant clinical and demographic heterogeneity, distinct patterns of
involvement, such as flexor/extensor distribution, site-specific morphologies, and gender-based differences, were
identified, emphasizing the need for personalized management.


