Abstract N°: 1644
Patient perspectives in moderate-to-severe chronic hand eczema: Understanding patient experience and
factors influencing treatment preference through in-depth qualitative patient interviews in the US
April W. Armstrong*
1
, Aseel Bin Sawad
2
, Nisha Hazra
3
, Sanjeev Balu
2
, Douglas Maslin
4
, Marjolaine Gauthier-
Loiselle
5
, Annie Guerin
5
1
University of California Los Angeles, Los Angeles, United States
2
LEO Pharma, Madison, United States
3
Analysis Group, Inc., London, United Kingdom
4
LEO Pharma, Maidenhead, United Kingdom
5
Analysis Group, Inc., Montreal, Canada
Introduction & Objectives:
Chronic hand eczema 
(
CHE
)
 is a form of eczema disease affecting the hands and wrists characterized by dry skin,
itch, pain, redness, cracking, flaking, blistering, and in some cases, bleeding, persisting for 
≥
 3 months or recurring
at least twice within 1 year. Due to its chronic nature, CHE can have a profound impact on patients
’
 lives and daily
activities. Currently, there are few approved treatment options for CHE globally, but several products are being
investigated in clinical trials and sometimes used off-label with varied effectiveness, safety, and modes of
administration. A better understanding of patient preferences and factors influencing treatment choices in this
population is thus required. This qualitative study evaluated treatment attributes important to adult patients with
moderate-to-severe CHE providing a basis to inform the design of a discrete choice experiment 
(
DCE
)
 survey.
Materials & Methods:
Semi-structured one-on-one qualitative interviews were conducted in adults 
(≥
18 years
)
 with moderate-to-severe
CHE recruited via a panel of geographically and demographically diverse individuals in the US. Eligible participants
self-reported 
(
1
)
 having CHE, and 
(
2
)
 inadequate response to prior topical corticosteroids or prior/current use of
systemic therapy or phototherapy for CHE. Participants were asked about their general treatment experience, and
factors of importance when choosing to start their current or most recent treatment. Additionally, participants
rated the importance of 16 attributes related to treatment effectiveness, safety, and logistical considerations 
(
1 to
5 scale, with 5 being the most important
)
.
Results:
Interviews were conducted with 15 patients 
(
mean age 44.5 years; 67% female; 53% White; 87% employed; 73%
diagnosed > 5 years ago
)
 across various CHE subtypes 
(
multiple subtypes: 60%; atopic only: 33%; other: 7%
)
. At
the time of the interviews, 67% were treated with topical corticosteroids, 33% with injectables, and 27% with oral
treatments, while phototherapy was least common 
(
7%
)
. Most participants reported their treatment helped
manage symptoms and alleviate flare-ups, though they expressed a lack of complete skin clearance. Most
participants found the frequency of treatment dosing schedules to be manageable and convenient.
When rating the importance of treatment attributes, effectiveness was consistently considered a key factor in
treatment decisions, with itch relief, skin appearance, duration of symptom improvement, and pain ranked among
the most important. Among effectiveness attributes, speed of onset was ranked lowest in importance. Safety
concerns, including risk of infections, malignancies, major cardiovascular events, were also key considerations.
Treatment logistics, including monitoring, costs, mode 
(
whether topical, oral, or injectable
)
 and frequency of

administration, were ranked as less important.
Conclusion:
Qualitative interviews highlighted effectiveness being a key driver of patient treatment preferences, followed by
safety considerations 
(
risk of infection, malignancies, and major cardiovascular events
)
. These findings provide a
basis for the design of a DCE study to quantify the relative importance of treatment attributes in moderate-to-
severe CHE.
