Abstract N°: 1800
Is Juvenile Plantar Dermatosis Associated with Atopy - a Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis
Israel Khanimov
1
, Maya Engler Markowitz
1
, Igor Snast
1
, Yehonatan Noyman
1
, Assi Levi
1
, Daniel Mimouni
1
, Shany
Sherman
1
1
Rabin Medical Center, Petah Tikva, Israel
Is Juvenile Plantar Dermatosis Associated with Atopy - a Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis
Introduction & Objectives:
Juvenile plantar dermatosis 
(
JPD
)
 is a challenging dermatological condition, clinically characterized by
erythematous patches exhibiting a characteristic shiny or glazed appearance, affecting the weight-bearing
surfaces of the feet. This condition may manifest with pruritus, tenderness, and discomfort. The precise
pathophysiology of JPD remains unclear. While studies have investigated a potential association between JPD and
atopy, current evidence remains inconclusive. Therefore, this study aims to perform a systematic review and meta-
analysis to further elucidate the association between JPD and atopy, especially in light of the estimated prevalence
of personal and familial atopy in the general population
—
10
–
30% and 20
–
40%, respectively.
Materials & Methods:
A systematic review was conducted. The literature search across PubMed, Google Scholar, ScienceDirect, CENTRAL
and ClinicalTrials.gov, covering all records from database inception to January 1, 2025. Random-effects model
meta-analysis was performed to quantify the pooled proportion 
(
with 95% confidence interval
)
 of patients with
atopic background among those diagnosed with JPD. Subgroup analyses were conducted to assess the proportion
of personal and familial atopic background. Heterogeneity was assessed using the I² statistic.
Results:
Among 958 citations identified from database searching, 25 studies with a total of 1220 subjects were included.
Among the 915 participants for whom sex was recorded, 488 
(
53%
)
 were male. The disease typically manifested
between the ages of 5 and 9 years, with a duration ranging from 3 to 10 years. In the majority of patients, the
dermatosis symmetrically affected the weight-bearing zones of both plantar surfaces, specifically the distal soles
and the plantar aspects of the toes, with sparing of the interdigital spaces.
The pooled proportion of personal atopic background among patients with JPD was 0.29 
(
22 studies; 95% CI 0.20-
0.39, I²=93.9%
)
, meaning that 1 in 3.4 patients with JPD had personal atopic background. The pooled proportion
of atopy among patients with JPD or their relatives 
(
first-degree or distant
)
 was 0.50 
(
25 studies; 95% CI 0.39-0.60,
I²=95.8%
)
, and in patients with JPD or first-degree relatives was 0.47 
(
16 studies; 95% CI 0.35-0.60, I²=98.2%
)
. Data
on IgE levels were available for a total of 77 patients across 5 studies. Of these patients, 10 
(
13%
)
 had elevated IgE
levels. Alternative diagnoses of allergic contact dermatitis and fungal infection were less likely as patch tests
yielded negative or non-relevant results and mycological tests were negative 
(
17 studies
)
.
Conclusion:
The findings of this systematic review and meta-analysis provide evidence supporting a positive association
between JPD and both personal and family history of atopy. These results may contribute to a better
understanding of the underlying pathophysiology of this condition.

Figure 1:
 Forest plot of prevalence of personal atopic background among patients with JPD
Figure 2:
 Forest plot of prevalence of atopy among patients with JPD or their relatives 
(
first-degree or distant
)
Figure 3:
 Forest plot of prevalence of atopy among patients with JPD or first-degree relatives


