Abstract N°: 1852
Dupilumab
’
s Impact on Sleep Disruptions in Atopic Dermatitis: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis
Dea Metko
1
, Shanti Mehta
2
, Grace Xiong
1
, Eric McMullen
3
, Vincent Piguet
3, 4
1
Michael G. DeGroote School of Medicine, McMaster University, Hamilton, Canada
2
Temerty Faculty of Medicine, University of Toronto, Toronto, Canada
3
Division of Dermatology, Department of Medicine, Temerty Faculty of Medicine, Toronto, Canada
4
Division of Dermatology, Women
’
s College Hospital, Toronto, Canada
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a common chronic and relapsing inflammatory condition, for which patients rank
pruritus and sleep impairment as the two most impactful factors in disease-related quality of life. In AD, scratching
is a well-recognized reason for sleep disturbance, however, other theories including dysregulated IL-4 and higher
morning serum IL-31 are correlated with poor sleep efficacy. Non-restorative sleep is in part a consequence of the
pruritus in AD. Dupilumab, a monoclonal antibody targeting the IL-4 and IL-13 signalling pathways, has
demonstrated efficacy and safety in managing AD. This systematic review and meta-analysis aims to examine
efficacy of dupilumab for improving sleep quality in individuals with AD.
Materials & Methods:
A systematic review and meta-analysis was conducted per PRISMA guidelines and registered in PROSPERO.
Statistical analysis was conducted to examine the impact of dupilumab on the Sleep Disturbance Numerical Rating
Scale 
(
NRS
)
, Pruritus NRS, and Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
 in patients with AD. A random-effects
model was used to calculate the pooled mean differences 
(
MDs
)
 with corresponding 95% confidence intervals
(
CIs
)
, with an I² statistic 
≥
 50% indicating significant heterogeneity.
Results:
Fifteen studies were identified and comprised ten retrospective cohorts, four prospective observational studies,
and one randomized controlled trial 
(
RCT
)
. Twelve studies qualified for quantitative synthesis based on outcome
homogeneity, 10/12 investigated dupilumab in 300 mg biweekly doses, and 2/12 with varied dosages. The cohort
of 2,522 patients had a mean age of 30.2 years, and a mean AD duration of 22.1 years. The mean baseline eczema
area and severity index 
(
EASI
)
 score was 27.5.
Overall, patients
’
 Sleep Disturbance NRS decreased by 5.1687 
(
95% CI: 5.8580, 4.4795, p>0.0001
)
 points on
average after dupilumab therapy. In the pediatric subgroup analyses, dupilumab had a slightly higher impact on
reducing NRS sleep scores than the adult group, with pooled MDs of 5.5128 
(
95% CI: 6.6281, 4.3975, p>0.001
)
 and
4.8122 
(
95% CI: 6.4384, 3.1859, p>0.001
)
 respectively. The difference between subgroups was not statistically
significant 
(
p=0.3239
)
, dupilumab may be comparably effective in both populations. Dupilumab was also effective
at reducing itch, with an average decrease in Pruritus NRS scores of 5.1824 
(
95% CI: 5.6666, 4.6983, p>0.0001
)
,
and improving quality of life, with an average DLQI score improvement of 12.3497 
(
95% CI: 14.6855, 10.0139,
p>0.0001
)
Conclusion:
We highlight the efficacy of dupilumab in improving Sleep Disturbance NRS, indirectly by managing AD related
pruritus. Improvements were observed in pediatric and adult populations, suggesting that dupilumab is effective

for sleep related outcomes irrespective of age. Impaired sleep due to AD can be a significant contributor to the
overall disease burden, with strong associations to chronic fatigue and insomnia, which serve as predictors of
poorer overall outcomes.
Limitations of the present study include its small sample size. Future research should ensure rigorous outcome
reporting to further investigate the role of dysregulated cytokines in sleep-related outcomes, including sleep
scores, quality, and duration. Further exploration is required to determine which demographic of patients with AD
is most likely to report improvement in sleep disturbances from dupilumab use.
