Abstract N°: 1856
Clinical Efficacy, Safety Profile, and Systemic Exposure of Benvitimod Cream 1% Twice-Daily in Pediatric
Atopic Dermatitis: Results from a Phase 3 Trial in China
Quanzhong Liu*
1
, Genhui Chen
2
1
Tianjin Medical University General Hospital, Department of Dermatology, Tianjin, China
2
Thederma Shanghai ltd., Shanghai , China
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a prevalent chronic inflammatory skin disease in children. This study evaluates the
efficacy, safety, and systemic exposure of benvitimod 
(
BEN
)
 1%, an aryl hydrocarbon receptor modulator, in
pediatric patients with moderate-to-severe AD. As the first Phase 3 trial in China focusing on BEN for pediatric AD,
this study addresses critical gaps in efficacy and safety data for this population.
Materials & Methods:
This multicenter, randomized, double-blind, vehicle-controlled Phase 3 trial 
(
CTR20231413
)
 enrolled participants
aged 2 years and older. Participants were randomized 2:1 to receive benvitimod cream 1% or vehicle twice daily
for 8 weeks. The pediatric subset 
(
aged 2
–
17 years
)
 met the Hanifin-Rajka criteria 
(
disease duration 
≥
6 months in
adolescents [12-17 years], 
≥
3 months in children [2-11 years]
)
; a baseline Investigator
’
s Global Assessment 
(
IGA
)
score of 
≥
3; and an affected body surface area 
(
BSA
)
 of 5
–
35%. Clinical efficacy endpoints included the
percentage of participants achieving a 75% reduction in the Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI75 response
rate
)
 and the percentage of participants achieving an IGA score of 0 or 1 with at least a 2-point reduction from
baseline 
(
IGA response rate
)
 at Day 56. Safety assessments included treatment-emergent adverse events 
(
TEAEs
)
,
and systemic exposure was evaluated through plasma concentrations after 56 days of treatment.
Results:
A total of 142 patients aged 2
–
17 years were randomized. At Week 8, both the primary and key secondary efficacy
endpoints were met with statistical significance in the benvitimod groups versus vehicle: EASI75 response rates
were 69.2% vs 31.0% 
(
P<0.001
)
; IGA response rates were 61.5% vs 28.6% 
(
P<0.001
)
. Notably, the treatment effect
was particularly pronounced in children aged 7
–
11 years, with EASI75 response rates of 83.9% vs 19.2% and IGA
response rates of 73.3% vs 23.1%. Further age-stratified efficacy data are presented in the table below.
TEAEs were predominantly mild to moderate, with incidence rates comparable between the benvitimod group and
vehicle group. The incidence of TEAEs across age subgroups was consistent with the overall population, with no
significant increase observed.
Systemic exposure 
(
n=10; age distribution: 4 [2-6 years], 5 [7-11 years], 1 [12-17 years]
)
 showed all plasma

concentrations below lower limit of quantification 
(
<0.1 ng/mL
)
, indicating minimal systemic absorption.
Conclusion:
This Phase 3 trial demonstrates that benvitimod cream 1% twice-daily is an effective and well-tolerated treatment
for pediatric patients 
(
2
–
17 years
)
 with moderate-to-severe AD. Significant improvements in EASI75 and IGA
response rates were observed, particularly in children aged 7
–
11 years. The favorable safety profile, with mostly
mild or moderate TEAEs and minimal systemic exposure, supports its use as a promising topical therapy for
pediatric AD.
