Abstract N°: 1880
Atopic Dermatitis with Prurigo Nodularis-like Lesions: Effective Response to Dupilumab in a Pediatric
Patient
Isabel Gainza Apraiz
1
, Nekane Martínez Peña
1
, Leopoldo Fernandez Domper
2
, Laura Blanch Rius
1
, Marc Juliá-
Manresa
1
, Rosa María Izu Belloso
1
1
Basurto University Hospital, Dermatology, Bilbao, Spain
2
Mendaro Hospital, Dermatology, Mendaro, Spain
Introduction & Objectives:
Prurigo nodularis 
(
PN
)
 is a chronic inflammatory disease of heterogeneous etiology, primarily affecting middle-
aged adults, though it is not uncommon in children. It often arises in the context of atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 or its
comorbidities. It is characterized by highly pruritic lesions and chronic scratching. Treatment is largely
unsatisfactory in most cases despite multiple therapeutic approaches. In recent years, Dupilumab has become
available for the treatment of PN in adults, marking the first drug specifically approved for this condition.
Materials & Methods:
Case report and literature review.
Results:
We present a 9-year-old female patient with a history of AD, asthma, allergic rhinoconjunctivitis, allergies to dust
mites, pollen, dog and cat epithelium, and multiple food allergies. Additionally, she had obesity under
endocrinological management and an anxiety syndrome undergoing psychological treatment. The patient had
mild AD from the first year of life, which was controlled with topical treatments. Over the past year, she developed
papulo-nodular, hyperkeratotic, highly pruritic lesions affecting the back of her arms and hands, as well as the
anterior aspect of her legs. Based on these findings, the patient was diagnosed with PN in the context of AD.
Treatment included potent corticosteroids under occlusion, oral antihistamines, oral corticosteroids, and narrow
band UVB, with no improvement in neither pruritus nor the skin lesions. Eventually, Dupilumab treatment was
initiated, resulting in a rapid improvement in both lesions and pruritus, which persisted at 24 weeks follow-up
without any side effect.
Conclusion:
Dupilumab represents a new effective and safe treatment for atopic dermatitis, and in our experience for atopic
dermatitis with a prurigo nodularis phenotype in children, as demonstrated in the case of this patient.
