Abstract N°: 1911
Online case-based education improved dermatologists
’
 knowledge, competence and confidence in
diagnosing patients with chronic hand eczema.
Elaine Bell
1
, Marinella Calle
1
, Ana Giménez-Arnau
2
1
Medscape Global Education, London, United Kingdom
2
SKiN Hospital del Mar Research Institute, Universität Pompeu Fabra, Barcelona, Spain
Introduction & Objectives:
Chronic hand eczema 
(
CHE
)
 is a challenging condition to recognize and accurately diagnose. We assessed
whether online education could improve dermatologists
’
 knowledge, competence and confidence in diagnosing
patients with this condition.
Materials & Methods:
Dermatologists participated in an online text-based Test and Teach activity entitled 
‘
Can you diagnose and
manage these patients with itchy and painful hands and wrists?
’
 The activity featured two patient cases with
questions that 
“
tested
”
 knowledge and discussion of the main 
“
teaching
”
 points. Educational effect was assessed
using a repeated-pair design, pre-/post-assessment. A paired samples t-test was conducted for significance
testing on overall average number of correct responses and for confidence rating, and a McNemar
’
s test was
conducted at the question level 
(
5% significance level, P <.05
)
. Cohen
’
s d with correction for paired samples
estimated the effect size of the education on number of correct responses 
(
<.20 modest, .20-.49 small, .59-.79
moderate, 
≥
.80 large
)
. Data were collected from 23 July to 19 September 2024.
Results:
1,237 dermatologists took the activity in the defined time period, and of these, 122 completed both pre and
post questions
Overall, this educational activity significantly improved dermatologists
’
 knowledge and competence in the
diagnosis of CHE 
(
54% average correct answers pre-activity, 92% post-activity; 
P
 <.001
)
 and had a large
educational impact 
(
Cohen
’
s d=1.51
)
Dermatologists 
(
n=122
)
 showed significant gains in knowledge regarding:
The role of validated patient-reported outcome measures when CHE is suspected 
(
20% correct answer
pre-activity, 83% post-activity; 
P
 < .001
)
Dermatologists 
(
n=122
)
 also showed significant gains in competence regarding:
Differential diagnosis of CHE 
(
61% correct answer pre-activity, 96% post-activity; 
P
 < .001
)
The position of epicutaneous patch testing in the diagnostic pathway 
(
80% correct answer pre-activity,
98% post-activity; 
P
 < .001
)
The percentage of dermatologists answering all 3 questions correctly rose from 8% at baseline to 80% post-
activity
 
 
34% of dermatologists reported a measurable increase in confidence in diagnosing & managing patients
with CHE, with an average confidence shift 
(
for those who increased confidence
)
 of 38%
Conclusion:
This interactive 
‘
test then teach
’
 activity was highly successful in educating dermatologists about recognizing the

signs of CHE & making a differential diagnosis. Dermatologists would likely benefit from further education to
reinforce and embed their knowledge and competence, and to support confidence in early diagnosis of CHE in
order to initiate effective therapies in a timely manner.
