Centre Hospitalier Universitaire de Brest, Service de Dermatologie, Brest, France
6
Centre Hospitalier Universitaire de Toulouse, Service de Dermatologie, Toulouse, France
7
Sorbonne Université, INSERM, Institut Pierre Louis d
’
Epidémiologie et de Santé Publique, AP-HP. Sorbonne
Université, Hôpital Tenon, Service de Dermatologie et d
’
Allergologie, Paris, France
8
Hôpital Hôtel-Dieu, Centre de Pathologies Professionnelles et Environnementales, AP-HP, Paris, France
Introduction & Objectives:
Chronic hand eczema 
(
CHE
)
 is a persistent inflammatory skin disease that significantly impairs patients
’
 quality of
life, particularly among individuals in high-risk occupations. Often historically misdiagnosed as atopic dermatitis,
CHE has long been subject to inconsistent and suboptimal management strategies. Recent advances in our
understanding of its epidemiology, socio-economic burden, and pathophysiology have highlighted the urgent
need for more targeted and effective treatment approaches.
This expert panel of French dermatologists and allergologists sought to review and update key clinical aspects of
CHE and develop a practical treatment algorithm for daily clinical use in France, rooted in both current evidence
and real-world practice.
Materials & Methods:
Over a six-month period, including a virtual meeting held in October 2024, a panel of seven French
dermatologists and allergologists engaged in structured discussions to explore the multifaceted nature of CHE.
The goal was to consolidate current evidence and clinical experience in order to develop a therapeutic algorithm
adapted to real-life dermatological practice in France.
Results:
The expert panel conducted an in-depth review of the latest data on CHE, reaffirming the disease
’
s complex
etiology, high prevalence in occupational settings, and considerable psychosocial and economic burden. An
agreement was reached on updated diagnostic criteria, emphasizing the importance of thorough clinical
evaluation, occupational history, and appropriate use of skin and patch testing. Subtypes such as irritant, allergic,
atopic, and protein contact dermatitis were discussed, with particular attention to overlapping forms and
diagnostic challenges in clinical practice.
Treatment pathways were critically appraised, distinguishing between approved and off-label therapies currently
available in France. The panel highlighted the emerging role of new targeted therapies, in managing moderate-to-
severe CHE. Preventive strategies, including patient education on irritant and allergen avoidance, were also
emphasized.

The result of these discussions was the development of a stepwise, pragmatic therapeutic algorithm tailored to
daily dermatological practice in France. This algorithm integrates disease severity, clinical phenotype, patient
response, current treatment options, and therapies that are soon to be available, and reflects both current
evidence and expert opinion.
Conclusion:
This expert opinion not only provides a practical treatment algorithm but also reinforces the need for earlier
recognition, multidisciplinary collaboration, and improved care strategies for CHE in everyday clinical practice.
