Introduction & Objectives:
Patient education in atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 has been shown to improve medication adherence, severity of disease,
and quality of life.1 One goal of our international survey was to determine the educational needs and support
needed as treatments and outcomes continue to evolve. Global perspectives were gathered patients and
caregivers residing in the US, Europe, Japan and the Gulf region.
Materials & Methods:
A cross-sectional anonymous survey was conducted online in the winter of 2024/5 and analyzed using the Survey
Monkey platform. Ethics exemption in the US was from BRANY IRB. Consented 1103 eligible respondents included
individuals aged 
≥
18 reporting a diagnosis of AD 
(
68%
)
 or being a caregiver of children aged 6-12 with AD 
(
32%
)
residing in the US 
(
56%
)
, France 
(
13%
)
, Germany 
(
12%
)
, UAE 
(
8%
)
, and Saudi Arabia 
(
5%
)
 and Japan 
(
6%
)
.
Results:
A total of 66% of patients and caregivers said they/the child had been diagnosed with AD within the last five
years. 75% had symptoms at least once a month, with 50% reporting daily to weekly symptoms 
(
Figure, A
)
. When
asked about managing their symptoms, 46% reported finding it difficult or very difficult 
(
Figure, B
)
. Respondents
both spent considerable time thinking about their AD 
(
62% reporting constantly or regular thoughts
)
 and were
clearly impacted by their AD, citing anxiety 
(
46%
)
, fatigue 
(
31%
)
, getting a good sleep 
(
29%
)
, depression 
(
28%
)
,
and ability to work 
(
27%
)
 as considerations.
It was positive to see that 61% of respondents found it easy or very easy to find information on AD; however,
other responses indicate interest in improving AD educational content. 46% of respondents would find it
beneficial to have a list of questions to use when talking to their healthcare team; in addition, 42% want to know
more about AD triggers, 40% a way to communicate to their doctor the impact of AD on daily life and mental
wellbeing; 39% seek information on what to bring to doctor
’
s appointments and 39% want more information on
the causes of AD.
With respect to new medications, more than 50% of patients and caregivers wish to understand safety and
duration of treatment information 
(
both >50%
)
. Similarly, respondents prioritize education on the mechanism of
action 
(
54%
)
 and safety and side effects 
(
53%
)
 as the most important factors in determining if they would try a
new AD drug.
Conclusion:
Patient and caregiver education in AD is essential for improving disease management and often-compromised

quality of life. While access to disease information in participants was is reasonably high, there is a clear
opportunity to refine content and provide actionable, patient-centered tools. Empowering patients and caregivers
with evidence-based, practical education 
—
 especially tools that facilitate doctor-patient communication at visits,
treatment options, and coping with emotional health and quality of life impacts 
—
 could enhance adherence,
reduce disease burden, and foster better health outcomes globally.
The survey was funded by GAAPP, supported by an educational grant from Sanofi.
