Abstract N°: 2007
Local Tolerability of Benvitimod Cream 1% Twice Daily in Adults and Children with Atopic Dermatitis:
Results from a Phase 3 Randomized Trial
Quanzhong Liu*
1
, Genhui Chen
2
1
Tianjin Medical University General Hospital, Tianjin, China
2
Thederma Shanghai ltd., Shanghai, China
Introduction & Objectives:
Benvitimod cream 1% twice-daily demonstrated significant efficacy in patients down to age 2 years with atopic
dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 in a phase 3 trial. Here, we report local tolerability outcomes.
Materials & Methods:
In this trial, patients were randomized to receive either benvitimod cream or vehicle cream twice daily for 8 weeks.
Local tolerability was assessed using the Local Tolerability Scale 
(
LTS
)
, which measures the frequency and severity
of local irritation symptoms, including burning/stinging, itching, and erythema. The LTS ranges from 0 
(
none
)
 to 3
(
severe
)
.
Results:
A total of 272 patients were randomized, with over 50% being pediatric participants. The highest LTS scores were
recorded on Day 7 of treatment, after which scores decreased. On Day 7, the mean LTS scores for the treatment
and control groups were 0.45 ± 0.714 and 0.55 ± 0.803, respectively. On Day 7, 15 participants 
(
8.3%
)
 in the
treatment group had an LTS score of 2, 1 participant 
(
0.6%
)
 had a score of 2.5, and 2 participants 
(
1.1%
)
 had a
score of 3. In the control group, 11 participants 
(
13.1%
)
 had an LTS score of 2, none had a score of 2.5, and 2
participants 
(
2.4%
)
 had a score of 3. Median LTS scores for both groups remained at 0 on Days 7, 14, 28, 56, and
84. Overall, LTS scores were comparable between the treatment and control groups, indicating good local
tolerability.
Conclusion:

Benvitimod cream demonstrated excellent local tolerability from the first application through Week 8, including in
sensitive skin areas. These findings support its use in the management of atopic dermatitis in both adults and
children.
