Abstract N°: 2040
Incorporating the QALYs of Comorbidities into Cost-Utility Analysis of Atopic Dermatitis
Leyi Huang
1
, Xiang Chen
1
, meng li
2
, Juan Su
1
, Yi Xiao*
1
1
Department of Dermatology, Xiangya Hospital, Central South University, Changsha, China
2
Center for the Evaluation of Value and Risk in Health, Institute for Clinical Research and Health Policy Studies,
Tufts Medical Center, Boston, United States
Incorporating the QALYs of Comorbidities into Cost-Utility Analysis of Atopic Dermatitis
Introduction & Objectives:
Comorbidities significantly impact patients
’
 health and economic burden. Certain diseases and their comorbidities
share similar pathogenesis, enabling some treatments to address multiple conditions simultaneously. However,
conventional quality-adjusted life-year 
(
QALY
)
 assessments focus on single indications, often derived from clinical
trials with limited comorbidity representation, thus underestimating broader therapeutic benefits.
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 exemplifies this challenge due to its heterogeneous comorbidities. AD has some associated
atopic comorbidities, which are demonstrated to related to the atopic march. Some monoclonal antibodies can
bring benefits to these kind diseases at the same time. Another major category of AD comorbidities shares a
similar pathogenesis with autoimmune diseases. Janus Kinase 
(
JAK
)
 inhibitors show good efficacy in these
diseases.
This study introduces total QALY 
(
T-QALY
)
 to capture a drug
’
s cumulative effectiveness across multiple
indications, using AD as a case study. We compare the T-QALY of dupilumab and upadacitinib to provide a
comprehensive value assessment for AD patients with comorbidities.
Materials & Methods:
According to indications of dupilumab and upadacitinib and the accessibility of data, ten comorbidities were
included in this case study for either dupilumab or upadacitinib, specifically allergic rhinitis, asthma, chronic
rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps, alopecia areata, eosinophilic esophagitis, rheumatoid arthritis, ulcerative colitis,
Crohn
’
s disease, psoriatic arthritis, and ankylosing spondylitis. We extracted QALYs of drugs for different diseases
from previous cost-effectiveness analyses. T-QALY was calculated by the QALY for AD, the QALYs for
comorbidities, and the prevalences of comorbidities among AD patients. Cost data were sourced from published
literature and Chinese clinical guidelines. The incremental cost-effectiveness ratio 
(
ICER
)
 was calculated using T-
QALYs and costs, then compared to the willingness-to-pay 
(
WTP
)
 threshold.
Results:
The T-QALY of dupilumab was calculated to be higher than that of upadacitinib, at 16.73 and 14.19, respectively.
The ICER for dupilumab versus upadacitinib was ¥52,482 in China. At the China
’
s 2024 GDP per capita threshold,
dupilumab exhibited an 87.53% probability of cost-effectiveness. This probability increased with higher WTP
thresholds, reinforcing dupilumab
’
s economic advantage.
Conclusion:
This study proposes a T-QALY model to evaluate treatment efficacy across syndemics, using AD as an example.
Dupilumab demonstrates cost-effectiveness compared to upadacitinib when accounting for comorbidities
’
 QALYs.

The T-QALY model supports personalized treatment selection for AD patients with diverse comorbidities. Future
applications could involve stratifying patients by comorbidity profiles, enabling physicians to leverage T-QALY for
tailored clinical decision-making across various therapeutic areas.
