Regeneron Pharmaceuticals, Inc., Tarrytown, United States
Introduction & Objective:
 Most patients with moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 have elevated levels of
total IgE, an allergen sensitization marker that contributes to the pathophysiology of other atopic conditions such
as food allergies and asthma. In AD, IgE sensitization is associated with increased severity and flares, and also has
been associated with atopic comorbidity development, especially in young children 
(
atopic march
)
. The aim of this
analysis is to report total IgE level differences at study baseline and after dupilumab treatment in different age
cohorts of 0.5 to <6-year-old children with moderate-to-severe AD.
Materials & Methods:
 In** LIBERTY AD PRESCHOOL 
(
NCT03346434
)
, children with moderate-to-severe AD aged
<6 years received dupilumab or placebo, both with topical corticosteroids 
(
TCS
)
, for 16 weeks. Baseline and Week
16 total IgE levels are reported, stratified by age cohort: 0.5 to <2 years, 
≥
2 to <4 years, and 
≥
4 to <6 years. A
mixed-effect model for repeated measures was used to compare the IgE ratio and IgE difference between Week
16 and baseline in dupilumab and placebo groups.
Results:
 At baseline, mean IgE levels 
(
kU/L
)
 showed age-cohort
–
dependent increase in both dupilumab 
(
n = 71
)
and placebo 
(
n = 69
)
 treatment arms: 0.5 to <2 years, 3,481.8 and 636.3; 
≥
2 to <4 years, 5,736.4 and 5,066.7; 
≥
4
to <6 years, 10,046.0 and 9,044.9, respectively. At Week 16, dupilumab + TCS vs placebo + TCS significantly
reduced IgE levels in children aged 0.5 to <2 years 
(
275.9 vs 918.0
)
, and in those aged 
≥
2 to <4 years 
(
1,233.3 vs
4,960.0
)
, and 
≥
4 to <6 years 
(
3,932.8 vs 9,510.4
)
. Overall, in young children, mean IgE levels were significantly
reduced by 70% in those treated with dupilumab + TCS 
(
P
 < 0.001
)
, while in those treated with placebo + TCS, IgE
levels increased by 30% 
(
P
 = 0.03
)
.
Conclusion:
 IgE levels increased with age in young children with moderate-to-severe AD managed with placebo +
TCS. In contrast, dupilumab + TCS treatment significantly decreased IgE levels and curbed IgE level increases in all
tested age cohorts. Given that total IgE progressively increases with increasing age in young children, early

intervention with dupilumab to reduce IgE levels in children with moderate-to-severe AD may contribute to
lowering the risk of developing atopic comorbidities.
