Comparative real-world analysis of national variations in advanced systemic therapy initiation in atopic
dermatitis across the UK
Piru Pathmarajah*
1
, Sarah Elyoussfi
2
, Nora Makary
3
, Lowri Edwards
2
, Zhi Lim
4
, Dalia Eid
5
, Vijaytha Muralidharan
5
,
Puo Nen Lim
6
, Wei Chern Gavin Fong
7
, Erma Qazi
8
, Kit Gallagher
9
, Maria-Angeliki Gkini
4
, Antoaneta Grunova
1
1
Mid and South Essex NHS Trust , Basildon, United Kingdom
2
Salford Royal NHS Trust , Manchester, United Kingdom
3
Lewisham and Greenwich NHS Trust, London, United Kingdom
4
Barts Health NHS Trust, London, United Kingdom
5
Aneurin Bevan University Health Board, Newport, United Kingdom
6
NHS Greater Glasgow and Clyde, Glasgow, United Kingdom
7
Nottingham University Hospitals NHS Trust, Nottingham, United Kingdom
8
University Hospitals Birmingham NHS Trust, Solihull, United Kingdom
9
University of Oxford, Oxford, United Kingdom
Introduction & Objectives:
Advanced systemic therapies, including biologic drugs and Janus kinase inhibitors, have revolutionized atopic
dermatitis management. The National Institue for Health and Care Excellence 
(
NICE
)
 has published their guidance
on atopic dermatitis, yet there seems to be national variations how patients access advanced systemic therapies.
This work focuses on describing and comparing traditional immunosupressant 
(
IS
)
 and advanced systemtic
therapies prescribing practice and potential delay of advanced systemic therapy inititiation between district
general hospitals versus specialist centres for patients with atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 in the UK. 
Materials & Methods:
This was a retrospective, multi-site study across 7 centres in the UK. Clinical records were reviewed from
November 2024 and the following data were recorded: demographic data, number and type of
immunosuppressant, choice of advanced systemic therapy, timeline of switching from immunosuppressant to
advanced systemic therapy and factors for delay in switching. A psoriasis cohort group has been included for
comparison to characterise the potential delay in switching to an advanced systemic therapy. Descriptive statistics
and relevant statistical models were used. A p-value <0.05 was deemed statistically significant.
Results:
This study included 350 patients from 7 different centres 
(
4 district general hospitals where AD patients are seen in
general dermatology clinics and 3 specialist centres with a dedicated AD clinic often having resources and
protocols optimized for AD management
)
 across the UK. The mean age was 43.9 years 
(
median age 42 years,
interquartile range 22 years
)
. This study included 56.2% male and 44.6% White patients. There was no significant
difference between age and gender distributions between the district general hospitals and the specialist centres.
A range of different immunosuppressants were used: methotrexate, ciclosporin and phototherapy 
(
in decreasing
order of frequency
)
 for both AD and psoriasis 
(
Figure 1
)
. Patients with AD spent half as long on
immunosuppressants than patients with psoriasis 
(
2.54 years vs 5.02 years
)
 
(
Figure 1
)
. There were significant
differences in side effects experienced on the different immunosuppressants 
(
Table 2
)
. Specialist centres
experienced significantly shorter delays in switching patients from immunosuppressants to advanced systemic

therapy 
(
1.9 months, compared to 3.7 months in district general hospitals, p-value =0.0027
)
 
(
Figure 3
)
. Homecare
(
a service where medicines can be directly delivered to the patient
’
s home address or care homes
)
 was the most
common reason for this delay which was significantly longer and more frequent for district general hospitals.
Conclusion:
Patients with AD at specialist centres experience a shorter delay before being initiated on an advanced systemic
therapy 
(
1.9 months vs 3.7 months
)
. Specialist centres also have significantly fewer homecare delays. This study
provides real life data demonstrating that in general, access and initiation of advanced systemic therapy differs
across the UK and is quicker in dedicated AD clinics where patient flow is more coordinated through regular
multidisciplinary team meetings and efficient setup of home care services which can impact treatment goal and
patient satisfaction.
