Abstract N°: 2110
Use of Dupilumab in Patients with Severe Atopic Dermatitis and History of Liver Transplantation: Report of
Two Cases.
Marina Gagliardi*
1
, María Walker
1
, Carla Castro
1
1
Austral University Hospital, Dermatology, Pilar Centro, Argentina
Introduction & Objectives:
Severe atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 in liver transplant recipients presents a major therapeutic challenge, as standard
systemic treatments are often contraindicated due to immunosuppression and hepatic dysfunction. The objective
of this report is to highlight the effectiveness and safety of Dupilumab in treating severe AD in this unique patient
population through two illustrative clinical cases.
Materials & Methods:
Two liver-transplanted patients with severe, treatment-refractory AD were evaluated. Both were under chronic
immunosuppressive therapy and had extensive disease not controlled with conventional treatments. Clinical
scores including EASI, SCORAD, IGA, Pruritus and Sleep NRS, DLQI, and BSA were recorded before and during
treatment with Dupilumab.
Results:
Case 1: A 27-year-old male with a history of two liver transplants presented with widespread lichenified
eczema, bacterial superinfections, and severe pruritus. Initial scores: EASI 30.3, SCORAD 53, IGA 4, Pruritus
NRS 10, Sleep NRS 8, DLQI 12. After 5 weeks of Dupilumab, there was a marked improvement: EASI 5.2,
Pruritus NRS 2, Sleep NRS 0, DLQI 1.
Case 2: A 20-year-old female developed severe AD after liver transplantation for autoimmune hepatitis.
Baseline: EASI 48.9, SCORAD 87.58, IGA 4, Pruritus/Sleep NRS 10, DLQI 19, BSA 92.9%. Under Dupilumab, the
disease significantly improved: EASI 1, SCORAD 7.6, NRS 0, BSA 3%.
Conclusion:
Dupilumab demonstrated a favorable safety profile and remarkable clinical efficacy in liver transplant recipients
with severe AD, offering a promising alternative when conventional therapies are contraindicated. Its mechanism,
independent of hepatic metabolism and targeting the Th2 axis, makes it especially suitable in this context. A
multidisciplinary approach was key to successful treatment implementation.
