17 SEPTEMBER - 20 SEPTEMBER 2025 
POWERED BY M-ANAGE.COM 


Abstract N°: 2142
Tolerance, efficacy, and quality of life of a dermocosmetic in subjects with atopic dermatitis and skin
disorders associated with dryness in South Africa
Tarryn Jacobs*
1
, Neo Sekgoma-Segaetsho
2
, Chioma Ede
3
, Karen Mosojane
4
, Kim Wiid
5
1
Private Practice, Pretoria, South Africa
2
Private Practice, Gaborone, Botswana
3
University of Witwatersrand, Helen Joseph Hospital , Johannesburg, South Africa
4
University of Botswana, Gabarone , Botswana
5
Loreal Dermatological Beauty, Johannesburg, South Africa
Introduction & Objectives:
Patients with dermatological conditions like atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
, eczema,  senile xerosis, psoriasis, and severe
xerosis often experience dry skin symptoms, including pruritus, pain, and discomfort. Regular use of emollients
play a pivotal role in restoring the skin barrier leading to improved dryness and skin discomfort. This study
assessed the local tolerance and efficacy of an 
‘
emollient plus
’
 dermocosmetic 
(
DC
)
, which contains ingredients
including Aqua Posae Filiformis 
(
APF
)
, microresyl, shea butter, and niacinamide, on the symptoms and quality of
life 
(
QOL
)
 in subjects with  AD and other dermatologic conditions with skin dryness.
Materials & Methods:
This was an open-label, observational study conducted over 2 months to assess the effect of a DC in subjects
above 16 years of age with AD and other skin diseases with dryness across different skin phototypes. The
participants were prescribed the emollient plus DC for once- or twice-daily application.  A dermatologist
assessment was conducted at week 8 on key metrics, including skin dryness, disease severity, pruritus, sleep
quality, and daily discomfort. Response to treatment was defined as at least one severity grade decrease on a five-
point scale.
 
 
 
The QOL was assessed using DLQI. To evaluate patient satisfaction, tolerance, and cosmetic acceptability,
dermatologist assessments, and patient self-assessments were conducted.
Results:
This study included 198 participants, the majority of whom were female 
(
99.4%
)
 
(
mean age ± SD: 31.5 ± 10.6
years
)
. Most patients suffered from severe xerosis 
(
67.4%
)
 and AD 
(
7.7%
)
 and patients with senile xerosis 
(
1.1%
)
and psoriasis 
(
0.6%
)
 were included. The subjects had phototypes II-VI, of which phototype V 
(
48.9%
)
 was the main
phototype observed. Most of the participants applied the emollient 
(
63.5%
)
 twice a day.
After 8 weeks of emollient use, there was a statistically significant improvement 
(
p<0.001
)
 in efficacy based on
evaluation by dermatologists compared to baseline for the parameters. Specifically, 93.3% of patients with at least
mild intensity at inclusion observed an improvement of at least one grade in their disease severity. Similarly, for
94.6% and 92.4% of patients with skin dryness and pruritus, an improvement was observed in at least one grade,
respectively.  For 95.5% of patients, there was an improvement in inflammatory lesions of at least one grade.
Regarding daily discomfort and quality of sleep, 98.1% and 95.0% of patients exhibited improvement in at least
one grade. Finally, after 8 weeks of use of the DC, the mean intensity of dryness and desquamation were reduced
by at least 91.2%.

A significant improvement was found in the mean intensities of parameters including skin pain 
(
95.3%
)
, burning
sensation 
(
92.5%
)
, tingling sensation 
(
92.7%
)
, numbness sensation 
(
88.0%
)
, itching sensation 
(
94.1%
)
, and sleep
quality 
(
96.1%
)
.
The QOL significantly 
(
p<0.001
)
 improved with a mean burden reduction of 96.8%, changing on average from
moderate to no impact. Overall tolerance was rated excellent by 97.0% of patients and 99.0% of investigators.
Conclusion:
The study demonstrates that a specifically formulated emollient plus DC had an improvement in the symptoms of
AD and other dermatologic conditions associated with skin dryness and severe xerosis across ages and
phototypes. Quality of life had remarkably improved and the DC was very well tolerated.
