Abstract N°: 2184
Antipruritic Efficacy of Azathioprine in Patients with Atopic Dermatitis
Yingyu Ding
1
, kochergin nikolay
1
, Kristina Myzina
1
1
Sechenov University, dermatology, moscow
Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disease characterized by
relapsing episodes of eczematous lesions and persistent pruritus. Its development is driven by a multifactorial
interplay of genetic susceptibility, impairment of the epidermal barrier, immune system dysregulation, and
environmental influences. The global prevalence of AD is estimated at 15
–
20% in children and around 10% in
adults.
Effective management of AD requires a comprehensive strategy that includes routine skin care, topical anti-
inflammatory agents, and, in more severe or refractory cases, systemic immunosuppressants such as cyclosporine
A, methotrexate, and azathioprine. Although azathioprine is commonly employed in clinical practice, its specific
role in mitigating AD-associated pruritus remains inadequately defined.
This study aimed to evaluate the antipruritic efficacy of azathioprine in patients with moderate-to-severe AD,
utilizing the Pruritus Index 
(
PI
)
 as a standardized quantitative measurement.
Materials & Methods:
 We collected data from five patients diagnosed with moderate to severe atopic dermatitis,
including four males and one females aged 18 to 29 years. All patients met established diagnostic criteria for
atopic dermatitis and presented with persistent, treatment-resistant pruritus. Each patient received systemic
therapy with oral azathioprine at a dosage of 50 mg twice daily for seven consecutive days.
Throughout the study period, pruritus intensity was assessed using the Pruritus Index 
(
PI
)
 on a 0
–
10 scale,
recorded three times daily 
(
morning, afternoon, evening
)
. In addition, all patients underwent laboratory
evaluations, including complete blood count 
(
CBC
)
, serum biochemistry.
Results:
 During the 7-day azathioprine therapy, significant reductions in pruritus severity were observed across all
time intervals. Morning PI scores decreased from an average of 7.67 to 2.67 
(
65.2% reduction
)
, afternoon scores
from 7.00 to 2.67 
(
61.9% reduction
)
, and evening scores from 7.00 to 3.33 
(
52.4% reduction
)
.

Conclusion:
 Azathioprine provided rapid and significant relief of refractory pruritus in moderate-to-severe AD,
demonstrating both antipruritic and immunomodulatory effects. These results support its potential role in
combination regimens for immune-mediated dermatoses. However, patient-specific considerations and regular
monitoring remain essential to ensure safety and maximize therapeutic benefit.
EADV Congress 2025, PARIS
17 SEPTEMBER - 20 SEPTEMBER 2025 
POWERED BY M-ANAGE.COM 


Abstract N°: 2196
Effectiveness and Safety of individual dosing alternatives of Upadacitinib in atopic dermatitis real-world
population
Lauffer Felix*
1
, Andreas Pinter
2
, Katharina Kreutzer
3
, Uwe Schwichtenberg
4
, Melanie Huber
5, 6
, Fatima Abousamra
6
,
Tobias Hagemann
7
, Stephan Weidinger
8
1
Department of Dermatology, Ludwig-Maximilians University Hospital, Munich, Germany
2
University Hospital Frankfurt, Department of Dermatology, Venereology and Allergology, Frankfurt am Main,
Germany
3
Derpartment of Dermatology, Venereology and Allergology, Bielefeld hospital, Bielefeld, Germany
4
Derma-Nord Hautarztpraxen Dr. Schwichtenberg, Bremen, Germany
5
Private University in the Principality of Liechtenstein 
(
UFL
)
, Triesen, Liechtenstein
6
AbbVie Deutschland GmbH & Co. KG, Wiesbaden, Germany
7
Dermatology Practice, Lippstadt, Germany
8
Department of Dermatology and Allergy, University Hospital Schleswig-Holstein, Kiel, Germany
Objectives:
In clinical trials, Upadacitinib
’
s efficacy was assessed separately for two doses 
(
15 and 30mg daily
)
. Real-world
data comparing the effectiveness and safety of different dosing regimens 
(
15mg, 30mg, flexible
)
 are lacking. This
analysis compares these dosing schemes.
Methods:
The analysis included patients with 6 months of follow-up and full documentation of Upadacitinib dosage at each
visit, as well as those who discontinued within the first 6 months. Results are reported as observed. Patients were
stratified into three groups based on their 6-month dosing: always 15mg, always 30mg, and flexible dosing for
those with at least one dose change.
Results:
A total of 361 patients were analyzed, with 81 patients on a constant 15mg dose, 199 consistently on 30mg, and
81 in the flexible dosing group 
(
12 started with 15mg, 69 with 30mg
)
. Reason and type of Upadacitinib medication
change is shown in Table 1.
The always 30mg group had more men 
(
62.3% vs. 43.2%
)
 and longer disease duration 
(
25.9 vs. 22.2 years
)
 than
the 15mg group. At baseline, patients in the always 30mg group had a numerically higher mean Eczema Area and
Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
 23.5 vs. 21.6 
(
always 15mg
)
 and 19.5 
(
flexible dosing
)
. At 1 month, mean EASI was 4.5 for
both constant dosing groups and 2.8 for the flexible dosing group. Similar tendencies were observed at month 3.
At month 1, 70.9% of the flexible dosing group achieved EASI 
≤
 3, compared to 57.7% and 58.6% for the always
15 mg and 30 mg groups, respectively. At month 3, this trend continued with 70.3% and 71.0% in the always 15
mg and 30 mg groups, respectively, versus 86.7% in the flexible dosing group.
Baseline mean Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
 was significantly higher in patients constantly receiving the
30mg dose 14.8 vs. 13.0 
(
always 15mg
)
 vs. 12.6 
(
flexible dosing
)
. At month 1, 44.9% of the patients in the flexible
dosing group achieved DLQI 0/1, compared to 32.8% and 32.2% in the always 15 mg and always 30 mg groups,
respectively. At month 3, DLQI 0/1 rates were 43.9%, 40.1%, and 47.9% for the always 15 mg, always 30 mg, and

flexible dosing groups, respectively.
No significant differences in itch were noted across groups at baseline 
(
WP-NRS ~7
)
. WP-NRS0/1 responses at
month 1 were similar 
(
~50%
)
 across all dosing schemes. However, by months 3 and 6, the flexible dosing group
showed a numerically higher WP-NRS0/1 rate, though not statistically significant.
386 adverse events 
(
AEs
)
 were reported in total. The most frequent AEs were dermatitis atopic, acne, and
nasopharyngitis. At least one AE was reported in 32 
(
39.5%
)
, 90 
(
45.2%
)
, and 49 
(
60.5%
)
 patients in the 15mg,
30mg and flexible dosing group, respectively. No serious AE was reported in the 15mg group, whereas 5 
(
2.5%
)
and 2 
(
2.5%
)
 patients reported serious AE in the 30mg and flexible dosing groups, respectively.
Conclusion:
There was a tendency towards higher disease burden in patients treated with the higher dose. Patients in the
flexible dosing group had slightly higher response rates at month 1, the rapid response led in 68% of the patients
to a dose reduction within the first 6 months of treatment. By comparing response rates across all groups, this
analysis confirms the effectiveness of Upadacitinib across different dose regimens in a real-world setting. The
reported safety aligns with the known safety profile of Upadacitinib and supports the current evidence. This is the
first data to show the effectiveness of Upadacitinib in real-world stratified by dose and including a flexible dosing
regimen.
