Abstract N°: 2224
Outcomes Following Discontinuation of Systemic Immunomodulatory Therapies in Adults with Atopic
Dermatitis: A Systematic Review
Noor Leelo*
1, 2
, David Gleeson
1, 2
, John Y. W. Lee
1, 2
, Manpreet K. Sagoo
1, 2
, Weiyu Ye
1, 2
, Ravi Ramessur
1, 2
,
Andrew Pink
1, 2
, Satveer Mahil
1, 2
, Catherine Smith
1, 2
1
St John
’
s Institute of Dermatology, London, United Kingdom
2
King
’
s College London Guy
’
s Campus, London, United Kingdom
Introduction & Objectives:
The widespread adoption of highly effective systemic immunomodulatory therapies such as biologics and small
molecule Janus Kinase 
(
JAK
)
 inhibitors has transformed the therapeutic landscape for atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
. As
more patients achieve clear or nearly clear skin, there is growing interest in whether disease control can be
maintained after discontinuing systemic immunomodulatory therapy. This question is especially important given
concerns surrounding the long-term use of these agents, including financial burden, risk of opportunistic
infections and implications for family planning.
Aim: To evaluate disease-related outcomes in adults with AD following discontinuation of systemic
immunomodulatory monotherapy, with a focus on maintenance of disease control.
Materials & Methods:
A systematic search was conducted across four databases 
(
Medline, Embase, Web of Science and Cochrane
Library
)
 from database inception to 8th January 2025. Eligible studies included randomised controlled trials 
(
RCTs
)
and cohort studies reporting outcomes after discontinuation of targeted or conventional systemic
immunomodulatory monotherapy in adults with AD who had achieved disease control. Two reviewers
independently screened records and extracted data. Study quality was assessed using the Cochrane Risk of Bias
tool for RCTs and the Newcastle-Ottawa Scale for observational studies. Data extracted included participant
baseline characteristics, maintenance of disease control, and outcomes on relapse and retreatment.
Results:
Nine studies 
(
2,558 participants
)
 met the inclusion criteria, including 8 RCTs and one cohort. Six studies examined
biologic agents 
(
amlitelimab, dupilumab 
(
2 studies
)
, tralokinumab, rezpegaldesleukin, rocatinlimab
)
 and three
evaluated JAK inhibitors 
(
baricitinib, abrocitinib, upadacitinib
)
. No eligible studies were identified for conventional
systemic immunomodulatory therapies. Amlitelimab, rocatinlimab and rezpegaldesleukin are investigational and
not currently approved for AD treatment. Overall risk of bias was generally low.
Across studies, there was considerable heterogeneity in definitions of disease control, treatment duration 
(
12 - 52
weeks
)
, and follow-up duration post-discontinuation 
(
4 - 72 weeks
)
. This limited direct comparisons and
precluded meta-analysis. Higher proportions of participants maintained EASI-75 responses post-discontinuation
with investigational agents: rezpegaldesleukin 
(
71.4% at 36 weeks
)
, rocatinlimab 
(
73
–
96% at 20 weeks
)
, and
amlitelimab 
(
61.6% at 28 weeks
)
. In contrast, a lower proportion of participants were able to maintain responses
with approved biologics: dupilumab 
(
30.4% at 36 weeks
)
 and tralokinumab 
(
26.4% at 36 weeks
)
. Rapid loss of
EASI-75 was observed with JAK inhibitors after discontinuation: baricitinib 
(
45.2% at 16 weeks
)
, abrocitinib 
(
42.3%
at 4 weeks
)
, and upadacitinib 
(
~20% at 72 weeks
)
.

Conclusion:
Emerging evidence suggests that sustaining disease control following discontinuation of systemic
immunomodulatory therapy is possible in some adults with AD. Differences in outcomes between therapies may
reflect variations in drug mechanisms or study design. To confidently identify individuals who may benefit from
treatment discontinuation, further studies are required with harmonised outcomes 
(
e.g. definition of disease
control used
)
 and standardised treatment and follow-up durations.  
