Abstract N°: 2239
Facial Redness Associated with Therapy: A case series
Karol Sabas Ortega
1
, sergio garcia-gonzalez
1
, Mariano Ara Martín
1
, ANA LUISA MORALES MOYA
1
, Sonia De La
Fuente
1
, Ignacio Rivera Fuertes
1
, Elena Aura Bularca
1
, Pablo Villagrasa
1
, Andrea Montes-Torres
1
, Lucía Prieto
Torres
1
1
Hospital Clinico Universitario Lozano Blesa, Zaragoza, Spain
Introduction & Objectives:
Facial redness 
(
FR
)
 has recently been recognized in patients with atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 treated with biologics and
immunemodulators, particularly dupilumab. The pathogenesis remains unclear. This case series aims to explore the
causes of FR in patients treated with different therapeutic agents.
Materials & Methods:
A retrospective observational study was conducted involving 10 patients with AD receiving biologics or
immunemodulators who developed FR. Data were collected from electronic medical records, including patient
demographics, treatment history, comorbidities, time to onset of FR, and serum IgE levels 
(
total and 
Malassezia
-
specific
)
. Statistical analysis was performed using JAMOVI, R, Excel, and STATA.
Results:
Of the 10 patients, 3 
(
30%
)
 experienced FR with multiple therapeutic agents. The majority of cases were associated
with dupilumab and tralokinumab, while abrocitinib and upadacitinib were linked to isolated occurrences. The
time to FR onset varied widely 
(
0.42
–
35 weeks
)
. Malassezia-specific IgE was elevated in 5 patients 
(
50%
)
, most of
whom responded to antifungal therapy. Statistical analysis showed no significant association between treatment
type and FR onset 
(
p > 0.05
)
.
Conclusion:
This case series broadens the understanding of FR, indicating that it can occur with various therapeutic agents
beyond dupilumab. The findings suggest multiple contributing factors, including Malassezia hypersensitivity and
treatment site failure. Further studies are needed to clarify the pathogenesis and guide management strategies.
EADV Congress 2025, PARIS
17 SEPTEMBER - 20 SEPTEMBER 2025 
POWERED BY M-ANAGE.COM 


Abstract N°: 2240
Integrated assessment of clinical, pathomorphological and microbiological parameters in atopic dermatitis
for the development of a digital severity scoring system
Sherzod Khonkhodjaev
1
, Ulugbek Sabirov
1
, Shakhnoza Mavlyanova
1
1
Republican Specialized Scientific-Practical Medical Center of Dermatovenerology and Cosmetology, Republic of
Uzbekistan, Dermatopathology, Tashkent, Uzbekistan
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disease with increasing incidence in dry, hot climate regions.
This study aimed to assess the correlation between clinical severity, histopathological features, and microbial
colonization in AD, and to develop a digital diagnostic system. Objectives included: 
(
1
)
 evaluating phenotypes and
histological changes in patients with varying AD severity; 
(
2
)
 analyzing skin and gut microbiota, focusing on
Staphylococcus aureus and Candida spp.; 
(
3
)
 integrating findings into a digital diagnostic tool.
Materials & Methods:
The study involved 355 patients aged 1
–
62 years. Severity was assessed using SCORAD and DISHS. Lesional skin
and stool samples were analyzed microbiologically. PCR was used for S. aureus genotyping 
(
MRSA vs. MSSA
)
. Skin
biopsies 
(
n=48
)
 were examined histologically. Statistical analysis used Student
’
s t-test and Pearson correlation. A
mobile application for AD severity scoring was developed, combining clinical, microbiological, and histological
inputs.
Results:
Mild, moderate, and severe AD were observed in 17.2%, 39.7%, and 43.1% of patients, respectively. Histological
changes included spongiosis in mild cases and pronounced acanthosis, fibrosis, and parakeratosis in severe cases.
S. aureus was detected in 94.7% of skin samples; MRSA strains were found in 45.6%, more frequently in children
(
up to 61.1%
)
. Candida spp. 
(
C. krusei 38.8%, C. albicans 27.3%
)
 were prevalent in stool. Severity correlated with S.
aureus colonization and histological chronicity 
(
r
≈
+0.8, p<0.05
)
, while Candida colonization inversely correlated
with S. aureus presence.
Conclusion:
Clinical AD severity strongly correlates with histopathology and microbial dysbiosis. High MRSA and Candida
prevalence reflects microbial contribution to disease chronicity. The digital scoring tool offers accurate, integrated
assessment and treatment guidance, especially valuable in climate-stressed regions. This approach supports
personalized AD care and future microbiota-targeted therapies.
EADV Congress 2025, PARIS
17 SEPTEMBER - 20 SEPTEMBER 2025 
POWERED BY M-ANAGE.COM 


Abstract N°: 2242
AtopicHealth3 - Quality of care for atopic dermatitis 2024/2025 in Germany: Interim analysis of quality
indicators
Anna Langenbruch*
1
, Luka Lomb
1
, Thomas Werfel
2
, Ralph von Kiedrowski
3
, Matthias Augustin
1
1
Institute for Health Services Research in Dermatology and Nursing 
(
IVDP
)
, Hamburg, Germany
2
Hannover Medical School, Clinic for Dermatology, Allergology und Venerology, Hannover, Germany
3
Company for Medical Study & Service Selters, Selters
(
Westerwald
)
, Germany
Introduction & Objectives:
AtopicHealth2 was the second national care study conducted between 2017-2019 with patients with atopic
dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 in dermatological care. Compared to AtopicHealth1 from 2010, there was no improvement in the
care situation. Therapeutic innovations are now available and the AtopicHealth3 study aims to investigate whether
the care situation has changed.
Materials & Methods:
The Germany-wide multicentre cross-sectional study 
‘
AtopicHealth3
’
 records clinical data, the severity of AD
(
SCORAD
)
, quality of life impairment 
(
DLQI
)
, therapies, preventive behaviour and patient-defined treatment
benefit 
(
PBI
)
. One focus is on the patient
’
s perspective. The care situation is assessed on the basis of previously
defined quality indicators. The inclusion of 1,000 patients with AD is planned.
Results:
Since May 2024, 107 dermatological practices and outpatient clinics throughout Germany have been recruited and
by mid-March 2025, 300 fully completed, valid questionnaire sets from 35 dermatological practices were returned
to the study centre, on which the present analysis is based. Of the 300 patients, 58% described themselves as
female, 41.7% as male and 0.3% as diverse. The mean age was 43 years. The mean SCORAD 
(
scale from 0=minimal
to 103=maximal severity of AD
)
 was 34.9±21.3, lower than the SCORAD of AtopicHealth2 
(
45.4±20.6
)
 and
AtopicHealth1 
(
42.3±18.6
)
. The mean DLQI 
(
scale from 0=minimum to 30=maximum impairment of dermatology-
related quality of life
)
 was 7.2±6.4 compared to 8.5±6.5 in the two previous studies. The proportion of patients
with high severity 
(
SCORAD>50
)
 was 26.6% compared to 41.6% in AtopicHealth2 and 32.2% in AtopicHealth1. The
mean PBI 
(
0=no benefit to 4=maximum patient-defined treatment benefit
)
 was 2.6±1.2 compared to 2.2±1.1 in
AtopicHealth2 and 2.4±1.1 in AtopicHealth1.
Conclusion:
Compared to the AtopicHealth studies 2010 and 2017-19, outcome indicators show an improved quality of care
for patients with atopic dermatitis in Germany. As this is a small sample, it remains to be seen whether this result
will be confirmed in the overall evaluation.
The study received financial support from Sanofi.
