Abstract N°: 2264
Efficacy and Safety of Abrocitinib in Elderly with Atopic Dermatitis: A Prospective Study
Wei Li*
1, 2, 3
, Hui Ye
1, 2
, Enyi Zhu
1, 2
, Hongkai Zheng
1, 2
, Yichuan Gan
1, 2
, Shilin Deng
1, 2
, Quan Luo
1, 2
, Jingyao
Liang
1, 2
, Xibao Zhang
1, 2
1
Guangzhou Medical University, Dermatology, Guangzhou, China
2
Guangzhou Dermatology Hospital, Dermatology, Guangzhou, China
3
First Affiliated Hospital of Jinan University, Dermatology, Guangzhou, China
Background:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 in the elderly has received widespread attention as a new clinical subtype of
AD. Treatment for moderate-to-severe AD requires systemic drugs, and their use may be challenging in elderly
due to complex comorbidities and progressive deterioration body functions. The efficacy and safety of abrocitinib
for the treatment of AD in the elderly has not been demonstrated in clinical trials and real-world studies.
Objectives:
 We aimed to assess the efficacy and safety of abrocitinib monotherapy in elderly with moderate-to-
severe atopic dermatitis from baseline to 12 weeks.
Methods:
 A study was conducted at Guangzhou Dermatology Hospital from March 2023 to January 2024.
Patients aged 60 years and older with moderate-to-severe AD were treated with abrocitinib 100 mg/d, Patients
’
scores of SCORing Atopic Dermatitis 
(
SCORAD
)
, Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
, Investigator Global
Assessment 
(
IGA
)
, Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
, Patient-Oriented Eczema Measure 
(
POEM
)
, Atopic
Dermatitis Control Tool 
(
ADCT
)
 and Peak Pruritus Numerical Rating Scale 
(
PP-NRS
)
 changes were recorded at
weeks 2, 4, 8, 10, and 12 of treatment to assess clinical efficacy, and blood counts, immunoglobulin E 
(
IgE
)
, hepatic
and renal function, D-dimer, lipids, and cardiac enzyme levels were monitored at weeks 0 and 12 of treatment.
Results:
 A total of 15 patients were included, of which 12 patients 
(
80%
)
 completed the study with a mean age of
(
70 ± 8.22
)
 years. From baseline to week 12, the differences in patients
’
 SCORAD, EASI, IGA, DLQI, POEM, ADCT,
and PP-NRS scores showed statistical significance at different time points 
(
all P < 0.05
)
. By the 12th week, all 12
patients reached SCORAD50, 66.7% 
(
8 patients
)
 reached SCORAD75, and 16.7% 
(
2 patients
)
 reached SCORAD90.
Additionally, all 12 patients attained EASI50, 66.7% 
(
8 patients
)
 attained EASI75, and 25% 
(
3 patients
)
 attained
EASI90. 41.7% 
(
5 patients
)
 of patients reached IGA0/1, 83.3% 
(
10 patients
)
 attained PP-NRS 
≤
4, and all 12
patients accomplished DLQI 
≤
5, POEM 
≤
7, and ADCT 
≤
7. The absolute value of eosinophils, percentage of
eosinophils, and IgE decreased significantly compared with the baseline values in the 12th week, all of which were
statistically different 
(
all P < 0.05
)
. One case each of abnormal AST, D-dimer and cardiac enzyme indices were
observed, which decreased to normal range upon follow-up review. No gastrointestinal reaction or infection was
noted.
Conclusion:
 In the 12-week study, abrocitinib was an effective and safe medication for the treatment of patients
60 years of age and older with moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis.
