Abstract N°: 2282
Atopic dermatitis increases risk for multimorbidity with unique orofacial and cardiometabolic signatures
Leon Miltner*
1
, Laura Loman
1
, Josué Almansa Ortiz
1
, Junfen Zhang
1, 2
, Aline B. Sprikkelman
1
, Marie-Louise
Schuttelaar
1
1
University Medical Center Groningen, Groningen, Netherlands
2
Dermatology Hospital, Guangzhou, China
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 has been proposed as a systemic disease due to underlying systemic inflammation and a
range of reported comorbidities, not only limited to other atopic diseases. Thus, this study aimed to examine the
associations between AD and 
(
non-atopic
)
 multimorbidity in a population-based cohort from the Northern
Netherlands, considering disease severity and age. Further we sought to identify whether there are AD-specific
multimorbidity patterns among participants with non-atopic multimorbidity.
Methods:
We assessed lifetime prevalence of 52 diseases, from 15 domains, combining data from questionnaires, validated
medication records and clinical assessments within the Lifelines Cohort. AD presence was assessed as self-
reported, physician-diagnosed AD over lifetime and disease severity was evaluated using the Patient Oriented
Eczema Measure. Multimorbidity was defined as lifetime presence of at least two diseases within an individual,
excluding AD, with non-atopic multimorbidity disregarding asthma, rhinitis and food allergy. A composite
morbidity score 
(
cMS
)
 indicated the degree of 
(
non-atopic
)
 multimorbidity. We analysed associations of AD and
AD severity with multimorbidity and the cMS using binary and multinomial logistic regression, adjusting for age
and sex. Another model additionally adjusted for socioeconomic and lifestyle factors. Patterns of non-atopic
multimorbidity based on 14 non-atopic domains were explored using Latent Class Analysis 
(
LCA
)
.
Results:
Among 37,193 participants, 8.7% had AD. Prevalence of 
(
non-atopic
)
 multimorbidity was higher in participants
with AD, compared to those without AD, increasing with AD severity. The most affected domains in the AD group
were 
hematologic
 
(
aneamia
)
 with 25.5% and 
respiratory
 
(
chonic obstructive pulmunary disease [COPD]
)
 with
21.2%, both significantly more prevalent than in those without AD. The risk for non-atopic multimorbidity was 47%
higher in participants with AD compared to those without, and increased with more severe disease 
(
Figure
)
. The
association strengthened with higher degrees of non-atopic multimorbidity, reaching 2.09-fold for 
≥
5 diseases.
Associations between AD and outcomes which considered the atopic diseases were even stronger. Further
adjustments for socioeconomic and lifestyle factors corroborated most results, especially for moderate-to-severe
AD. Lastly, we identified five distinct multimorbidity classes in participants with AD, of which two classes differed
compared to those without AD. We found the presence of an 
orofacial
 class 
(
periodontitis
)
 and unique
distribution of a composite class characterised by 
cardiovascular
 
(
heart failure, atrial fibrillation
)
, 
endocrinologic
(
hypercholestorolemia, thyroid disease, diabetes
)
 and 
respiratory
 
(
COPD
)
 involvement in those with AD.
Conclusion:
Participants with AD, especially moderate-to-severe cases, were at higher risk for both multimorbidity and non-
atopic multimorbidity. Additonally, we identified partially AD-specific patterns of multimorbidity, suggesting
differences regarding orofacial disease and a metabolic syndrome-like class. Our results highlight the importance

of screening for comorbidities alongside their risk factors and interdesciplinary approaches in the treatment of
patients with atopic dermatitis. Whether our findings result from the underlying systemic inflammation in AD,
needs further investigation.
