Introduction & Objectives:
The superiority of once daily orally administered upadacitinib 
(
UPA
)
, a selective Janus kinase 1 inhibitor, over
biweekly and subcutaneously administered dupilumab 
(
DUPI
)
, an IL-4Ra inhibitor, was demonstrated in adult and
adolescent patients with moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 in international randomized controlled trials
(
RCTs
)
. The benefits of switching DUPI-treated patients with AD to UPA has also been demonstrated via open-
label and extension studies. The CAN UpTIMISE study sought to prospectively assess the real-world effectiveness
of daily UPA 15 mg 
(
UPA15
)
 and 30 mg 
(
UPA30
)
 in patients with AD who were inadequate responders to DUPI or
discontinued DUPI due to safety/tolerability reasons. The current analyses report the effectiveness of UPA switch
in terms of severity and extent of skin clearance across different body regions.
Materials & Methods:

CAN UpTIMISE 
(
NCT05394792
)
 was a Canadian observational study of adult patients treated with DUPI for 
≥
16
weeks as previous line of systemic therapy for moderate-to-severe AD and for whom the decision to change
treatment to UPA had been made, in accordance with the local label and prior to enrolment in the study.
Effectiveness was assessed over 4 months using scores for validated Investigator Global Assessment for AD 
(
vIGA-
AD
)
, facial IGA, Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
 and localized EASI by body regions, body surface area
(
BSA
)
, and Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
. Results are presented using descriptive statistics.
Results:
A total of 108 patients 
(
n=44 [UPA15]; n=64 [UPA30]
)
 were included in the analysis 
(
mean age: 42.9 years [SD
16.7]; male gender: 55.6%; race: mainly White [61.1%] or Asian [33.3%]
)
. Most patients 
(
83.3%
)
 discontinued DUPI
due to suboptimal disease control per investigator
’
s judgment, while 16.7% of patients discontinued due to
safety/tolerability reasons. Mean and median exposure to DUPI prior to enrolment were 577 and 309 days,
respectively. At baseline, 94.4% of patients had vIGA-AD scores 
≥
 2 
(
mild through severe
)
. As a primary endpoint,
65.9% 
(
95% CI: 55.1 
–
 75.2%
)
 of patients achieved vIGA-AD 0/1 at Month 4 with either UPA dose 
(
Figure 1
)
. From
baseline to Month 4, there were notable increases in the proportion of patients with facial IGA score of 0/1 
(
from
23.1% to 77.5%
)
 or 0 
(
from 12.0% to 52.5%
)
, with most improvements seen as early as Month 1 post-UPA
initiation 
(
Table 1
)
. Throughout the study period, rapid and generally sustained improvements were observed in
the global EASI score 
(
percent change from baseline [%CFB]: 
​
73.1%
)
, localized EASI scores 
(
head and neck
[
​
68.6%], trunk [
​
80.1%], upper limbs [
​
74.3%], and lower limbs [
​
81.8%]
)
, and BSA 
(​
67.5%
)
, with 39.2% of the patients
with baseline BSA > 2% achieving BSA 
≤
 2% at Month 4. These clinical improvements were accompanied by
similarly rapid and generally sustained reductions in the impact of AD on quality of life 
(
DLQI
)
. UPA was well-
tolerated, and its safety profile aligned with findings from RCTs.
Conclusion:
The CAN UpTIMISE study shows that UPA is effective and well-tolerated in AD patients who either did not respond
adequately to DUPI or discontinued DUPI due to safety or tolerability concerns. The switch to UPA treatment
resulted in significant improvements in the severity and extent of skin disease across body areas, including the
face. Improvements in quality of life were seen as early as Month 1 and generally sustained by Month 4. No new
safety signals were reported.


