Abstract N°: 2499
Tralokinumab for Cronic Hand Eczema: 52-week experience and flexible dosing outcomes
Daniel Blaya Imbernón
1
, Malena Finello
1
, Andres Grau Echevarría
1
, Elena Pérez Zafrilla
1
, Ángel González García
1
,
Alba Nácher Albiach
1
, Laura Giménez Cuenca
1
, Altea Esteve Martínez
1
, Esther Díez Recio
1
, Violeta Zaragoza Ninet
1
1
Consortium General University Hospital of Valencia, Dermatology, València, Spain
Introduction & Objectives:
Chronic hand eczema 
(
CHE
)
 remains a therapeutic challenge, especially in patients with disease refractory to
topical treatment and without clearly defined atopic phenotypes. While systemic therapies often involve off-label
use and carry significant safety concerns, emerging biologics targeting Th2 inflammation have shown promise.
Tralokinumab, a monoclonal antibody against IL-13, has demonstrated efficacy in atopic dermatitis but its long-
term effect in CHE is not well established. Our objective was to evaluate the 52-week response to Tralokinumab in
patients with severe CHE and assess outcomes following dose deintensification.
Materials & Methods:
We conducted an observational study including 16 patients with severe or very severe chronic hand eczema
refractory to topical therapy. All patients were treated with subcutaneous Tralokinumab at an initial dose of 600
mg followed by 300 mg weekly. Disease severity was measured using the Hand Eczema Severity Index 
(
HECSI
)
.
Patients achieving sustained clinical response were evaluated for dose deintensification to 300 mg every 4 or 6
weeks.
Results:
By week 52, all 16 patients had achieved HECSI-75, with 13 
(
81.3%
)
 reaching HECSI-90 and 8 
(
50%
)
 achieving
complete clearance 
(
HECSI-100
)
. Nine patients underwent dose deintensification: six to 300 mg every 4 weeks,
and three to every 6 weeks. All maintained their clinical responses without relapse during follow-up. No significant
adverse events were observed.
Conclusion:
Tralokinumab demonstrates sustained efficacy in chronic hand eczema up to week 52, including in patients with
non-atopic and hyperkeratotic phenotypes. Dose deintensification appears feasible in responders, maintaining
disease control with reduced treatment burden. These findings support the potential role of Tralokinumab as a
long-term therapeutic option in refractory CHE.
