Abstract N°: 2503
Lebrikizumab: A New Effective Alternative for the Treatment of Severe Atopic Dermatitis in Patients
Resistant to Previous Therapies
Andrea San José Rodríguez
1
, Luis Felipe Godoy Villalón
1
, Gabriel Rodríguez Vega
1
, Elia Lizeth Díaz Torres
1
,
Federica Liuti
1
, Laura Rodríguez Lago
1
, María Zaida Hernández Hernández
1
1
Hospital Insular Materno Infantil, Dermatology Department , Las Palmas de Gran Canaria
Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a highly heterogeneous inflammatory skin disorder. It
affects approximately 2-10% of adults. Improved understanding of AD pathogenesis has enabled the development
of new therapeutic targets in recent decades.
Lebrikizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody that selectively binds to IL-13, preventing the
heterodimerization of subunits IL-4R
α
 and IL-13R
α
1 of Type 2 receptor and its subsequent signaling.
Materials & Methods:
 A retrospective, descriptive, and observational study was conducted to evaluate the
effectiveness of lebrikizumab in the AD patients treated to date in our service. Four adult patients with severe
atopic dermatitis treated with lebrikizumab from December 2024 to the present were included.
Patients were administered lebrikizumab following SPC 
(
subcutaneously, starting with loading dose of 500 mg at
weeks 0 and 2, followed by 250 mg every two weeks until week 16, then 250 mg monthly
)
.
Results:
 Three of the patients were women with a mean age of 19 
(
range 17 to 23
)
. Among the comorbidities,
one patient had rhinitis and another had blepharoconjunctivitis. All patients had previously been treated with
cyclosporine; one patient had also failed tralokinumab, and another had failed multiple systemic treatments
(
azathioprine, ustekinumab, upadacitinib, dupilumab, and tralokinumab
)
. The baseline EASI and IGA average
scores of the patients were 39 and 4, respectively. All patients achieved EASI < 6 and IGA = 1 after an average of
10 weeks of treatment. The therapeutic response was rapid in all cases, showing improvement between weeks 1
and 3. Two patients reported a sensation of dry eye without signs of conjunctivitis. No serious adverse effects
have been identified to date.
Discussion:
 Severe forms of AD requiring systemic treatment have a significant impact on patients
’
 quality of life,
affecting their daily activities and social relationships. This justifies the need for developing new effective drugs for
its treatment. Lebrikizumab has shown that patients who respond at week 16 subsequently present a sustained
response, improving pruritus, sleep quality, and overall quality of life.
Conclusion:
 We present four cases of patients with severe AD successfully treated with lebrikizumab.
Lebrikizumab has proven to be a rapid and effective drug and, therefore, positions itself as an alternative for the
treatment of moderate-to-severe AD, even in patients who have previously failed other biological treatments.
