17 SEPTEMBER - 20 SEPTEMBER 2025 
POWERED BY M-ANAGE.COM 


Abstract N°: 2540
Efficacy and Safety of Benvitimod in Patients with Moderate-to-Severe Chronic Hand Eczema: A
Prospective, Single-Center, Parallel-Group, Open-Label Randomized Trial
Yuan Chang
1
, Gongfeng Tang
1
, Fenglin Zhuo*
1
1
Capital Medical University, Beijing Friendship Hospital, Department of Dermatology, Beijing, China
Introduction & Objectives:
 Topical corticosteroids are the first-line treatment for chronic hand eczema 
(
CHE
)
.
However, data from randomized trials supporting their use are limited. Topical benvitimod is a potential
alternative for long-term maintenance. To evaluate the efficacy and safety of benvitimod cream compared to
halometasone in the treatment of moderate-to-severe CHE.
Materials & Methods:
 A prospective, single-center, parallel-group, open-label randomized trial was conducted,
involving 64 patients with moderate-to-severe CHE. Patients were randomly assigned to either the benvitimod
group 
(
n=32, 1% benvitimod cream twice daily for 8 weeks
)
 or the halometasone group 
(
n=32, halometasone
cream twice daily for 8 weeks
)
. The primary endpoint was the proportion of patients achieving 
“
treatment
success
”
 at week 12. Secondary endpoints included changes from baseline to week 12 of the Hand Eczema
Severity Index 
(
HECSI
)
, Patient Global Assessment 
(
PaGA
)
, Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
, and Quality of
Life in Hand Eczema Questionnaire 
(
QOLHEQ
)
. The relapse rate and dverse events were also recorded.
Results:
 A total of 30 patients in the benvitimod group and 29 patients in the halometasone group completed the
trial. At week 12, the treatment success rate was 26.7% 
(
8/30
)
 in the benvitimod group and 24.1% 
(
7/29
)
 in the
halometasone group 
(
p = 0.824
)
. Both groups showed significant reductions in HECSI, PaGA, DLQI, and QOLHEQ
scores from baseline to week 12 
(
p < 0.05
)
, but there were no statistically significant differences in the changes
between the two groups. Among patients achieving treatment success, 25.0% 
(
2/8
)
 patients in benvitimod group
and 57.1% 
(
4/7
)
 patients in halometasone group relapsed at week 24 
(
p=0.315
)
. The main adverse events
associated with benvitimod were skin pigmentation, pruritus, and skin dryness, none of which led to treatment
discontinuation.
Conclusion:
 The efficacy of benvitimod in treating moderate-to-severe CHE is comparable to that of
halometasone, with a favorable safety and low recurrence rate.
