Abstract N°: 2545
Achieving minimal disease activity with Upadacitinib for moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis: a 3-year
real-world study
Luca Mastorino
1
, Michela Ortoncelli
1
, Giovanni Cavaliere
1
, Niccolo Siliquini
1
, Simone Ribero
1
, Pietro Quaglino
1
,
Francesco Leo
1
1
University of Turin, medical sciences, Torino
Achieving minimal disease activity with Upadacitinib for moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis: a 3-year
real-world study
Introduction & Objectives:
Minimal Disease Activity 
(
MDA
)
 is an emerging treatment goal in atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
, representing a state of
optimal disease control with minimal symptoms. Despite its relevance, MDA remains underexplored in real-world
settings. To assess the achievement of MDA in patients with moderate-to-severe AD treated with upadacitinib
over three years and identify predictive factors.
Materials & Methods:
This retrospective study included all patients treated with upadacitinib at the Dermatology Clinic of the University
of Turin between December 2020 and December 2024. MDA was defined as the simultaneous achievement of
EASI-90 
(≥
90% improvement in Eczema Area and Severity Index
)
 and P-NRS 
(
Pruritus Numerical Rating Scale
)
 
≤
1. Effectiveness was assessed at 28, 52, 104, and 156 weeks.
Results:
At 28 weeks, 50.0% of patients achieved MDA, with sustained results over three years. Female sex was significantly
associated with MDA achievement 
(
OR 4.14, p = 0.034
)
, while other factors showed no significant correlation.
Conclusion:
Upadacitinib effectively achieves and maintains MDA in moderate-to-severe AD over three years. Female sex was a
positive predictor of MDA. Further studies are warranted to confirm these findings.
