Abstract N°: 2635
Calcineurin inhibitors compared to corticosteroids for atopic dermatitis treatment in children: a systematic
review and meta-analysis
Lorhayne Kerley Capuchinho Scalioni Galvao
1
, Deivyd Vieira Silva Cavalcante
2
, Ana Clara Felix de Farias Santos
3
,
Fernanda Valeriano Zamora
1
, Ana Carolina Putini Vieira
4
, Belisa Brunow Ventura Biavatti
5
, Bezalel Hakkeem
6
, Ana
Carolina Ventura de Santana de Jesus
7
, Monaly Ribeiro*
8
1
Federal University of Minas Gerais, Belo Horizonte, Brazil
2
Federal University of Maranhão, São Luis, Brazil
3
City University of São Paulo, São Paulo, Brazil
4
University of Santo Amaro, São Paulo, Brazil
5
State University of Campinas, Campinas, Brazil
6
Jubilee Mission Medical College and Research Institute, Thrissur, India
7
Bahiana School of Medicine and Public Health, Salvador, Brazil
8
Faculdade São Leopoldo Mandic, Campinas, Brazil
Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is the most common inflammatory skin disorder in the
developed world. While current treatments for AD primarily focus on anti-inflammatory therapy using topical
corticosteroids 
(
TCS
)
, topical calcineurin inhibitors 
(
TCIs
)
 present a promising steroid-sparing alternative. In light
of this, we conducted an updated meta-analysis comparing TCIs and TCS in the pediatric population. 
Materials & Methods:
 We searched the PubMed, Embase, and Cochrane databases up to December 2024 for
studies comparing topical calcineurin inhibitors 
(
TCIs
)
 such as pimecrolimus, tacrolimus, or cyclosporine with
topical corticosteroids 
(
TCS
)
 in children and adolescents with atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
. The studies included
outcomes measured by the Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
 or the modified EASI 
(
mEASI
)
, the Investigator
Global Assessment 
(
IGA
)
, and reported side effects. We calculated risk ratios 
(
RRs
)
 for binary outcomes and mean
differences 
(
MDs
)
 for continuous outcomes, along with 95% confidence intervals 
(
CIs
)
. The heterogeneity of the
studies was assessed using I² statistics.
Results:
 12 studies comprising 2,583 patients were included, of whom 1,405 
(
54.39%
)
 underwent TCI treatment.
The results were similar between groups regarding EASI 
(
MD -0.45 points; 95% CI -1.07, 0.17; I2 = 0; p = 0.154
)
and mEASI 
(
MD 0.32 points; 95% CI -0.71, I2 = 75.3; p = 0.54
)
 and IGA scored as clear/improvement 
(
RR 1.14; 95%
CI 0.53, 2.46; I2 93.3; p = 0.734
)
. Concerning side effects, the burning sensation was more common in the
intervention group 
(
RR 3.20; 95% CI 1.91, 5.35; I2 = 56.4; p < 0.001
)
.
Conclusion:
 In our analysis, TCIs demonstrated similar effectiveness to TCS in children with atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
.
For patients unable to use TCS, TCIs can serve as a suitable alternative in the search for steroid-sparing or
alternative therapies. However, it is important to consider the higher risk of side effects when weighing the
benefits and risks of these two.
