Abstract N°: 2648
Development and characterization of IAR129, a novel bispecific antibody co-blocking IL-4Ra and OX40L
for atopic dermatitis
Chuxing Liu
1
, Xiguang Zhang
1
, Huijun Sha
1
, Jiayu Hou
1
, Liu Li
1
, Mengyan Zhai
1
, Meng Ni
1
, Zhihai Wu
1
, Yao Xiong
1
,
Shuaixiang Zhou
1
, Li Li
1
, Huizhong Xiong
1
1
Innovent Biologics, Suzhou, China
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 has been characterized as a type 2 dominant inflammation. Recent data indicate that
additional proinflammatory pathways can play a role besides the core Th2 response. Current targeted therapies
focus on type 2 immunity, primarily the IL-4/IL-13 pathway, and have achieved good clinical response in AD
patients. Nevertheless, there is still room to improve the depth, width and duration of response. OX40L is an
emerging target with a key upstream function in priming, differentiation and memory response of T cells including
Th2 and the other subsets. OX40 and OX40L antagonist antibodies are in active clinical development, and the
OX40L antibody, amlitelimab, has demonstrated promising response with inhibition of Th2, Th1 and Th17/22
cytokines. Here, we report a novel, half-life extended bispecific antibody, IAR129, that simultaneously blocks both
IL-4Ra and OX40L. It aims at suppressing both the core type 2 cytokines and the OX40L pathway for effective and
durable response in patients with type 2 dominant as well as a more mixed cytokine profile. We examine IAR129
for its 
in vitro
 and 
in vivo
 activity, developability and PK/PD in NHP.
Materials & Methods:
The 
in vitro
 blocking activity of each arm was assessed by IL-4 and IL-13 blockade respectively in TF-1 cell
proliferation assay, as well as suppression of IL-2 release in activated primary human T cells. To evaluate the
combinatorial effect of the bsAb, an 
in vitro
 allogeneic mixed lymphocyte reaction 
(
MLR
)
 assay of DC-T cell
coculture was performed with PBMC from different donors. Additionally, we established mouse models of IL13-
induced airway inflammation and TSLP-induced atopic dermatitis to evaluate the 
in vivo
 activity of IAR129. PK
profile was evaluated in cynomolgus monkeys via single-dose intravenous administration at 10 mg/kg.
Developability tests were performed according to industrial standards for biologics.
Results:
IAR129 strongly inhibited IL-4, IL-13 and OX40L signaling comparable to benchmarks dupilumab 
(
anti-IL4R
)
 or
amlitelimab 
(
anti-OX40L
)
. In human DC-T cell coculture assay, IAR129 robustly suppressed both IL-5 and IL-13
production, better than dupilimab and amlitelimab alone. Furthermore, IAR129 effectively alleviated airway
inflammation and atopic dermatitis in mice. In cynomolgus monkeys, IAR129 exhibited favorable PK profile with a
clearance of 0.43 mL/h/kg and a half-life of 377 hours. Importantly, IgE was reduced in these monkeys for over 40
days after a single injection. Besides, IAR129 had favorable developability with a viscosity of 6.15 cp at 150 mg/mL
formulation, making it possible for high concentration formulation and subcutaneous administration.
Conclusion:
IAR129 is a first-in class IL4R and OX40L bispecific antibody with half life extension. It potently blocks IL-4, IL-13
and OX40L 
in vitro
 comparable to dupilumab and amlitelimab. It shows combinatorial activity superior to single
blockade in DC-T coculture, and is highly effective in suppressing both airway inflammation and atopic dermatitis
in mice. It has good PK profile and durable PD response in monkeys. The favorable developability profile enables

high concentration formulation and subcutaneous administration.
