Abstract N°: 2657
Efficacy and safety of a topical cream containing Lignosus rhinocerus in mild-to-moderate atopic
dermatitis: a 6-week split-site pilot study
Rebecca Kai Jan Choong
1
, Winn Hui Han
1
, Shin Yee Fung
2
, Shin Shen Yong
1
, Hao Dong Tan
3
, Zhenli Kwan
2
1
University of Malaya, Faculty of Medicine, Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia
2
University of Malaya, Department of Molecular Medicine, Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia
3
University of Malaya, Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia
Introduction & Objectives:
Lignosus rhinocerus 
(
L. rhinocerus
)
, commonly known as the tiger milk mushroom, is a wild edible mushroom
indigenous to the jungles of Asia, including Malaysia. It was successfully cultivated in 2009, which subsequently
spurred extensive research into its therapeutic applications. Previous studies have successfully highlighted the
anti-inflammatory and immunomodulatory properties of 
L. rhinocerus
, including downregulation of the Th2
pathway which is crucial in the development of atopic dermatitis. Due to its anti-inflammatory,
immunomodulatory, antifungal and antibacterial properties, we investigated the use of 
L.rhinocerus
 as a steroid-
sparing topical treatment option for atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
.
Materials & Methods:
A six-week, randomized, split-site study involving 22 patients aged 12 years and above evaluated the efficacy and
safety of a topical cream containing 
L. rhinocerus
 as a treatment for mild-to-moderate AD compared to
hydrocortisone 1% cream. Site-specific disease severity was assessed using the Peak Pruritus Numerical Rating
Scale 
(
PNRS
)
 and the Atopic Dermatitis Severity Index 
(
ADSI
)
. The occurrence of any adverse events 
(
AEs
)
 was
recorded.
Results:
The study cream containing 
L. rhinocerus
 showed a significant improvement in site-specific disease severity 
(
mean
ADSI 4.4 at baseline to 2.1 at Week 6, p=0.010
)
 and pruritus 
(
mean PNRS 2.2 at baseline to 1.1 at Week 6,
p=0.018
)
 at the end of the study. These results at 6 weeks were comparable to the hydrocortisone 1% cream arm
with reduction in mean ADSI from 4.3 at baseline to 1.9 
(
p=0.010
)
 and mean PNRS from 2.3 to 0.8 
(
p=0.002
)
. No
adverse effects were reported.
Conclusion:
L. rhinocerus
 is a steroid-sparing topical agent with potential utility in the treatment armamentarium for adults
and adolescents with AD, especially for areas of thinner skin or body folds, where the risk of adverse events of
topical corticosteroids may be more pronounced.
