17 SEPTEMBER - 20 SEPTEMBER 2025 
POWERED BY M-ANAGE.COM 


Abstract N°: 2668
Emollients containing urea and/or propylene glycol as humectant have varying effects on the skin barrier
of patients with atopic dermatitis, while all remain gentle on the skin microbiome
Tina Holm
1
, Sharon Lim
1
, Anna Fasth
1
, Dominik Schmaltz
2
, Gunja Springmann
2
, Cécile Artus-Arduise
3
1
Perrigo, Stockholm, Sweden
2
SGS proderm GmbH, Schenefeld, Germany
3
Perrigo, Paris, France
Introduction & Objectives:
 The imparied skin barrier plays a pivotal role in the pathogenesis of Atopic Dermatitis
(
AD
)
, and the most common symptoms are itch and dry skin. Regular emollient application is the mainstay of
xerosis management. While almost any emollient can improve skin dryness, their long term effect on the skin
barrier vary, with some even shown to impair the barrier. Therefore, it
’
s crucial to assess the effect of an emollient
on the skin barrier before recommending it to a patient with AD, who needs daily moisturization.
Recently, there has been growing interest in how emollients affect the resident skin microbiome. Propylene glycol
has some antimicrobial effect, and concerns regarding potential negative effects that preservatives in skincare can
have on the skin microbes have been raised. Therefore, it is becoming increasingly important to consider the
impact of emollients on the skin microbiome. The aim of the study was to compare the barrier-strengthening
properties of a new emollient, containing 5% urea and 20% propylene glycol 
(
test emollient
)
, to 1
)
 a propylene
glycol containing emollient 2
)
 an emollient containing urea and 3
)
 no treatment. The impact of these emollients
on the skin microbiome was also analysed.
Materials & Methods:
 A double-blind prospective within-subject multilateral randomized controlled study in
adults with a personal history of AD. Enrolled participants were instructed to treat the lower or upper parts of the
lower volar forearms 
(
i.e. 4 treatment areas, includes no treatment control, randomized allocation
)
 with the 3
products twice daily. After 4 weeks of treatment, all 4 areas were challenged with the irritant 1 % sodium lauryl
sulphate 
(
SLS
)
. The primary outcome was skin sensitivity to the irritant. In addition, a skin swab from each
treatment area 
(
and untreated skin
)
 of each participant was collected and sent for skin microbiome analysis using
16S rDNA sequencing.
Results:
 43 patients completed treatment with no major protocol deviations and were included in the analysis.
The primary objective was achieved; the test emollient was shown to be superior to no treatment and to the
emollient containing propylene glycol in reducing TEWL, indicating that the skin barrier became stronger and
more resilient after treatment. The skin barrier strengthening effect was equal to that of the urea emollient. The
skin also became less red following application of skin irritant on areas treated with the new emollient compared
to untreated skin.
Skin swabs collected from each treatment area were analyzed for positive or negative impact of the skin emollients
on the resident microbiome. No significant changes were observed in the microbiome after treatment of any of
the emollients, indicating that the emollients in this study are gentle to the microbiome. The study treatments
were all well tolerated.
Conclusions:
 Treatment for 4 weeks with the test emollient containing urea and propylene glycol showed
significant protection from SLS-induced skin irritation, resulting from a more resilient skin barrier. In contrast, the
protection provided by the emollient containing only propylene glycol was no different from untreated skin, which
highlights that not all emollients have positive influence on the skin barrier function. By helping to correct a major

pathophysiological process in AD, the test emollient has the potential to improve the long-term control of this
condition without disrupting the resident microbiome.
