Abstract N°: 2691
Early Super-Responder to Lebrikizumab in Moderate-to-Severe Atopic Dermatitis: A Real-World Clinical
Case
Ismael Khiar Fernández
1
, Juan Carlos Hernández Rodriguez
1
, Alonso García Núñez
1
, Juan Manuel Liñán Barroso
1
,
José Juan Pereyra Rodríguez
1
, Julián Conejo-Mir Sánchez
1
1
Hospital Universitario Virgen del Rocío, UGC Dermatología, Sevilla
Introduction & Objectives:
 We present the case of a 23-year-old male with long-standing moderate-to-severe
atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 and insufficient response to prior systemic therapies, who showed rapid improvement after
starting lebrikizumab. By week 4, he met criteria for both super-responder 
(
Spanish Delphi consensus
)
 and
Minimal Disease Activity 
(
MDA; Silverberg et al.
)
. This case emphasizes lebrikizumab
’
s potential as a fast-acting
option for refractory AD.
Materials & Methods:
 The patient had perinatal-onset AD with comorbid allergic rhinitis and seasonal asthma.
Prior therapies included high-potency topical corticosteroids, calcineurin inhibitors, short systemic corticosteroids,
subcutaneous methotrexate 
(
15 mg/week
)
, and cyclosporine 
(
4 mg/kg/day
)
, which lost efficacy after 8 months.
Due to poor control, lebrikizumab was initiated per approved regimen: 500 mg SC at weeks 0 and 2, then 250 mg
every two weeks to week 16, followed by 250 mg every 4 weeks. At baseline, he had involvement of the face
(
frontal region, upper eyelids
)
, antecubital folds, dorsal/lumbar areas, and feet dorsum. EASI: 22.81; IGA: 3; PP-
NRS: 8 
(
pruritus
)
, 7 
(
sleep disturbance
)
.
Results:
 At week 4, he showed marked improvement
—
particularly in the face and back
—
with IGA 1, EASI 1.41, PP-
NRS 3 
(
pruritus
)
, and 2 
(
sleep
)
. Response was maintained without rescue therapy or adverse events. Lebrikizumab,
a high-affinity IgG4 monoclonal antibody, targets IL-13, a key cytokine in type 2 inflammation, blocking IL-
13R
α
1/IL-4R
α
 signaling without affecting IL-13R
α
2. Phase 3 ADvocate 1 and 2 trials demonstrated efficacy as
monotherapy by week 16 
(
EASI-75 >50%
)
 with sustained results and favorable safety. The 
“
super-responder
”
phenotype, per Liñán-Barroso et al., describes rapid 
(≤
4 weeks
)
, sustained improvement and QoL gains. Silverberg
et al. proposed MDA targets 
(
EASI <5, pruritus/sleep 
≤
3
)
 for individualized treatment goals.
By week 4, our patient had EASI <2, pruritus reduced from 8 to 3, sleep from 7 to 2, and IGA 1
—
meeting both
definitions. Rapid facial and dorsal lesion resolution was especially meaningful psychosocially. These outcomes
align with ADvocate and TREBLE trial findings showing early pruritus 
(
week 2
)
 and EASI 
(
week 4
)
 improvements.
Chovatiya et al. showed that over 85% of patients on tralokinumab reached H&N-EASI 
≤
1 at week 152,
correlating with reduced discomfort and embarrassment from facial AD. Similarly, early frontal and eyelid lesion
clearance in our patient was highly impactful, reinforcing anti
–
IL-13 agents
’
 role in systemic control and hard-to-
treat regions like the face and neck.
Conclusion:
 Lebrikizumab is a safe, effective therapy for moderate-to-severe AD, offering rapid symptom relief
and potential for deep, sustained responses. This case illustrates how even treatment-refractory patients can
achieve early super-responder and MDA status, supporting the clinical value of lebrikizumab.
