Introduction & Objectives:
 In adults with moderate to severe Chronic Hand Eczema 
(
CHE
)
, delgocitinib cream, a
topical pan-Janus kinase 
(
JAK
)
 inhibitor, demonstrated significant improvement in primary and all secondary
efficacy endpoints and was well tolerated in DELTA-1 
(
NCT04871711
)
 and DELTA-2 
(
NCT04872101
)
 with a safety
profile comparable to cream vehicle. The objective of this pooled 
post hoc
 analysis of DELTA-1/-2 was to further
analyse the tolerability of delgocitinib cream to help clinicians communicate expectations to their patients by
exploring the relationship between tolerability and CHE.
Materials & Methods:
 Adults with moderate to severe CHE, who were treated with delgocitinib cream 20 mg/g
(
N=638
)
 or cream vehicle 
(
N=321
)
 twice daily for 16 weeks, were included. Patients and investigators provided
local tolerability assessments up to Week 
(
W
)
16. Patients reported worst stinging/burning experienced over the
last week in association with trial drug application 
(
severity grade: 0-3
)
. Investigators evaluated the skin of patients
for signs of possible local reactions to trial drug. Tolerability was also examined in relation to baseline Hand
Eczema Symptom Diary 
(
HESD
)
 cracking subscore 
(
weekly average; 0-3; 4-6; 
≥
7
)
 or baseline Hand Eczema
Severity Index 
(
HECSI
)
 fissure subscore 
(
0-2; 3-4; 
≥
5
)
.
Results:
 Most patients experienced no to mild local symptoms at any timepoint from W1-16 
(
[W1/4/16]
delgocitinib: 79.7/87.7/97.6%; cream vehicle: 71.5/77.7/97.7%
)
. The proportion of patients with severe symptoms
was low, with symptoms quickly resolving from W1-16 
(
[W1/4/16] delgocitinib: 5.0/2.5/0.2%; cream vehicle:
12.3/5.5/0.0%
)
. Few 
(
0.5% [3/638]
)
 delgocitinib-treated patients discontinued due to adverse events related to
treatment. According to the investigator assessment, almost all patients had no suspected local skin reactions
related to trial treatment during W1-16 
(
[W1
(
n/N
)
/16
(
n/N
)
] delgocitinib: 99.8% [620/621]/100% [596/596]; cream
vehicle: 98.4% [302/307]/100% [263/263]
)
. Similar results were seen for patients who terminated early
(
delgocitinib/cream vehicle: 97.1% [34/35]/90.2% [37/41]
)
. Any stinging/burning sensations reported by
delgocitinib-treated patients were likely related to underlying disease, particularly cracking or fissures. Patients
with higher cracking or fissures scores tended to report more symptoms versus those with lower scores. From W1-
16, mild to severe symptoms decreased in all observed categories for baseline HESD cracking 
(
[W1/4/16] scores 0-
3: 41.6/35.7/5.9%; 4-6: 44.8/33.6/11.1%; 
≥
7: 56.6/47.5/7.2%
)
 and baseline HECSI fissure scoring 
(
[W1/4/16]
subscores 0-2: 51.6/36.1/8.1%; 3-4: 48.8/39.9/7.1%; 
≥
5: 61.1/47.3/10.8%
)
. Overall, few patients reported local skin
infections of the hands or wrists, with similar rates observed between the treatment groups 
(
[number of events/R]
delgocitinib: 5/2.5; cream vehicle: 3/3.2
)
.
Conclusion:
 In DELTA-1/-2, most delgocitinib-treated patients reported no or mild symptoms, e.g.,
stinging/burning, with symptoms quickly resolving/improving through W16. Although infrequent, it is important

to inform and educate clinicians about possible symptoms in patients with CHE, to maximize treatment
compliance and appropriately manage expectations.
