Introduction & Objectives:
 The topical Janus kinase 
(
JAK
)
 inhibitor, delgocitinib cream, was well tolerated and
significantly improved all efficacy endpoints in moderate to severe Chronic Hand Eczema 
(
CHE
)
 in 2 phase 3 trials
(
DELTA-1/2
)
. The primary endpoint was Week 
(
W
)
16 Investigator
’
s Global Assessment for CHE 
(
IGA-CHE
)
 score of
0/1 
(
clear/almost clear skin
)
, with 
≥
2-step improvement from baseline. The newly redefined and stringent 
‘
almost
clear
’
 category in the IGA-CHE scale does not fully capture the full range of responses to delgocitinib. Here we
investigate other measures of clinical response, and treatment kinetics, to help clinicians contextualize and explain
potential benefits of delgocitinib to patients with CHE.
Materials & Methods:
 Patient-reported outcome measures 
(
PROMs
)
, including Hand Eczema Symptom Diary
(
HESD
)
-itch, HESD-pain, Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
, and Hand Eczema Impact Scale 
(
HEIS
)
, were
assessed in pooled DELTA-1/2 patients treated with twice-daily delgocitinib 
(
N=639
)
 or cream vehicle 
(
N=321
)
through Week 
(
W
)
16, according to baseline primary aetiological and morphological CHE subtype.
Results:
 Responder rates for 
≥
4-point improvement in HESD-itch/HESD-pain/DLQI at W16 were greater with
delgocitinib than cream vehicle across all CHE subtypes. For example, in vesicular CHE, 53.0%/57.0%/72.9% of
patients treated with delgocitinib achieved these clinically meaningful improvements versus 21.6%/28.1%/47.1%
with cream vehicle. Significantly greater response in each of these endpoints was achieved with delgocitinib versus
cream vehicle 
(
P<0.05
)
 within 4 weeks for all subtypes, and as early W1 for DLQI 
(
for ICD/AD/vesicular
)
. Similar
response time was observed for HEIS, with clinically meaningful responses from W2. Greater improvements from
baseline in absolute HESD-itch/HESD-pain were seen with delgocitinib versus cream vehicle 
(
P<0.05
)
 for all
subtypes by Day 10, and as early as Day 1.
Conclusion:
 Delgocitinib demonstrated clinically meaningful improvements at W16 in itch, pain, and quality of life
in adults across all major subtypes of moderate to severe CHE. These improvements were statistically significant
with delgocitinib versus cream vehicle as early as W1, demonstrating the rapid efficacy of delgocitinib across
subtypes.
