Abstract N°: 2749
PKM2 is a promising novel target in atopic dermatitis
Tom Ashton
1
, Sabine Broc
1
, Gareth Davies
1
, Martin Domville
1
, Eva Palsson McDermott
1, 2
, Luke O
’
Neill
1, 2
, Mark
Whitmarsh
1
, Lisa Pierre
1
, Madduri Ravin Rao
1
, Iain Kilty
1
1
Sitryx Therapeutics Ltd, Oxford, United Kingdom
2
School of Biochemistry and Immunology, Trinity Biomedical Science Instritute, Trinity College Dublin, Dublin,
Ireland
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a complex, chronic inflammatory, non-contagious skin disease with high unmet need
and no curative treatment options. Pyruvate kinase M2 
(
PKM2
)
 is a key enzyme in the central metabolic pathway
of glycolysis and is specifically upregulated in inflammatory tissues. Here we describe PKM2 as a novel target in
AD and demonstrate efficacy of novel PKM2 modulators in several mouse models of inflammatory skin disease:
KLH-induced delayed-type hypersensitivity 
(
DTH
)
, 2,4 dinitrofluorobenzene 
(
DNFB
)
 and oxazolone induced
contact dermatitis models. A PKM2 modulator has recently entered into a clinical trial for AD.
Materials & Methods:
PKM2 expression was studied in human healthy skin and AD skin using immunohistochemistry and RNA
sequencing. The 
in vitro
 pharmacology of PKM2 modulators was studied using biochemical assays and pyruvate
kinase activity in human CD4+ T cells. The 
in vivo
 pharmacology of PKM2 modulators was determined in 3 murine
models. DNFB or oxazolone were used to induce contact dermatitis and KLH was used to induce DTH. Efficacy was
measured using ear thickness, excoriations, ear RNA sequencing or ear Luminex 
(
IL-1
β
, IL-4, IL-6, IL-13, IFN
γ
, IL-
17a, IL-31
)
.
Results:
PKM2 was overexpressed in the immune cell infiltrate and in keratinocytes in severe lesional atopic dermatitis
compared to healthy skin. Similarly, PKM gene expression was elevated in AD biopsies, notably in chronic lesions.
As a potential AD therapeutic, novel, small molecule PKM2 modulators were shown to potently activate human
and mouse PKM2 enzymes. Pyruvate kinase activity was also observed in lysates from human CD4+ T cells pre-
treated with these compounds. In the DNFB model, PKM2 modulators resulted in a significant decrease in ear
thickness, a surrogate for inflammation, and were accompanied by decreases in ear IL-13, IL-17A and IL-6 and
decreases in chemokine gene expression. Similarly, there was a decrease in IL-31, a cytokine associated with itch.
PKM2 modulation also normalised expression of genes required for barrier function, including filaggrin, loricrin
and involucrin. A decrease in ear thickness was also observed in the oxazolone model, accompanied by a decrease
in skin excoriations, consistent with itch reduction. Similarly, PKM2 modulators decreased ear thickness in the KLH
model.
Conclusion:
PKM2 modulation offers an oral, targeted, disease-modifying anti-inflammatory approach, targeting a critical
enzyme that regulates cell metabolism. The proposed mechanism of action is believed to have targeted effects at
sites of inflammation, enabling a differentiated safety profile. The studies show that a novel PKM2 modulator
inhibits both inflammation and itch, whilst promoting skin barrier repair. This modulator has recently entered
clinical trials in healthy volunteers and patients with moderate to severe AD. This PKM2 modulator could address

the major unmet needs of AD as a safe oral, convenient and effective therapy where there are limited treatment
options under the current standard of care.
Figure 
–
 Resolution of inflammation in atopic dermatitis with a PKM2 modulator
