Introduction & Objectives:
With the expansion of targeted systemic drugs for atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
, comparative data on effectiveness and
tolerability are needed. This study aimed to 
(
i
)
 compare the 2-year drug survival 
(
DS
)
 of biologics and Janus
kinase inhibitors 
(
JAKi
)
 and identify associated predictors and 
(
ii
)
 evaluate the impact of the introduction of new
targeted therapies on dupilumab DS.
Materials & Methods:
This multicenter, prospective cohort study included adult AD patients treated with biologics 
(
dupilumab,
tralokinumab
)
 or JAKi 
(
abrocitinib, baricitinib, upadacitinib
)
 between Jan 2021 - Oct 2024 from the BioDay
registry. Dupilumab TEs from Oct 2017 - Oct 2024 were included in the secondary analysis. Subgroup analyses
included biologic-/JAKi-naïve and -experienced patients. Uni- and multivariable Cox regression models corrected
for within-subject correlation were used to compare DS and identify potential predictors per discontinuation
reason and treatment type.*
Results:
1583 treatment episodes 
(
TEs
)
 were included: 865 dupilumab, 227 tralokinumab, 144 abrocitinib, 106 baricitinib,

241 upadacitinib. The 2-year overall DS rates were 75.9% for dupilumab, 48.4% for tralokinumab, 27.1% for
abrocitinib, 28.9% for baricitinib, and 51.7% for upadacitinib, and were mostly associated with ineffectiveness
(
n=260, 16.4%
)
. Multivariable analyses found superior overall DS for dupilumab compared to tralokinumab 
(
HR
2.4, 95% CI 1.8-3.2, p<.0001
)
, abrocitinib 
(
HR 3.3, 95% CI 2.4-4.4, p<.0001
)
, baricitinib 
(
HR 5.0, 95% CI 3.6-7.1,
p<.0001
)
, and upadacitinib 
(
HR 2.2, 95% CI 1.6-2.9, p<.0001
)
. Analyses in naïve and experienced patients found
similar superior dupilumab DS, though differences were less pronounced. Dupilumab DS decreased after the
introduction of new therapies in 2021 
(
HR 2.0, 95% CI 1.4-2.5, p<.0001
)
. Predictors for discontinuation due to
ineffectiveness in the total cohort were use of 
≥
2 prior conventional immunosuppressants 
(
HR 1.3, 95% CI 1.1-1.5,
p=.0007
)
 and higher Numeric Rating Scale 
(
NRS
)
 itch score at baseline 
(
HR 1.2, 95% CI 1.1-1.4, p=.003
)
. Age 
(
HR
1.4, 95% CI 1.1-1.7, p=.004
)
 and pulmonary disease 
(
HR 1.5, 95% CI 1.1-2.1, p=.02
)
 were predictors for
discontinuation due to adverse effects. Higher NRS itch scores at baseline 
(
HR 1.4, 95% CI 1.1-1.7, p=.002
)
 and
concomitant immunosuppressant use at baseline 
(
HR 1.9, 95% CI 1.3-2.6, p=.0001
)
 were predictors for higher
discontinuation rates of dupilumab, and older age for lower discontinuation rates of baricitinib 
(
HR 0.5, 95% CI
0.3-0.8, p=.002
)
.
Conclusion:
In this prospective, multicenter cohort, dupilumab had the highest overall DS, followed by upadacitinib,
tralokinumab, and abrocitinib, with baricitinib showing the lowest DS rates. Ineffectiveness was the main reason for
treatment discontinuation across all therapies. Dupilumab consistently showed superior DS in both naïve and
experienced subgroups. Several predictors for DS per discontinuation reason and treatment type were identified.
Dupilumab DS declined following the introduction of new targeted systemic therapies, indicating that the
expanding treatment landscape influences treatment persistence with established therapies. These comparative
DS rates and predictors can support patients and physicians in expectation management and enhance shared
decision-making.
