Introduction & Objectives:
 Poorly controlled AD may impact the whole family. Roflumilast cream 0.15% and
0.05% have demonstrated efficacy, safety, and tolerability, compared with vehicle, in patients aged 
≥
6 years and
aged 2
–
5 years with atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 in the INTEGUMENT-1/2 and INTEGUMENT-PED phase 3 trials,
respectively. Poorly controlled AD may impact the whole family. The objectives of this analysis of patients in
INTEGUMENT-PED/NCT04845620 were to evaluate symptom and health-related quality-of-life changes with
roflumilast versus vehicle.
Materials & Methods:
 Eligible patients were aged 2
–
5 years with mild to moderate AD. Roflumilast cream 0.05%
or vehicle cream was applied once daily by a caregiver for 4 weeks. The primary endpoint was Validated
Investigator Global Assessment for AD 
(
vIGA-AD
)
 success 
(
clear/almost clear [0/1] plus 
≥
2-grade improvement
from baseline
)
 at week 4. Worst Itch-Numeric Rating Score 
(
WI-NRS
)
 success 
(≥
4-point improvement in patients
with baseline score 
≥
4
)
, SCORing AD 
(
SCORAD
)
, Patient-Oriented Eczema Measure 
(
POEM
)
, Children
’
s
Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
CDLQI; aged 
≥
4 years
)
, Infant DLQI 
(
IDLQI; aged <4 years
)
, and the Dermatitis
Family Impact questionnaire 
(
DFI
)
 were used to evaluate PROs, as reported by caregivers.
Results:
 There were 652 patients randomized into roflumilast cream 0.05% 
(
n=437
)
 and vehicle cream 
(
n=215
)
treatment groups. After 4 weeks, vIGA-AD success was achieved by significantly greater proportions of patients
who received roflumilast versus vehicle 
(
25.4% vs 10.7%; 
P
<0.0001
)
. Significantly greater proportions 
(
P
<0.0002
)
also achieved WI-NRS success with roflumilast 
(
35.3% [97.5% CI, 29.7
–
41.4]
)
 versus vehicle 
(
18.0% [12.0
–
26.1]
)
.
Least-squares mean 
(
LSM
)
 improvement in SCORAD from baseline to week 4 was significantly greater for the
roflumilast versus vehicle group overall 
(
19.30 vs 12.30; 
P
<0.0001
)
 and for individual components of pruritus 
(
3.11
vs 1.85; 
P
<0.0001
)
, sleep loss 
(
2.05 vs 1.29; 
P
=0.0005
)
, and dryness 
(
0.59 vs 0.43; 
P
=0.0061
)
. Significant
improvements 
(
all 
P
<0.05
)
 with roflumilast versus vehicle occurred for POEM overall 
(
7.5 vs 3.7
)
 and individual
component scores 
(
itching, 1.30 vs 0.73; sleep, 1.01 vs 0.60; skin bleeding, 0.91 vs 0.55; skin weeping/oozing, 0.40
vs 0.25; skin cracking, 1.35 vs 0.60; skin flaking, 1.19 vs 0.54; dryness/roughness, 1.47 vs 0.60
)
. After 4 weeks,
roflumilast improved quality of life measures significantly more than vehicle 
