Introduction & Objectives:
Skin pain is an increasingly recognized symptom in atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
, contributing to patient discomfort and
reduced quality of life. Despite its importance, the nature and prevalence of skin pain in AD remain poorly
understood. This study aimed to assess the frequency, severity, and psychosocial impact of skin pain in a large,
diverse population of AD patients, as part of the 
“
Scars of Life
”
 initiative, which seeks to build an international

database evaluating the burden of AD.
Materials & Methods:
This project is part of the Scars of Life initiative, dedicated to skin diseases, to examine and celebrate life journeys
related to dermatoses. A cross-sectional observational study was conducted between June and September 2024,
involving 21,687 adults from 27 countries across 5 continents. Participants were selected using the quota method
to ensure representativeness. They self-reported a clinical diagnosis of AD, its age of onset 
(
childhood,
adolescence, or adulthood
)
, and severity via the POEM score. Four groups were defined: AD with an onset in
childhood, adolescence, adulthood, and a control group without skin conditions. Skin pain prevalence and its
psychological impact 
(
via ABS-A5 and PUSH-D scores
)
 were analyzed, with statistical significance assessed
(
p<0.05
)
.
Results:
Of the 15,223 patients with AD, 20.2% reported skin pain, compared to 4.4% in the control group 
(
p<0.001
)
. Skin
pain was more frequent in women 
(
57.3% vs. 42.7% in men, p<0.007
)
 and increased with AD severity: 9.5% in mild,
27.3% in moderate, and 45.0% in severe cases 
(
p<0.001
)
. Patients with childhood-onset AD reported the highest
prevalence 
(
27.9%
)
 compared to adolescence 
(
18.3%
)
 or adulthood 
(
18.5%
)
 onset 
(
p<0.001
)
. Multivariate analysis
identified male gender, childhood onset, and burning sensations as key predictors of skin pain 
(
p<0.05
)
.
Psychosocial burden was significantly higher in those with pain, with ABS-A5 scores of 27.5 vs. 12.7 and PUSH-D
scores of 30.7 vs. 15.1 
(
p<0.001
)
.
Conclusion:
This survey confirms skin pain as a critical factor in AD, strongly associated with disease severity and early onset,
particularly in childhood. The elevated psychosocial burden and burning sensations highlight the need for
multidisciplinary management, combining dermatological and psychological approaches. Surprisingly, pruritus did
not differ between groups with and without pain, contrasting with prior published data. These findings emphasize
the importance of addressing skin pain associated with AD to improve patient outcomes and quality of life.
