Abstract N°: 2868
Erythrodermic atopic dermatitis successfully treated with combination of upadacitinib and dupilumab
Suzana Ozanic Bulic
1
, Bernarda Misanovic Markovic
2
, Nives Pustisek
1
, Marta Navratil
3
, Eva Brenner
4
, Luka Buli
ć
4
1
Children
’
s Hospital Zagreb, Department of paediatrics, Paediatric dermatology, Zagreb, Croatia
2
General Hospital Pula, Department of dermatology and venerology, Pula, Croatia
3
Children
’
s Hospital Zagreb, Department of paediatrics, Zagreb, Croatia
4
School of Medicine, University of Zagreb, Zagreb, Croatia
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic, inflammatory, pruritic skin disease commonly affecting children and adults
with personal or family history of atopy. Although sensitization to environmental or food allergens is clearly
associated with AD, in most patients it seems to be a contributory factor and not a causative agent. Erythrodermic
AD is a severe clinical subtype, characterized by marked erythema, severe pruritus, and scaling of extensively
affected skin 
(≥
90% body surface area
)
. A high risk of infection accompanies erythrodermic AD because
inflammation, fissuring, and excoriation increase susceptibility to bacteria and other microbes.
A healthy 15-year-old male presented with six-month history of erythroderma with AD background. Patient
initially received topical and systemic steroids, following ciclosporin up to 5 mg/kg/day with modest improvement
in erythrodermic AD. Few courses of systemic antibiotics were completed for septicaemia.
Materials & Methods:
Extensive workup for lymphoma was performed. Blood tests were in normal range, apart from total IgE >2000 IU,
and increased specific IgE to pollens and cat dander. Repeated skin biopsies confirmed AD. Extensive
lymphadenopathy required lymph node biopsies that were in line with dermatopathic lymphadenopathy. 
CARD14
variant was not found.**
Results:
Oral JAK inhibitor upadacitinib 15 mg OD was introduced with improvement in AD. Ten days into treatment,
patient became erythrodermic with Staphylococcus aureus positive skin swabs. Intravenous antibiotics improved
skin and upadacitinib was increased to 30 mg OD. Four weeks into treatment, erythroderma persisted and
treatment was stopped. Dupilumab 300 mg SC every other week was commenced immediately, with no
improvement in erythroderma after 8 weeks of treatment. Upon receiving repeated skin and lymph node biopsy
results confirming AD, the combination of dupilumab and upadacitinib 15 mg OD resulted in EASI-75 eight weeks
into treatment. Patient continued combined therapy over the last 18 months with complete resolution of AD
(
EASI-100
)
.
Conclusion:
Erythrodermic AD requires extensive workup with repeated skin biopsies to exclude cutaneous lymphoma and
other causes of erythroderma. Our patient had suffered a severe attack of AD in his teen years. Shared decision-
making, his optimism and parents
’
 support, along with multidisciplinary team support, resulted in timely and
aggressive treatment that brought this story to a happy ending.
