Introduction & Objectives:
The approval of upadacitinib for the treatment of atopic dermatitis in patients aged 12 years and older reflects the
efficacy and safety demonstrated in clinical trials. An increasing number of real-world studies support this
evidence, although most focus on short-term outcomes and do not specifically address subgroups such as
patients who are naïve to other advanced therapies.
Materials & Methods:
We conducted a study involving patients treated with upadacitinib across 16 hospitals in the Community of
Madrid, aiming to evaluate its short- and long-term safety and effectiveness. The study included patients with
atopic dermatitis who had not previously received any of the recently approved advanced systemic therapies for
the disease 
(
biologics or other JAK inhibitors
)
. This is an ambispective cohort study with up to 52 weeks of follow-
up, including patients who initiated treatment in real-life clinical practice between December 2022 and October
2024.
Results:
A total of 125 patients were included. Most patients showed marked clinical improvement as early as week 4,

which was maintained through week 52 of follow-up. The mean EASI score decreased to 5.09 at week 4 
(
N=117
)
,
4.76 at week 16 
(
N=113
)
, and 4.92 at week 52 
(
N=71
)
. At week 16, 73% of patients achieved EASI75 and 48%
achieved EASI90. By week 52, 75% had reached EASI75 and 53% had reached EASI90. At week 16, 65% of patients
achieved an IGA score of 0 or 1, increasing to 69% by week 52 
(
Fig. 3
)
. Pruritus, measured using the NRS,
decreased to a mean of 2.24 at week 4 
(
N=111
)
, 2.39 at week 16 
(
N=113
)
, and 2.69 at week 52 
(
N=68
)
. A pruritus
score of 0 or 1 was achieved by 45% of patients at week 4 and by 44% at week 52. Quality of life, measured
through the DLQI, also showed significant improvement throughout the treatment period. Aquí tienes la frase
traducida y adaptada al estilo científico en inglés: A total of 35.24% of patients achieved both a pruritus NRS score
of 0 or 1 and EASI90 at week 16. Regarding safety, a total of 108 adverse events were reported in 71 patients
(
56.8%
)
, the majority of which were mild and did not require treatment modification.
A total of 24 treatment discontinuations were recorded, most of which occurred after week 24. Seven were due to
adverse events, ten due to loss of efficacy 
(
one primary and nine secondary failures
)
, three due to well-controlled
atopic dermatitis, and three due to poor adherence.
Conclusion:
The data obtained in our cohort are consistent with those reported in clinical trials and support findings from
other real-world studies. These results confirm the high effectiveness and favorable safety profile of upadacitinib
in patients who were naïve to other advanced therapies
