Abstract N°: 2907
TRIV-509, a dual inhibitor of KLK5 and KLK7, normalizes epidermal hyper-proliferation, improves barrier
integrity and reduces secretion of inflammatory cytokines in atopic dermatitis skin explants
Elizabeth Mateer*
1
, Eva Asp
1
, Kejie Li
1
, Marta Silva e Sousa
2
, Marta Bertolini
2
, Ilaria Piccini
2
, Janin Edelkamp
2
,
Bhaskar Srivastava
1
, Jennifer Dovey
1
1
Triveni Bio, Watertown, United States
2
QIMA Life Sciences, QIMA Monasterium GmbH, Münster, Germany
Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 results from a combination of barrier impairment, pruritus,
and inflammation. Approved biologics such as dupilumab, lebrikizumab, and tralokinumab directly target the IL-
4/IL-13 inflammatory pathway. However, only ~20-40% of patients achieve an Investigator
’
s Global Assessment
(
IGA
)
 score of 0 or 1 after 16 weeks of these treatments. Novel therapeutics that directly target all three
components of AD pathology are needed. We have developed a dual-targeting monoclonal antibody, TRIV-509,
that selectively and potently inhibits kallikreins 
(
KLK
)
 5 and 7. KLK5 and KLK7, members of a serine protease
family, directly contribute to barrier dysfunction 
(
including corneodesmosome degradation
)
, pruritus 
(
through a
KLK7-dependent pathway
)
, and inflammation 
(
via KLK5-PAR2 signaling
)
. In normal skin, KLK5 and KLK7 activity is
limited to the top layers of the epidermis to enable natural desquamation. In AD, KLK5 and KLK7 activity and
expression is higher and penetrate deeper into the epidermis, resulting in cleavage of structural barrier proteins
(
e.g., DSG1
)
 and impaired structural barrier integrity.
Materials & Methods:
 To further understand the mechanisms of KLK5 and KLK7, biopsies were collected from
lesional and peri-lesional skin from patients with a clinical diagnosis of AD 
(
IGA ³ 2
)
. Biopsies were then cultured
with TRIV-509 or control IgG for 72 hours. The biopsies were processed for histology/protein analysis, and
supernatant was collected for cytokine analysis.
Results:
 TRIV-509 significantly reduced epidermal hyperplasia and parakeratosis in active AD skin from all three
patients. Furthermore, TRIV-509 rapidly normalized hyperproliferation, resulting in significant reduction 
(
~80%
)
 of
Ki-67+ cells compared to control IgG. Additionally, TRIV-509 increased the number of desmoglein 1 
(
DSG1
)
positive cells and filaggrin 
(
FLG
)
 expression in the granular layers of the epidermis. Finally, TRIV-509 reduced the
secretion of Th1/Th2/Th17-related cytokines.
Conclusion:
 Inhibition of KLK5 and KLK7 ameliorates the AD epidermal phenotype in 
ex vivo
 explant cultures by
normalizing epidermal keratinocyte proliferation, improving barrier integrity, and reducing secretion of AD-
associated cytokines. These data support therapeutic rationale for KLK5 and KLK7 inhibition and ongoing clinical
development of TRIV-509 in AD.
