Abstract N°: 2970
Clinical overlap between Psoriasis, Atopic Dermatitis and Prurigo Nodularis. A rare phenotipic presentation
Flavia Daniele*
1, 2
1
FD Dermatology & Aesthetics, Inmunodermatology, Buenos Aires, Argentina
2
Swiss Medical Center , Inmunodermatology, Buenos Aires, Argentina
**
Introduction & Objectives:
 Psoriasis, 
(
Pso
)
 atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
, and prurigo nodularis 
(
PN
)
 are common T-
cell
–
mediated inflammatory skin disorders that can be treated with targeted cytokine inhibitors or broader
immunomodulatory agents. We report a case highlighting the clinical and histopathological overlap of these
conditions in a male patient with longstanding disease
’
s history. 
Materials & Methods:
 A 43-year-old male with
a 20-year history of generalized plaque psoriasis accompanied by a long history of Atopia, consistent on large,
oozing, and crusted patches predominantly in flexural areas. Initial assessments using Hanifin and Rajka
’
s criteria
showed an AD with SCORAD 80 and a PASI 20 score. The patient had notable comorbidities, including smoking, a
sedentary lifestyle, and a BMI of 35. He was started on methotrexate 20 mg weekly with folic acid 5 mg
supplementation to assess both features.As well as dietary and exercise assessment, and avoid smoking
recommendation. After one month, he experienced a flare-up with extensive erythematous, scaly plaques
involving the genitalia, face, trunk, arms, and legs, raising his PASI score to 90. During the initial laboratory test
latent tuberculosis infection was diagnosed based on a 20 mm PPD, and isoniazid was initiated for six months.
Due to severe psoriasis, guselkumab 100 mg every 8 weeks was introduced, yielding a PASI 90 improvement
within the first 6 months. However, the patient later developed deeply infiltrated dermal nodules on the extensor
surfaces of the forearms, thighs, back, and groin, accompanied by intense pruritus. The biopsy showed Prurigo
Nodularis 
(
PN
)
. Therapy was adjusted to include fexofenadine 180 mg daily, methotrexate 20 mg weekly, and
continued guselkumab. Despite this drug regime, he experienced disease progression. At the one-year mark,
guselkumab was discontinued due to lack of efficacy, and treatment was switched to ixekizumab 80mg monthly.
This regimen achieved PASI 100 and SCORAD 10, right after induction period ended, indicating marked
improvement in psoriasis and moderate response in PN and AD. After 3 months on ixekizumab ,he developed
complications, including acute  myocardial infartation 
(
AMI
)
, recurrent dental and urinary tract infections, which
precluded further therapies combination. Seven months later he continues improving psoriasis with ixekizumab
reaching PASI 100 for the last 3 months and currently regression of the nodules 
(
PN
)
 on both forearms and
muscles. 
Conclusion:
 The Flip Flop phenomenon was described between psoriasis and atopic dermatitis. This case
shows how prurigo nodularis and AD can both be also part of the phenomenon. It underscores the importance of
careful clinical evaluation, individualized treatment strategies, and vigilance for systemic complications, especially
in patients receiving long-term target therapy.
