, Yayoi Tomura
1
, Saki Fukaya
1
, Kotaro Hayashi
1
, Atsuko Fukuyasu
1
, Takamitsu Tanaka
1
, Takeko Ishikawa
1
,
Yayoi Tada
1
1
Teikyo University School of Medicine, Dermatology, Tokyo, Japan
Introduction & Objectives: Lebrikizumab, an anti-interleukin 
(
IL
)
-13 antibody, has shown efficacy in
clinical trials for patients with moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
, with dosing every two weeks
(
q2w
)
 or every four weeks 
(
q4w
)
. In Japan, lebrikizumab was approved for AD in May 2024, with an initial
loading dose of 500 mg administered at Weeks 0 and 2, followed by a maintenance dose of 250 mg q2w
starting at Week 4. Depending on the patient
’
s condition, a q4w maintenance regimen beginning at Week
4 is also permitted. Given the small differences in efficacy between q2w and q4w observed in clinical trials
and the benefits of reduced dosing frequency, q4w dosing from Week 4 onward could be a viable
alternative. However, to date, no data have been reported regarding the effectiveness of this regimen. In
our department, most patients received lebrikizumab q4w from Week 4 following the two initial loading
doses. This study aims to evaluate the real-world effectiveness and safety of lebrikizumab with the q4w
regimen from Week 4 onward in AD patients.
Materials & Methods: Patients with AD who initiated lebrikizumab treatment at our department between
May 2024 and January 2025 were retrospectively included in this study. Effectiveness, safety, and
laboratory findings were assessed over a 3-month period.
Results: A total of 36 patients were included in the analysis. The mean age of patients without prior
exposure to systemic therapy 
(
naïve group, n=15
)
 was 35.9 ± 3.76 years, while that of patients with it
(
switch group, n=21
)
 was 38.4 ± 3.36 years. Prior treatments in the switch group included dupilumab
(
n=16
)
, tralokinumab 
(
n=2
)
, baricitinib 
(
n=3
)
, and upadacitinib 
(
n=6
)
, with some overlap. At baseline,
the mean values for the naïve and switch groups were as follows, respectively: total Eczema Area and
Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
, 23.8 ± 2.95 / 18.2 ± 3.07; head/neck EASI, 2.49 ± 0.36 / 1.78 ± 0.36; affected body
surface area 
(
BSA
)
, 46.4 ± 5.43% / 37.0 ± 4.84%; visual analogue scale 
(
VAS
)
 for pruritus, 65.3 ± 7.39 /
45.5 ± 6.58; Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
, 12.9 ± 1.94 / 9.15 ± 1.60; Patient-Oriented Eczema
Measure 
(
POEM
)
, 17.8 ± 1.45 / 6.58 ± 1.23; lactate dehydrogenase 
(
LDH
)
, 267 ± 36.2 / 228 ± 19.3 U/L;
thymus and activation-regulated chemokine 
(
TARC
)
, 2778 ± 972 / 4751 ± 2746 pg/mL; and IgE, 14,725 ±
7618 / 10,330 ± 5930 IU/mL. All patients except two received lebrikizumab q4w from Week 4 following the
two initial loading doses at Weeks 0 and 2. After 3 months of treatment, the naïve group demonstrated
significant improvements in all outcomes except for IgE, with mean reductions of: IGA, 55.6% 
(
p <
0.0001
)
; total EASI, 83.5% 
(
p = 0.0002
)
; head/neck EASI, 81.7% 
(
p = 0.0003
)
; BSA, 84.9% 
(
p = 0.0007
)
;
VAS for pruritus, 70.0% 
(
p = 0.0054
)
; DLQI, 77.7% 
(
p < 0.0001
)
; POEM, 58.1% 
(
p = 0.0025
)
; LDH, 36.2% 
(
p
= 0.0302
)
; TARC, 70.2% 
(
p = 0.00453
)
; and IgE, 46.4% 
(
p = 0.5256
)
. The switch group showed statistically
significant improvements only in IGA 
(
33.3% reduction, p = 0.0165
)
 and total EASI 
(
54.7% reduction, p =
0.0179
)
. Regarding safety, conjunctivitis was observed in 8 patients. Anaphylaxis, psoriasis-like eruptions,
AD exacerbation, and injection site reactions were each observed in one patient and led to treatment
discontinuation.

Conclusion: This real-world study demonstrates that lebrikizumab administered q4w from Week 4
following two loading doses is an effective and generally well-tolerated treatment option for AD patients.
