Abstract N°: 3066
Assessing the impact of Atopic Dermatitis on Quality of Life using DLQI: A Real world cross sectional study
from India
Dhiraj Dhoot*
1
, tripti sharma
1
, ashwin balasubramanian
1
, saiprasad patil
1
, Hanmant Barkate
1
1
Glenmark Pharmaceuticals Ltd, Global Medical Affairs, Mumbai, India
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is the most prevalent chronic inflammatory skin disease globally, exerting a substantial
burden not only on patients but also on caregivers and families. Quantifying this impact is essential for guiding
clinical management and optimizing support strategies. The Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
 is the most
widely used instrument to assess quality of life 
(
QoL
)
 in AD patients. This study aimed to examine gender-based
differences in QoL domains, stratify findings by disease severity, and evaluate the association between socio-
economic status 
(
SES
)
 and DLQI scores.
Materials & Methods:
A cross-sectional study was conducted between November 2024 and March 2025 involving consecutive clinically
diagnosed AD patients attending outpatient department from 110 centres across India with Independent Ethics
committee approval. Disease severity was stratified by DLQI total score into mild 
(
0
–
5
)
, moderate 
(
6
–
10
)
, and
severe 
(
>11
)
 categories. Gender differences across six QoL domains
—
Symptoms, Daily Activities, Leisure,
Work/School, Personal Relationships, and Treatment
—
were analysed using unpaired t-tests. Differences in DLQI
scores across socioeconomic status 
(
SES
)
 groups 
(
Low, Middle, High
)
 were assessed using one-way ANOVA with
Tukey
’
s post hoc analysis.
Results:
A total of 660 AD patients were included in the study, of which 320 
(
48.5%
)
 were female and 340 
(
51.5%
)
 were
male. The mean age was 27.9 ± 14.7 years, with a mean disease duration of 3.5 ± 4.2 years. The average DLQI
score was 10.8 ± 6.7. No significant gender differences were found across QoL domains or total DLQI scores in the
overall population 
(
p > 0.05
)
. However, among patients with severe disease 
(
DLQI >11
)
, males reported
significantly greater impairment only in the Leisure domain 
(
t = 2.09, p = 0.038
)
. SES was significantly associated
with DLQI scores 
(
F = 10.86, p < 0.0001
)
, with patients in the low SES group reporting greater QoL impairment
compared to those in the medium and high SES groups. No significant difference was observed between medium
and high SES groups 
(
p = 0.263
)
.
Conclusion:
QoL outcomes in adults with AD were influenced by socio-economic status and, in severe cases, by gender. Lower
SES was associated with greater overall impairment, and male patients with severe disease report higher
disruption in leisure activities. These findings highlight the need for tailored interventions targeting patients with
severe disease and socio-economic vulnerability.
