Abstract N°: 3134
Bruton
’
s Tyrosine Kinase Inhibitor Attenuates Skin Lesions in an Atopic Dermatitis Mouse Model by
Suppressing Th2 Inflammation and Enhancing Skin Barrier Function
Zhao Wang
1
, Xi Zhao
1
, Yuxin Zhang
1
, Weihui Zeng
1
1
The Second Affiliated Hospital, Xi
’
an Jiaotong University, Department of Dermatology, x
Introduction & Objectives:
Bruton
’
s tyrosine kinase 
(
BTK
)
 is a key mediator of B cell receptor signaling and an established therapeutic target
in autoimmune diseases. Covalent BTK inhibitors 
(
BTKis
)
, such as remibrutinib, have demonstrated clinical efficacy
in conditions like chronic spontaneous urticaria. Beyond B cells, BTK also participates in T cell receptor signaling,
suggesting broader immunomodulatory potential. Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is primarily driven by Th2-mediated
inflammation, but the therapeutic potential of BTK inhibition in AD remains underexplored. This study investigates
the efficacy and underlying mechanisms of BTKi treatment in a murine model of AD.
Materials & Methods:
Bruton
’
s tyrosine kinase 
(
BTK
)
 is a key mediator of B cell receptor signaling and an established therapeutic target
in autoimmune diseases. Covalent BTK inhibitors 
(
BTKis
)
, such as remibrutinib, have demonstrated clinical efficacy
in conditions like chronic spontaneous urticaria. Beyond B cells, BTK also participates in T cell receptor signaling,
suggesting broader immunomodulatory potential. Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is primarily driven by Th2-mediated
inflammation, but the therapeutic potential of BTK inhibition in AD remains underexplored. This study investigates
the efficacy and underlying mechanisms of BTKi treatment in a murine model of AD.
Results:
Remibrutinib treatment significantly ameliorated AD-like skin lesions and reduced scratching behavior compared
to untreated controls. Histologically, it diminished MC903-induced hyperkeratosis, inflammatory cell infiltration,
and mast cell degranulation. RNA-seq analysis revealed that remibrutinib suppressed Th2-, Th17-, and Treg-
related pathways while restoring expression of skin barrier-related genes.
Conclusion:
Remibrutinib alleviates clinical and histological features of AD by inhibiting mast cell activation, downregulating
pro-inflammatory cytokines, and enhancing skin barrier function. These findings support the potential of BTK
inhibition as a novel therapeutic strategy for atopic dermatitis.
