Abstract N°: 3185
Real-world effectiveness and safety of upadacitinib for atopic dermatitis between dupilumab-experienced
and dupilumab-naïve patients
Aikaterini Tsiogka
1
, Ioannis-Alexios Koumprentziotis
1
, Ileana Afroditi Kleidona
1
, Michail Bakakis
1
, Eleni
Hatzidimitriou
1
, Theodora Douvali
1
, Styliani Mastraftsi
1
, Aristeidis Vaiopoulos
1
, Alexander Stratigos
1
, Stamatis
Gregoriou*
1
1
1st Department of Dermatology and Venereology, Medical School, National and Kapodistrian University of
Athens, 
“
A. Sygros
”
 Hospital for Skin and Venereal Diseases, Athens, Greece
Introduction & Objectives:
 Upadacitinib, a selective Janus kinase inhibitor 
(
JAK
)
1 inhibitor, has demonstrated a
favourable aggregate response benefit in skin clearance and itch reduction over dupilumab as demonstrated in
the Heads Up, phase 3 study. It has also been reported to improve outcomes in patients with previous dupilumab
use, in the open-label extension of the same study. Real-world data on patients with atopic dermatitis needing to
switch from dupilumab to upadacitinib treatment are currently limited. Therefore, the aim of this study was to
summarize our centre
’
s experience and compare the outcomes of upadacitinib in dupilumab-experienced and
dupilumab-naïve patients.
Materials & Methods:
 We retrospectively included all patients treated with upadacitinib at our centre and further
analysed their corresponding data based on previous dupilumab exposure. Clinical and patient-reported
outcomes were recorded and assessed at each follow-up visit and included the eczema area severity index 
(
EASI
)
,
investigator global assessment 
(
IGA
)
, scoring atopic dermatitis 
(
SCORAD
)
, dermatology life quality index 
(
DLQI
)
and the worst pruritus numerical scale score 
(
WP-NRS
)
. Drug-related adverse events 
(
AEs
)
 were assessed and
documented.
Results:
 In total, 36 patients 
(
10 dupilumab-experienced, 26 dupilumab-naïve
)
 were identified and included.
Regarding those that had previously discontinued dupilumab, nine patients had discontinued due to inadequate
disease management 
(
non-achieving or maintaining EASI75 or according to patient preference due to severely
impacted quality of life
)
 and one patient due to AEs 
(
conjunctivitis
)
. EASI75/90/100 were achieved by 70/50/10%
of the dupilumab-experienced patients after 4 weeks of treatment and by 87.5/ 87.5/62.5 after 16 weeks. In the
dupilumab-naïve cohort, EASI75/90/100 were achieved by 88.5/53.8/23.1% after 4 weeks and by 100/100/55%
after 16 weeks. At weeks 48, complete skin clearance was achieved by all five 
(
100%
)
 dupilumab-exposed patients.
Eight 
(
72.7%
)
 of the dupilumab-naïve patients had a clear skin at week 48. For both groups, all patients that
reached week 96 had achieved EASI100 along with the other clinical endpoints. Concerning safety, two 
(
20%
)
 of
the dupilumab-exposed patients experienced AEs, one hepatic enzyme abnormalities, and the other, who
discontinued medication after one-month, recurrent urinary tract infections. Among the dupilumab naïve patients,
four 
(
15.4%
)
 suffered from AEs, two from hepatic enzyme abnormalities with one discontinuing medication, one
had anaemia with a significant reduction in the haemoglobin and haematocrit counts and one from recurrent
respiratory infections, both leading to upadacitinib discontinuation.
Conclusion:
 This study confirmed the clinical effectiveness and good safety profile of upadacitinib treatment in
both dupilumab-experienced and dupilumab-naive patients with atopic dermatitis in a real-world setting. Further
research is warranted with larger studies, prospective in design, to evaluate the long-term effectiveness and safety
of upadacitinib in real-world settings.
