14
Shanghai Skin Disease Hospital, Tongji University School of Medicine, Shanghai, China
15
Tongji Hospital Affiliated to Tongji Medical College of Huazhong University of Science and Technology, Wuhan,
China
Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disease marked by pruritis and
polymorphic rashes, significantly impacting patients
’
 quality of life. Abrocitinib, a Janus kinase 
(
JAK
)
 inhibitor, has
shown great efficacy in moderate-to-severe AD in clinical trials. However, real-world data on its use among
Chinese AD patients is lacking. The Abrocitinib Chinese Registry on AD 
(
AHEAD
)
 is an ongoing prospective,
multicenter registry including AD patients from 42 sites in China. Here we conducted the 2nd interim analysis of
AHEAD data, describing the treatment patterns and effectiveness of Abrocitinib in Chinese AD patients.
Materials & Methods:
 Patients were eligible if they were 
≥
18 years old, Abrocitinib-naïve, or had discontinued
Abrocitinib for >30 days at enrollment. The index date was the first prescription date of Abrocitinib during the
study. Data were collected during routine clinical visits at week 2, 4, 12, 24, and 52, including demographic
characteristics, Abrocitinib treatment patterns, and AD clinical outcomes. The AD Clinical outcomes included AD
flare-ups, Investigator
’
s Global Assessment 
(
IGA
)
, Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
, Peak Pruritus Numerical
Rating Scale 
(
PP-NRS
)
, and other patient- or physician-reported outcomes.
Results:
 From 18 Oct 2023 to 10 Feb 2025, a total of 1117 AD patients were enrolled with a mean 
(
SD
)
 follow-up
of 178.4 
(
117.3
)
 days. The mean 
(
SD
)
 age of all participants was 42.9 
(
16.4
)
, and 47.4% were female. In all 1104
patients with records of medication history, 187 
(
16.9%
)
 had received at least one form of systemic treatment,
among whom, 66 
(
35.3%
)
 received traditional treatments, 78 
(
41.7%
)
 received biologics, and 64 
(
34.2%
)
 received
small molecule JAK inhibitors. 982 
(
89.4%
)
 of patients started with 100mg daily Abrocitinib, while 117 took 200mg
daily. 37.9% of patients underwent Abrocitinib dose adjustment. Among them, 39.0% decreased the dose, 12.7%

increased the dose, and 62.7% discontinued Abrocitinib treatment 
(
patients who experienced multiple dose
adjustments could be included in more than one category
)
. As of the cutoff date, 1031 
(
93.6%
)
 of patients had
good adherence 
(
proportion of days covered [PDC] 
≥
 0.8
)
 to Abrocitinib. 47
(
18.1%
)
 of patients who discontinued
Abrocitinib experienced AD flare-ups during their follow-up, with a mean 
(
SD
)
 time-to-flare-up of 30.9 
(
35.8
)
days. 47.2% and 69.2% of patients achieved IGA success at weeks 24 and 52, respectively. EASI-90 response rates
increased from 9.8% at week 2 to 44.0% at week 24 and 57.7% at week 52. EASI-75 response rates also increased
from 23.4% at week 2 to 67.6% at week 24 and 76.9% at week 52. PP-NRS 4 achievement rates rose from 56.6% at
week 2 to 66.2% at week 4, and remained stable until week 52. 
(
Figure 1
)
 Changes in IGA, EASI, PP-NRS, and other
AD clinical outcomes from baseline during the follow-up were presented in Table 1. Patients on Abrocitinib 200mg
daily showed numerically more changes from baseline in EASI and PP-NRS throughout the 12-week follow-up.
(
Figure 2
)
Conclusion:
 This interim analysis demonstrated that Abrocitinib consistently improved AD clinical outcomes over
a one-year follow-up. Patients receiving dose of 200mg daily showed numerically more improvement from
baseline in EASI and PP-NRS than those on 100mg since week 2.
