Abstract N°: 3286
Long-term efficacy with tralokinumab in patients with moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis: final results
from the 5-year ECZTEND study
Andrew Blauvelt* , Andrew Blauvelt
1
, Chih-Ho Hong
2
, Norito Katoh
3
, Vivian Laquer
4
, Ketty Peris
5
, Julien
Seneschal
6, 7
, Juan Salvador
8
, Niels Bennike
9
, Farzaneh Safavimanesh
9
, Ann-Marie Tindberg
9
, Richard B. Warren
10,
11
1
Blauvelt Consulting, LLC, Annapolis, United States
2
University of British Columbia, Department of Dermatology and Skin Science, Vancouver, Canada
3
Kyoto Prefectural University of Medicine Graduate School of Medical Science, Kyoto, Japan
4
First OC Dermatology Research, Fountain Valley, United States
5
Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore, Dermatologia, Rome, Italy
6
Hôpital Saint-André, Department of Dermatology and Pediatric Dermatology, National Reference Center for Rare
Skin Disorders, Bordeaux, France
7
Univ. Bordeaux, CNRS, Immuno ConcEpT, Bordeaux, France
8
Hospital General Universitario Dr Balmis, Dermatology Department, Alicante, Spain
9
LEO Pharma A/S, Ballerup, Denmark
10
Northern Care Alliance NHS Foundation Trust, Dermatology Centre, Manchester, United Kingdom
11
Manchester Academic Health Science Centre, NIHR Manchester Biomedical Research Centre, Manchester
University NHS Foundation Trust, Manchester, United Kingdom
Introduction & Objectives:
 To ensure minimal residual disease and to prevent relapses, recently published
consensus-based recommendations have defined long-term treatment targets for atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 [1].
Tralokinumab, a fully human monoclonal antibody that specifically neutralizes interleukin-13, is approved for the
treatment of moderate-to-severe AD in adults and adolescents in multiple countries. ECZTEND 
(
NCT03587805
)
was a 5-year, open-label extension study that investigated the long-term safety and efficacy of tralokinumab. Here,
the objectives were to determine the proportion of tralokinumab-treated patients with moderate-to-severe AD
who achieved long-term treatment targets and maintained stable disease control, with no or minimal fluctuations,
during and up to 5 years of treatment in ECZTEND.
Materials & Methods:
 The ECZTEND study assessed tralokinumab 300 mg every other week with optional topical
corticosteroids in patients 
(≥
12 years
)
 with moderate-to-severe AD who completed any of 9 parent trials. Efficacy
endpoints analyzed included the proportion of patients meeting recommended skin response targets 
(
EASI-75,
EASI 
≤
7, EASI-90, and EASI 
≤
3
)
 at Week 248 and the proportion of patients maintaining response with no or
minimal disease fluctuations 
(
at 
≥
80% of days after first endpoint achievement up to end of treatment
)
.
Results:
 A total of 1647 patients were enrolled in ECZTEND. At Week 248, EASI-75 and EASI 
≤
7 
(
95% confidence
interval
)
 were observed in 92.9% 
(
85.3-96.7
)
 and 95.2% 
(
88.4-98.1
)
 of patients, respectively; EASI-90 and EASI 
≤
3
were detected in 81.0% 
(
71.3-87.9
)
 and 83.3% 
(
73.9-89.8
)
 of patients, respectively. Proportions of patients who
maintained EASI-75 and EASI-90 with no or minimal fluctuations were 75.1% 
(
72.9-77.2
)
 and 54.7% 
(
52.0-57.3
)
,
respectively.**
Conclusion:
 Optimal skin responses 
(
EASI-90; EASI 
≤
3
)
 occurred in more than 80% of patients treated with
tralokinumab 300 mg every other week for up to 5 years, with a large proportion maintaining stable response with
no or minimal disease fluctuations 
(
at 
≥
80% of days after first endpoint achievement up to end of treatment
)
.

