Abstract N°: 3315
Mechanisms of blood-brain barrier disruption and central nervous system inflammation in calcipotriol-
induced atopic dermatitis mice
Zhiyan Zhang
1
, Siming Yang*
1
, jie yang
1
, Yunda Guo
1
, minglu xiao
1
, tong peng
1
1
the Fourth Medical Center␀PLA General Hospital , Beijing, China
Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis␀AD␀ and its central nervous system complications are a significant
concern for many patients in clinical practice, but the specific connections and mechanisms remain unclear.We aim
to investigate whether there is a destruction of the blood-brain barrier when the skin lesions occur simultaneously
and whether there are inflammatory changes in the central nervous system, as well as the mechanisms that
produce these changes.
Materials & Methods:
 Continuous application of calcipotriol solution 
(
2 nmol/L
)
 on the back of C57/BL6 mice
induces AD. Skin paraffin sections are stained with HE and Masson to observe pathological changes. The open
field test, Y-maze test, and tail suspension test are used to assess behavioral changes in mice. After intravenous
injection of Dextran dye, brain frozen sections are observed under laser confocal microscopy to assess changes in
blood-brain barrier permeability. After Iba-1 staining of mouse brain frozen sections, microglial activation is
observed under laser confocal microscopy. GFAP staining of brain paraffin sections is used to observe astrocyte
activation. Transmission electron microscopy is used to observe changes in vesicular transport in brain vascular
endothelial cells. Western blot analysis is conducted to observe changes in tight junction proteins of the blood-
brain barrier. ELISA experiments validate changes in inflammatory factors in serum. High-performance liquid
chromatography is used to measure the concentration of calcipotriol in mouse blood. A combined analysis of
blood proteomics and brain transcriptomics is performed to screen pathways that may lead to central nervous
system complications in AD mice.
Results:
 When mice were continuously administered calcipotriol for 8 days, both macroscopic and pathological
changes resembling AD were observed, with significant behavioral abnormalities 
(
P<0.05
)
, marked leakage of the
blood-brain barrier 
(
P<0.001
)
, and full activation of microglia and astrocytes indicating the occurrence of
neuroinflammation 
(
P<0.05
)
. Additionally, there was an increase in vesicular transport in brain endothelial cells,
and tight junction proteins were significantly disrupted 
(
P<0.05
)
. Calcipotriol was not detected in the blood of the
mice. The IL-17 pathway showed significant differences in blood proteomics and brain transcriptomics 
(
P<0.05
)
.
After intervention with an IL17A inhibitor, the skin lesions in the mice showed some improvement, with a
significant reduction in blood-brain barrier leakage and alleviation of neuroinflammation.
Conclusion:
 In the AD mouse model induced by calcipotriol, there was a disruption of the blood-brain barrier and
inflammation of central nervous. During the process of central nervous lesions, the IL-17 pathway played an
important role, providing novel insights for the diagnosis and treatment of patients with AD accompanied by
central nervous complications in clinical practice.



