Abstract N°: 3353
Atopic Eruption of Pregnancy: insights from our experience
María Walker*
1
, Marina Gagliardi
1
, Maria Laura Marchese
1
, Carla Castro
1
1
Hospital Universitario Austral, Pilar, Argentina
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic eruption of pregnancy 
(
AEP
)
 is a condition that includes atopic dermatitis that either worsens or begins
during pregnancy, as well as prurigo of pregnancy and pruritic folliculitis of pregnancy. It was reclassified in 2006
by Ambros-Rudolph et al., who proposed grouping these entities under the umbrella of AEP based on their shared
clinical features and a possible association with atopy.
AEP most commonly begins in the first trimester, and approximately 20% of patients have a prior history of atopy.
Clinically, it presents in one-third of patients as an eczematous eruption 
(
AEP type E
)
 and in another third as a
papular eruption 
(
AEP type P
)
. Lesions may appear anywhere on the body, including the palms and soles.
Diagnosis is generally clinical, and treatment is symptomatic, involving oral antihistamines and topical or oral
corticosteroids. This condition poses no maternal or fetal risk, unlike other pregnancy dermatoses that do, such as
gestational pemphigoid and intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy.
The objective of this study is to describe the characteristics of patients diagnosed with AEP at the Pregnancy
Dermatoses Unit of the Austral University Hospital.
Materials & Methods:
We conducted an observational study including patients treated at the Pregnancy Dermatoses Unit of the Austral
University Hospital with a diagnosis of AEP. Data collected included maternal age, gestational age at diagnosis,
number of previous pregnancies and deliveries, type of AEP 
(
E or P
)
, history of atopy, need for skin biopsy for
histopathological analysis, and treatment administered.
Results:
A total of 30 patients were included in the study, with a mean age of 31.7 years. The average gestational age at
diagnosis was 24.6 weeks, ranging from 9 to 39 weeks. Three patients were diagnosed during the immediate
postpartum period 
(
within 30 days after delivery
)
. A third of the patients were primiparous, and all pregnancies
involved a single fetus. Regarding the type of AEP, 50% presented with an eczematous eruption 
(
type E
)
 and 50%
with a papular eruption 
(
type P
)
. Only three patients reported a personal history of atopy. In two cases, a skin
biopsy was performed for histopathological diagnosis. All patients were treated with topical corticosteroids,
combined with oral antihistamines in most cases. In six patients 
(
20%
)
, oral corticosteroids were necessary to
control the symptoms.
Conclusion:
In our population, AEP presented with most of the characteristics described in the literature, although with some
differences. The average gestational age at diagnosis was slightly later, although the majority of cases still
occurred during the first trimester. Half of the patients presented with type P AEP, whereas the literature usually
describes this presentation in only a third of cases.
We emphasize the importance of recognizing the different types of specific dermatoses of pregnancy to achieve

early diagnosis and assess their potential maternal or fetal risks.
