Abstract N°: 3368
German AD-Best TREAT Registry
Luka Lomb
1
, Charlotte Willers
1
, Christian Termeer
2
, Dirk Maaßen
3
, Ralph Von Kiedrowski
4
, Matthias Augustin
1
1
Institut für Versorgungsforschung 
(
IVDP
)
, Hamburg, Germany
2
Dermatological Practice Prof. Dr. C. Termeer, Stuttgart, Germany
3
Psoriasis Practice Network South-West, Maxdorf, Germany
4
Praxis Dr. von Kiedrowski, Selters, Germany
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disease characterized by severe pruritus and skin
inflammation, leading to significant psychosocial burden and a substantial reduction in quality of life. The
development of new treatment options has improved the management of AD, however, these therapies require
real-world evaluation to assess their benefits, risks, and specific treatment profiles.
Materials & Methods:
ADBest-TREAT is a digital, web-based, large-scale, non-interventional, observational registry designed to collect
real-world data on systemic AD therapies from dermatological practices in Germany. Developed in cooperation
with dermatology, biostatistics, and IT experts, as well as patients, ADBest-TREAT aims to enroll 5000 AD patients
over five years, with a possible extension to ten years. The registry collects data on the effectiveness, patient-
reported outcomes, treatment patterns, and safety of systemic AD therapies, including biologics and Janus kinase
inhibitors 
(
JAKi
)
. Additionally, it supports predictive model development and guideline optimization. The data
presented were collected from enrollment visits between January 2023 and March 2025 at the IVDP of the
University Medical Center Hamburg-Eppendorf as well as the dermatology practices 
“
Dr. Dirk Maaßen
”
 in Maxdorf
and 
“
am Löwenmarkt
”
 in Stuttgart.
Results:
Between 2023 and 2025, 
175
 patients were enrolled in ADBest-TREAT, 53,14
%
 
(
n=93
)
 female and 46,86% 
(
n=82
)
male. 
86,86%
 
(
n=152
)
 of patients received systemic treatment. The most commonly prescribed systemic therapy
was 
Dupixent
 
(
n=77
)
, followed by 
Lebrikizumab
 
(
n=26
)
, 
Tralokinumab
 
(
n=22
)
, 
Abrocitinib
 
(
n=13
)
,
Upadacitinib 
(
n=10
)
, 
Baricitinib
 
(
n=3
)
, and others 
(
n=2
)
. In total, 
66,86%
 
(
n=117
)
 of patients received topical
treatment, with 
45,71%
 
(
n=80
)
 treated with tacrolimus, 
38,86%
 
(
n=68
)
 with pimecrolimus, and 
12,57%
 
(
n=22
)
with topical steroids.
Conclusion:
ADBest-TREAT provides an efficient platform for large-scale data collection, offering valuable insights into the
real-world management of AD in Germany. With the anticipated inclusion of additional centers in the coming
years, its capacity to generate comprehensive real-world evidence is expected to further expand.
