9
Henry Ford Health System, Detroit, MI, United States
Introduction & Objectives:
 Ruxolitinib cream demonstrated efficacy and was well tolerated in children aged 2
–
11
years with atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 in the phase 3 TRuE-AD3 study 
(
NCT04921969
)
. Here, we evaluated long-term
disease control and time off treatment among patients initially randomized to ruxolitinib cream who applied 
≥
1
dose in the long-term safety 
(
LTS
)
 period of TRuE-AD3 
(
0.75%/1.5% ruxolitinib cream, n=119/114
)
.
Materials & Methods:
 In TRuE-AD3, children with mild to moderate AD 
(
Investigator
’
s Global Assessment [IGA]
score of 2/3 and 3%
–
20% affected body surface area [BSA]
)
 were randomized 
(
2:2:1
)
 to twice-daily ruxolitinib
cream 
(
0.75% or 1.5%
)
 or vehicle for 8 weeks. At Week 8, patients remained on ruxolitinib cream, and patients in
the vehicle group were rerandomized to either strength of ruxolitinib cream for 44 weeks of as-needed treatment
(
LTS period
)
.
Results:
 Disease control was evident with mean affected BSA reduced from baseline 
(
0.75%/1.5% ruxolitinib
cream, 9.9%/11.1%
)
 to Week 52 
(
2.0%/1.9%
)
. Clear 
(
IGA 0
)
 or almost clear 
(
IGA 1
)
 skin was achieved at least once
in the LTS period by 80.7% and 91.2% of patients who applied 0.75% and 1.5% ruxolitinib cream, respectively.
Patients in the 0.75% and 1.5% ruxolitinib cream groups achieved IGA 0/1 for a mean of 60.0% and 69.0% of all
visits in the LTS period, respectively, among those with 
≥
2 visits from Weeks 12
–
52 
(
n=101 and n=106
)
. The
median 
(
range
)
 number of days with no treatment due to lesion clearance in the LTS period was 136.0 
(
8
–
296
)
and 151.0 
(
2
–
296
)
 days in the 0.75% and 1.5% ruxolitinib cream groups, respectively; patients spent 50.4% and
50.2% of the LTS period 
(
median cumulative percentage
)
 off treatment due to lesion clearance. Ruxolitinib cream
was well tolerated, with no serious treatment-related adverse events over 52 weeks.
Conclusion:
 Children aged 2
–
11 years with mild to moderate AD achieved long-term disease control with as-
needed ruxolitinib cream monotherapy. Clear or almost clear skin was maintained for the majority of the as-
needed treatment period, with substantial time off treatment due to lesion clearance.
