Abstract N°: 3512
successful treatment 
‘
dupilumab induced exacerbation of atopic dermatitis
’
 with upadacitinib
Ozan Güner
1
, Nilgun Senturk
1
1
Ondokuz Mayıs University-Faculty of Medicine, Samsun, Türkiye
Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a common chronic inflammatory disease that is
characterized by severe itching and recurrent eczematous lesions. Many immunological components, including IL-
4, IL-5, IL-13, IL-31, IL-33, TSLP, contribute to its complicated pathogenesis. Dupilumab is a monoclonal antibody
that inhibits interleukin-4 
(
IL-4
)
 and IL-13 signaling. Although it
’
s effectiveness has been shown in AD, several
adverse effects reported after dupilumab therapy; including local injection site reactions, conjunctivitis, headache,
nasopharyngitis, face and neck rash, psoriatic dermatitis, lichenoid eruption and exacerbation of AD. Upadacitinib
is an oral Janus kinase 
(
JAK
)
 inhibitor, modulates different combinations of kinases than dupilumab, addressing a
wider range of proinflammatory mediators associated with AD.
Materials & Methods:
 Here we present a 85-year-old male patient who has diagnosed as atopic dermatitis with
diffuse itchy rash on his body and by histopathological examination. One month after initiation of dupilumab
therapy exacerbation of the rash and itch were observed. Thereupon, dupilumab therapy was discontinued and
the patient was started on narrowband UVB, topical steroids and mouisturizers. Histopathological examination
revealed spongiosis, compact hyperkeratosis, widespread parakeratosis, silent psoriasiform hyperplasia. According
to histopathological examination exacerbation of the dermatitis was considered. Since he was 85 years old,
upadacitinib 15 mg/day has been started and the patient
’
s complaints significantly improved after one month.
Results:
 While biologics are largely effective and safe, some patients develop paradoxical reactions, defined as the
development of inflammatory manifestations in patients treated with biologics for immune-mediated
inflammatory diseases. Exacerbation of facial, head and neck dermatitis after dupilumab has been described in the
literature. The cause of eczematous dermatitis after initiation of dupilumab might be an immune shift from Th2- to
Th1- and/or Th17-mediated auto-inflammatory processes. By selectively inhibiting JAK1, upadacitinib abrogates
the signaling of a wider range of proinflammatory mediators, including IL-4, IL-13, IL-22, IL-31, IFN-
γ
, and TSLP.
Therefore, upadacitinib has emerged as an effective option when eczematous lesions appear following dupilumab.
The simultaneous inhibition of multiple pathways may contribute to the efficacy of upadacitinib. Since the patient
was 85 years old lower dose of upadacitinib was started. Although no side effects were observed during the first
month, long-term monitoring of JAK inhibitors is necessary due to possible side effects in elderly patients.
Conclusion:
 In conclusion, the exacerbation of AD following dupilumab were effectively managed with
upadacitinib, highlighting the use of JAK inhibitors as a possible treatment of paradoxical reactions after
dupilumab for atopic dermatitis.
