, Guan Jiang
2
, Sanquan Zhang
3
, Jun Bao
4
, Yuanmin He
5
, Yong Zhang
6
, Shereen Hammad
7
, Qichen
Fan
7
1
Peking Union Medical College Hospital, Beijing, China
2
The Affliliated Hospital of Xuzhou Medical University, Xuzhou, China
3
Guangzhou Dermatology Hospital, Guangzhou, China
4
Nanjing Drum Tower Hospital, Nanjing, China
5
The Affiliated Hospital of Southwest Medical University, Luzhou, China
6
Wuhan Tongji Hospital, Tongji Medical College of Huazhong University of Science and Technology, Wuhan, China
7
AbbVie China, Shanghai, China
Introduction & Objectives:
Upadacitinib 
(
UPA
)
 is an oral selective JAK inhibitor, approved for the treatment of patients with moderate to
severe atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
. Real-world data on safety and efficacy of UPA are limited, especially in China. The
minimal disease activity 
(
MDA
)
 concept from the AHEAD consensus recommendations is gradually adopted; MDA
combines treat-to-target principles with patient / clinician shared decision-making principles. P24-965 study aims
to characterize the real-world utilization patterns and effectiveness of UPA via achievement of MDA in adults and
adolescents AD patients in clinical practice in China.
Materials & Methods:
P24-965 is an ongoing, observational, prospective study to assess the use of UPA in clinical practice among 1000
patients in 40 AD centers in China for 1 years. Adults and adolescents initially treated with UPA for AD are being
enrolled. Primary outcomes included UPA utilization patterns, and important secondary outcomes included
Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
, Peak Pruritus Numeric Rating Scale 
(
PP-NRS
)
, while moderate and optimal
targets per AHEAD consensus recommendations were regarded as exploratory outcomes. This interim analysis
(
data cutoff 14th Feb, 2025
)
 reports baseline data and 3-month UPA utilization patterns and effectiveness data
among patients who were enrolled at least 3 months by the data cutoff date or discontinued from the study.
Results:
304 patients were included in this interim analysis, of whom 61 
(
20.1%
)
 were adolescent; the mean EASI was
20±12; the mean PP-NRS was 6±2. 5.6% patients had prior conventional systemic treatments, 1.0% had
dupilumab and 0.3% had systemic JAK inhibitors other than UPA 
(
Table 1
)
.
The most common reasons for starting on UPA were 88.8% inadequate response and 0.7% intolerability to prior
treatment; 297 
(
97.7%
)
 patients started on UPA 15 mg. 282 
(
92.8%
)
 remained on UPA at month 3. 3 
(
1.0%
)
patients received dose escalation of UPA within 3 months all for inadequate disease control, and 21 patients
received dose tapering within which due to remission or disease stabilization 
(
95.2%
)
, and adverse events 
(
4.8%
)
 .
In month 3, EASI 75 or EASI <=7 was achieved in 84.7% patients, and EASI 90 or EASI <=3 in 39.8% patients. PP-
NRS improvement >=4 was in 72.4% patients with baseline PP-NRS >=4, and PP-NRS 0/1 was 54.3% patients
(
Figure 1
)
. As for selection of treatment outcomes for assessment, 78.3% dermatologists chose EASI as clinical

report outcome 
(
ClinRO
)
, and 54.3% chose SCORD and 43.1% chose IGA with BSA. 90.8% chose PP-NRS as patient
report outcome 
(
PRO
)
 via share decision making with patients, and 74.0% chose POEM, 43.4% chose sleep NRS,
31.3% chose Hospital Anxiety and Depression Scale 
(
HADS
)
, 25.7% chose pain NRS and 43.4% chose DLQI 
(
cDLQI
for 12 to 17-year-old patients
)
. 54.3% patients achieved moderate target in month 3, and 34.1 achieved optimal
target.
The AE with reasonable possibility of being drug related, AE leading to discontinuation of study drug and SAE
were low in this analysis 
(
Table 2
)
. Death was not observed.
Conclusion:
The results of this analysis indicate that UPA treatment can feasibly achieve moderate and high treatment clinical
and patients targets in real-world settings in China AD patients, where most AD patients remained adhered to
UPA after 3 months of initiation. Safety profile is consistent with the known safety profile of UPA with no new
signals identified.


