Abstract N°: 3625
Blood Biomarkers as Predictors of Treatment Response to Biological Agents in Prurigo Nodularis: A Single-
Center Retrospective Analysis
Chisa Nakashima
1
, Atsushi Otsuka
1
1
Kindai University Faculty of Medicine, Department of Dermatology, Osaka, Japan
Introduction & Objectives:
Prurigo nodularis 
(
PN
)
 is a chronic, intensely pruritic skin condition that significantly impairs quality of life.
Biological agents, including dupilumab and nemolizumab, have shown efficacy in treating PN, but predictive
biomarkers for treatment response remain poorly characterized. This study aimed to identify blood biomarkers
that might predict treatment response to these biologics.
Materials & Methods:
We retrospectively analyzed 28 patients with PN who visited our dermatology department between April 1, 2024,
and September 30, 2024, and received either dupilumab 
(
n=13
)
 or nemolizumab 
(
n=19
)
. Visual Analog Scale
(
VAS
)
 for pruritus and Investigator
’
s Global Assessment for Prurigo Nodularis 
(
IGA-PN
)
 were evaluated before
treatment and at 4, 12, and 24 weeks after treatment initiation. Treatment response was defined as 
≥
50%
reduction in VAS at 12 weeks. Blood parameters including IgE, TARC, and eosinophil percentage were analyzed
using various cutoff values to determine optimal thresholds for predicting treatment response.
Results:
In the dupilumab group, patients with IgE 
≥
500 IU/mL showed higher response rates 
(
66.7%
)
 compared to those
with IgE <500 IU/mL 
(
16.7%
)
. Patients with TARC 
≥
1000 pg/mL demonstrated a 100% response rate to
dupilumab, while those with TARC <1000 pg/mL had only a 12.5% response rate. For nemolizumab, patients with
IgE 
≥
200 IU/mL showed an 85.7% response rate compared to 44.4% in those with IgE <200 IU/mL. Similarly,
patients with eosinophils 
≥
5% showed a substantially higher response rate to nemolizumab 
(
88.9%
)
 compared to
those with eosinophils <5% 
(
37.5%
)
. Combined biomarker analysis revealed that patients with both elevated IgE
and TARC levels appeared to show optimal response to both biologics. The presence of comorbid atopic
dermatitis did not seem to significantly affect nemolizumab efficacy, whereas dupilumab appeared potentially
more effective in patients without atopic dermatitis.
Conclusion:
Our preliminary findings suggest that blood biomarkers may help predict treatment response to biological agents
in patients with PN. IgE 
≥
500 IU/mL and TARC 
≥
1000 pg/mL might be considered as predictive biomarkers for
dupilumab response, while IgE 
≥
200 IU/mL and eosinophils 
≥
5% may predict nemolizumab response. Patients
with both elevated IgE and TARC levels might benefit from either biologic. These potential biomarker-based
selection criteria could contribute to more personalized treatment approaches for PN. However, the small sample
size limits definitive conclusions, and larger prospective studies are needed to validate these findings.
