Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic, burdensome skin disease affecting various body areas, associated with
negative impact on patients
’
 quality of life and sleep. Tralokinumab is a monoclonal antibody targeting
interleukin-13, indicated for the treatment of moderate-to-severe AD and was effective and well-tolerated in
Phase 3 clinical trials. This study assesses the real-world effectiveness and safety of 12-months tralokinumab
treatment in patients with AD.
Materials & Methods:
TRACE is a prospective, non-interventional, international, multi-center, single-cohort study of adults with AD, who
were prescribed tralokinumab according to national approved label at treating physician
’
s discretion; concomitant
medications were allowed. Of the 835 patients enrolled 
(
Nov-2021 to Jun-2023
)
, 824 comprised the full analysis
set and 825 the safety analysis set. Assessments included AD localization and overall AD disease severity scores,
i.e., investigator global assessment 
(
IGA
)
 and/or eczema area and severity index 
(
EASI
)
, as per individual clinical
practice, as well as adverse events 
(
AE
)
 reporting. Data are presented as observed.
Results:
At baseline, patients were 47.6% female, 52.4% male and 75.7% White, with a mean age of 44.1 years and mean
AD duration of 18.8 years. Treatment with tralokinumab reduced AD severity across all endpoints 
(
Table 1
)
. Of
those with data, the proportion of patients with clear or almost clear skin 
(
IGA 0/1
)
 increased from 2% at baseline
(
n=780
)
 to 39% 
(
n=632
)
 and 66% 
(
n=400
)
 at months 3 and 12. Among patients with baseline IGA 
≥
2, 46%
(
n=608
)
 at month 3 and 71% 
(
n=383
)
 at month 12 had a 
≥
2-point IGA improvement. The proportion of patients
with severe disease 
(
IGA 4
)
 decreased markedly from 34.1% at baseline 
(
281/823
)
 to 4.0% 
(
27/674
)
 and 1.9%
(
8/420
)
 at months 3 and 12. Mean EASI scores improved from 20.1 
(
n=640
)
 at baseline to 6.6 
(
n=517
)
 and 2.9
(
n=355
)
 at months 3 and 12. The proportion of patients with EASI 
≤
7 
(
no or mild eczema
)
 increased dramatically
from 14% 
(
n=640
)
 at baseline to 71% 
(
n=517
)
 and 89% 
(
n=355
)
 at months 3 and 12. Treatment with
tralokinumab was generally well-tolerated and no new safety signals were identified. AEs were mostly non-serious
and of mild or moderate severity. The well-known adverse drug reactions 
‘
conjunctivitis
’
 and 
‘
injection site
reaction
’
 were the most frequent AEs 
(
all non-serious
)
 and reported in <5% of patients 
(
Table 2
)
.

Conclusion:
12-months tralokinumab treatment in adults with AD in a real-world setting decreased AD severity across all
endpoints with no new safety signals identified.
