Abstract N°: 3709
Biomarkers associated with therapeutic response and adverse reactions in atopic dermatitis patients: A
systematic review
Eva Hilger
1
, Kaitlyn Chan
1
, Alphonsus Yip*
1
, Xingyue Maria Wang
1
, Conor Broderick
1
, Karin van Bart
2
, Bernd
Arents
3
, Kilian Eyerich
4, 5
, Stephan Weidinger
6
, Joseph Rastrick
7
, Carsten Flohr
1
1
St John
’
s Institute of Dermatology, Paediatric & Population Based Dermatology Research Team, London, United
Kingdom
2
Royal College of Physicians, National Guideline Centre, London, United Kingdom
3
Dutch Association for People with Atopic Dermatitis, Nijkerk, Netherlands
4
University of Freiburg, 4Department of Dermatology and Venerology, Medical Center and Faculty for Medicine,
Freiburg, Germany
5
Karolinska Institutet, Division of Dermatology and Venerology, Department of Medicine Solna, Stockholm,
Sweden
6
University Hospital Schleswig, Department of Dermatology and Allergy, Kiel, Germany
7
UCB Pharma, Immunology Research, New Medicines, Slough, United Kingdom
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic, inflammatory cutaneous disease characterised by intense itch, affecting
approximately 20% of children and up to 10% of adults. BIOmarkers in Atopic Dermatitis and Psoriasis 
(
BIOMAP
)
is a large European consortium that aims to improve precision medicine and availability of individualised AD
treatment by identifying key predictive biomarkers for AD treatment response and treatment-related adverse
events 
(
AEs
)
, disease trajectory, and comorbidity development. Our systematic review aims to identify biomarkers
with clinical significance for predicting response to systemic therapies and treatment-related AEs for patients with
moderate-to-severe AD.
Materials & Methods:
A systematic search was conducted across the electronic databases Embase and MEDLINE from database
inception to September 2024. All records were independently screened for inclusion by four authors at both the
title/abstract and full-text screening stages. Articles were included if they were randomised clinical trials, cohort
studies, or case-control studies and included relevant outcome data for investigating associations between
biomarkers and therapeutic response and adverse reactions. Data extraction was performed by the same three
authors. All discrepancies or conflicts at any stage were resolved through adjudication and by a senior reviewer.
Results:
We identified 32 papers addressing the association between biomarkers and treatment response, and 12
evaluating the association between biomarkers and adverse events. Most studies investigated the association
between biomarkers and dupilumab, and studies reporting associations between biomarkers and AEs were limited
to dupilumab-related conjunctivitis and ocular surface disease 
(
OSD
)
. Interestingly, few studies evaluated the
relationship between biomarkers and conventional systemic treatments. Our findings are heterogeneous,
stemming from large variance in included study methodologies, such as follow-up biomarker collection times,
treatments assessed, and the definition of elevated biomarker levels, and therefore meta-analysis was not possible.
The primary findings on treatment response include four studies reporting a significant association between high

baseline total eosinophil count and poorer response to dupilumab, and five studies that identified an association
between high baseline LDH levels and poorer treatment response to dupilumab. The most frequently studied AE
was OSD, in particular conjunctivitis. There was an association between inflammatory markers such as high
baseline IgE, TARC, and TEC, and the development of OSD.
Conclusion
Predicting therapeutic responses and identifying patients at risk of developing AEs will be increasingly important
with an expanding range of treatment options for AD. Finding robust predictive biomarkers for AD, however, is
complicated by its clinical heterogeneity and multifactorial aetiology. Collaborative projects, such as BIOMAP, hold
great potential to advance precision medicine and enhancing individualised care.
