Abstract N°: 3787
Dupilumab Induced Musculoskeletal Symptoms: A Case Series and Description of Clinical Features
Chong Han Peh*
1
, Kah Wai Cheong
2
, Yik Weng Yew
2
1
National University of Singapore, Singapore, Singapore
2
National Skin Centre, Singapore, Singapore
Introduction & Objectives:
Dupilumab is a humanised monoclonal antibody that binds to the interleukin-4 receptor, causing a blockade of
the IL-4 and IL-13 pathways which are critical in the pathophysiology of atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
. As we increase our
use of dupilumab to treat moderate-to-severe AD, we notice a growing number of patients presenting with
musculoskeletal symptoms.
Materials & Methods:
We describe five patients presenting with musculoskeletal symptoms, their treatment outcomes and our proposed
approach in management.
Results:
A 24-year-old Chinese male presents with mild arthralgias of the neck, bilateral fingers, wrists, and ankle joints 2
years after starting dupilumab. The pain is worse on motion, while weight-bearing, and in the morning.
Rheumatology concluded that symptoms were not secondary to inflammatory arthritis. Dupilumab at 300 mg
every 4 weeks relieves symptoms, while more frequent injections induce recurrence. Both his eczema and joint
symptoms were quiescent on a 4-weekly regimen.
A 66-year-old Indian female presents with mild joint pains affecting the left index finger and left thumb 1 month
after initiation of dupilumab. There is no early morning stiffness or swelling.  Reducing dosage to 300 mg every 3
weeks resolved her musculoskeletal symptoms, and she elected to continue dupilumab at this dose.
A 36-year-old Caucasian female presents with right shoulder pain 3 months after starting dupilumab, progressing
to the left shoulder, right hip, and right knee. Orthopaedic surgery diagnosed right supraspinatus full-thickness
tear, left supraspinatus partial tear, and right patellar tendinitis. Rheumatology excluded inflammatory
enthesopathy. A trial of dupilumab discontinuation did not abate her symptoms and caused her eczema to flare.
She opted to continue dupilumab every 2 weeks while continuing orthopaedic treatment.
A 46-year-old Chinese male presents with mild bilateral ankle and elbow stiffness 4 months after starting
dupilumab, worse in the morning and at rest. Inflammatory markers, rheumatoid factor, and ankle radiography
were normal. A trial of dupilumab on a 5-weekly regimen failed to relieve his musculoskeletal symptoms but
remained efficacious for eczema control.
A 67-year-old Chinese male presents with right shoulder arthralgia 5 months after starting dupilumab. There is no
association with motion or early morning stiffness. He is currently trialled on a reduced regimen of 300 mg every 4
weeks.
Conclusion:
In this case series, musculoskeletal symptoms on dupilumab were mild, non-specific 

and do not meet rheumatologic diagnostic criteria for inflammatory arthritis. However, a suggestive timeline,
dose-response relationship, and lack of alternative aetiologies should raise clinical suspicion. The latency after
dupilumab initiation is variable, ranging from 1 month to 2 years. As symptoms are often non-debilitating, we
suggest a trial of dupilumab at a reduced frequency for these patients, which may result in acceptable control of
musculoskeletal symptoms, but at increased risk of flaring eczema. Discontinuation is usually unnecessary. Shared
decision making between clinicians and patients remains paramount in ensuring optimal patient outcomes.
EADV Congress 2025, PARIS
17 SEPTEMBER - 20 SEPTEMBER 2025 
POWERED BY M-ANAGE.COM 


Abstract N°: 3827
Characterization of underdiagnosed atopic dermatitis in colombian school-age children
David Alfredo Castillo Molina
1
, Samanda J. Suárez-García
1
, Jesus Daniel Fierro Lozada
1
, Saida García-Mayorca
1
,
Valentina Gómez-Ortiz
1
1
Funinderma, Dermatology, Bogotá, Colombia
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin condition. It develops mainly in infancy and childhood,
reaching 20% of these ages globally. Misdiagnosis or delayed treatment could negatively affect both the physical
and mental health of the child. The aim of this study is to characterize the behavior of AD in an underserved
school population in Colombia. 
Materials & Methods:
Cross-sectional study conducted between March 2023 and November 2024 in students under 18 years of age with
AD, evaluated in free medical brigades carried out in Colombia. Sociodemographic variables, age at lesion onset,
age at diagnosis, time of lesion duration and disease severity were included using SCORAD, DLQI and POEM. Data
were expressed in absolute and relative frequencies.
Results:
Of 222 patients, 56.8% were female; 47.7% 
(
n=106
)
 were between 6 and 11 years old. Most patients did not know
their socioeconomic level 
(
39.6%
)
, 35.6% were from low strata. 
According to SCORAD, the majority had mild AD 
(
49.5%
)
. Of the patients with severe and moderate disease,
64.7% and 61.5% were female, respectively. Of those with severe AD, 41.2% belonged to low socioeconomic level.
POEM results showed that 31.1% had mild dermatitis; women were the 78.6% and 67.4% in the category of very
severe and severe disease, respectively.
Regarding DLQI, 35.1% showed mild impairment of quality of life. Women had severe and moderate impairment
in 75.0% and 63.3%, respectively.
Most lesions appeared under 6 years of age 
(
42.8%
)
. Regarding the time of lesions onset, 34.2% of the population
had had them for more than 5 years. At the time of assessment, 26.1% of patients had no diagnosis. 
It is worth mentioning that nearly half of the patients had not been evaluated by general physicians 
(
43.2%
)
 or
dermatologists 
(
53.6%
)
 for AD. 
Conclusion:
We found that, in contrast to what has been reported, the prevalence in our population was slightly higher in
women. As in the literature, our sample presented the same behavior with respect to the age of onset of the
lesions and the degree of severity according to the three scales applied. However, women showed a considerably
higher frequency of severe disease with respect to the scales employed. This makes it necessary to consider
greater attention to this population at the time of diagnosis, and to propose studies to explain the causal factors
that help to prevent the progression of the disease and to individualize treatment. 
We identified a non-negligible number of patients who had a delay in diagnosis, highlighting that there is still a

need for improvement strategies for the identification of AD.
Regarding socioeconomic status, our population showed a higher prevalence of lower socioeconomic strata,
different from the findings of other authors. We consider that this occurred because most of the people who
attended the screening sessions belonged to these strata, in addition, they could present barriers in access to
health care and the disease is being underdiagnosed in this population. 
Although AD is a common and often mild condition, it can be difficult to manage. To better address this
condition, parents, caregivers and school teachers should be educated about the identification and natural history
of the disease, as well as general measures that can be taken to control and prevent the intrinsic complications of
AD. 
