Abstract N°: 3847
Frequency of ADHD in school population with atopic dermatitis in Colombia
David Alfredo Castillo Molina
1
, Samanda J. Suárez-García
1
, Jesus Daniel Fierro Lozada
1
, Andrea Carolina Díaz-
Manrique
1
, Lilia Ballesteros-Egurrola
1
, Paula Andrea Martinez-Medina
1
, Gabriella Isabella Vera-Benavides
1
1
Funinderma, Dermatology, Bogotá, Colombia
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disease with a high prevalence in children. The presence of
behavioral disorders such as attention deficit hyperactivity disorder 
(
ADHD
)
 has been described in patients with
AD. In our country there are no published studies in this regard, so this study aims to describe the frequency of
ADHD in school-aged population with AD in Colombia
Materials & Methods:
A cross-sectional study was conducted between March and November 2024 among school-aged children under
18 years from different departments in Colombia 
(
Cundinamarca, Bolívar, Risaralda
)
. Sociodemographic variables,
severity of AD measured by the SCORAD index, and the ADHD Rating Scale-IV were included. Patients were
categorized into three groups according to ADHD Rating Scale-IV: absence of hyperactivity 
(
0-17 points
)
, some
traits of hyperactivity 
(
18-36 points
)
, and marked hyperactivity 
(
37-54 points
)
. The variables were expressed in
absolute and relative frequencies. Data were obtained from medical records from patients who assisted the
screening sessions in the places mentioned above.  
Results:
Of 32 patients, 56.3% were male. Regarding age ranges, 50.0% were patients aged between 6 and 11 years
(
n=16
)
, 40.6% were adolescents 
(
n=13
)
, and 9.4% 
(
n=3
)
 were under 6 years of age.
Regarding the scores of  ADHD Rating Scale-IV, 68.7% 
(
n=22
)
 exhibited absence of hyperactivity, while 31.3%
(
n=10
)
 displayed some traits of hyperactivity. No patient showed marked hyperactivity.
SCORAD was obtained for 19 patients, of whom 7 
(
36.8%
)
 had severe AD, 7 
(
36.8%
)
 had moderate AD, and 5
(
26.3%
)
 had mild AD. Among patients with severe and moderate AD, 5 
(
71.4%
)
 scored for absence of hyperactivity
in each case. Finally, of those with mild AD, 3 
(
60.0%
)
 had an absence of hyperactivity and 2 
(
40.0%
)
 exhibited
some traits of hyperactivity.
Conclusion:
Consistent with previous research, AD was slightly more prevalent in males. However, this study found a higher
frequency of AD in children aged 6 to 11 years, which contrasts with other reports that usually identify the 2 to 5
year age group as the most affected.
Despite the small sample size and the absence of patients with marked hyperactivity, the study suggests that a
significant percentage of individuals present hyperactivity traits. Difficulties in data collection and the study
’
s
preliminary condition affected the number of patients assessed with the SCORAD. The severity of AD in our
sample differed from previous descriptions, with more moderate and severe cases found. No differences were
noted in ADHD symptoms based on AD severity.

The results highlight a comorbidity that affects quality of life and psychosocial development, and emphasizing the
need for the application of effective and context-specific assessment tools.
In conclusion, AD impacts beyond the skin, so an interdisciplinary approach is required to identify and treat
conditions such as ADHD to mitigate its consequences. As this is a preliminary study, it may not have captured
enough ADHD cases, being the first study of its kind in Colombia focused directly on patients with AD.
