Abstract N°: 3856
Cardiovascular disease risk among patients with atopic dermatitis: A case-control study
Raquel Sanabria-de la Torre*
1, 2
, Andrés Oliver-Ramírez
3
, Salvador Arias-Santiago
2, 3
, Trinidad Montero-Vilchez
2, 3
1
University of Granada 
(
UGR
)
, Department of Biochemistry, Molecular Biology III and Immunology, Deparment of
Medicine, Granada, Spain
2
ibs.GRANADA Instituto de Investigación Biosanitaria, Granada, Spain
3
University of Granada 
(
UGR
)
, Deparment of Medicine, Granada, Spain
Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disease with a rising incidence.
Systemic inflammation has been associated with an increased risk of cardiovascular disease 
(
CVD
)
. Other
dermatological conditions, such as psoriasis and hidradenitis suppurativa, have been linked to a higher CVD risk;
however, evidence regarding this association in AD remains controversial. The aim of this study was to assess CVD
risk in adults with AD.
Materials & Methods:
 A cross-sectional study was conducted including adults with AD and healthy controls.
Clinical interviews were used to collect sociodemographic data and lifestyle factors 
(
diet, physical activity
)
.
Anthropometric parameters 
(
BMI, waist circumference
)
 were measured through physical examination.
Hemodynamic parameters 
(
blood pressure 
(
BP
)
, pulse wave velocity 
(
PWV
)
, cardiac output 
(
CU
))
 were assessed
using the IEM Mobil-O-Graph device and I3 software. Analytical parameters 
(
cholesterol, triglycerides, glucose
)
were analyzed following standard hospital protocols.
Results:
 A total of 100 participants were included: 50 AD patients 
(
mean age 38.3 ± 15.3 years, 48% 
(
24/50
)
female
)
 and 50 healthy controls 
(
mean age 38.3 ± 15.2 years, 48% 
(
24/50
)
 female
)
, with no significant differences
in sociodemographic characteristics between groups.
Patients with AD had a higher BMI 
(
27.2 ± 5.8 vs. 24.6 ± 2.9, p=0.006
)
 and a larger waist circumference, especially
in women 
(
88.6 ± 13.9 vs. 79.3 ± 10.7, p=0.01
)
, with a trend in men 
(
96.1 ± 15.6 vs. 89.4 ± 8.8, p=0.06
)
. AD
patients also exhibited poorer lifestyle habits, with lower adherence to the mediterranean diet 
(
7.9 ± 2.0 vs. 10.0 ±
1.4, p<0.001
)
 and higher levels of sedentary behavior 
(
443.2 ± 144.0 vs. 381.6 ± 140.2, p=0.03
)
.
Regarding metabolic and cardiovascular parameters, AD patients showed higher glucose 
(
86.5 ± 18.9 vs. 79.9 ±
8.6, p=0.03
)
 and triglyceride levels 
(
99.1 ± 64.3 vs. 77.7 ± 31.2, p=0.04
)
. They also had higher systolic BP 
(
SBP
)
(
132.3 ± 18.5 vs. 118.6 ± 12.4, p<0.001
)
, diastolic BP 
(
DBP
)
 
(
81.0 ± 12.3 vs. 72.7 ± 10.1, p=0.004
)
, mean arterial
pressure 
(
103.7 ± 14.7 vs. 93.6 ± 9.5, p=0.001
)
, pulse pressure 
(
52.2 ± 14.3 vs. 46.2 ± 12.8, p=0.03
)
, CU 
(
4.8 ± 0.7
vs. 4,52 ± 0.7, p=0.04
)
, central PWV was also higher in AD patients, with a trend approaching statistical
significance 
(
6.7 ± 1.7 vs. 6.1 ± 1.5, p=0.08
)
.
Conclusion:
 Adults with AD might exhibit a higher CVD risk profile, including increased BMI, central adiposity,
poorer dietary habits, and greater sedentary behavior. Additionally, they demonstrated elevated glucose,
triglycerides, and blood pressure. These findings highlight the importance of cardiovascular risk assessment and
lifestyle interventions in AD patients to mitigate potential long-term complications.
