’
s University, Department of Medicine, Kingston, Canada
Introduction & Objectives:
 Upadacitinib 
(
UPA
)
, an oral selective Janus kinase 
(
JAK
)
 inhibitor administered once
daily, is approved for the treatment of moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 in adolescents and adults. While
multiple clinical studies have demonstrated the efficacy and safety of UPA, real-world evidence is needed on the
achievement of optimal treatment targets. Here we assess the effectiveness and the achievement of minimal
disease activity 
(
MDA
)
1 in a real-world population of patients with AD and up to a year of exposure to UPA.
Materials & Methods:
 AD-VISE is an ongoing observational, prospective, multi-country study evaluating the
clinical effectiveness of UPA in adults and adolescents with AD over 2 years. In this interim analysis 
(
data cutoff:
Jan 16, 2025
)
, we report effectiveness for patients who enrolled 
≥
12 months before data cutoff date or had
discontinued the study. Outcome measures included validated Investigator Global Assessment for Atopic
Dermatitis 
(
vIGA-AD
)
 0/1 
(
primary
)
, Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
, Worst Pruritus Numeric Rating Scale
(
WP-NRS
)
, Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
, Patient Oriented Eczema Measurement 
(
POEM
)
, and Atopic
Dermatitis Control Tool 
(
ADCT
)
, per clinically relevant response thresholds. Achievement of minimal disease
activity 
(
MDA
)
1 was defined as the simultaneous achievement of EASI 
≤
3 and WP-NRS 0/1. Results are reported
using non-responder imputation with multiple imputation.
Results:
 A total of 856 patients were included in this analysis with 465 patients initially receiving UPA 15 mg and
391 receiving UPA 30 mg. Most patients were adults 91.2% 
(
n=781
)
 and 43.9%
(
n=376
)
 were female. The most
common countries of residence were Canada 
(
26.2%; n=224
)
, Spain 
(
9.9%; n=85
)
, and Greece 
(
8.2%; n=70
)
. The
most common reason for initiating UPA 15 mg was in attempt to use the lowest possible effective dose 
(
43.1%;
n=160
)
 while patients mostly initiated UPA 30 due to high disease burden-severity of skin 
(
39.1%; n=129
)
. Mean
(
SD
)
 UPA exposure was 439.9 
(
±217.61
)
 days and 13.2% 
(
n=113
)
 discontinued the study. Beginning at 2 months,
most patients 
(
53.0%
)
 achieved vIGA 0/1, increasing to 54.8% at 12 months. Similarly, many patients achieved
EASI 90 
(
48.4%
)
, EASI 
≤
3 
(
58.6%
)
, and WP-NRS 0/1 
(
39.2%
)
 at 2 months, with similar rates at 12 months 54.5%,
64.2%, and 32.4% for EASI 90, EASI 
≤
3, and WP-NRS 0/1, respectively 
(
Figure 1
)
. Importantly, MDA 
(
EASI 
≤
3 +
WP-NRS 0/1
)
 was achieved by 31.1% of patients at 2 months and 28.7% at 12 months 
(
Figure 1
)
. Clinically
meaningful improvements and minimal disease burden thresholds in other patient-reported outcomes measures
(
DLQI, POEM, and ADCT
)
 at 2 months reported similar results or slight declines at 12 months 
(
Table 2
)
.

Conclusion:
 Consistent with findings from AD clinical trials, results of this interim real-world analysis suggest that
most patients with AD achieved clear/almost clear skin and clinically meaningful itch improvement by 2 months of
UPA initiation, with generally similar results after 12 months of treatment. Additionally, about 30% of patients
receiving UPA achieved MDA after up to 12 months of treatment.


