Introduction & Objectives:
 Systemic treatment options for paediatric patients with atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
,
particularly infants, are limited. While topical corticosteroids 
(
TCS
)
 are the primary treatment for these patients,
long-term TCS use is associated with adverse effects. Dupilumab is currently approved for moderate-to-severe AD
in patients aged 
≥
6 months in China, where real-world data in paediatric patients remain scarce. The aim of this
interim analysis of the ADOPED-STAD study was to evaluate patient profiles in infants, children and adolescents
with AD receiving dupilumab, as well as treatment effectiveness and safety outcomes at Week 24 
(
W24
)
.
Materials & Methods:
 The ongoing, prospective, observational ADOPED-STAD study is enrolling patients with AD
aged 
≥
6 months to <18 years who were prescribed dupilumab from 15 clinical sites. This analysis included
baseline 
(
BL
)
 data collected within 30 days before dupilumab initiation 
(
disease severity, atopic comorbidities,
concomitant AD treatment
)
 from patients who were enrolled from 28 Sep 2022 to the data cut-off date 
(
31 Aug
2024
)
. Effectiveness outcomes at W24 included Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
-75, absolute changes in
body surface area 
(
BSA
)
, pruritus- and sleep disturbance-Numerical Rating Scale 
(
p-NRS and sd-NRS
)
 and
Children
’
s Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
CDLQI
)
. Changes in concomitant AD treatments and safety outcomes
were also assessed.
Results:
 At the data cut-off, 580 patients with AD had been screened, 563 were enrolled 
(
75 [13.3%] aged 
≥
6
months to <6 years, 366 [65.0%] aged 
≥
6 to <12 years and 122 [21.7%] aged 
≥
12 to <18 years
)
. At BL, EASI
scores and affected BSA were lower in the 
≥
6 months to <6 years group than in older patients, but p-NRS, sd-NRS
and CDLQI scores were higher 
(
Table 1
)
. The most common atopic comorbidities were food allergy 
(
26.7%
)
 and

allergic rhinitis 
(
25.3%
)
 in patients aged 
≥
6 months to <6 years, and allergic rhinitis in those aged 
≥
6 to <12
years or 
≥
12 to <18 years 
(
52.5% and 73.0%
)
. At W24, the proportion of patients achieving EASI-75 was 75.6%
across all patients. Absolute mean reductions from BL to W24 were observed in BSA 
(−
27.5%
)
, p-NRS 
(−
4.5
)
, sd-
NRS 
(−
3.8
)
 and CDLQI 
(−
7.0
)
 scores, with similar trends across age groups 
(
Table 2
)
. The proportion of patients
receiving TCS and topical antimicrobials was also reduced from BL 
(
69.1% and 31.1%
)
 to W24 
(
43.9% and 21.0%
)
,
with similar reductions observed across all age groups 
(
Table 2
)
. Adverse events 
(
AEs
)
 occurred in 196/563
patients 
(
34.8%
)
; 14 
(
2.5%
)
 had serious AEs, none of which were considered related to dupilumab.
Conclusion:
 In this real-world population of patients aged 
≥
6 months to <18 years with AD receiving dupilumab
in China, younger patients had less severe disease, but poorer patient-reported outcomes. Dupilumab was
effective across all age subgroups at W24 of treatment and had no new safety concerns. Use of TCS and topical
antimicrobials decreased during dupilumab treatment.


