Abstract N°: 4007
A Multicenter, Randomized, Double-Blind, Placebo-Controlled Phase 2b Part 1 Study of a Topical GPCR19
Agonist in Patients with Mild-to-Moderate Atopic Dermatitis
Vlada Groysman
1
, Joseph Raoof
2
, Matthew Mardiney
3
, Neil Sadick
4
, Soojin Jun
5
, Jungjoong Hwang*
5
, Sunwoong
Hong
5
, Pil-Su Ho
5
, Seung-Yong Seong
5
1
Cahaba Dermatology & Skin Health Center, Birmingham, United States
2
Encino Research Center, Los Angeles, United States
3
Continental Clinical Solutions, LLC, Towson, United States
4
Sadick Dermatology, New York, United States
5
Shaperon, Seoul, Korea, Rep. of South
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disorder characterized by eczematous lesions and intense
pruritus. Despite available therapies, there remains an unmet need for safe and effective topical treatments. The
NLRP3 inflammasome has been identified as a key driver of inflammation in AD. Taurodeoxycholate 
(
TDCA
)
, a
selective agonist of G protein
–
coupled receptor 19 
(
GPCR19
)
, has demonstrated anti-inflammatory effects in
preclinical AD models by inhibiting both the priming 
(
cAMP
–
PKA
–
NF-
κ
B
)
 and activation 
(
P2X7R
–
NLRP3
)
 phases
of inflammasome signaling. These effects were abolished in GPCR19-KO mice, underscoring the essential role of
GPCR19 in mediating TDCA
’
s mechanism of action. Based on these findings, we conducted a multicenter,
randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled Phase 2b trial to assess the clinical potential of NuGel, a topical
TDCA formulation, in patients with mild-to-moderate AD. This report presents findings from Part 1, a dose-
escalation cohort 
(
n = 33
)
 designed to evaluate the safety, tolerability, and preliminary efficacy of NuGel and to
inform dose selection for Part 2.
Materials & Methods:
33 adult patients with mild-to-moderate AD were randomized to receive one of four NuGel concentrations 
(
0.5%,
1%, 2%, or 4%
)
 or placebo, applied topically twice daily for 8 weeks. Efficacy was assessed at baseline and Weeks
2, 4, and 8 using the Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
, Investigator
’
s Global Assessment 
(
IGA
)
, pruritus
Numeric Rating Scale 
(
NRS
)
, Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
, and Patient-Oriented Eczema Measure
(
POEM
)
. Safety assessments included treatment-emergent adverse events 
(
TEAEs
)
, adverse drug reactions 
(
ADRs
)
,
serious adverse events 
(
SAEs
)
, serious ADRs 
(
SADRs
)
, and TEAEs leading to treatment discontinuation, study
withdrawal, or death.
Results:
NuGel demonstrated robust clinical responses by Week 8, with EASI score reductions of 
–
78.6%, 
–
73.6%, and 
–
70.4% in the 0.5%, 1%, and 2% groups, respectively, compared with 
–
42.5% in the placebo group and 
–
12.8% in
the 4% group. In line with these results, EASI-50 was achieved in 80%, 100%, and 83.3% of patients in the 0.5%,
1%, and 2% groups, respectively, compared with 44% in the placebo group and 25% in the 4% group. IGA-TS was
achieved by 50% of patients in the 2% group, 33.3% in the 1% group, 20% in the 0.5% group, 11.1% in the
placebo group, and none in the 4% group.
Patient-reported outcomes, including itch NRS, DLQI, and POEM, improved across all groups except 4%. The 1%
and 2% NuGel groups demonstrated the greatest reduction in pruritus and the most notable shifts toward

improved quality of life and milder symptom categories by Week 8.
No major safety concerns were identified during the 8-week treatment period. ADRs occurred in two placebo
patients and in one patient each in the 0.5%, 1%, and 4% groups. No SAEs, SADRs, or treatment discontinuations
were reported.
Conclusion:
In this Phase 2b Part 1 study, NuGel was well tolerated and demonstrated consistent improvements across
multiple clinician- and patient-reported outcomes in adults with mild-to-moderate AD. Notably, dose-dependent
improvements were observed in IGA-TS, a primary endpoint commonly used in Phase 3 trials, supporting the
potential for future clinical success. These findings highlight the clinical potential of NuGel and support the
selection of the 1% and 2% concentrations for further evaluation in the ongoing Phase 2b Part 2 expansion cohort.
