Abstract N°: 4050
Real-World Outcomes of Treat-to-Target 
(
T2T
)
 Strategy in Atopic Dermatitis Management
Zhao Wang*
1
, Luyue Zhang
1
, Songmei Geng
1
1
The Second Affiliated Hospital, Xi
’
an Jiaotong University, Department of Dermatology, Xi
’
an, China
Introduction & Objectives:
The treat-to-target 
(
T2T
)
 approach has been widely implemented in the management of chronic diseases. Atopic
dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is characterized by chronic inflammation of the skin, T2T consensus and shared decision-making
Combining treat-to-target principles has been proposed by international experts. However, there is lack of study
to compare the real-world data with the proposed consensus. By utilizing real-world data, our objective was to
compare the proportion of patients achieving goals according to the consensuses to provide the evident based
insights of the real-world effectiveness of the T2T consensus.
Materials & Methods:
We systematically collected data from atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 patients attending outpatient clinics in our
department. Treatment regimens were determined by dermatologists in accordance with established guidelines
and the treat-to-target 
(
T2T
)
 approach. Baseline assessments and follow-ups at 2, 4, 8, and 16 weeks included the
Patient Global Impression of Severity 5-point scale 
(
PGIS-5
)
, SCORing Atopic Dermatitis 
(
SCORAD
)
, weekly
average of peak daily pruritus numerical rating scale 
(
PP-NRS
)
, Patient-Oriented Eczema Measure 
(
POEM
)
, and
Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
. Treatment targets were categorized as acceptable or optimal. Acceptable
targets included PGIS-5 reduction 
≥
1, EASI 50, SCORAD 50, pp.NRS reduction 
≥
3, DLQI reduction 
≥
4, and POEM
reduction 
≥
4. Optimal targets were defined as PGIS-5 
≤
2, EASI 75, EASI 
≤
7, EASI 75 or 
≤
7, SCORAD 75, SCORAD
≤
24, SCORAD 75 or 
≤
24, pp.NRS 
≤
4, DLQI 
≤
5, and POEM 
≤
7.
Results:
A total of 61 patients were enrolled. Along the time, at 2, 4, 8, and 16 weeks respectively, the proportion of
patients achieving PGIS-5 reduction 
≥
1 was 55.74%, 80.33%, 85.25%, and 93.55%. EASI 50 was reached by 24.59%,
32.79%, 60.66%, and 80.65%, and SCORAD 50 by 26.23%, 37.70%, 62.30%, and 80.65%. pp.NRS reduction 
≥
3 was
observed in 42.62%, 50.82%, 65.57%, and 96.77%, DLQI reduction 
≥
4 in 29.51%, 50.82%, 59.02%, and 77.42%, and
POEM reduction 
≥
4 in 54.10%, 75.41%, 83.61%, and 87.10%. For optimal targets, PGIS-5 
≤
2 was achieved by
52.46%, 86.89%, 91.80%, and 87.10%; EASI 75 by 13.11%, 19.67%, 36.07%, and 70.97%; EASI 
≤
7 by 57.38%,
73.77%, 80.33%, and 93.55%. SCORAD 75 was met by 13.11%, 19.67%, 34.43%, and 70.97%, and SCORAD 
≤
24 by
26.23%, 40.98%, 65.57%, and 74.19%. pp.NRS 
≤
4 was achieved by 57.38%, 67.21%, 81.97%, and 90.32%; DLQI 
≤
5
by 26.23%, 32.79%, 59.02%, and 80.65%; and POEM 
≤
7 by 24.59%, 27.87%, 55.74%, and 87.10%.
Conclusion:
This study elucidates a real-world implementation of the treat-to-target 
(
T2T
)
 approach and lays the groundwork
for refining the T2T framework.
