Abstract N°: 4057
Prediction of dupilumab-associated ocular surface disease in atopic dermatitis: a model for clinical practice
Nienke Veldhuis*
1
, Roselie Achten
1
, Nicolaas Zuithoff
1
, Coco Dekkers
1
, Chantal van Luijk
1
, Joke de Boer
1
, Marlies
De Graaf
1
, Inge Haeck
1
, Marjolein De Bruin-Weller
1
1
University Medical Center Utrecht, Utrecht
Introduction & Objectives:
Dupilumab-associated ocular surface disease 
(
DAOSD
)
 is a frequently reported side effect in dupilumab-treated
atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 patients. As the number of treatment options for moderate-to-severe AD increases, early
identification of patients at high risk for DAOSD could support clinical decision-making. This study aims to
develop a clinical prediction model based on a prospective real-world cohort to assess the risk of DAOSD in
dupilumab-treated patients with moderate-to-severe AD.
Materials & Methods:
This prospective study included moderate-to-severe AD patients between February 2020 and January 2025.
Patients were examined by an ophthalmologist and a dermatologist before and during dupilumab treatment 
(
at
baseline, week 4, and week 28
)
. Additional ophthalmological examinations were performed if indicated. DAOSD
was assessed by using the Utrecht Ophthalmic Inflammatory and Allergic disease 
(
UTOPIA
)
 score, and was defined
as an increase of 
≥
 3 points in UTOPIA score from baseline. Potential predictive factors were selected based on
previous literature, such as baseline AD severity, eyelid eczema, and seasonal allergic conjunctivitis. Logistic
regression with Firth
’
s correction was used to identify predictive factors for the development of DAOSD. C-
statistics were used for the initial evaluation of the prediction model.
Results:
A total of 150 patients 
(
median age 40.1 years [IQR 27.3-52.9], 52.7% male
)
 with a median baseline Eczema Area
and Severity Index of 13.9 
(
IQR 9.8-20.9
)
 were included. Eyelid eczema was reported in 68.0% 
(
102/150
)
 of
patients in the past year. Self-reported seasonal allergic conjunctivitis was present in 75.3% 
(
113/150
)
 of patients,
and a history of non-allergic ocular disease was reported in 9.3% 
(
14/150
)
 of patients. At baseline, 60.0% 
(
90/150
)
of patients reported ocular symptoms. Ophthalmologically confirmed DAOSD was identified in 32.7% 
(
49/150
)
 of
patients. The c-statistic of the initial model was 0.68, indicating moderate discriminative ability. Further results of
the clinical prediction model, including patient characteristics, dermatological characteristics, ophthalmological
history, and ocular symptoms, are currently pending.
Conclusion:
DAOSD was identified in 32.7% of the dupilumab-treated AD patients. The initial prediction model demonstrated a
moderate ability to discriminate between patients who will and will not develop DAOSD. As the model includes
only clinical predictors, it is highly applicable in dermatological practice to identify patients at risk of DAOSD. The
prediction model could support decision-making between different therapies and enable timely referral to an
ophthalmologist. The clinical prediction model is currently being further developed, final results will follow.
