Abstract N°: 4087
Activation of LIGHT/HVEM Signaling in CD4+ T cells Redounds to Aggregation of Atopic Dermatitis
Ying Zou
1
1
Shanghai Skin Disease Hospital, Tongji University School of medicine, Allergic Dermatoses Clinical Center,
Shanghai, China
Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disease with CD4+ T cells.
LIGHT-HVEM pathway signaling has been identified as a costimulatory signal for T cells, yet if the LIGHT signaling
of CD4+ T cells contributes to AD is still unknown. The aim is to identify the correlation and mechanism of LIGHT-
HVEM pathway in AD.
Materials & Methods:
 To elucidate the underlying mechanisms that how the LIGHT signaling contributes to AD.
We analyzed to identify the expression of LIGHT and HVEM in AD lesions by the spatial and single-cell
transcriptomics data and immunofluorescence microscopy. The severity of lesions and subtypes of CD4+ T cells
were analyzed to explore the effects of activating and inhibiting LIGHT/HVEM signaling on AD using exogenous
LIGHT, HVEM antibody and CD4+ T cells-specific HVEM-deficient mice. The levels of T cells to proliferate,
differentiate, and secrete cytokines in vitro were measured by flow cytometry to explore the regulatory effects of
LIGHT/HVEM on CD4+ T cells.
Results:
 We showed that LIGHT/HVEM was markedly expressed in CD4+ T cells in AD patients than healthy
control. LIGHT/HVEM activation aggravated MC903-induced AD-like dermatitis and enhanced Th2-mediated
immune response. CD4+ T cells-specific HVEM-deficient didn
’
t alleviate the overall severity of AD but hinder the
Th2 expansion in AD. LIGHT/HVEM promoted the proliferation, differentiation and cytokine production of CD4+ T
cells but has no effects on directing the polarization of CD4+ T cells in vitro.
Conclusion:
 Our study revealed that LIGHT/HVEM signaling in CD4+ T cells could exacerbate AD by promoting
the proliferation, differentiation and cytokine production of Th2 cells and thus enhancing type 2 immune
response. It may a potential target for the therapy of AD.
