Abstract N°: 4090
Herpes Zoster Occurrence in Atopic Dermatitis Patients Treated with JAK Inhibitors 
–
 A Systematic Review
Aleksandra Fr
ą
tczak
1
, Karolina Perlak
2
, Wiktor Kruczek
2
, Karina Polak
1
, Beata Bergler-Czop
1
1
Chair and Department of Dermatology, Medical University of Silesia, Katowice, Poland, Katowice, Poland
2
Students
’
 Scientific Association at the Department of Dermatology, School of Medicine in Katowice, Medical
University of Silesia, Katowice, Poland, Katowice, Poland
Introduction & Objectives
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disorder that significantly diminishes quality of life. In recent
years, Janus kinase 
(
JAK
)
 inhibitors have emerged as a novel and effective treatment option for moderate-to-
severe AD, offering rapid symptom relief and the convenience of oral administration. Despite their promising
efficacy, concerns have been raised regarding the safety profile of these agents, particularly in relation to viral
reactivation. Among the most commonly reported infectious complications in clinical trials is herpes zoster 
(
HZ
)
,
caused by the reactivation of latent varicella zoster virus 
(
VZV
)
. This review aims to evaluate current evidence on
the association between the use of JAK inhibitors in AD and the risk of herpes zoster development.
Materials & Methods
A systematic search of the PubMed database was conducted to explore the relationship between JAK inhibitors
and herpes zoster in the treatment of atopic dermatitis. The search spanned from the inception of the database
until March 2025, adhering to PRISMA guidelines and utilizing EMTREE and MeSH strategies. Eight key studies
published between 2021 and 2025 were identified and included in the analysis.
Results
The analyzed literature indicates a moderately elevated risk of herpes zoster among AD patients treated with JAK
inhibitors compared to those receiving placebo or other systemic therapies. The incidence of HZ varied based on
factors such as the specific JAK inhibitor used, treatment duration, patient age, and presence of comorbidities.
While most reported cases of HZ were mild to moderate and resolved with antiviral therapy, some instances
necessitated temporary discontinuation of JAK inhibitor therapy. Proposed mechanisms for viral reactivation
include the suppression of interferon signaling pathways and natural killer 
(
NK
)
 cell function, both of which are
critical for maintaining control over latent viral infections.
Conclusion:
JAK inhibitors represent a significant advancement in the treatment of atopic dermatitis. However, clinicians
should remain vigilant regarding the potential for herpes zoster reactivation. Risk mitigation strategies, such as
pre-treatment screening, patient education, and consideration of varicella zoster vaccination
—
particularly for
older adults and immunocompromised individuals
—
may enhance the overall safety of these therapies. Continued
pharmacovigilance and real-world evidence are essential to further clarify the risk profile and optimize clinical
outcomes for patients receiving JAK inhibitors.
