Abstract N°: 4108
A Pilot Study to Establish an Ovalbumin-induced Atopic Dermatitis Chronic Recurrence Mouse Model
Ying Zou
1
1
Shanghai Skin Disease Hospital, Tongji University School of medicine, Allergic Dermatoses Clinical Center,
Shanghai, China
Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a common, chronic and relapsing inflammatory skin
disease. To date, the mechanisms of chronic recurrence in AD are less understood. We aimed to establish an
animal model to demonstrate AD recurrent inflammatory process effectively.
Materials & Methods:
 Here, six-week-old female C57 mice were randomly divided into five groups 
(
control
group, acute group, regression group, relapse group and restimulation group; n = 6
)
 and underwent induction of
AD-like dermatitis by Epicutaneous 
(
EC
)
 sensitized ovalbumin 
(
OVA
)
 after repeated tape-stripping. Gross
phenotype, histopathological manifestations, serum total immunoglobulin E 
(
IgE
)
 level and skin lesion resident
memory T cell 
(
TRM
)
 subsets infiltration were performed and analyzed.
Results:
 The results showed that the OVA-induced AD-like lesions in the relapse group and restimulation group
exhibited highest disease severity, epidermal thickness and serum IgE. Furthermore, we found the predominance
of CD4+TRM cells in the basal layer of the epidermis and the follicular root sheaths of AD lesions, with CD4+TRM
expansion upon OVA re-exposure.
Conclusion:
 Collectively, our data demonstrate that this chronic relapsing AD mouse model renders it very
attractive to study AD pathogenesis and sustained immunologic abnormalities involved in AD development,
maintenance and recurrence. Furthermore, this model might be useful for preclinical studies aiming at unraveling
new therapeutic strategies to treat AD.
