Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is the most common chronic type 2 inflammatory skin disease, with a lifetime prevalence of
up to 20% and significant quality-of-life impact. This subgroup analysis, based on the ongoing ChinaSTAD Registry
Study 
(
NCT05023668
)
, a prospective observational study, focused on describing baseline disease severity and
quality of life, healthcare economic burden and treatment patterns in moderate-to-severe AD patients and the
effectiveness in systemic treatment patients.
Materials & Methods:
Patients aged 
≥
12 years with moderate-to-severe AD 
(
SCORing AD [SCORAD] 
≥
25, Investigator Global
Assessment 
≥
3, or Eczema Area and Severity Index 
≥
24
)
 not controlled by topical therapy were included. In this
analysis, patients who were on systemic therapy at baseline were excluded, defined as systemic corticosteroids,
biologics, immunosuppressants, and JAK inhibitors. Disease severity and patient-reported outcomes were assessed
at baseline using SCORAD, Peak Pruritus Numerical Rating Scale 
(
PP-NRS
)
, Patient-Oriented Eczema Measure
(
POEM
)
, Dermatitis Life Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
 or children
’
s DLQI 
(
cDLQI
)
, and Atopic Dermatitis Control Test
(
ADCT
)
. Patients who initiated systemic therapy at baseline were grouped as systemic group 
(
SG
)
, others as non-
systemic group 
(
NSG
)
. Descriptive statistics are used to summarize baseline values.
Results:
A total of 7,490 patients were enrolled across 84 sites from July 2021 to June 2023, with 6,392 patients without
systemic therapy history included in this analysis 
(
1,930 in SG and 4,442 in NSG
)
. The mean 
(
SD
)
 age was 45.6
(
21.4
)
 years in SG and 41.3 
(
20.1
)
 years in NSG, with males accounting for 58.4% and 56.1%, respectively 
(
Table 1
)
.

At baseline, the mean SCORAD score for assessing AD severity was 55.7 
(
16.3
)
 in the SG and 52.8 
(
16.1
)
 in the
NSG. The PP-NRS scores were 7.3 
(
2.2
)
 and 6.9 
(
2.3
)
, respectively 
(
Table 2
)
. Additionally, 90.9% of patients in the
SG and 88.0% in the NSG had an ADCT score 
≥
7. All the scores gradually declined during the 4-month follow-up
of the SG cohort. For disease-related healthcare economic burden, the average cost per outpatient visits the year
before enrollment 
(
CNY
)
 was numerically higher in SG 987.5 
(
1,906.9
)
 vs NSG 495.3 
(
1,031.5
)
. In the SG, 49.3% of
patients treated with topical corticosteroids, and 43.4% with antihistamines. In the NSG, the most treatments were
topical corticosteroids 
(
62.1%
)
 and antihistamines 
(
62.6%
)
 
(
Table 1
)
. In systemic therapy initiated at baseline, the
most common was dupilumab 
(
69.8%
)
, JAK inhibitor 
(
15.1%
)
 
(
Table 3
)
.
Conclusion:
This analysis of ChinaSTAD demonstrated that the disease burden in both groups was high, with a substantial
proportion of patients still experiencing poor disease control and non-systemic treated patients still mainly used
topical corticosteroids and antihistamines. Among patients on systemic therapy at baseline, dupilumab was the
most commonly used systemic treatment in real-life clinical practice.

