Abstract N°: 4157
Effect of tralokinumab on QoL in atopic patients with head and neck involvement
Luca Mastorino*
1
, Michela Ortoncelli
1
, Giovanni Cavaliere
1
, Niccolo Siliquini
1
, Simone Ribero
1
, Pietro Quaglino
1
1
Italy, medical sciences, Torino
Introduction & Objectives:
AD 
(
atopic dermatitis
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disease that can affect multiple body areas, with H&N 
(
head
and neck
)
 AD having been observed in up to 72% of patients with moderate-to-severe AD in real-world
populations. Due in part to its high visibility, patients with H&N AD greatly value treatments that provide
complete/almost complete skin clearance. H&N AD is incredibly challenging to treat compared with AD
involvement in other body regions, and notably one recent real-world study observed that 68% of patients still
displayed H&N AD after 2 years of treatment with dupilumab. Tralokinumab is a high-affinity monoclonal
antibody that targets explicitly IL-13 and is approved for the treatment of moderate-to-severe AD. So far, data
reporting on the effectiveness of anti-IL on different body site involvement are scant. The different distribution can
impact the perception of the body and affect daily social life. We aim to describe the effectiveness of
tralokinumab in the H&N region.
Materials & Methods:
This is a prospective, non-interventional study on patients receiving tralokinumab. The objective was to evaluate
effectiveness on H&N involvement in patients with AD in a real-world clinical practice setting. Patients were
followed until week 52. Primary and secondary endpoints were assessed at W0, and then W16, 32, and 52. The
primary endpoint was 
H&N EASI 
(
eczema area severity index
)
 reduction from baseline to week 16 
(
reaching
EASI75
)
. Secondary endpoints include Dermatology Life Quality index analysis, total EASI reduction analysis, the
correlation between DLQI values and H&N EASI values, NRSp 
(
numerical rating scale pruritus
)
 trend, and
reduction of at least 4 points of NRSp, change of World Health Organization Quality of Life brief version
questionnaire, Anxiety Brief Symptom Inventory 
–
 18, and Depression Patient Health Questionnaire-9  from
baseline to W16, 32, and 52.
Results:
Of 100 AD patients, 89.9% showed H&N involvement. Total EASI fell from 24.3 at baseline to 5.1 at one year of
treatment, similarly, H&N EASI fell from 2.44 to 1.21. At 16 weeks 20.3% of patients reached EASI 75, H&N EASI75
was reached by 55.1% of patients, and progressive improvement was observed at the following time points. DLQI
dropped from 12 to 6.6 at the same time points, NRSp from 8.3 to 3.8. Two-thirds of patients experienced a
reduction of at least 4 points in NRSp after 52W of treatment. Impact on QoL was higher for H&N patients with
higher DLQI values at every time point, with significant differences in the comparison of the absence of H&N
involvement at 16W 
(
7.1 vs 2.3 p=0.041
)
. Mean H&N values strength correlates with mean DLQI values with a
significant correlation with Pearson correlation
’
s test 
(
p<0.05
)
 at each time point. Progressive reductions were
shown by the BSI-18 test and PhQ9 test with values ranging from 6 to 3, and 6.3 to 0, respectively, at baseline and
one year of treatment. Progressive improvement in two domains of WHOQoL-BREF was observed during the
observation 
(
physical health, and psychological health
)
, with stable results for social relationships and
environment domains.
Conclusion:

Tralokinumab showed effectiveness in the reduction of H&N and Total EASI at 52W, and in the QoL improvement
in the domain of depression and anxiety. Higher proportion of patients achieved H/N EASI75 as compared to
Total EASI75 at W16. The involvement of the Head and Neck correlates with higher QoL impact according to DLQI.
