Introduction & Objectives:
Upadacitinib has been approved for the treatment of moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 in both adult and
adolescent patients. Effectiveness and safety are demonstrated in clinical trials. However, data on the effectiveness
and safety of upadacitinib in adolescent patients with AD in daily practice is limited. Therefore, we prospectively
evaluated the effectiveness and safety of upadacitinib in daily practice in adolescents with moderate-to-severe AD.
Materials & Methods:
Adolescent AD patients from the BioDay registry who were treated with upadacitinib 15mg once daily between
November 2021 and February 2025 were evaluated at the start of upadacitinib treatment 
(
baseline
)
 and after 4, 8,
16, and 28 weeks of treatment. Effectiveness was assessed by using the Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
and the Investigator Global Assessment 
(
IGA
)
. Patient-reported outcome measures 
(
PROMs
)
 included the
Numeric Rating Scale 
(
NRS
)
 of the average weekly pruritus and the presence of sleep disturbance. Adverse events
(
AE
)
s were evaluated at each visit.
Results:
A total of 15 adolescent AD patients were included, of whom 73.3% had previously used dupilumab and 53.3%
conventional immunosuppressive therapy. The mean EASI score significantly decreased from 13.4 
(
95% CI 8.5 
–
18.3
)
 to 7.8 
(
95% CI 2.4 
–
 13.3
)
 after 28 weeks 
(
P=.002
)
. Similarly, the mean NRS pruritus significantly decreased
from 6.5 
(
95% CI 4.9 
–
 8.2
)
 to 4.8 
(
95% CI 3.0 
–
 6.6
)
 after 28 weeks of treatment 
(
P=.003
)
. At week 16, the
probability of achieving EASI 
≤
7 and NRS pruritus 
≤
4 was 75.0% 
(
95% CI 46.8 
–
 91.1
)
 and 50.0% 
(
95% CI 25.4 
–
74.6
)
, respectively. In total, 31 AEs were reported in 10 patients 
(
66.7%
)
, with the most frequent being skin
infections 
(
19.4%
)
, acne 
(
9.7%
)
 and headache 
(
9.7%
)
. Five patients 
(
33.3%
)
 discontinued treatment: 2 
(
13.3%
)
 due
to ineffectiveness, 2 
(
13.3%
)
 due to side effects, and 1 
(
6.7%
)
 due to needle phobia, making laboratory monitoring
impossible.
Conclusion:
Upadacitinib is an effective and relatively safe treatment for moderate-to-severe AD in adolescents. However,
33.3% discontinued treatment due to ineffectiveness, side effects or laboratory monitoring problems. Future
studies are needed to confirm the effectiveness and safety of upadacitinib in adolescents in a daily practice setting.
