Introduction & Objectives:
Tralokinumab, an anti-interleukin-13 monoclonal antibody, has demonstrated efficacy in the treatment of atopic
dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 in both clinical trials and real-world settings. However, limited data exist on the long-term use of
tralokinumab in real-world settings.
A multicentric Italian study, evaluating tralokinumab
’
s drug survival 
(
DS
)
 and efficacy up to 24 months of
treatment among 471 patients with severe AD was conducted.

Materials & Methods:
The study enrolled 471 patients with severe AD receiving tralokinumab across fifteen tertiary centers in Italy.
Clinical scores such as Eczema area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
 and Pruritus Numerical Rating Scale 
(
P-NRS
)
 were
recorded. Drug survival 
(
DS
)
 was analyzed using unadjusted Kaplan-Meier survival curves to estimate the risk and
time to discontinuation, while log-rank test was applied to assessed differences in drug survival across baseline
clinical characteristic.
Results:
The entire study population consisted of 471 patients. Patients naïve to biologics or JAK-inhibitors 
(
Group A
)
accounted for 355, while 116 patients 
(
Group B
)
 had been previously treated with these treatments. At the data
lock, 373 were still undergoing treatment, 15 were lost to follow-up, and 83 discontinued tralokinumab. The
reasons for discontinuation included adverse events 
(
AEs
)
 in 12 patients, inefficacy in 59, and other reasons in 12.
Overall DS rates were 81.5% and 70.5% at 12 and 24 months respectively. When considering discontinuation due
to inefficacy, although not statistically significant, a difference was observed between group A and B. Statistically
significant differences between the two groups were observed at month 
(
T
)
 4 
(
p=0.028
)
, T8 
(
p=0.028
)
, T10
(
p=0.005
)
 and T12 
(
p=0.007
)
, with Group B showing slower clinical improvement. Similar trends were seen for the
endpoint EASI-50/EASI-75/EASI-90/EASI-100 at T4 
(
p=0.019, 
χ
²=0.152
)
 and T10 
(
p=0.017, 
χ
²=10.096
)
.
Conclusion:
Tralokinumab showed a good drug retention rate and a significant clinical improvement, with patients with a
history of previous use of dupilumab or JAK-inhibitors showing a slower improvement.
