HagaZiekenhuis, Dermatology, Den Haag, Netherlands
5
Reinier de Graaf Gasthuis, Dermatology, Delft, Netherlands
6
Maastricht UMC+, Dermatology, Maastricht, Netherlands
7
Spaarne Hospital , Dermatology, Hoofddorp, Netherlands
8
Medical Center Leeuwarden, Dermatology, Leeuwarden, Netherlands
9
Catharina Ziekenhuis, Dermatology, Eindhoven, Netherlands
Introduction & Objectives:
Dupilumab is approved for the treatment of moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 in children aged 
≥
6
months and has been shown to be a safe and effective treatment option in both clinical trials and daily practice
studies. In adult patients, it was previously shown that dupilumab could be successfully tapered while maintaining
disease control. However, there is a lack of data on tapering of dupilumab in the pediatric population. Therefore,
this study aims to evaluate the successful tapering of dupilumab in a pediatric population in daily practice.
Materials & Methods:
This prospective multicenter study included pediatric patients 
(
<18 years
)
, participating in the BioDay Registry,
who were treated with dupilumab between April 2018 and February 2025. Tapering was defined as interval
prolongation of dupilumab standard label dose, according to age and weight, in case of 
≥
52 weeks persistent
controlled AD [Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
 
≤
7 for 
≥
6 months]. The percentage of 
(
successful
)
 tapering
over time and the mean EASI and Numeric Rating Scale 
(
NRS
)
 pruritus over time after initiating tapering were
assessed.
Results:
A total of 316 pediatric patients were included, of whom 183 were eligible for tapering. The mean age was 11.6
(
standard deviation 
(
SD
)
 ±4.4
)
 years and 53.3% were male. A total of 81 patients prolonged their dupilumab
interval [mean EASI 1.6 
(
95% CI 1.2-2.0
)
; NRS pruritus 2.5 
(
95% CI 2.1-2.9
)
; at tapering baseline], of which 91.4%
was successful. Reasons for tapering were controlled disease only 
(
79.0%
)
, controlled disease with administration
problems 
(
12.4%
)
, and controlled disease with side effects 
(
8.6%
)
. More than half of the patients 
(
57.7%
)
 who
initiated tapering remained on a dose of 50-74% of the label dose after 3 years of dupilumab treatment. After
initiating tapering, the EASI score and NRS itch remained low, with the highest estimated mean values being 3.8
(
95% CI 3.2-4.3
)
 and 4.0 
(
95% CI 3.5-4.5
)
, respectively, after 24 months of tapering.
Conclusion:

This study shows that dupilumab could be successfully tapered in 91.4% of pediatric patients with AD who
attempted tapering, while maintaining disease control. The majority of the patients who initiated tapering
remained on a dose of 50-74% of the label dose.
Figure 1.
 Overview of monthly dupilumab dose per visit for the cohort of patients who initiated tapering; 100%,
label dose; 75-99%, 50-74%, 25-49% and 0-24% percentage of label dose. Abbreviations: M, month; Y, year.
