Introduction & Objectives:
OX40 receptor 
(
OX40R
)
 is transiently expressed on activated effector and memory T cells and plays a key role in
atopic dermatitis pathogenesis by driving T-cell imbalance and amplifying the proinflammatory activity of
OX40R+ pathogenic T cells. Rocatinlimab 
(
ROCA; AMG 451/KHK4083
)
, a first-in-class T-cell
–
rebalancing therapy,
inhibits and reduces pathogenic T cells by targeting OX40R. The global phase 3 ROCKET program is evaluating the
efficacy and safety of ROCA in moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 in adults and adolescents. ROCKET-
Horizon 
(
NCT05651711
)
 previously reported ROCA 300 mg Q4W monotherapy significantly improved signs and
symptoms of AD in adults vs PBO by specifically reducing OX40R+ pathogenic T cells. We report the results of
ROCA monotherapy in ROCKET-Ignite 
(
NCT05398445
)
.
Materials & Methods:
760 adults 
≥
18yrs with AD were randomized 3:2:2 to subcutaneous ROCA 300 mg, ROCA 150 mg, or PBO Q4W
(
plus Wk-2 loading dose
)
 for 24 wks. Co-primary endpoints were 
≥
 75% reduction in EASI from baseline 
(
EASI 75
)
and vIGA-AD
™
 0/1 with 
≥
 2-point baseline reduction at Wk 24. Rescue therapy 
(
RT
)
 was permitted from study
Day 1. Efficacy was analyzed using all randomized patients; 
(
Full Analysis Set
)
, with RT users considered
nonresponders in the primary analysis; a prespecified sensitivity analysis was conducted regardless of RT use 
(
All
Data analysis
)
. Missing data were imputed using non-responder imputation for both analyses. Safety analyses
included all patients receiving 
≥
 1 study drug dose.
Results:
Demographics were well balanced among a diverse global population 
(
Table 1
)
. Of the study population, 59%

previously used systemic therapy for AD; 21% had used a biologic or systemic JAK inhibitor. RT use was lower for
ROCA vs PBO through Wk 24 
(
any RT, 28% vs 53%; topical RT, 26% vs 43%; systemic RT, 2% vs 10%
)
. Both ROCA
arms demonstrated statistical significance vs PBO for the co-primary endpoints at Wk 24. EASI 75 response rates
were 42.3% 
(
ROCA 300 mg
)
 and 36.3% 
(
ROCA 150 mg
)
 vs 12.8% 
(
PBO; 
P
 < 0.001, both ROCA arms
)
; vIGA-AD 0/1
response rates were 23.6% 
(
ROCA 300 mg
)
 and 19.1% 
(
ROCA 150 mg
)
 vs 8.7% 
(
PBO; 
P
 < 0.001, ROCA 300 mg vs
PBO; 
P
 = 0.002, ROCA 150 mg vs PBO
)
. The All Data analysis regardless of RT demonstrated greater improvement
for ROCA vs PBO 
(
EASI 75, 54.3% [ROCA 300 mg] and 49.3% [ROCA 150 mg] vs 20.1% [PBO; 
P
 < 0.001, both
ROCA arms]; vIGA-AD 0/1, 27% [ROCA 300 mg] and 22.8% [ROCA 150 mg] vs 11.9% [PBO; 
P
 < 0.001, ROCA
300 mg; 
P
 < 0.01, ROCA 150 mg]
)
. ROCA met all key secondary endpoints 
(
primary and All Data analyses
)
including EASI 90 and 
≥
 4-point reduction in weekly average of daily worst pruritus NRS at Wk 24. Efficacy in both
ROCA arms was continuing to increase at Wk 24. TEAEs were balanced between treatment arms: 69.3%, 70.6%,
and 69.3% for ROCA 300 mg, ROCA 150 mg, and PBO, respectively. SAE rates for ROCA were comparable to PBO
(
ROCA 300 mg, 4.6%; ROCA 150 mg, 2.8%; PBO, 5.5%
)
. Infection rate did not increase for ROCA vs PBO 
(
ROCA
300 mg, 38.3%; ROCA 150 mg, 38.8%; PBO, 40.8%
)
.
Conclusion:
ROCKET-Ignite met its co-primary endpoints in a diverse treatment-experienced AD patient population. ROCA
significantly improved AD clinical signs and symptoms with even greater efficacy observed in the All Data analyses
regardless of RT and no indication of efficacy plateau at Wk 24 and was generally well tolerated. Along with the
previously reported ROCKET-Horizon study, data from ROCKET-Ignite confirm the efficacy of ROCA monotherapy
in adults with AD.
