Abstract N°: 4440
Abrocitinib and quality-of-life in severe atopic dermatitis: beyond clinical outcomes
Andrea Isabel Rodriguez Sanna
1
, Trinidad Montero-Vilchez
1
, Julia Castro Martín
1
, Carmen García Moronta
1
,
Francisco Javier León Pérez
1
, Salvador Arias-Santiago
1
1
Hospital Universitario Virgen de las Nieves, Dermatology and Venereology, Granada, Spain
Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic skin condition that significantly impacts patients
’
quality-of-life 
(
QoL
)
. It can be severe enough to require systemic treatments, including JAK inhibitors. Abrocitinib,
a JAK1-selective inhibitor, has demonstrated significant improvement of AD severity; nevertheless, there is less
information regarding its impact on patients
’
 QoL in a real-world setting.** This study aimed to describe the
clinical and sociodemographic characteristics of patients treated with abrocitinib for the first time and to assess its
effectiveness in improving their QoL.
Materials & Methods:
 A prospective observational study was conducted, including patients with severe AD
starting abrocitinib treatment for the first time. Disease severity was evaluated using the Eczema Area Severity
Index 
(
EASI
)
 and the SCORing Atopic Dermatitis 
(
SCORAD
)
 score. Symptom intensity was measured with the
Worst Pruritus Numerical Rating Scale 
(
WP-NRS
)
 and the Sleep Disturbances Numerical Rating Scale 
(
SD-NRS
)
.
QoL was assessed through five instruments: the Atopic Dermatitis Control Tool 
(
ADCT
)
, Dermatology Life Quality
Index 
(
DLQI
)
, Patient-Oriented Eczema Measure 
(
POEM
)
, Skindex mini, and the World Health Organization Quality
of Life 
(
WHOQOL
)
 questionnaire. All assessments were conducted at baseline and after 16 weeks of treatment
Results:
 Fourteen patients participated, with a mean age of 33.93 years 
(
SD 14.32
)
, being men 71.4% of them. At
baseline, the mean scores for disease severity and symptoms were as follows: EASI 21.90 
(
SD 3.43
)
, SCORAD 51.74
(
SD 12.40
)
, WP-NRS 7.21 
(
SD 2.67
)
, and SD-NRS 4.29 
(
SD 3.65
)
. Baseline quality-of-life scores were as follows:
ADCT 10.70 
(
5.66
)
, DLQI 9.20 
(
5.98
)
, POEM 15.30 
(
7.20
)
, Skindex mini 9.30 
(
5.50
)
, and WHOQOL 57.33 
(
20.62
)
.
After 16 weeks of treatment with abrocitinib, all patients improved disease severity, WP-NRS and SD-NRS.
Moreover, QoL scores significantly improved to the following: ADCT 
(
-4.1, 
p
 = 0.03
)
, DLQI 
(
-4.4, 
p
 = 0.04
)
, POEM 
(
-
6.3, 
p
 = 0.02
)
, and Skindex mini 
(
-3.5, 
p
 = 0.05
)
. WHOQOL also improved 
(
+9.78
)
, but did not reach statistical
significance 
(
p
 = 0.85
)
.
Conclusion:
 Abrocitinib appears to be effective both improving AD severity and patients
’
 QoL.
