Abstract N°: 4478
Facial Discoid Dermatosis: A Rare and Recalcitrant Facial Dermatosis
—
A Case Series
Deniz Akkaya
1
, Ayris Öztürk*
2
, Cuyan Demirkesen
3
, Ayse Serap Karadag
4
1
Liv Hospital, Dermatology and Venereology, Istanbul, Türkiye
2
İ
zmir City Hospital, Dermatology and Venereology, Izmir, Türkiye
3
Acıbadem University School of Medicine, Pathology, Istanbul, Türkiye
4
Private Clinic, Dermatology and Venereology, Istanbul, Türkiye
Facial Discoid Dermatosis: A Rare and Recalcitrant Facial Dermatosis
—
A Case Series
Introduction & Objectives:
Facial discoid dermatosis 
(
FDD
)
 is an uncommon and underrecognized dermatological condition characterized by
persistent, psoriasiform plaques localized to the face. Due to its clinical resemblance to various dermatoses such as
psoriasis, seborrheic dermatitis, and discoid lupus erythematosus, FDD poses significant diagnostic and
therapeutic challenges. Although its nosological position remains debated, some findings suggest a possible
overlap with pityriasis rubra pilaris 
(
PRP
)
. In this case series, we aim to share the clinical and histopathological
features of two patients with chronic, treatment-resistant facial plaques diagnosed as FDD, and to contribute to
the growing recognition of this distinct entity.
Materials & Methods:
Two adult patients, a 45-year-old female and a 38-year-old male, presented with slowly progressive, well-
demarcated, orange-pink discoid plaques on the face, persisting over several years. Detailed clinical evaluations
were performed, including dermoscopic assessment, laboratory workup to exclude infectious, autoimmune, and
granulomatous conditions, and histopathological examination through punch biopsies. Previous treatment
regimens and their outcomes were reviewed.
Results:
In both patients, clinical presentations were limited to the facial region and involved symmetrical, scaly plaques
over the cheeks, forehead, and chin. Both reported minimal pruritus and denied photosensitivity or systemic
symptoms. Dermoscopy revealed mild scaling and dotted vessels. Histopathological examination revealed
psoriasiform epidermal hyperplasia, focal parakeratosis, follicular plugging, and superficial perivascular
lymphocytic infiltrates. Additional findings included mild spongiosis, telangiectatic vessels, mucin deposition, and
in one case, the presence of fibroblastic giant cells. Despite multiple courses of topical corticosteroids, calcineurin
inhibitors, retinoids, antimicrobials, and pulsed-dye laser therapy, therapeutic responses remained limited and
transient. The chronicity, clinicopathologic correlation, and exclusion of other dermatoses supported the diagnosis
of FDD.
Conclusion:
FDD should be considered in patients with chronic, treatment-refractory facial plaques showing histological
features that overlap with psoriasiform dermatoses but lack systemic involvement. Although histopathology is
often non-specific, it remains essential for excluding mimickers such as tinea faciei, cutaneous lupus, or
parapsoriasis. FDD is notably resistant to conventional therapies, though emerging evidence suggests some
benefit from topical retinoids and biologics in selected cases. Increased clinical recognition and further

documentation through case-based observations are needed to refine diagnostic criteria and establish effective
treatment strategies for this persistent and cosmetically distressing condition.
