Abstract N°: 4530
Eczema herpeticum in an atopic dermatitis patient treated with JAK1 inhibitor and UVB phototherapy: a
case report
Remylyn Marynela Gutierrez
1
, Ma. Teresa Dimagiba
1
1
Makati Medical Center, Dermatology, Makati, Philippines
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin condition frequently treated with topical agents,
phototherapy, and systemic immunomodulators. With the increasing use of Janus kinase 
(
JAK
)
 inhibitors,
particularly abrocitinib, understanding their safety profiles is critical. Eczema herpeticum 
(
EH
)
, a disseminated
herpes simplex virus 
(
HSV
)
 infection often due to HSV-1, is a rare but serious complication in AD patients. This
case aims to highlight the potential synergistic risk of developing EH when narrowband UVB 
(
NBUVB
)
phototherapy is combined with systemic JAK inhibition.
Materials & Methods:
A case of a 32-year-old Filipino-Middle Eastern male with chronic AD since childhood is presented. Previously
maintained on topical corticosteroids and moisturizers, the patient developed worsening flares and was started on
NBUVB phototherapy and oral abrocitinib.
Results:
Over the course of ten NBUVB sessions and concurrent abrocitinib at 200 mg every other day, the patient
developed worsening pruritus, hyperpigmented lichenified plaques, and weight loss. A day prior to admission, he
developed vesicles on the axillae and trunk, followed by fever 
(
40.1°C
)
 and chills. He was admitted as a case of EH.
Empiric oral antivirals and antibiotics were given. The patient improved significantly and was discharged after 8
days. Follow-up two weeks post-discharge showed marked resolution of lesions and absence of systemic
symptoms.
Conclusion:
This case underscores the potential additive immunosuppressive effect of combining JAK1 inhibitors and NBUVB
phototherapy in AD patients, which may predispose them to viral complications such as EH. While both modalities
are effective individually, their simultaneous use warrants caution. Clinicians must maintain vigilance for cutaneous
infections and adopt a multidisciplinary approach to manage immunomodulatory treatments in atopic patients.
Further studies are needed to evaluate the safety of combining phototherapy with JAK inhibitors in real-world
settings.
