Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 can be burdensome in older adults due to age-related skin
barrier dysfunction and immunological shifts. This study used an integrated analysis from the Measure Up 1 and
Measure Up 2 Phase 3 clinical trials to assess the efficacy and safety of upadacitinib monotherapy at the approved
dose of 15 mg once daily in patients aged 
≥
65 years with moderate-to-severe AD.
Materials & Methods:
 Trial design for Measure Up 1 
(
NCT03569293
)
 and Measure Up 2 
(
NCT03607422
)
 have
been previously described. This post-hoc integrated analysis included patients aged 
≥
65 years randomized to
double blinded daily oral upadacitinib 15 mg or placebo for 16 weeks, who then completed a blinded long-term
extension period through Week 140 with 15 mg daily upadacitinib. The study assessed the achievement of optimal
treatment targets as outlined by Aiming High in Eczema/Atopic Dermatitis 
(
AHEAD
)
 recommendations. Optimal
targets included clinician-reported outcomes, such as clear or almost clear skin 
(≥
90% Eczema Area and Severity
Index improvement; EASI 90
)
, and patient-reported outcomes, such as little-to-no itch 
(
Worst Pruritus Numeric
Rating Scale of 0 or 1; WP-NRS 0/1
)
 and no impact of AD on quality of life 
(
Dermatology Life Quality Index of 0 or
1; DLQI 0/1
)
. Achievement of Minimal Disease Activity 
(
defined as simultaneous achievement of both EASI 90 &
WP-NRS 0/1; MDA
)
 and clinically meaningful improvement in AD-related sleep disturbance 
(≥
12-point
improvement on Atopic Dermatitis Impact Scale Sleep domain score; 
∆
ADerm-IS Sleep
≥
12
)
 were also assessed.
Response rates were based on non-responder imputation 
(
NRI
)
 during the PCP 
(
through Week 16
)
 and observed
cases 
(
OC
)
 for the extension period 
(
through Week 140
)
. Adverse events of special interest 
(
AESIs
)
 were assessed
through Week 16 and long-term 
(
up to Week 140
)
.
Results:
 At Week 16, upadacitinib-treated patients 
(
n=28
)
 achieved optimal outcomes for skin clearance 
(
50.0%
achieved EASI 90
)
 and itch relief 
(
42.9% achieved WP-NRS 0/1
)
, as well as MDA 
(
32.1% achieved simultaneous
EASI 90 and WP-NRS 0/1
)
 
(
Figure
)
. Additionally, 42.3% reported DLQI 0/1, and 57.1% experienced 
∆
ADerm-IS
Sleep
≥
12. At Week 16, no placebo-assigned patients 
(
n=22
)
 achieved WP-NRS 0/1 or MDA 
(
Figure
)
; 18.2%
achieved EASI 90, 13.6% reported DLQI 0/1, and 11.8% experienced 
∆
ADerm-IS Sleep
≥
12. Most upadacitinib-

treated patients achieved target outcomes at Week 140 
(
n=15
)
 
(
Figure
)
: 80.0% EASI 90, 73.3% WP-NRS 0/1,
60.0% EASI 90 & WP-NRS 0/1, 64.3% DLQI 0/1, and 81.8% 
∆
ADerm-IS Sleep
≥
12. Exposure-adjusted rates for the
following AESIs remained low through Week 140 
(
n=38; 88.3 patient-years [PY]
)
: serious infections 
(
1.1 events per
100 PY [E/100PY]
)
, herpes zoster 
(
4.5E/100PY
)
, malignancy excluding non-melanoma skin cancer 
(
1.1 incidents
per 100 patient-years [n/100PY]
)
, and major adverse cardiovascular events 
(
3.5n/100PY
)
, with no opportunistic
infections, gastrointestinal perforations, or thromboembolic events reported. None of these AESIs were reported
at Week 16.
Conclusion:
 Older adults aged 
≥
65 years treated with oral upadacitinib 15 mg once daily achieved optimal skin
clearance and itch relief, along with meaningful improvements in quality of life and sleep. Most maintained higher
response rates through Week 140. Upadacitinib was well tolerated, with no new important safety concerns
identified, underscoring its utility as a treatment option for moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis in this
population.
