Abstract N°: 4727
Efficacy and safety of stapokibart in adolescents with moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis: a multicenter,
randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled phase 3 trial
Jianzhong Zhang
1
, Furen Zhang
2
, Aihua Wei
3
, Cheng Zhou
1
, Yan Zhao*
1
, Bo Chen
4
, Yanqiu Huang
4
, Wei Liu
4
1
Peking University People
’
s Hospital, Beijing, China
2
Hospital for Skin Diseases, Shandong First Medical University; Provincial Institute of Dermatology and
Venereology,  Shandong Academy of Medical Sciences, Jinan, China
3
Beijing TongRen Hospital, Capital Medical University, Beijing, China
4
Keymed Biosciences 
(
Chengdu
)
 Co., Ltd, Chengdu, China
Efficacy and safety of stapokibart in adolescents with moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis: a multicenter,
randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled phase 3 trial
Introduction & Objectives:
 Adolescents with moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 have significant disease
burdens, with limited treatment options available. Stapokibart, a novel humanized monoclonal antibody targeting
IL-4R
α
, has been approved for the treatment of adults with moderate-to-severe AD in China. A multicenter,
randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled phase 3 trial 
(
NCT06277765
)
 was designed to evaluate the efficacy
and safety of stapokibart in the adolescents with moderate-to-severe AD.
Materials & Methods:
 Eligible patients 
(
aged 
≥
12 to <18 years
)
 were randomly assigned 
(
2:1
)
 to stapokibart 300
mg group 
(
n=120; loading dose, 600 mg
)
 or placebo group 
(
n=60
)
 during an 18-week treatment period. Patients
with baseline weight 
≥
60 kg received subcutaneous stapokibart 
(
n=54
)
 or placebo 
(
n=26
)
 every two weeks, while
those weighting 30 to <60 kg received treatment every three weeks 
(
n=66 and n=34, respectively
)
. Coprimary
endpoints were the proportions of patients achieving 
≥
75% improvement from baseline in Eczema Area and
Severity Index 
(
EASI-75
)
 and an Investigator
’
s Global Assessment 
(
IGA
)
 score of 0/1 with 
≥
2-point reduction from
baseline 
(
IGA response
)
 at week 18. Safety was also evaluated.
Results:
 Between March 21, 2024, and September 25, 2024, of 198 screened patients, 180 were enrolled 
(
mean
[SD] age, 14.5 [1.8] years; 98 [54.4%] female
)
, with 178 
(
98.9%
)
 completing treatment. Patients
’
 demographics and
baseline characteristics were well-balanced between groups. At baseline, the mean EASI scores were 25.2 in the
stapokibart group and 23.4 in the placebo group, and the proportions of patients with IGA score of 3 were 55.8%
and 55.0%, respectively. Stapokibart achieved both coprimary endpoints at week 18. 73.9% of stapokibart-treated
patients achieved EASI-75 versus 43.3% in the placebo group 
(
P<0.0001
)
; IGA response rates were 57.1% and
25.0% 
(
P<0.0001
)
, respectively 
(
Figure 1
)
. Significant improvement was observed for the least-square mean
percent change from baseline in weekly average of daily Peak Pruritus Numerical Rating Scale score at week 18 
(
-
49.5% vs. -25.3%; P<0.0001
)
. Stapokibart also significantly improved patients
’
 quality of life. The incidence of
treatment-emergent adverse events 
(
TEAEs
)
 was comparable between groups 
(
62.5% vs. 61.7%
)
, with most events
being mild to moderate in severity 
(
Table 1
)
. The most common TEAE of infections and infestation was upper
respiratory tract infection. No conjunctivitis occurred throughout the study.
Conclusion:
 Stapokibart demonstrated significant efficacy and a favorable safety profile in adolescents with
moderate-to-severe AD.

