Abstract N°: 4754
Lebrikizumab in Atopic Dermatitis: Real-World Clinical Experience in Our Center
ELIA BOZA VILLAR
1
, Samuel Corral Pavanelo
1
, Pedro José Navarro Guillamón
1
, Rosa María Castillo Muñoz
1
, Jorge
Alonso Suárez Pérez
1
, Enrique Herrera Acosta
1
1
Málaga, DERMATOLOGÍA, Málaga, Spain
Introduction & Objectives:
 ** 
 
 
 
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disease that significantly
impacts sleep and quality of life, especially in moderate to severe cases. In the past year, new therapeutic options
have been approved to improve and control this condition, including lebrikizumab. Lebrikizumab is an IgG4
monoclonal antibody that binds with high affinity to interleukin-13 
(
IL-13
)
 and selectively inhibits its signaling
through the IL-4 receptor alpha 
(
IL-4R
α)
/IL-13 receptor alpha 1 
(
IL-13R
α
1
)
 heterodimer, thus blocking the
downstream effects of IL-13 binding and markedly reducing the inflammation involved in the pathogenesis of the
disease, leading to significant symptomatic improvement.
Materials & Methods:
 
 
 
 
We conducted a prospective observational study including 12 patients with
moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis treated with lebrikizumab in our center. We assessed their clinical
response at weeks 4, 12, and 24 by evaluating EASI, BSA, IGA, and NRS itch scores.
Results:
 ** 
 
 
 
Twelve patients initiated treatment with lebrikizumab for moderate-to-severe AD in our department;
9 
(
75%
)
 were women and 3 
(
25%
)
 were men, with a mean age of 33.08 years 
(
SD 9.27
)
 and an average disease
duration of 7.16 years 
(
SD 6.74
)
. All patients 
(
100%
)
 exhibited the classic phenotype. Facial and cervical
involvement predominated in 9 
(
75%
)
 patients, and 7 
(
58.3%
)
 had hand involvement. Three 
(
25%
)
 were active
smokers, and 9 
(
71.42%
)
 were non-smokers.
Regarding comorbidities, 6 
(
50%
)
 had bronchial asthma, 4 
(
33.33%
)
 allergic rhinitis, 5 
(
41.67%
)
 food allergy, 2
(
16.67%
)
 allergic conjunctivitis, and none had nasal polyposis or eosinophilic esophagitis.
In terms of prior treatments, 50% had received systemic corticosteroids, 100% cyclosporine, 16.67% phototherapy,
and none methotrexate. Additionally, 1 
(
8.33%
)
 had been treated with the biologic dupilumab, 2 
(
16.67%
)
 with
tralokinumab, and 6 
(
50%
)
 with JAK inhibitors: upadacitinib 
(
25%
)
, abrocitinib 
(
16.67%
)
, and baricitinib 
(
8.33%
)
.
Baseline mean scores were: EASI 20.88 
(
SD 6.43
)
, IGA 3.5 
(
SD 0.52
)
, BSA 23.92 
(
SD 13.83
)
, and NRS itch 7.5 
(
SD
2.11
)
. At week 4, data from 8 patients showed mean EASI 9.66 
(
SD 5.41
)
, IGA 2.25 
(
SD 0.89
)
, BSA 16.13 
(
SD 11.49
)
,
and NRS itch 4.75 
(
SD 2.82
)
. Only 1 
(
8.33%
)
 required concomitant oral immunosuppressive therapy. At week 12,
data from 7 patients showed mean EASI 2.36 
(
SD 2.73
)
, IGA 1 
(
SD 0.58
)
, BSA 5.14 
(
SD 3.93
)
, and NRS itch 1.86 
(
SD
2.85
)
. At week 24, data from 3 patients showed mean EASI 0.97 
(
SD 1.67
)
, IGA 0.33 
(
SD 0.58
)
, BSA 5 
(
SD 7.81
)
, and
NRS itch 0.33 
(
SD 0.58
)
.
Only one patient 
(
8.33%
)
 reported an adverse event
—
ocular involvement in the form of conjunctivitis. No patient
required discontinuation of treatment during the study period.
Conclusion:
 
 
 
Lebrikizumab has demonstrated efficacy in the treatment of moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis in patients
who did not respond to other biologics such as dupilumab or JAK inhibitors including baricitinib and upadacitinib.
It was associated with early symptom improvement and reduced need for adjunctive systemic immunosuppressive
therapy.

