Abstract N°: 4770
Epidermal IL18 and IL22RA1 Co-expression Reflects Hyperplasia and Barrier Dysfunction in AD
Robert Byers*
1
, Paul Andrew
1
, Sura Sahib
1, 2, 3
, Oludolapo Katibi
1, 2, 3
, Kirsty Brown
1
, Anna Poyner
1
, John Chittock
1
,
Samuel Williams
1
, Laura Johnson
2
, Fiona Wright
4
, Stephen Matcher
5, 6
, Petra Arlert
7
, Jacob P Thyssen
7
, Ole E
Sørensen
7
, Britta C Martel
7
, Michael J Cork
1, 2, 3
, Simon G Danby
1, 5
1
Sheffield Dermatology Research, The University of Sheffield, Sheffield, United Kingdom
2
The Paediatric Dermatology Clinic, Sheffield Children
’
s Hospital, Sheffield, United Kingdom
3
Department of Dermatology, The Royal Hallamshire Hospital, Sheffield, United Kingdom
4
School of Medicine & Population Health, The University of Sheffield, Sheffield, United Kingdom
5
INSIGNEO, Sheffield, United Kingdom
6
Department of Electronic & Electrical Engineering, The University of Sheffield, Sheffield, United Kingdom
7
LEO Pharma A/S, Ballerup, Denmark
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a complex chronic skin condition characterized by persistent itching, skin barrier
dysfunction, and inflammation dominated by Th2 cells, with contributions from Th1 and Th22 cells. While Th2
targeting therapies have improved patient outcomes, many still experience uncontrolled symptoms or side effects,
highlighting the need for therapies with different modes of action. This study 
(
NCT04295824
)
 explores the role of
interleukin-18 
(
IL-18
)
 and interleukin-22 receptor subunit alpha 1 
(
IL-22RA1
)
, in AD pathophysiology. IL-22RA1,
involved in epidermal regeneration, defense, and inflammation, is expressed only by epithelial cells, while IL-18, a
pro-inflammatory cytokine that can promote Th2 cytokine production, is expressed by both epithelial and
immune cells. IL-22 can induce IL-18 expression in keratinocytes. Investigational therapies directed against IL-18
and IL-22RA1 are in development for the treatment of AD, however, clinical effects relating to co-expression of IL-
18 and IL-22 pathways are poorly understood.
Materials & Methods:
Skin biopsies were collected from lesional and non-lesional cubital fossae in 42 AD patients with varying severity
(
11 unaffected, 39 mild, 14 moderate and 20 severe
)
 and 30 healthy controls. 
In-situ
 hybridization detected 
IL18
and 
IL22RA1
 mRNA, while immunohistochemistry quantified epidermal thickness, cytokeratin-16 
(
CK16
)
, and
filaggrin. Carboxylate functional groups, were quantified with FTIR and trans-epidermal water loss 
(
TEWL
)
 was
measured to assess barrier function. The local clinical severity of each lesion was graded using an adapted EASI-
based scoring system.
Results:
Both 
IL18
 and 
IL22RA1
 mRNA were elevated in severe AD lesions compared to healthy controls. Expression was
co-localized within the epidermis, and mRNA levels were significantly correlated 
(
r=0.72
)
. 
IL18
 and 
IL22RA1
expression was, respectively, positively associated with clinical signs of both acute 
(
erythema: r=0.35 and 0.43;
papulation: r=0.36 and 0.44
)
 and chronic 
(
lichenification: r=0.25 and 0.38; xerosis: r=0.43 and 0.50
)
 AD, with a
consistently higher correlation for 
IL22RA1
. Xerosis showed the strongest association, supporting its potential role
as a clinical proxy for cytokine-driven barrier disruption.
Prominent hyperplasia and barrier disruption were present in lesional skin compared to healthy skin, evidenced by
rising mean epidermal thickness 
(
60.7 to 97.9µm
)
 and TEWL 
(
12.9 to 30.2g/m2/h
)
. For both 
IL18
 and 
IL22RA1

respectively, epidermal thickness 
(
r=0.59, 0.56
)
 and CK16 
(
r=0.40, 0.38
)
 were correlated, suggesting both play a
role in the progression of hyperplasia. In terms of barrier function, TEWL 
(
r=0.46, 0.49
)
 was positively associated,
while filaggrin relative to epidermal area 
(
r=-0.44, -0.34
)
 was inversely related. To further evidence this,
carboxylate groups, found in components of filaggrin breakdown, were depleted 
(
r=-0.41, -0.38
)
.
Conclusion:
IL18
 and 
IL22RA1
 mRNA expression was concertedly linked to clinical severity, epidermal hyperplasia, and barrier
dysfunction in AD, highlighting that both are promising targets in AD. Co-expression of 
IL18
 and 
IL22RA1
 in
keratinocytes suggests that inhibition of IL-22RA1 would likely affect IL-22 induced 
IL18
 expression in
keratinocytes. Results from clinical trials targeting IL-18 and IL-22RA1 will increase the understanding of this co-
expression and whether it translates to clinical effects.
