Abstract N°: 4802
Basophil Modification in Atopic Dermatitis: Differential Effects of Dupilumab and Abrocitinib on
Phenotypic and Functional Responses
Wenjing Jiang*
1
, Xinyang Xie
1
, Juiwen Chang
1
, Wenhui Liu
1
, Rui Tang
2
, Xiru Tang
1
, Ruixin Yu
1
, Wanying Zhai
1
,
Huilin Su
1
, Fang Wang
2
1
The First Affiliated Hospital, Sun Yat-sen University, Dermatology, Guang Zhou Shi, China
2
Dermatolgy Hospital of Southern Medical University, Guang Zhou Shi, China
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic, pruritic inflammatory skin disease that significantly reduces quality of life.
Basophils are increasingly recognized as key contributors to AD pathogenesis, driving itch flares and amplifying
type 2 inflammation. Although dupilumab, an IL-4 receptor 
α
 antagonist, and abrocitinib, a selective JAK1
inhibitor, are approved treatments for AD, their specific effects on basophil phenotype and function remain
unclear. This study investigates how these therapies modulate basophil activity in the context of AD, aiming to
clarify their immunomodulatory roles and potential implications for disease management.
Materials & Methods:
This single-center, prospective observational cohort study enrolled 113 participants
—
24 healthy controls 
(
HCs
)
and 89 patients with atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
. Among the AD group, 52 received systemic therapy 
(
25 with
dupilumab, 27 with abrocitinib
)
. Clinical severity was measured using the Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
and Peak Pruritus Numeric Rating Scale 
(
PP-NRS
)
. Longitudinal blood samples were collected at baseline and
after 3 months of treatment. Flow cytometry was used to analyze basophil surface markers 
(
Fc
ε
RI
α
, MRGPRX2,
CD63, CD203c, CCR3, CXCR4
)
 in both HCs and AD patients. Purified basophils were also stimulated in vitro with
fMLP and anti-IgE to assess functional responses.
Results:
In patients with AD, basophils showed marked alterations in both phenotype and function, which were
differentially modulated by dupilumab and abrocitinib. Compared to HCs, AD patients exhibited increased
basophil infiltration in skin tissue and elevated peripheral blood basophil counts. Flow cytometry revealed
significantly higher expression of Fc
ε
RI
α
, MRGPRX2, and CD63 on circulating basophils in AD patients versus HCs
(
p < 0.01
)
. Upon fMLP or anti-IgE stimulation, purified basophils from AD patients displayed enhanced MRGPRX2
expression relative to HCs. Both dupilumab and abrocitinib achieved significant clinical improvement, with
reductions in EASI and NRS scores. However, they exerted distinct effects on basophil markers. Dupilumab
treatment led to significant downregulation of MRGPRX2 
(
p < 0.01
)
, alongside upregulation of CD63 
(
p < 0.1
)
,
CD203c 
(
p < 0.05
)
, and CCR3 
(
p < 0.05
)
. In contrast, abrocitinib selectively reduced CD203c expression 
(
p < 0.05
)
.
Functionally, anti-IgE
–
induced MRGPRX2 activation remained intact after dupilumab treatment, while fMLP failed
to induce MRGPRX2 upregulation following treatment with either drug.
Conclusion:
Basophils in AD exhibit distinct phenotypic and functional changes that are differentially modulated by dupilumab
and abrocitinib. Basophils may serve not only as biomarkers of disease activity and treatment response but also as
potential targets for tailored therapeutic strategies in AD.

