Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a common chronic inflammatory skin condition linked to sleep disturbances 
(
SD
)
 in 47
–
80% of children and 33
–
90% of adults. While pruritus is widely seen as the main cause of SD, studies show that

42.3% of patients with cutaneous disorders, including AD, report SD, with factors like burning sensations or skin
pain also predictive. No prior research has specifically examined SD in AD patients without pruritus, particularly
sleep-onset insomnia 
(
difficulty falling asleep
)
 and sleep-maintenance insomnia 
(
nighttime awakenings
)
. This
study aims to address this gap by assessing the prevalence and risk factors of SD in adults with AD without
pruritus, using a large international cohort.
Materials & Methods:
As part of the 
“
Scars of Life
”
 initiative, a cross-sectional observational study was conducted from June to
September 2024, involving 30,801 adults from 27 countries across five continents. A representative sample was
selected using the quota method. Participants, aged 18 and older, completed an online questionnaire on
sociodemographic data, AD confirmed by a healthcare professional, absence of pruritus, and skin pain
experiences. AD severity was assessed with the POEM score. SD was evaluated via questions on sleep-onset
insomnia 
(“
>20 min to fall asleep
”)
 and sleep-maintenance insomnia 
(“
difficulty falling back asleep after
waking
”)
. When both types are present, we refer to a mixed situation. The analysis compared 5,267 AD patients
without pruritus to 5,394 controls without skin disease.
Results:
AD patients without pruritus showed a significantly higher SD prevalence 
(
OR = 2.04
)
 than controls, with 55.3%
reporting sleep-onset insomnia 
(
vs 39.2%
)
 and 58.3% sleep-maintenance insomnia 
(
vs 44%
)
. SD patterns varied
with AD severity: isolated insomnia decreased with severity 
(
e.g., 21.2% mild to 15.9% severe for sleep-onset
)
,
while mixed insomnia rose 
(
51.7% mild to 74.5% severe
)
. Overall SD prevalence increased with severity of AD:
63.3% 
(
mild
)
, 83.5% 
(
moderate
)
, 85.2% 
(
severe
)
. Adjusted odds ratios confirmed higher risk with severity: OR =
2.94 
(
moderate
)
 and OR = 3.33 
(
severe
)
. Risk factors included age <40, female gender, and BMI >30, especially for
mixed insomnia.
Conclusion:
These findings reveal that SD persists in AD patients without pruritus and correlates with disease severity,
suggesting mechanisms beyond itching, such as transepidermal water loss, proinflammatory cytokines 
(
IL-6, IL-
31
)
, or psychological stress. As the first study to analyze insomnia subtypes in this context, it highlights the need
for early SD detection and tailored interventions, particularly for young adults and at-risk groups 
(
obese, females
)
.
It calls for further research into cytokines and melatonin
’
s roles in AD-related SD.
