Abstract N°: 4878
emerging role of apremilast in hand dermatoses
Cheena Langer*
1
, Vivek Mahajan
2
1
Aastha Skin and Dermato-Cosmetic Centre, dermatology, Jammu, India
2
Government Medical College and Hospital, pharmacology, Jammu, India
Introduction & Objectives:
Chronic hand dermatoses involves different dermatoses affecting hands like hand eczema and palmoplantar
psoriasis. These conditions are notoriously challenging to treat, particularly when resistant to conventional topical
therapies. The functional and psychosocial burden of hand involvement is disproportionately high, often
necessitating systemic intervention. However, treatment options that are both effective and well-tolerated remain
limited . The objective of study is to highlight the pharmacological profile, mechanism of action, and emerging
clinical role of Apremilast in the management of chronic, treatment-refractory hand dermatoses.
Materials & Methods:
Apremilast, an oral phosphodiesterase-4 
(
PDE4
)
 inhibitor, modulates inflammatory pathways by decreasing the
production of pro-inflammatory cytokines such as TNF-
α
, IL-17, and IL-23. Here , we will be presenting 10
resistant cases of hand dermatoses treated with oral apremilast 
(
dose given according to
recommendations
)
,topical steroids and preventive measures .The patients were followed at 1 and 3 weeks and
also monitor improvement in different scores like hand eczema clinical severity index
(
HECSI
)
 , visual analogue
scale 
(
VAS
)
 score for pruritus & quality of life in hand eczema questionnre
(
QOCHEQ
)
Results:
It offers many advantages over traditional immunosuppressants: oral administration, minimal monitoring
requirements, and a favourable safety profile. Clinical improvement is often noted by 1-4 weeks, with benefits in
both disease severity and quality of life.
Conclusion:
Apremilast represents a valuable systemic option for chronic hand dermatoses, especially in cases where localized
disease is unresponsive to topical therapy and where biologics may not be appropriate. Further clinical trials are
warranted to strengthen its evidence base in this niche which is an impactful area of dermatology.
EADV Congress 2025, PARIS
17 SEPTEMBER - 20 SEPTEMBER 2025 
POWERED BY M-ANAGE.COM 


Abstract N°: 4886
Advanced systemic therapies for atopic dermatitis: real-world comparison of clinical and analytical
outcomes between biologics and JAK inhibitors after 12 months in a reference hospital in Spain.
Alvaro Prados Carmona
1
, Francisco José Navarro Triviño
1
, Maria Martinez Perez
2
, Juan Pablo Velasco-Amador
1
,
Jose Molina Espinosa
1
, Pedro José Ezomo Gervilla
1
, Husein Husein-ElAhmed
3
, Ricardo Ruiz-Villaverde
1
1
Hospital Universitario Clínico San Cecilio, Department of Dermatology, Granada, Spain
2
Hospital Universitario Clínico San Cecilio, Hospital Pharmacy, Granada, Spain
3
Hospital de Baza, Department of Dermatology, Baza, Spain
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic Dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disorder characterized by intense pruritus and eczematous
lesions. Advanced systemic therapies, including biologic drugs and JAK inhibitors 
(
JAKi
)
, have revolutionized AD
management by specifically targeting immune pathways involved in its pathogenesis. However, real-world
comparative analyses of these therapies remain limited. Given the rapid expansion of available treatment options,
their high costs, and the ongoing challenges in clinical practice, robust real-world data are urgently needed to
support clinical decision-making and optimize treatment outcomes. This study aims to evaluate and compare the
clinical efficacy, safety profiles, and analytical impacts
—
including variables related to cardiovascular risk
—
of
biologic therapies versus JAK inhibitors in patients with AD after 12 months of continuous treatment at a tertiary
referral hospital in Spain
Materials & Methods:
We conducted an observational, analytical, ambispective study including patients diagnosed with AD who initiated
advanced systemic treatments between 2019 and 2024 at a tertiary referral hospital in Spain. Patients were divided
into two groups: those treated with biologic therapies 
(
Group 1
)
 and those receiving JAK inhibitors 
(
Group 2
)
.
Baseline demographic, anthropometric, clinical, and laboratory data were collected, along with efficacy and safety
outcomes over a 12-month follow-up period. Descriptive and inferential statistical analyses were performed to
compare the two groups. Measures of central tendency were expressed as mean 
(
± standard deviation
)
 and, when
relevant, minimum and maximum values were also provided. Statistical significance was set at p < 0.05. All
analyses were carried out using SPSS v.24 
(
SPSS Inc., Chicago, IL, USA
)
.
Results:
A total of 202 patients with AD were included and analyzed based on sociodemographic characteristics, body
measurements, disease history, and both clinical and analytical treatment outcomes over 12 months of follow-up.
Among them, 134 
(
66.34%
)
 were treated with biologic agents 
(
dupilumab, tralokinumab, lebrikizumab
)
, while 68
(
33.66%
)
 received JAK inhibitors 
(
upadacitinib, abrocitinib, baricitinib
)
. Statistically significant differences were
observed between groups regarding baseline disease severity
—
greater in the biologics group 
(
higher EASI and
BSA scores, p < 0.01
)
. These differences influenced initial treatment responses, which favored JAK inhibitors at
earlier time points but equalized by week 52. Prior exposure to advanced therapies differed, with a higher
proportion of treatment-naïve patients in the biologics group 
(
p = 0.0001
)
. Distinct safety profiles were observed,
with adverse events consistent with those previously reported in clinical trials. Biologics were associated with a
higher frequency
—
though not statistically significant
—
of disease phenotype shift 
(
p = 0.0818
)
. Analytical
parameters, including lipid profiles and inflammatory markers, were also compared.

Conclusion:
These findings provide valuable real-world insights into the comparative performance of advanced systemic
therapies for AD in a tertiary referral center. Such evidence is essential to complement data from clinical trials and
to support clinicians in making informed decisions when selecting among available treatment options.
