Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic Dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disease, often localized to the head and neck 
(
H&N
)
 area.
These specific body regions can respond differently to topical or systemic therapy. Lebrikizumab 
(
LEB
)
 is a
monoclonal antibody that binds to interleukin 
(
IL
)
-13 with high affinity, blocking its effects. It has demonstrated
clinical efficacy and safety in both adults and adolescents. A 16-week treatment with LEB significantly improved
the Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
 
(
H&N
)
 in patients with moderate-to-severe AD, as shown in three
randomized, placebo-controlled phase 3 trials. Herein, we report a sub-analysis of a retrospective, multicenter
cohort study conducted across 29 Italian tertiary referral centers. 
Materials & Methods:
This cohort includes 78 adult AD patients that underwent LEB 250mg every 2 weeks 
(
LEBQ2W
)
 monotherapy, with
a 500 mg loading dose at baseline and week 2. They have been observed for 16 weeks. This sub-analysis includes
19 patients with AD involving the H&N area and previously treated with dupilumab. Switching to LEB was due to
lost of efficacy 
(
n=9
)
, lack of efficacy 
(
n=3
)
, adverse events 
(
4 due to conjunctivitis
)
 and patient decision 
(
n=3
)
.
Characteristics at baseline: mean age was 33.8 years old, EASI 22.3 and EASI H&N was 4.3. In addition, during the
anamnesis 10 patients reported 1 or more comorbidities: asthma 
(
n=6
)
 and allergic rhinitis 
(
n=7
)
. 
Results:
By week 4, among the 15 patients available for evaluation, the mean total EASI score had decreased to 10.2, and
the H&N EASI score had declined to 1.7, representing a 60.5% improvement in the H&N region. At week 16,
among the 11 patients still under evaluation, the mean total EASI score had further decreased to 2.8, while the
H&N EASI score dropped to 0.8, corresponding to an overall improvement of 81.4% in the affected area. No
adverse events were reported during the treatment period.
Conclusion:
This cohort of patients treated with lebrikizumab contributes to the growing body of evidence supporting its
efficacy and safety in the AD management. The rapid onset of action, combined with sustained clinical
improvement, highlights its therapeutic potential even in challenging cases involving the H&N region and in
patients previously exposed to biologic therapies. Further research is needed to investigate the variability in
clinical responses and to elucidate the underlying mechanisms driving these differences, with the ultimate goal of
developing personalized treatment strategies.
