Abstract N°: 5018
Recalcitrant Atopic Dermatitis: Switch from Tralokinumab to Lebrikizumab
Gabriel Rodríguez Vega
1
, Luis Felipe Godoy Villalón
1
, Andrea San José Rodríguez
1
, Elia Lizeth Di
́
az Torres
1
,
Federica Liuti
1
, Laura Rodríguez-Lago
1
, Zaida Hernández Hernández
1
1
Complejo Hospitalario Universitario Insular Materno - Infantil, Las Palmas de Gran Canaria., Dermatology, Las
Palmas de Gran Canaria., Spain
Introduction:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory disease primarily mediated by the Th2 immune
pathway, in which interleukin 13 
(
IL-13
)
 plays a key role in its pathophysiology. Monoclonal antibodies that
selectively target this cytokine, such as lebrikizumab and tralokinumab, have emerged as targeted therapeutic
options. Although both share the same molecular target, they present different mechanisms of action, which may
influence clinical response.
Case Report:
 We present the case of a 17-year-old female, with no relevant medical history, diagnosed with
atopic dermatitis for over five years. The patient had shown resistance to multiple topical treatments
(
corticosteroids, calcineurin inhibitors, antibiotics
)
 and systemic therapies 
(
cyclosporine
)
.
Due to poor disease control, and with baseline scores of EASI 21, IGA 4, sleep NRS 5, itch NRS 7, and DLQI 9,
standard-dose tralokinumab was initiated in June 2024, along with a gradual tapering of cyclosporine. After five
months, primary therapeutic failure was observed, with less than 25% improvement in EASI. Consequently,
treatment was switched to lebrikizumab in January 2025, administered following SmPC 
(
subcutaneously, starting
with dose of 500 mg at weeks 0 and 2, followed by 250 mg every two weeks until week 16, then 250 mg monthly
)
.
At 6 weeks, a marked clinical improvement was observed, with an EASI of 6,2. Other outcome measures also
improved significantly: IGA 1, sleep NRS 0, itch NRS 3, and DLQI 1.
Discussion:
 Lebrikizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody 
(
IgG4
)
 that binds with high affinity to IL-13 and
selectively inhibits its signaling through the blockage of the heterodimerization of subunits IL-4R
α
 and IL-13R
α
1
of Type 2 receptor. Its binding epitope of IL-13 differs from other anti
–
IL-13 antibodies, allowing IL-13 to retain its
interaction with the decoy receptor 
(
IL-13R
α
2
)
, which is thought to promote cytokine clearance via lysosomal
degradation. Its differential mechanism of action together with its high affinity binding to IL-13 may explain its
efficacy in patients who do not respond to other IL-13 inhibitors.
Recently, Hagino et al. published the first case series involving 13 patients who switched from tralokinumab to
lebrikizumab, observing a favorable clinical response at weeks 4, 12, and 16.
Conclusion:
 We present a case of recalcitrant atopic dermatitis with a rapid and well-tolerated clinical response to
lebrikizumab after switching from tralokinumab. This case supports the use of lebrikizumab as an effective
alternative in selected patients.
EADV Congress 2025, PARIS
17 SEPTEMBER - 20 SEPTEMBER 2025 
POWERED BY M-ANAGE.COM 


Abstract N°: 5054
Genetically proxied inhibition of lipid pathways and risk of atopic dermatitis: a multi-ancestry Mendelian
randomisation study
John Tetteh*
1
, John Bowes
1
, Darren Ashcroft
2
, Zenas Yiu
1, 3
, Sizheng Steven Zhao
1
1
Division of Musculoskeletal and Dermatological Science, School of Biological Sciences, Faculty of Biology,
Medicine and Health, University of Manchester, Manchester, United Kingdom
2
Centre for Pharmacoepidemiology and Drug Safety, School of Health Sciences, Faculty of Biology, Medicine and
Health, University of Manchester, Manchester, United Kingdom
3
Dermatology Department, Salford Royal Hospital, Northern Care Alliance NHS Foundation Trust, Manchester
Academic Health Science Centre, Manchester, United Kingdom
Introduction & Objectives:
Cholesterol-lowering therapies have been associated with the risk of atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 in observational
studies, but their causal role across different ancestries remains unclear. This study aimed to evaluate the impact of
genetically proxied inhibition of lipid-lowering targets; HMG-CoA reductase 
(
HMGCR
)
, Niemann-Pick C1-Like 1
(
NPC1L1
)
, Proprotein convertase subtilisin/kexin type 9 
(
PCSK9
)
, and Cholesteryl ester transfer protein 
(
CETP
)
 on
the risk of AD across European 
(
EUR
)
, East Asian 
(
EAS
)
, and African 
(
AFR
)
 populations.
Materials & Methods:
We applied drug-target Mendelian Randomisation 
(
MR
)
 approach using single nucleotide polymorphisms 
(
SNPs
)
within or near the 
HMGCR
, 
NPC1L1
, 
PCSK9
, and 
CETP
 genes to proxy the therapeutic perturbation of each lipid
lowering drug/class: statins, ezetimibe, PCSK9, and CETP inhibitors respectively. Genetic association data for AD
were obtained from 60,653 cases and 804,329 controls of EUR ancestry, supplemented by data from FinnGen
(
31,245/432,874
)
, EAS 
(
2,385/209,651
)
, and AFR 
(
7,063/15,879
)
 populations. Genetic variants strongly associated
with cholesterol traits were obtained from the Global Lipid Genetics Consortium which includes data from
1,320,016 individuals of EUR, 146,492 of EAS, and 99,432 of admixed AFR ancestry. Fixed-effect inverse-variance
weighted analysis was used to estimate the effect for each drug-target then pooled across ancestries. We applied
colocalisation analysis to test for genetic confounding by linkage disequlibrium 
(
LD
)
 and examined potential
pleiotropy using MR-Egger.
Results:
Genetically proxied HMGCR inhibition was not associated with AD risk across ancestries. NPC1L1 inhibition was
associated with increased AD risk in EUR 
(
OR=1.32; 95%CI=1.02
–
1.70 per SD reduction in total cholesterol
)
, but
reduced AD risk in AFR population 
(
OR=0.18; 95%CI=0.05
–
0.65 per SD LDL reduction
)
. PCSK9 inhibition lowered
AD risk in EUR 
(
OR=0.91; 95%CI=0.85
–
0.97 per SD LDL reduction
)
, while increased risk was observed among EAS
population 
(
OR=1.38; 95%CI=0.78-2.42
)
 though not statistically significant. CETP inhibition was associated with
low risk of AD in EUR and AFR individuals through reduction in LDL 
(
OR=0.62; 95%CI=0.46-0.84
)
 and non-HDL
(
OR=0.84; 95%CI=0.75-0.94
)
, however, total cholesterol reduction via CETP inhibition was associated with a
fourfold increased AD risk in AFR population 
(
OR=4.63; 95%CI=2.31
–
9.30
)
. Colocalisation analyses suggested no
evidence of genetic confounding by LD in the AFR ancestry, however, other analyses were underpowered. There
was no statistical evidence of horizontal pleiotropy.
Conclusion:

Genetic proxies for cholesterol pathway inhibition suggest ancestry-specific association with AD. Genetically
predicted cholesterol reduction via NPC1L1 inhibition was associated with an increased risk of AD in EUR
population, but a decreased risk in AFR population. PCSK9** inhibition was associated with reduced AD risk
among EUR. CETP inhibition, although protective in some contexts, was associated with an increased AD risk in
AFR ancestry group. These findings underscore the need for further investigation through triangulation with
clinical trial data or real-world evidence to better understand ancestry-specific effects and their implications.
