Abstract N°: 5206
Prevalence of Sleep Disorders In Patients with Atopic Dermatitis at Cu Chi Regional General Hospital
Quang Minh Nguyen
1
, Quan Nguyen Thanh Tra
1
, Trinh Ngo Binh
2
1
Cu Chi Regional General Hospital, Ho Chi Minh, Viet Nam
2
University of Medicine and Pharmacy at Ho Chi Minh City, Ho Chi Minh, Viet Nam
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis is a common chronic skin condition characterized by persistent itching and frequent relapses,
significantly affecting patients
’
 quality of life. One often-overlooked issue in this population is sleep disturbance,
which can exacerbate dermatological symptoms and negatively impact mental health. This study aims to
determine the prevalence of sleep disorders 
(
SD
)
 among patients with atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 and to describe
related clinical features at Cu Chi Regional General Hospital in 2024.
Materials & Methods:
A descriptive cross-sectional study was conducted on 100 adult AD patients at the Dermatology Clinic of Cu Chi
Regional General Hospital from May to August 2024. Data were collected via structured interviews and
questionnaires. The SCORAD index was used to assess AD severity, and the Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index 
(
PSQI
)
was employed to evaluate sleep quality.
Results:
The prevalence of sleep disorders among AD patients was 42%, with most patients experiencing moderate to
severe sleep disturbances 
(
Table 1
)
. A significant association was found between PSQI scores and clinical
parameters, including SCORAD severity 
(
Table 2
)
 and itch intensity 
(
5-D itch scale
)
 
(
Table 3
)
. Sleep disturbance
was closely linked to pruritus and nocturnal discomfort.
Conclusion:
Sleep disorders are common in patients with atopic dermatitis and are significantly associated with pruritus
severity and disease extent. Early identification and integrated management of sleep disturbances should be
considered in the holistic care of AD patients.
