Abstract N°: 5343
Burden of Chronic Hand Eczema 
(
CHE
)
 in France Using the French Nationwide Claims Database
Bruno Halioua*
1
, Berchi Celia
2
, Pertus Delphine
3
, Jessica Gonnet
3
, Richard Marie-Aleth
4
1
Dermatologist, Paris, France
2
Normandie Univ, UniCaen, Laboratoire NIMEC, Caen, France
3
LEO Pharma, Paris, France
4
Universitary Hospital Timone, Assistance Publique Hôpitaux de Marseille, APHM, 5CEReSS-EA 3279, Research
Centrer in Health Services and Quality of Life Aix Marseille University, Dermatology Department, Marseille, France
Introduction & Objectives:
CHE is a dermatological disease that significantly impacts patients
’
 quality of life and professional activity. Despite
its high prevalence, data on its economic impact remain limited. The main objective was to assess the economic
impact of CHE using the French Nationwide claims database 
(
SNDS
)
. One of the secondary objectives was to
delve deeper into the patient care pathway, focusing on the use of medications for anxiety, depression, and sleep
disorders. A post-hoc analysis explored the use of topical corticosteroids 
(
TCS
)
.
Materials & Methods:
Observational cohort study of CHE patients identified using the SNDS between 2017 and 2021, which included
two groups: i
)
 Alitretinoin CHE group: patients with at least one alitretinoin dispensing 
(
first dispensing as index
date
)
; ii
)
 Algorithm-based CHE group: patients identified using an algorithm combining sick leave in high-risk
professions 
(
medical sector; butchery, food industry and catering; print shop; hair and beauty care; traditional and
industrial bakeries; construction/building; cleaning activity; maintenance and repair of motor vehicles
)
, and
concomitant dispensing of potent TCS, ciclosporin, topical tacrolimus, antihistamines, oral alitretinoin, or
methotrexate 
(
first sick leave as index date
)
. Each group was matched 
(
1:4
)
 with controls from the general
population on age, sex, and city, after assigning the same calendar index date as the matched case. Cost analysis
from the payer perspective 
(
including consultations, drug dispensation, sick leave, hospitalizations, thermal
therapy, phototherapy sessions
)
 compared the healthcare expenditures between cases and controls using
univariate analysis of variance 
(
ANOVA
)
.
Results:
A total of 23,906 CHE patients 
(
11,150 Alitretinoin CHE and 12,756 Algorithm CHE
)
 were matched with 88,358
controls and followed for mean periods of 3.7 and 3.6 years, respectively.
In each group, 51% were men, with a mean age of 45 years at the index date.
General practitioners were the primary healthcare professionals involved in the patient care pathway, with CHE
patients consulting them on average 7 times per year versus 4 times for the control group. Specialist visits were
rare. A similar pattern was noted in Alitretinoin CHE patients.
During the follow-up period, mean total annual cost was 2 times higher in CHE patients than in controls 
(
3878
€
vs. 1879
€
, p<0.0001
)
. After the exclusion of sick leave, which was one criterion used to define CHE patients in the
algorithm, it remained almost 2 times higher. Individual expenditures were also significantly higher in CHE
patients 
(
p<0.0001
)
, with drug dispensing costs 3 times higher 
(
989
€
 vs. 330
€)
 and consultation costs doubled
(
189
€
 vs. 98
€)
.

CHE patients had an average sick leave duration of 37 days per year, controls 11 days, and 13 days for Alitretinoin
CHE patients specifically. In CHE patients, there was a higher frequency of anxiety and depression medication
dispensing versus controls 
(
48% vs. 33%
)
. This was also observed for sleep disorders medications, though to a
lesser extent 
(
14% vs. 9%
)
. 91% of CHE patients underwent at least 3 treatment cycles with potent or very potent
TCS, each cycle averaging 48 days.
Conclusion:
This study highlights the CHE burden with a significant extra cost and impact on patients
’
 professional activity and
mental health. Moreover, these results demonstrate the need for alternatives to repeated, prolonged use of TCS in
CHE and for the adoption of holistic treatment approaches.
