Abstract N°: 5373
Flex-Up Study: Evidence of Residual Inflammation Persists in Patients Who Do Not Achieve or Maintain a
High Treatment Target in Atopic Dermatitis
Kilian Eyerich*
1
, Christopher Bunick
2
, Shinichi Imafuku
3
, Alvaro Moreira
4
, Nadia Ibrahim
4
, Henrique Teixeira
4
, James
Fann
5
, Yingtao Bi
5
, Namita Vigna
4
, Sara Murdock
5
, Bit na Kang
5
, Julie Parmentier
5
1
Medical Center-University of Freiburg, Department of Dermatology, Freiburg, Germany
2
Yale School of Medicine, Department of Dermatology; Program in Translational Biomedicine, New Haven,
Connecticut, United States
3
Fukuoka University Hospital, Fukuoka, Japan
4
AbbVie, North Chicago, Illinois, United States
5
AbbVie, Worcester, Massachusetts, United States
Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin condition characterized by
eczematous lesions and intense pruritus. Upadacitinib 
(
UPA
)
, a selective oral Janus kinase inhibitor, has shown
efficacy in treating moderate-to-severe AD, with both the 15mg and 30mg doses being approved for use. The
phase 3b/4 Flex Up study is designed to address a critical evidence gap to provide descriptive data on the efficacy
and safety of dose escalation and dose reduction based on patients
’
 achievement of an optimal clinical target. To
gain a mechanistic understanding of how dose escalation and dose reduction can drive achievement and
maintenance of high treatment targets, serum disease biomarkers and circulating absolute eosinophil counts
(
AEC
)
 were assessed.
Materials & Methods:
 Patients were randomized 1:1 to receive UPA 15mg or UPA 30mg daily, during a 12-week
double-blind period, followed by an additional 12 week single-blind period. At Week 12, UPA 15mg patients who
did not achieve an 
≥
90% improvement in Eczema Area and Severity Index score 
(
EASI90
)
 were dose-escalated to
UPA 30mg, while those achieving EASI90 continued on UPA 15mg. Patients in the UPA 30mg group continued on
UPA 30mg if they did not achieve EASI90 or were dose-reduced to UPA 15mg if 
≥
EASI90 was achieved. Disease-
associated inflammatory proteins 
(
CCL17, CCL18, CCL22, CCL26, IL-22, BD2
)
 and circulating AEC were analyzed
using samples collected from baseline through Week 24.
Results:
 Samples from 230 of the 461 patients in the Flex Up study were utilized for biomarker analysis 
(
UPA 15,
N=112; UPA 30, N=118
)
 and plotted against biomarker data from healthy controls 
(
healthy control serum samples
purchased from Discovery Life Sciences
)
. Demographics were consistent between the current biomarker cohort
and the clinical study cohort. At Week 24, patients achieving EASI90 demonstrated a decrease in inflammatory
proteins to healthy control levels, whereas levels remained elevated compared to healthy controls in patients who
did not achieve EASI90. At week 12, patients dose escalated from UPA 15mg to UPA 30mg had higher baseline
inflammatory protein levels compared to those achieving 
≥
EASI90 without escalation. Similarly, patients dose
reduced from UPA 30mg to UPA 15mg that failed to maintain an EASI90 response at Week 24 also had elevated
baseline inflammatory protein levels. Patients achieving 
≥
EASI90 had reductions in AEC approaching healthy
control levels after treatment, while AEC remained elevated in patients who did not achieve EASI90 at Week 24.
There was an association between all baseline inflammatory protein biomarker values and baseline EASI scores,
and between inflammatory protein biomarker values and EASI scores at Week 24.
Conclusion:
 The analysis of disease-associated biomarkers provides a mechanistic rationale for why some patients
with moderate-to-severe AD require UPA 30mg to achieve and sustain high treatment targets such as EASI90.
Patients not achieving EASI90 at Week 24 exhibited signs of persistent inflammation, regardless of their starting

dose or dose adjustments made at Week 12. Patients with higher baseline inflammatory protein levels may
particularly benefit from a UPA 30mg to achieve and sustain an EASI90 response, suggesting that a 30mg dose
may be necessary for optimal efficacy in these individuals. Combined with clinical efficacy results, these data
provide evidence to guide clinical practice in personalization of moderate-to-severe AD management.
