Abstract N°: 5449
Matching-Adjusted Indirect Comparison of the Efficacy at week 12 of Delgocitinib and topical PUVA in the
treatment of Severe Chronic Hand Eczema
April W. Armstrong*
1
, Anthony Bewley
2
, Andreas Wollenberg
3, 4, 5
, H. Chih-ho Hong
6
, Rie von Eyben
7
, Emilie
Jonsen
7
, Douglas Maslin
7, 8
, Bryony Langford
9
, Katie Walker
10
, Raj Chovatiya
11, 12
1
Department of Dermatology, University of California, Los Angeles, Los Angeles, United States
2
Barts Health NHS Trust, London, United Kingdom
3
Department of Dermatology and Allergy, Augsburg University Hospital, Augsburg, Germany
4
Department of Dermatology and Allergy, Ludwig-Maximilian University of Munich, Munich, Germany
5
Comprehensive Center for Inflammation Medicine, University of Lübeck, Lübeck, Germany
6
Department of Dermatology and Skin Science, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, Canada
7
LEO Pharma A/S, Ballerup, Denmark
8
Department of Dermatology Addenbrooke
’
s Hospital, Cambridge, United Kingdom
9
Symmetron Ltd, London, United Kingdom
10
Symmetron, London, United Kingdom
11
Chicago Medical School, Rosalind Franklin University of Medicine and Science, Chicago, United States
12
Center for Medical Dermatology + Immunology Research, Chicago, United States
Introduction & Objectives:
Chronic Hand Eczema 
(
CHE
)
 is a persistent, inflammatory skin disease affecting the hands and wrists,
characterised by key symptoms of itch and pain, together with signs such as erythema, scaling, fissures and
vesiculation. Current therapies, including topical corticosteroids, phototherapy 
(
including psoralen ultraviolet A
[PUVA]
)
, and alitretinoin 
(
approved for severe CHE in Europe, Canada, Israel and South Korea
)
, may be hindered
by side effects and poor patient adherence. Delgocitinib, a topical pan-JAK inhibitor cream approved in the EU,
UK, Switzerland, and UAE for moderate to severe CHE 
(
Anzupgo cream 20mg/g LEO Pharma A/S 2%
)
, offers an
alternative treatment option which has demonstrated significant efficacy in the Phase 3 DELTA FORCE trial versus
oral alitretinoin for the treatment of severe CHE 
(
ClinicalTrial.gov ID NCT05259722
)
.
There are currently no head-to-head trials comparing topical delgocitinib to PUVA. However, recent efficacy data
from the Phase 3 ALPHA trial 
(
ISRCTN ID 80206075
)
, which evaluated PUVA versus alitretinoin in combination with
as required topical corticosteroids 
(
TCS
)
, enabled a matching-adjusted indirect comparison 
(
MAIC
)
 to assess the
efficacy of delgocitinib cream alone versus PUVA ± TCS in treating severe CHE.
Materials & Methods:
An anchored MAIC was conducted to indirectly compare delgocitinib to PUVA ± TCS using individual patient data
(
IPD
)
 from the DELTA FORCE trial and aggregate data from the ALPHA trial, via their common alitretinoin
“
anchor
”
 comparator.
IPD from DELTA FORCE were reweighted to match the following ALPHA trial baseline characteristics: sex, race
(
white: yes/no
)
, mean age and mean HECSI at baseline. For the analysis of DLQI, patients were also matched on
mean DLQI at baseline. After matching, outcomes assessed at week 12 included PGA/IGA-CHE score of 0/1, HECSI
change from baseline 
(
CFB
)
, and DLQI CFB. The primary outcome assumed equivalence between the PGA used in
the ALPHA trial and IGA-CHE in the DELTA Force trial to allow comparative analysis of delgocitinib versus PUVA ±

TCS.
Results:
Across all assessed endpoints at week 12, delgocitinib demonstrated greater efficacy than PUVA ± TCS. Patients
treated with delgocitinib were statistically significantly more likely to achieve a PGA/IGA-CHE score of 0/1
compared to those treated with PUVA ± TCS 
(
odds ratio [OR] = 2.5; 95% confidence interval [CI]: 1.3, 4.9; p =
0.009
)
. Patients treated with delgocitinib experienced a statistically significantly greater reduction in HECSI score
CFB at week 12 compared to those treated with PUVA ± TCS 
(
Difference [Diff] = -13.4; 95% CI: - 24.3, -2.4; p =
0.017
)
. Similarly, delgocitinib led to a significantly greater reduction in DLQI score from baseline to week 12
compared to PUVA ± TCS 
(
Diff = -2.3; 95% CI: -4.1, -0.6; p= 0.008
)
.
Conclusion:
The results of this MAIC analysis demonstrate that treatment with delgocitinib significantly improves both clinical
and quality-of-life outcomes compared to PUVA plus as required TCS in severe CHE at week 12, including
PGA/IGA-CHE, HECSI, and DLQI. These findings support delgocitinib as a highly efficacious and accessible
alternative to PUVA ± TCS for managing severe CHE. As a topical treatment, delgocitinib also offers a more
convenient alternative to PUVA based treatment, reducing the need for frequent clinic visits, alongside offering a
favourable tolerability and safety profile.
