Abstract N°: 5452
Impact of Dupilumab on Mental and Emotional Well-Being in Patients With Moderate-to-Severe Atopic
Dermatitis
Alexa Kimball
1
, Ashley Holub
2
, Min Yang
2
, Bruno Martins
2
, Jingdong Chao
3
, Gaëlle Bégo-Le-Bagousse
4
, Kerry
Noonan
5
, Zhixiao Wang
3
1
Harvard Medical School, Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center, Boston, United States
2
Analysis Group Inc., Boston, United States
3
Regeneron Pharmaceuticals Inc, Tarrytown, United States
4
Sanofi, Gentilly, France
5
Sanofi, Cambridge, United States
Introduction & Objective:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic systemic disease that has a negative impact on mental health in children,
adolescents, and adults, which can lead to cumulative life course impairment 
(
CLCI
)
, the persistent and
accumulating disease burden over time that leads to irreversible negative effects in various domains of patient life.
There are limited data examining the extent that AD affects patients
’
 CLCI and how treatment may affect the lives
of these patients. Here, we report the impact of AD and dupilumab treatment on mental and emotional well-
being.
Materials & Methods:
Adult patients with moderate-to-severe AD enrolled in RELIEVE-AD 
(
a prospective, long-term survey study
)
 who
completed the Year 6 survey, were invited to participate in qualitative one-on-one interviews lasting up to 60
minutes. The semi-structured interview consisted of questions focusing on different domains of life before and
after initiating dupilumab. Content analysis using ATLAS.ti was conducted to analyze the qualitative data obtained
from the interviews.
Results:
50 patients participated in the interview study. The mean age was 26.3 years at the time of AD diagnosis and 45.4
years at the time of dupilumab initiation, and 31 
(
62.0%
)
 were female participants. Here we report data on the
psychological impact of AD, focused on mental and emotional well-being, and changes after initiating dupilumab
treatment. All participants discussed the negative impact of AD on mental and emotional well-being. Participants
reported poor self-image 
(
78%
)
, being stressed/worried 
(
76%
)
, poor concentration 
(
58%
)
, shame/embarrassment
(
56%
)
, and experiencing stigma/discrimination 
(
52%
)
 due to AD. Participants also experienced hopelessness
(
46%
)
, frustration 
(
40%
)
, depression 
(
40%
)
, exhaustion/fatigue 
(
40%
)
, anxiety 
(
34%
)
, unhappiness 
(
34%
)
, and
were irritable with others 
(
12%
)
 due to their AD. After initiating dupilumab treatment, participants reported less
stress/worry 
(
78%
)
, increased happiness 
(
66%
)
, improved self-image 
(
66%
)
, improved concentration 
(
42%
)
, less
anxiety 
(
32%
)
, and less shame/embarrassment 
(
22%
)
. Participants also reported feeling relief 
(
18%
)
, decreased
depression 
(
18%
)
, increased hope 
(
16%
)
, less stigma/discrimination 
(
14%
)
, less frustration 
(
12%
)
, and less
exhaustion/fatigue 
(
10%
)
, following dupilumab treatment. A majority of the participants 
(
76%
)
 reported that areas
of their life might have been different if they had started dupilumab treatment earlier, with 40% highlighting the
impact on their mental health.
Conclusion:

Participants reported substantial mental and emotional burden due to uncontrolled moderate-to-severe AD.
Following dupilumab treatment, the majority of participants reported improvements in their mental and emotional
well-being. Early use of effective treatment for AD not only can improve patients
’
 psychosocial well-being but also
likely can help minimize irreversible life course impairment due to AD.
