Introduction & Objectives:
Increased disease severity is correlated with decreased quality of life for young children with atopic dermatitis
(
AD
)
 and their caregivers.** Reductions in disease extent and severity as measured by the Eczema Area and
Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
 have been reported in children with severe AD treated with dupilumab for up to 2 years. We
assessed changes in AD severity in separate anatomical regions and safety in children aged 6 months to 11 years
with severe AD treated with dupilumab for up to 3 years
.
Materials & Methods:
Children aged 6 months to 5 years with moderate-to-severe AD and 6 to 11 years with severe AD, who had
previously participated in dupilumab studies 
(
NCT03346434, NCT03345914
)
 were eligible to enroll in an open-
label extension 
(
OLE
)
 study 
(
PED-OLE, NCT02612454
)
. Dupilumab dosing was based on age and body weight: 6
months to 5 years, 200 mg 
(
5 to <15 kg
)
 or 300 mg 
(
15 to <30 kg
)
 every 4 weeks; 6 to 11 years, 200 mg 
(
<60 kg
)
or 300 mg 
(≥
60 kg
)
 every 2 weeks. Topical AD treatments were allowed. Improvements in EASI scores at Week
152 
(
mean, 75% and 90% improvements [EASI-75, EASI-90]
)
 across 4 anatomical regions 
(
head/neck 
–
 H/N, trunk
–
 T, upper extremities 
–
 UE, lower extremities 
–
 LE
)
, and safety were evaluated in patients with severe AD, defined
as an Investigator
’
s Global Assessment score of 4 at PED-OLE baseline. Data are reported as observed.
Results:
A total of 50 
(
6mo
–
5years
)
 and 75 patients 
(
6
–
11years
