Abstract N°: 5475
Chronic Hand Eczema has a Profound Impact on Daily Life, Social Interactions and Emotional Wellbeing:
Results from a Global Patient Survey
Marie Louise Schuttelaar
1
, Patricia Viard
2
, Camilla Kehlet
3
, Ana Giga
3
, Vivian Y Shi
4
1
University Medical Centre Groningen, University of Groningen, Groningen, Netherlands
2
Oracle Life Sciences, Paris, France
3
LEO Pharma A/S, Ballerup, Denmark
4
University of Washington, Seattle, United States
Introduction & Objectives:
Chronic Hand Eczema 
(
CHE
)
 is a frequent inflammatory skin disease with pain and pruritus, and clinical signs 
(
e.g.,
erythema, scaling, lichenification, hyperkeratosis, vesicles, oedema, and fissures
)
, carrying significant occupational,
functional, social, and psychological burden. Despite available treatments, unmet needs remain for tailored
management strategies. To better understand the impact of the disease, we surveyed CHE patients on multiple
aspects of their daily lives, with an emphasis on areas not routinely addressed during dermatology consultations.
The objective of this study was to quantify, from the patients
’
 perspective, the experiences and behaviors
influenced by CHE, addressing functional and social abilities, including work life, personal relationships, and
emotional wellbeing.
Materials & Methods:
An international general population-based quantitative online survey was conducted among 192 CHE patients
throughout Europe 
(
N=67; France, Germany, Italy and Spain
)
, the United-Kingdom 
(
N=28
)
, North America 
(
N=
47; Canada, the United States
)
 and Asia 
(
N=50; China, Japan
)
. Adult patients currently receiving treatment for
moderate to severe CHE, either ongoing at inclusion or documented as prior medical history were recruited.
Patients were asked to rate on a Likert scale, ranging from 1 
(
completely disagree
)
 to 7 
(
completely agree
)
, their
level of agreement with statements related to life activities and mental wellbeing 
(
11 categories
)
. The percentage
of patients who strongly agreed, pooling ratings 5 to 7, are presented.
Results:
Nearly half of CHE patients felt they were a burden to their families, struggling with household chores 
(
45%
)
 or
childcare responsibilities 
(
47%
)
. Of note, CHE also influenced patients
’
 work lives, with 52% avoiding certain
occupations and 50% needing to take time off. Among manual workers, 42% reported frequent painful lesions,
compared to 22% of office workers. Overall, pain was often underreported with the majority 
(
58%
)
 of CHE patients
downplaying their pain, likely underestimating its impact on their life choices. Importantly, 80% of CHE patients
wished the disease was not on visible parts of their skin, 60% attempted to conceal their condition, even from
partners. Additionally, patients reported that their condition greatly affected personal relationships 
(
49%
)
 and
socializing 
(
51%
)
, possibly linked with how negatively they felt others perceived their condition 
(
56%
)
. Indeed,
63% often avoided holding hands with their partners, family or friends, 51% felt that their condition kept them
from socializing as much as they would like, and 46% declared feeling lonely. As a result, half of CHE patients
(
55%
)
 reported that their emotional wellbeing was deeply affected.
Conclusion:
These findings emphasize that CHE profoundly impacts multiple aspects of patients
’
 lives, including their work,

social interactions, and personal relationships, leading to significant emotional distress. Our study highlights key
areas to address routinely in clinical practice, such as the less visible signs of the disease, including pain and
mental health. This research supports dermatologists in making informed therapeutic decisions, emphasizing
timely interventions to improve care and quality of life for CHE patients. Given the significant personal, work, and
societal burdens, patients should seek prompt medical care, as effective management is crucial to mitigating the
disease
’
s impact.
