Abstract N°: 5492
Development and Utility Investigation of Educational Artificial Intelligence 
(
AI
)
 Software for Atopic
Dermatitis Severity Assessment
Kenichi Yamanaka
1
, Tomohiro Hirose
1
, Yagmur Pak
2
, Yoshihiro Inda
3
, Toshiaki Nomi
4
, Keisuke Ogaki
4
, Kouki
Nakamura
1
1
Pfizer Japan Inc., Speciality Care Medical Affairs, Tokyo, Japan
2
Pfizer Belgium, Chief Medical Office, Brussels, Belgium
3
Pfizer Japan Inc., Digital, Tokyo, Japan
4
M3 Inc., Tokyo, Japan
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is characterized by chronic eczematous skin lesions and pruritus accompanied by repeated
exacerbation and remission. Since disease manifestations are highly variable, precise assessment of severity of AD
lesions is crucial for appropriate treatment. The Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
 is a globally-used
assessment tool for objectively evaluating the severity of AD across the entire body. Scores are calculated from the
percentage of skin affected by eczema and the severity of the four clinical features of AD 
(
erythema,
papule/edema, abrasion, and lichenification
)
 for the four regions of the head and neck, upper limbs, trunk, and
lower limbs, and are presented as a composite score from 0 to 72. One disadvantage of EASI in real-world settings
is the possibility of the inter-rater variability of results. Therefore, the amount of experience and training of the
rater in visually assessing AD is considered to be an important variable factor. Recent advancements in artificial
intelligence 
(
AI
)
 technology have facilitated the extraction of feature quantities from complex image data, thereby
enabling more precise predictions. This study aimed to develop AI software for educational purposes to determine
the severity of four clinical features of AD 
(
erythema, papule/edema, abrasion, and lichenification
)
, and to further
evaluate its diagnostic accuracy. We employed this technology to assess skin images for disease severity in AD
patients. The AI tool demonstrated an accuracy that was comparable to, or even surpassed, that of medical
specialists.
Materials & Methods:
This was a multicenter, non-interventional, prospective, observational study in patients with AD conducted in
Japan. Patients 18 years or older, who had been or who were diagnosed with AD by a dermatologist and had
given written informed consent prior to participation, were included in the study. Up to four skin images were
collected for each participant. ConvNeXt, a Convolutional Neural Network 
(
CNN
)
 model incorporating Vision
Transformer technology, was used in the development of the AI tool to simultaneously output continuous values
for the severity of four symptoms. The model was trained to minimize the prediction error for the severity of each
symptom based on a single image input. Using five-fold cross-validation, all obtained samples were used for
evaluation. The data were divided into training, validation, and test sets on a patient basis. Test Time
Augmentation was employed during evaluation. Receiver Operating Characteristic 
(
ROC
)
 curves were plotted
based on the severity prediction scores for each symptom and the correct labels for each endpoint 
(≥
1, 
≥
2, 
≥
3
)
.
Results:
A total of 3,679 images from 1,016 individuals collected from 16 sites in Japan were analyzed by the AI tool. The
area under the curve 
(
AUC
)
, sensitivity, and specificity for severity 
≥
2 are shown in the table.

Conclusion:
The CNN-based AI software developed in this study demonstrated an AUC exceeding 0.8 for each of the four
clinical features required for the calculation of the EASI score, where the severity level was evaluated at 2 or higher.
The AI software has promising potential to contribute to the education of severity diagnostic capability of the EASI
score and may enable more precise and objective evaluations of AD disease severity.
