Probity Medical Research, Peterborough, Canada
12
York Dermatology Clinic & Research Centre, Richmond Hill, Canada
13
Dalhousie University, Division of Dermatology, Department of Medicine, Halifax, Canada
14
McMaster University, Division of Clinical Immunology and Allergy, Department of Medicine, Hamilton, Canada
15
Allergy Research Canada Inc., Niagara Falls, Canada
16
AbbVie Corporation, Saint-Laurent, Canada
17
University of Calgary, Division of Dermatology, Department of Medicine, Calgary, Canada
18
University of Calgary, Division of Community Pediatrics, Department of Pediatrics, Calgary, Canada
19
University of Calgary, Division of Pediatric Rheumatology, Department of Pediatrics, Calgary, Canada
20
Skin Health & Wellness Centre, Calgary, Canada
21
Dermatology Research Institute, Calgary, Canada
22
Probity Medical Research, Calgary, Canada
Introduction & Objectives:
Open-label studies have shown that switching to upadacitinib 
(
UPA
)
, a once-daily oral Janus kinase 1 selective
inhibitor, can improve clinical outcomes in patients with atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 who were previously treated with
or inadequately responded to dupilumab 
(
DUPI
)
, a commonly prescribed, biweekly and subcutaneously injected
IL-4R
α
 inhibitor. The CAN UpTIMISE study assessed the real-world effectiveness of daily UPA 15 mg and 30 mg in
patients with AD who were inadequate responders to DUPI or discontinued DUPI due to safety/tolerability
reasons. To report the effectiveness of UPA switch from DUPI through selected patient-reported outcomes of
disease activity/severity, treatment satisfaction, and patient preference.
Materials & Methods:
The CAN UpTIMISE 
(
NCT05394792
)
 study was a Canadian, multicentre, observational study in adult patients with

moderate-to-severe AD who had been treated with DUPI for 
≥
16 weeks as a prior line of systemic therapy. The
decision to switch to UPA was made before study entry, and per the product label. Effectiveness was measured
over 4 months with the validated Investigator Global Assessment for AD 
(
vIGA-AD; primary outcome
)
, Eczema
Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
, Worst Pruritus Numerical Rating Scale 
(
WP-NRS
)
, Patient Oriented Eczema
Measurement 
(
POEM
)
, Patient Global Impression of change 
(
PGI-C
)
, severity 
(
PGI-S
)
, and treatment 
(
PGI-T
)
, and
modified Oral versus Injectable Questionnaire scores. Results are presented using descriptive statistics.
Results:
Overall, 108 patients were included in the analysis, of which 59.3% initiated UPA 30 mg. The mean patient age was
42.9 years 
(
SD 16.7
)
, with the majority of patients being male 
(
55.6%
)
 and White 
(
61.1%
)
 or Asian 
(
33.3%
)
. Most
patients 
(
83.3%
)
 discontinued DUPI due to suboptimal disease control, while 16.7% stopped DUPI because of
safety/tolerability concerns. At baseline, only 5.6% of patients had a vIGA-AD score of 0/1. As a primary endpoint,
65.9% 
(
95% CI: 55.1% 
–
 75.2%
)
 of patients achieved vIGA-AD 0/1 at Month 4, with either UPA dose. At Month 4,
76.8%/54.9%/23.2% of patients achieved EASI 75/90/100, respectively. Between baseline and Month 4, the
proportion of patients experiencing little to no itch 
(
WP-NRS 0/1
)
 increased from 6.8% to 46.7%. Improvements in
POEM scores were also observed, with 77.3% of patients achieving a reduction of 
≥
4 points from baseline and
34.3% achieving a POEM score 
≤
2 at Month 4, in patients with baseline POEM >4 and >2, respectively. Most of
the improvements seen with UPA were observed as early as Month 1 and were generally maintained at Month 4.
By the end of the study, 75.7% of patients scored the change in disease activity as 
‘
very much/much improved
’
(
PGI-C
)
, 54.7% scored their disease severity as 
‘
absent/minimal
’
 
(
PGI-S
)
, and 60.0% were 
‘
extremely/very satisfied
’
with the treatment 
(
PGI-T
)
. Most patients preferred an oral vs injectable AD treatment, both at baseline and
Month 4. UPA was well-tolerated, and its safety profile was consistent with observations made across RCTs.
Conclusion:
The CAN UpTIMISE study demonstrates that UPA is effective and well-tolerated in AD patients who were
inadequate responders to DUPI or discontinued DUPI due to safety/tolerability reasons. Most patients rapidly
achieved clear/almost clear skin and clinically meaningful improvements across patient-reported outcomes,
disease activity/severity, and treatment satisfaction assessments, with no new safety signals reported.
