Abstract N°: 5518
Study on Relapse of Dupilumab in the Treatment of Atopic Dermatitis
Shi-Yuan Zheng
1
, Ye He
1
, Ya-Xuan Zhu
1
, Ya-Xuan Zheng
1
, Hui-Chun Su
1
1
Fujian Medical University Union Hospital, Department of Dermatology, Fuzhou, Fujian, China
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
, a chronic relapsing inflammatory skin disease driven by Th2-type immunity, exhibits
marked heterogeneity in relapse mechanisms across individuals. While dupilumab
—
a monoclonal antibody
targeting IL-4R
α—
effectively alleviates AD symptoms by blocking IL-4/IL-13 signaling, relapse occurs in
approximately 30% of patients in real-world clinical practice, with risk factors remaining poorly characterized. The
objective of this study is to identify factors which contribute to relapse in dupilumab-treated moderate-to-severe
AD.
Materials & Methods:
This prospective study enrolled a cohort of 164 patients with moderate-to-severe AD, defined by Scoring Atopic
Dermatitis 
(
SCORAD
)
 index 
≥
25 and diagnosed according to Hanifin & Rajka criteria. Data on demographic
characteristics, personal/family medical history, lifestyle, dupilumab treatment pattern, as well as patient-reported
relapse events and their associated triggers were collected via structured questionnaires. Disease severity was
evaluated using standardized clinical metrics, including SCORAD, Excema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
,
Numerical Rating Scale 
(
NRS
)
, and Atopic Dermatitis Control Tool 
(
ADCT
)
 . Cox proportional hazards models were
used to identify relapse risk factors, while the cumulative recurrence risk and gender-stratified analyeses were
applied to evaluate cumulative relapse risk.
Results:
Among the 164 patients, 53 patients 
(
32.32%
)
 remained relapse-free, while relapse occurred in 45 patients
(
27.44%
)
 within 1-month post-treatment, 11 patients 
(
6.71%
)
 between 1-3 months, 39 patients 
(​​
23.78%
)​​
 at 3 to 6
months, and 
​​
16 patients 
(
9.76%
)​
 beyond 6 months.  Multivariate Cox analysis identified age stratification
(
HR=1.28, 95%CI:1.02
–
1.60
)
, pet ownership 
(
HR=2.84, 95%CI:1.15
–
7.01
)
, and asthma history 
(
HR=1.82,
95%CI:1.00
–
3.31
)
 as independent risk factors 
(
P<0.05 for all
)
 for relapse. Notably, the relapse group exhibited a
higher proportion of early EASI50 responders at week 2 compared to the non-relapse group 
(
50.00%
vs. 23.08%, 
P
 = 0.024
)
. In contrast, the non-relapse group demonstrated superior long-term efficacy outcomes at
weeks 12
–
16, with significantly higher response rates for both SCORAD50 
(
75.00% vs. 26.09%
)
 and EASI75
(
66.67% vs. 26.09%
)
 
(
P
 < 0.05 for both
)
. Patient-reported relapse triggers predominantly include climate 
(
42.70%
)
and sweating 
(
29.88%
)
.
Conclusion:
This study reveals a distinct efficacy pattern in dupilumab-treated AD, where rapid initial improvement does not
guarantee sustained remission. Age, pet exposure, and asthma history independently elevate relapse risk,
highlighting the need for personalized maintenance strategies in high-risk subgroups. Results from this study
suggest that mitigation of environmental triggers and management of comorbidity may be critical for relapse
prevention. Overall, these findings  underscore the importance of regular follow-ups and tailored interventions to
achieve long-term outcomes in AD management.

