Introduction & Objectives:
 Efficacy and safety of lebrikizumab 
(
LEB
)
 for the treatment of moderate-to-severe
atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 was demonstrated throughout the extensive clinical development program. Moreover,
patient-reported outcomes 
(
PROs
)
 have also been collected and have provided valuable insights in the
randomized controlled trials. Real-world data on LEB in AD remains scarce as of today. Here, we report interim
PROs data and safety from the non-interventional study 
(
ADlife
)
.
Materials & Methods:
 This is an open, prospective, multi-center, observational, cohort study to evaluate AD
control under LEB at week 
(
W
)
 52. Patients were included if they were 
≥
18 years old, had moderate-to-severe AD
requiring systemic therapy, received LEB treatment as per label according to physician´s decision, and provided
informed consent. PRO reported measures included: percent 
(
%
)
 change from baseline 
(
CFB
)
 of Dermatology Life
Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
, % of patients with DLQI 
≤
5%, % of patients with DLQI 0/1, CFB of Patient-Oriented Eczema
Measure 
(
POEM
)
, % of patients with POEM 
≥
4-points improvement from baseline, % CFB of Sleep-Loss Scale
(
sleep-loss
)
, and % of patients with sleep-loss 
≥
1-point improvement from baseline 
(
W16
)
. Safety outcomes
reported were adverse drug reactions 
(
ADRs
)
 and serious ADRs. This interim analysis covered patients completing
16W of treatment. Data are reported as observed. T-Test was performed for CFB analyses.
Results:
 A total of 100 patients were included 
(
8 patients dropped out of the study until W16
)
. Baseline
characteristics: mean 
(
SD
)
 age of 41.3 
(
15.0
)
 years and 57% were female; baseline severity: mean 
(
SD
)
 scores of
EASI 21.6 
(
11.5
)
, IGA 3.4 
(
0.6
)
, 24h-peak pruritus NRS 7.2 
(
2.1
)
 and DLQI 17.1 
(
7.2
)
, POEM 19.8 
(
6.1
)
 and sleep-
loss 2.1 
(
1.1
)
. The most common atopic comorbidities: 63% of patients presented rhinitis allergic/hay fever, 40%
asthma, 27% food allergy and 60% other allergies. Thirty percent 
(
30/100
)
 of patients have been previously
treated with advanced systemic treatments 
(
of which 76.7% [23/30] of patients had been pre-treated with
dupilumab
)
 and 86% 
(
86/100
)
 with TCS. At baseline/W4/W16, 48/36/26 patients received TCS as concomitant
topical prescription. PRO data 
(
W16
)
: percent CFB of DLQI was -66.0% 
(
p<0.0001
)
, 58.5% of patients had DLQI
≤
5, 31.7% of patients achieved DLQI 0/1, percent CFB of POEM was -50.0% 
(
p<0.0001
)
, 76.8% of patients
reported POEM 
≥
4-points improvement from baseline, percent CFB of sleep-loss was -53.1% 
(
p<0.0001
)
, and
71.6% of patients presented sleep-loss 
≥
1-point improvement from baseline. In this preliminary analysis, ADRs
under lebrikizumab were consistent with those in clinical trials. No new safety signals were observed.
Conclusion:
 These findings provide reassurance on the clinical benefits of lebrikizumab to patients in a real-world
setting in a population suffering from several comorbidities and with patients priorly treated with other advanced
systemics.

