Introduction & Objectives:
 Lebrikizumab 
(
LEB
)
 is a monoclonal antibody that binds with high-affinity to IL-13,
blocking its downstream effects. Results of LEB efficacy based upon absolute EASI scores 
(
range 0-72 [clear-
maximum severity]
)
, considered clinically relevant as they indicate remaining disease regardless of baseline
severity, have been reported before up to 52 weeks 
(
W
)
 of treatment.1 Here, we present the proportion of W16
per-protocol responders from the ADvocate1 and ADvocate2 studies who achieved absolute EASI scores of 
≤
7,
≤
5, 
≤
3 and 
≤
1 up to 2 years of continuous LEB treatment in the ADjoin long-term extension 
(
LTE
)
 study
(
NCT04392154
)
.
Materials & Methods:
 ADvocate1&ADvocate2 were two identically designed, randomized, placebo-controlled,
LEB monotherapy Phase 3 trials in adult 
(≥
18 years
)
 and adolescents 
(
12 to <18 years, 
≥
 40 kg
)
 with moderate-
to-severe AD. Patients were randomized 2:1 to receive LEB 250 mg every 2 weeks 
(
Q2W
)
 
(
loading dose of 500 mg
given at baseline and W2
)
 or placebo Q2W until W16 
(
Induction period
)
. After W16, LEB Q2W patients who met
response-criteria 
(
EASI75 or IGA0/1 with 
≥
2-point improvement without rescue medication
)
 were rerandomized
2:2:1 
(
LEB 250 mg Q2W : LEB 250 mg Q4W : PBO [LEB-withdrawal]
)
. Patients who completed W52 
(
Maintenance
period
)
 were enrolled in the ADjoin-LTE 
(
W54-W152
)
, continuing the same dose regimen, except patients in the
LEB-withdrawal group which were offered LEB 250 mg Q2W, but were not included in the analysis population. This
analysis assessed the proportion 
(
%
)
 of W16 per-protocol responders who achieved absolute EASI scores of 
≤
7,
≤
5, 
≤
3, and 
≤
1 up to 2 years of treatment 
(
W104
)
. Topical corticosteroids were allowed at the ADjoin. Data were
reported as observed using all collected data regardless of rescue medication use.
Results:
 291 patients 
(
ADvocate1&2
)
 achieved EASI75 or IGA0/1 at W16, were rerandomized, and entered in the
maintenance-period until W52. Of these, 82 
(
LEB Q2W
)
 and 99 
(
LEB Q4W
)
 entered in the ADjoin-LTE. Out of LEB
W16 per-protocol responders who entered in the ADjoin-LTE, after 2 years of continuous LEB treatment 
(
LEB Q4W
and LEB Q2W
)
, 96.4% and 92.8% of patients reported EASI 
≤
7, 91.6% and 88.4% of patients reported EASI 
≤
5,
85.5% and 82.6% of patients achieved EASI 
≤
3 and 66.3% and 60.9% of patients reported EASI 
≤
1, respectively.

Less than 13% of patients received concomitant topical therapy through 104 weeks of lebrikizumab treatment.
Conclusion:
 The proportion of LEB W16 responders who entered in the ADjoin-LTE reporting EASI 
≤
7 
(
mild
disease
)
 and 
≤
5 remained sustained after 2 years of continuous treatment, while the proportion of patients
reporting more stringent endpoints like EASI 
≤
3 
(
optimal target
)
 and EASI 
≤
1 
(
clear/almost clear skin
)
 continued
to increase over time up to 2 years, with most patients not using concomitant topical therapy. This data supports
the long-term sustained disease control with LEB.
