Introduction & Objectives:
 Lebrikizumab 
(
LEB
)
 is a monoclonal antibody that binds with high-affinity to IL-13
thereby blocking its downstream effects. Here, we analyze the results of skin signs, itch and sleep assessed by the
Patient Oriented Eczema Measure 
(
POEM
)
 in moderate-to-severe AD patients treated with LEB from the
ADvocate1 and ADvocate2 induction period who responded to treatment at week 
(
W
)
16 and continued into the
ADjoin long-term extension 
(
LTE
)
 study 
(
NCT04392154
)
 with up to 3 years of continuous LEB treatment.
Materials & Methods:
 ADvocate1 and ADvocate2 were two identically designed, randomized, placebo-
controlled, LEB monotherapy Phase 3 trials in adult 
(≥
18 years
)
 and adolescents 
(
12 to <18 years, 
≥
 40 kg
)
 with
moderate-to-severe AD. Patients were randomized 2:1 to receive LEB 250 mg every 2W 
(
Q2W
)
 
(
loading dose of
500 mg given at baseline and W2
)
 or placebo Q2W until W16 
(
Induction period
)
. After W16, LEB Q2W patients
who met response-criteria 
(
EASI75 or IGA0/1 with 
≥
2-point improvement, without rescue medication
)
 were
rerandomized 2:2:1 
(
LEB 250 mg Q2W; LEB 250 mg Q4W; PBO [LEB-withdrawal]
)
. Patients who completed W52
(
Maintenance period
)
 were offered to be enrolled in the ADjoin-LTE 
(
W54-W152
)
 study, taking the same dose
regimen assigned during the Maintenance period, except patients in the LEB-withdrawal group which were offered
LEB 250 mg Q2W, but were not included in the analysis population. POEM is a patient-reported measurement of
signs 
(
skin appearance
)
 and symptoms 
(
itch and sleep
)
 of AD, where a score of 0
–
2 indicates clear/almost clear,
3
–
7 mild, 8
–
16 moderate, 17
–
24 severe, and 25-28 very severe eczema. The following outcomes were assessed as
observed: POEM mean total score, proportion of patients with POEM 
≤
7 
(
among patients with POEM >7 at
ADvocate1&2 studies baseline
)
, proportion of patients with POEM 
≥
4-point improvement 
(
among patients with
POEM 
≥
4-points at ADvocate1&2 studies baseline
)
, and proportions of patients according to POEM individual
items of itching and sleep disturbance.

Results:
 291 patients 
(
ADvocate1&2
)
 were responders at W16, and were rerandomized, and entered the
maintenance-period until W52. Of these, 99 
(
LEB Q4W
)
 and 82 
(
LEB Q2W
)
 entered into the ADjoin-LTE 
(
mean
POEM at baseline of 20.1 and 21.0, respectively
)
. In W16 LEB-responders, POEM mean values were sustained and
further improved up to 3 years of continuous treatment with similar values between LEB Q4W and LEB Q2W
(
mean POEM of 7.7 and 8.6 at W16 and 5.3 and 5.5 after 3 years, respectively
)
. Likewise, the proportion of patients
achieving POEM 
≥
4-points improvement/POEM 
≤
7 at W16 also were sustained and further increased up to 3
years 
(
92.7% and 98.0% / 69.1% and 77.6%
)
 for both Q4W and Q2W respectively. A total 
(
LEB Q4W and LEB
Q2W
)
 of 34.5% and 38.0% of patients reported no days of itching and 85.5% and 74.0% reported no days of sleep
disturbance at 3 years, respectively.
Conclusion:
 LEB improved patient-reported skin signs and symptoms in adult and adolescent patients with
moderate-to-severe AD, measured by total POEM scores during the long-term extension study up to 3 years. In
W16 LEB-responders, POEM score improvement were sustained and further increased up to 3 years of continuous
treatment. Similar results were observed with both treatment arms. Improvements were also observed at 3 years in
the POEM individual itching and sleep disturbances scores.
