Introduction & Objectives:
 Lebrikizumab 
(
LEB
)
 is a novel, high-affinity monoclonal antibody targeting
interleukin-13 for moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
. Improvements in both skin severity and itch are
important as they translate into substantial improvements in patient
‑
reported AD outcomes1. Here, we analyze the
composite skin clearance and itch relief with LEB at week 
(
W
)
 16 and W52 from pooled ADvocate1 
(
ADv1
)
 and
ADv2 
(
NCT04146363 & NCT04178967
)
 clinical trials.
Materials & Methods:
 ADv1&ADv2 were two identically designed, randomized, placebo-controlled, LEB
monotherapy Phase 3 trials in adult 
(≥
18 years
)
 and adolescents 
(
12 to <18 years, 
≥
 40 kg
)
 with moderate-to-
severe AD. Patients were randomized 2:1 to receive LEB 250 mg every 2 weeks 
(
Q2W
)
 
(
loading dose of 500 mg
given at baseline and W2
)
 or placebo Q2W until W16 
(
Induction period
)
. Responders were defined as patients
achieving EASI75 or IGA 0/1 with 
≥
2-point improvement from baseline 
(
without rescue medication
)
 at W16. W16
LEB responders were re-randomized 2:2:1 
(
LEB 250 mg every 4 weeks [Q4W], LEB 250 mg Q2W or placebo [PBO-
LEB withdrawal]
)
 for 36W additional 
(
Maintenance Primary population, MPP
)
 
(
topical rescue medication was
permitted
)
. This post-hoc analysis reports the proportion of patients achieving the following composite scores:
EASI75 AND pruritus NRS 
≥
4-point improvement, EASI90 AND pruritus NRS 
≥
4-point improvement, and EASI 
≤
7
AND pruritus NRS 
≥
4-point improvement at W16 and W52, respectively. The pooled Intention-to-Treat 
(
ITT; all
randomized patients
)
 population for ADv1 and the modified ITT 
(
mITT
)
 population for ADv2 for W16 and the
pooled modified MPP 
(
mMPP
)
 for W52 were used. Analyses were performed at W16 and W52 
(
on W16 LEB
responders
)
. Missing data due to lack of efficacy or data after rescue medication usage were imputed as non-
responder imputation. Other missing data were imputed using multiple imputation.
Results:
 At W16, 31.9% of LEB Q2W treated patients vs 5.2% of PBO patients achieved EASI75 AND pruritus NRS
≥
4-point improvement, 22.1% vs 2.8% achieved EASI90 AND pruritus NRS 
≥
4-point improvement and 32.1% vs
5.8% achieved EASI 
≤
7 AND pruritus NRS 
≥
4-point improvement, respectively 
(
p<0.0001, all cases
)
. Significant
results in all composite scores were already reported at W4. At W52 
(
LEB Q4W, LEB Q2W, PBO-LEB withdrawal
)
,
52.1%, 54.3% and 45.2% of patients achieved EASI75 AND pruritus NRS 
≥
4-point improvement, 41.7%, 45.0% and
31.3% of patients achieved EASI90 AND pruritus NRS 
≥
4-point improvement, and 53.2%, 52.0% and 46.0%
achieved EASI 
≤
7 AND pruritus NRS 
≥
4-point improvement, respectively.
Conclusion:
 LEB Q2W provides significant higher rates of composite skin clearance and itch relief at W16 vs PBO

as early as W4. From the W16 LEB responders, high rates of composite skin clearance and itch relief are observed
at W52 in both maintenance dosing regimens 
(
LEB Q4W and Q2W
)
.
