Introduction & Objectives:
 Lebrikizumab 
(
LEB
)
 is a novel, high-affinity monoclonal antibody that selectively
binds to IL-13. LEB safety data have been reported in individual trial disclosures and multiple long-term integrated
analyses. LEB does not require laboratory monitoring, has no expected drug
–
drug interactions and has no
significant hepatic metabolism or renal elimination. Here, we present the integrated safety data reported in LEB
’
SmPC 
(
cut-off date: 6th June 2022
)
.
Materials & Methods:
 LEB
’
s SmPC safety section includes integrated data from the following 8 studies: LEB
monotherapy and LEB + topical corticosteroids 
(
TCS
)
 studies 
(
ADvocate1&ADvocate2,
[NCT04146363&NCT04178967], KGAF [NCT03443024], ADhere [NCT04250337], ADjoin [NCT04392154], ADore
[NCT04250350], ARBAN [NCT02465606], and TREBLE [NCT02340234]
)
. Study size-adjusted percentages were
calculated for the pooled placebo 
(
PBO
)
-controlled analysis of the monotherapy 
(
ADvocate1, ADvocate2, and
KGAF
)
 and TCS combination therapy 
(
ADhere
)
 studies 
(
induction period: W0-16
)
, for the maintenance period
(
ADvocate1&ADvocate2
)
 
(
W16-52
)
, and for all LEB studies. For adverse events 
(
AEs
)
 analyzed as a cluster,
searches of the following MedDRA preferred terms were conducted: conjunctivitis 
(
conjunctivitis and conjunctivitis
bacterial
)
, keratitis 
(
keratitis; allergic keratitis; ulcerative keratitis; vernal keratoconjunctivitis; and atopic
keratoconjunctivitis
)
, and injection site reactions 
(
injection site-related: pain, erythema, reaction, discomfort,
dermatitis, pruritus, swelling, and rash
)
.
Results:
 From W0-16 
(
783 patients treated with LEB 250 mg Q2W±TCS and 404 patients with PBO±TCS
)
, AEs
occurring in 
≥
1% of patients are described in the Table, being all non-serious and most of them mild or moderate
in severity. Most injection site reactions and all conjunctivitis and dry eye events were mild or moderate in severity
and the majority did not lead to treatment discontinuation. Herpes zoster, eosinophilia, blepharitis and keratitis
AEs occurred in <1% of patients, these events being non-serious, mild or moderate in severity, and most of them
not leading to discontinuation 
(
only keratitis led to discontinuation in n=2 [0.3%] patients
)
. From W16-52 / For all
LEB studies 
(
118 patients LEB 250 mg Q4W±TCS ADvocate1&ADvocate2 / 1720 patients treated with at least one
dose of LEB, respectively
)
, the conjunctivitis incidence rate did not increase over time 
(
n=6, 5.0% LEB 250 mg
Q4W±TCS ADvocate1&ADvocate2, and n=12, 0.7% across all LEB studies, respectively
)
.

Table
Patients with 
≥
1 adverse event, n 
(
%
)
Lebrikizumab vs placebo-controlled dataseta
Lebrikizumab 250 mg Q2W±TCSb 
(
N=783
)
Conjunctivitisc
54 
(
6.9
)
Conjunctivitis allergic
14 
(
1.8
)
Injection site reactions
20 
(
