Dermatology Unit, Fondazione IRCCS Ca
’
 Granda Ospedale Maggiore Policlinico, Milan, Italy
8
Department of Dermatology and Skin Science, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, Canada
9
Almirall S.A., Barcelona, Spain
10
Department of Dermatology and Allergy, University Hospital Schleswig-Holstein, Campus Kiel, Kiel, Germany
Introduction & Objectives:
 Patients with moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 might show a significant
impairment of their psychological well-being. Lebrikizumab 
(
LEB
)
 is a high-affinity monoclonal antibody targeting
interleukin-13 indicated for moderate-to-severe AD. The objective was to assess the effect of LEB combined with
topical corticosteroids 
(
TCS
)
 on psychological well-being calculated by thresholds of response, and depression
suspicion, in patients with moderate-to-severe AD included in the ADvantage study.
Materials & Methods:
 Eligible patients were adults and adolescents 
(≥
12 to <18 years
)
 with moderate-to-severe
AD who were inadequately controlled with or ineligible for ciclosporin A 
(
CsA
)
. Patients were randomized 2:1 to
LEB 250 mg or placebo 
(
PBO
)
 every two weeks 
(
Q2W
)
. After a 16-week 
(
W
)
, randomized, double-blind, PBO-
controlled, induction period, all patients received LEB 250 mg Q2W during an open-label 36-W maintenance
period. All patients received concomitant mid-potency TCS through W16; from W16 to W52, TCS use was at
investigator discretion. Well-being was assessed at baseline, W16 and W52 through the 5-item WHO Well-being
Index 
(
WHO-5; range 0-100, 100=maximal well-being; mean WHO-5 score in the general population of the
countries participating in the ADvantage study was calculated to be 64.7; when WHO-5 is used for the screening of
depressive symptoms, a cutoff score of 
≤
50 is used [20-50 mild and 
≤
28 moderate-to-severe depressive
symptoms], with depression suspicion defined as answering 0 or 1 to any of the 5 items, and/or total score <52
)
.
Analyses were performed in the full analysis set. Missing data was not imputed, an observed cases approach was
used.
Results:
 331 patients were randomized 
(
220 LEB+TCS, 111 PBO+TCS; 12% adolescents
)
. In adults/adolescents,
mean WHO-5 score increased from 41.0/43.0 and 39.6/45.8 at baseline to 60.9/65.0 and 51.3/58.3 at W16 in the
LEB+TCS and PBO+TCS arms, respectively. At W52, mean WHO-5 score was 66.2/64.7 and 66.1/74.4 for
LEB+TCS/LEB±TCS and PBO+TCS/LEB±TCS arms, respectively. At baseline, 31.4% and 36.5% of patients in the
LEB+TCS 
(
n=204
)
 and PBO+TCS 
(
n=96
)
 arms, respectively, had a WHO-5 score
≤
28 and the percentages

decreased to 8.2% and 16.5% at W16 
(
with 75.8% and 58.8% of patients having a WHO-5 score>50 at W16,
respectively
)
. At W52, 81.1% and 81.0% of patients in the LEB+TCS/LEB±TCS 
(
n=164
)
 and PBO+TCS/LEB±TCS
(
n=79
)
 arms had a WHO-5 score>50. Similar trends were observed for subgroups of adults and adolescents. At
baseline, 29.9% and 21.9% of patients in the LEB+TCS 
(
n=204
)
 and PBO+TCS 
(
n=96
)
 arms, respectively, did not
show depression suspicion and the percentages increased to 70.0% and 52.6% at W16. At W52, 76.8% and 77.2%
of patients in the LEB+TCS/LEB±TCS 
(
n=164
)
 and PBO+TCS/LEB±TCS 
(
n=79
)
 arms did not have depression
suspicion.
Conclusion:
 At baseline, patients included in the ADvantage study showed a significant impairment of their
psychological well-being, with one third of them reporting WHO-5 scores compatible with moderate-to-severe
depressive symptoms. In only 16 weeks of treatment, lebrikizumab improved the psychological well-being of both
adults and adolescents, achieving a well-being status similar to the general population, which was maintained or
improved after 52 weeks.
