Abstract N°: 5575
Correlation Between Atopic Dermatitis and Head-and-Neck Skin Lesions: A Recent Frontier Review
Ye He
1
, Jia-Yu Ye
1
, Jia-Hao Huang
1
, Ya-Xuan Zheng
1
, Hui-Chun Su
1
1
Fujian Medical University Union Hospital, Department of Dermatology, Fuzhou, Fujiian, China
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disorder, which profoundly impacts patients
’
 quality of life.
AD patients may develop head-and-neck dermatitis 
(
HND
)
, a distinct clinical subtype of AD characterized by
erythematous, desquamative lesions localized on the head and neck region. Furthermore, AD patients are at
increased risk of developing comorbid head-and-neck dermatoses,  including systemic lupus erythematosus
(
SLE
)
-specific facial lesions, rosacea, and perioral dermatitis. However, the association between AD and head-and-
neck skin lesions remains incompletely understood. Therefore, we aim to conduct a systematic review to enable
better understanding of their association.
Materials & Methods:
A comprehensive literature search was conducted using PubMed, Web of Science databases up to February 2025,
targeting studies on the clinical manifestations, diagnosis and treatment, and research progress of AD, HND as
well as AD comorbidity. The search terms included atopic dermatitis, head-and-neck dermatitis, and comorbidities.
Results:
The systematic review of selected articles revealed that AD patients had a significantly higher prevalence of
autoimmune comorbidities, including SLE and psoriasis, compared to non-AD controls. Additionally, they
exhibited greater susceptibility to inflammatory dermatoses 
(
acne, rosacea, contact dermatitis
)
, which was
associated with impaired epidermal barrier function. Among AD patients receiving topical therapies, there was a
higher prevalence of HND, with clinical features indicative of synergistic effects between epidermal barrier
dysfunction and IL-4/IL-13 pathway activation. Although topical corticosteroids provided initial symptom relief, a
substantial proportion of HND cases developed treatment-related adverse effects in facial regions. These findings
underscore the critical need to concurrently target inflammation and maintain epidermal barrier integrity in AD
management.
Conclusion:
This study demonstrates a potential association between AD and HND, as well as AD comorbidities, which is
mediated by epidermal barrier dysfunction and potentially exacerbated by topical therapies.  However, further
studies are needed to fully elucidate the pathophysiological relationship linking with AD, its therapeutic
interventions, and  the development of both head-and-neck skin lesions  and other comorbidities.
