Abstract N°: 5585
TGM3 in Atopic Dermatitis Pathogenesis: A Preliminary Study on the Proliferation, Apoptosis and
Inflammation of HaCaT Cells Mediated by TGM3
Ya-Xuan Zhu
1
, Jia-Hao Huang
1
, Jin-Cong Chen
1
, Hui-Chun Su
1
1
Fujian Medical University Union Hospital, Department of Dermatology, Fuzhou, Fujian, China
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disease, with autoimmunity present in 18%
–
91.4% of
patients. Elevated immunoglobulin E 
(
IgE
)
 targeting keratinocytes 
(
KC
)
-derived self-antigens contributes to AD
pathogenesis. Transglutaminase 3 
(
TGM3
)
, an epidermis-specific enzyme, has been shown to induce elevation of
specific IgE 
(
sIgE-TGM3
)
 in AD patient sera that positively correlates with skin lesion severity. However, whether
TGM3 modulates KC activity to promote AD pathogenesis remains to be investigated. By using HaCaT cell as a
keratinocyte model, this study investigates TGM3-mediated effects on HaCaT cell proliferation, apoptosis,
transcriptions and inflammatory responses. The effect of TGM3 on skin barrier function was also investigated.
Materials & Methods:
TGM3 knockdown 
(
TGM3KD
)
 and overexpression 
(
TGM3OE
)
 HaCaT models were constructed via small interfering
RNA technique 
(
siRNA
)
 and plasmid transfection, respectively, with controls including siRNA negative control
group 
(
Ctrl siRNA group
)
 and empty vector group. Ultramicroscopic structural changes of KCs upon alterations of
TGM3 expression were analyzed by transmission electron microscopy 
(
TEM
)
. Proliferation and apoptosis were
measured via CCK-8 and flow cytometry. qPCR quantified mRNA levels of barrier proteins 
(
FLG, LOR, IVL
)
 and
cytokines 
(
TNF-
α
, IL-4, IL-13, IL-31, IL-33, ST2, IL-17, IL-22, IL-25, TSLP
)
. Next-generation RNA Sequencing
identified the major inflammatory pathways affecting HaCaT cells, with western blot 
(
WB
)
 validating the changes
key proteins
’
 level.
Results:
TEM revealed normal ultrastructure in blank group, mild damage in empty vector controls, and pronounced
disruption in Ctrl siRNA, TGM3KD and TGM3OE groups. Compared to the blank group, significant proliferation
reduction occurred in Ctrl-siRNA, TGM3KD, empty vector and TGM3OE group, with no apoptosis differences
observed among the five groups. TGM3 modulation did not alter barrier proteins FLG/LOR/ IVL mRNA levels.
TGM3 knockdown decreased IL-33, IL-25 and IL-17 mRNA levels 
(
TGM3KD vs. Ctrl siRNA group, P =0.0186,0.0054
and P<0.0001
)
, while TGM3 overexpression upregulated their levels 
(
TGM3OE group vs. empty vector group, P
=0.0324, 0.0062 and <0.0001
)
. After the stimulation of TGM3 protein for 48h, mRNA expressions of IL-33, ST2, IL-
31 and TSLP in HaCaT cells were significantly increased compared with the blank group 
(
P=0.0027,0.0003,0.0001
and 0.0015
)
. PI3K-Akt pathway was activated in TGM3 overexpression 
(
TGM3OE group vs. empty vector group,
P=0.0260
)
.
Conclusion:
TGM3 dysregulation induced structural and proliferative alterations in HaCaT cells, independent of apoptotic
regulation or barrier protein expression. TGM3 overexpression promoted inflammation through upregulation of
pro-inflammatory cytokines 
(
IL-17, IL-25, IL-33, TSLP
)
 and activation of the PI3K-Akt pathway. Despite further
studies are required, preliminary findings from this study reveal the potential mechanistic roles of TGM3 in AD
pathogenesis: 1
)
 compromising keratinocyte structural integrity and growth dynamics, and 2
)
 promoting

inflammatory responses through cytokine dysregulation.
