Abstract N°: 5593
Pooled Safety Analysis of Nemolizumab in moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis: Results from two phase 3
trials
Andrew E. Pink
1, 2
, Jonathan I. Silverberg
3
, Andreas Wollenberg
4
, Carle Paul
5
, Jacek Szepietowski
6, 7
, Cheong Soo
Yeon
8
, Anna Ryzhkova
9
, Agnes Drahos
9
, Liliana Ulianov
9
, Christophe Piketty
9
1
St John
’
s Institute of Dermatology, London, United Kingdom
2
King
’
s College , London, United Kingdom
3
GW School of Medicine and Health Sciences, Washington, United States
4
Augsburg University Hospital, Augsburg, Germany
5
Toulouse University, Toulouse, France
6
4th Military Hospital in Wroclaw, Wroclaw, Poland
7
Wroc
ł
aw University of Science and Technology, Wroc
ł
aw, Poland
8
Galderma Laboratories, Dallas, United States
9
Galderma Research & Development, Zug, Switzerland
Introduction & Objectives: A
topic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a heterogeneous disease with significant unmet medical
need, particularly for patients with moderate-to-severe disease. Understanding the safety profile of new
treatments is crucial, & pooling data from 2 pivotal trials provides better overview of nemolizumab safety profile.
Pruritus was reported as the most burdensome symptom of AD & should be a primary treatment goal for these
patients. Nemolizumab uniquely inhibits the IL-31-signalling pathway targeting all pillars of AD: itch, inflammation
& epidermal dysregulation. In two large-scale phase 3 randomised controlled trials 
(
ARCADIA 1&2
)
, nemolizumab
demonstrated fast & safe improvement in key signs & symptoms of AD. This abstract reports the pooled safety
data from these two trials.
Materials & Methods:
 A pooled analysis was conducted on two phase 3 pivotal trials involving 
≥
12-year-old
patients with moderate-to-severe AD 
(
16 week initial treatment period & 32 week maintenance period
)
. Patients
were randomized 
(
2:1
)
 to receive either nemolizumab 30 mg 
(
60 mg at baseline
)
 or placebo, administered
subcutaneously every 4 weeks 
(
Q4W
)
 in combination with topical corticosteroids 
(
TCS
)
 of medium/low potency
and/or topical calcineurin inhibitors 
(
TCI
)
 for 16 weeks. During 32-week maintenance period clinical responders
(
Investigator
’
s Global Assessment score 0/1 [clear/almost-clear] or 
≥
75% improvement in the Eczema Area &
Severity Index
)
 to nemolizumab at week 16 were re-randomized 
(
1:1:1
)
 to receive nemolizumab 30 mg Q4W,
nemolizumab 30 mg Q8W, or placebo 
(
nemolizumab withdrawal
)
 Q4W subcutaneously in combination with TCS
and/or TCI. The incidence & severity of treatment-emergent adverse events 
(
TEAEs
)
, including adverse events of
special interest 
(
AESI
)
 & serious adverse events 
(
SAEs
)
 were reported.
Results:
 A 16-week post-hoc pooled analysis showed that the proportion of patients with 
≥
1%TEAE was similar
between the nemolizumab & placebo groups 
(
23.2% vs 17.6%
)
. In both groups the most common TEAE
(
incidence 
≥
5%
)
 was AD 
(
nemolizumab: 9.9% vs placebo: 8.4%
)
. During the 32-week maintenance period the
most common TEAEs 
(
incidence 
≥
5%
)
 were nasopharyngitis 
(
nemolizumab Q4W: 8.2%, nemolizumab Q8W: 4.8%,
nemolizumab withdrawal: 7.1%
)
, COVID-19 
(
nemolizumab Q4W: 7.6%, nemolizumab Q8W: 7.8%, nemolizumab
withdrawal: 11.3%
)
 & AD 
(
nemolizumab Q4W: 7.6%, nemolizumab Q8W: 6.6%, nemolizumab withdrawal: 10.7%
)
.
Most TEAEs were non-serious & mild/moderate in intensity. AESI 
(
injection-related reactions, asthma, infections, &
peripheral/facial oedema
)
 occurred at similar rates across the treatment arms. AESI in the field of AD during 32
week maintenance period were conjunctivitis allergic 
(
nemolizumab Q4W: 1.2%, nemolizumab Q8W: 0.6%,

nemolizumab withdrawal: 0.6%
)
 & infective conjunctivitis 
(
nemolizumab Q4W: 0%, nemolizumab Q8W: 0.6%,
nemolizumab withdrawal: 0%
)
. No fatal SAE were reported in both the initial & the maintenance periods. The
pooled safety data from the maintenance period up to week 48, reported at the 2024 American Academy of
Dermatology Annual meeting, showed that the safety profile of nemolizumab remained consistent with that
during the initial treatment period.
Conclusion:
 The pooled safety analysis demonstrates that targeting the IL-31 pathway with nemolizumab has
acceptable safety profile at 16 & 48 weeks. The low incidence of conjunctivitis & herpes zoster further supports
the safety of nemolizumab in long-term use.
