Abstract N°: 5603
Initial Real-World Clinical Practice Experiences with Lebrikizumab in the Management of Severe Atopic
Dermatitis
Joana Amat Calbet
1
, Marc Armengol Gairí
1
, Antonio Guilabert
1
, Carlos Muñoz Santos
1
1
Hospital general de granollers, Granollers, Spain
Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory disease affecting 3% of the
population, with a significant psychosocial impact. The therapeutic ladder includes topical treatments,
phototherapy, and systemic immunomodulators. In Spain, cyclosporine is the first-line treatment for moderate-to-
severe AD; however, the risk of nephrotoxicity limits its long-term use, often necessitating a switch to safer and
more effective agents 
(
biologics/JAK inhibitors
)
. Lebrikizumab is a recently approved anti-IL-13 biologic indicated
for moderate-to-severe AD in patients aged 12 years and older. We present three cases treated with lebrikizumab
in our department, providing one of the first real-world clinical experiences in Catalonia.
Results:
A 44-year-old male, smoker, with a 20-year history of atopic dermatitis previously treated with topical therapies,
cyclosporine, and methotrexate. He presented after a 5-year lapse in follow-up with eczematous lesions, some
exhibiting prurigo nodularis
–
like features, and an EASI score of 45 
(
BSA 36%, IGA 4, PP-NRS 9, SCORAD 67.6
)
.
After 7 weeks of treatment with lebrikizumab, he showed marked clinical improvement, with a reduction in EASI to
11 
(
BSA 25%, IGA 2, PP-NRS 4, SCORAD 43.9
)
.
A 21-year-old female from Senegal, with a 4-year history of atopic dermatitis previously treated with UVB
phototherapy and cyclosporine, showing a partial response. Baseline EASI was 22 
(
BSA 20%, IGA 3, PP-NRS 8,
SCORAD 48.2
)
. After 8 weeks of treatment with lebrikizumab 
(
in combination with low-dose cyclosporine
)
, EASI
was reduced to 5 
(
BSA 12%, IGA 1, PP-NRS 4, SCORAD 31.5
)
.
A 58-year-old female, with no significant medical history, diagnosed with atopic dermatitis since childhood. She
has received multiple treatments, including cyclosporine, methotrexate, dupilumab, abrocitinib, and upadacitinib.
Treatment with lebrikizumab was initiated in combination with oral prednisone 30 mg. Baseline assessment: EASI
28, BSA 28%, IGA 3, PP-NRS 7, SCORAD 56%. Follow-up evaluation pending.
Conclusion:
Lebrikizumab has demonstrated efficacy over placebo in clinical trials, achieving EASI-75 in up to 58.8% of cases at
week 16 
(
and up to 69.5% when combined with topical corticosteroids
)
. It shows a favorable safety profile, with
the most common adverse events being conjunctivitis 
(
6.9%
)
, injection site reactions 
(
2.6%
)
, and eosinophilia
(
0.4%
)
. In the cases presented, lebrikizumab induced a rapid response, with a mean EASI improvement of 76% at
7
–
8 weeks, good tolerability, and no notable adverse effects. Further studies are needed to assess its long-term
efficacy and safety in larger cohorts and real-world settings.
