Abstract N°: 5636
Sleep disturbance is twice as prevalent in children and adolescents with atopic dermatitis: A population-
based cohort study in English primary care
Carsten Flohr*
1
, Mandy Wan
2, 3
, Shona Cameron
1
, Maciej Czachorowski
4
, Andrew Wildman
5
, Charlotte Curtis
5
,
Melissa Watkins
6
1
Paediatric and Population-Based Dermatology Research, St John
’
s Institute of Dermatology, King
’
s College
London and Guy
’
s and St Thomas
’
 NHS Foundation Trust, London, United Kingdom
2
Evelina London Children
’
s Hospital, Guys
’
 & St Thomas
’
 NHS Foundation Trust, London, United Kingdom
3
Institute of Pharmaceutical Science, King
’
s College London, London, United Kingdom
4
Pfizer Ltd, Tadworth, United Kingdom
5
Momentum Data, St. Albans, United Kingdom
6
Pfizer Inc, New York, United States
Introduction & Objectives:
 Young people with atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 often experience delayed sleep onset and
may wake during the night, reducing both sleep duration and quality. This can impact their quality of life, and
affect other family members. Although AD is common in childhood and sleep disturbance has widespread effects,
population-based research is limited. Existing studies have focused on selected groups, which do not represent the
full spectrum of paediatric patients with AD. Therefore, this study aimed to estimate the burden of sleep
disturbance in children and adolescents with AD, using a nationally representative primary care database in
England, and to identify those groups most at risk.
Materials & Methods:
 The population comprised all eligible children and adolescents 
(
aged between 2-<18 years
years
)
 registered among 1720 general practices in England contributing data to Clinical Practice Research Datalink
(
CPRD
)
 Aurum database of routinely collected medical records 
(
March 1, 2003 - March 1, 2023
)
, with linkage to
Office for National Statistics 
(
ONS
)
 mortality data and Index of Multiple Deprivation. Individuals with active AD
were identified using a previously validated algorithm, requiring at least one AD-specific Read or SNOMED code
in addition to two AD treatment codes within 365 days of each other. Cases with active AD were matched with up
to four unaffected controls on sex, age 
(
categorical
)
, ethnicity, deprivation score and geographical region, with
matching on nearest neighbour for age and duration of practice registration. The outcome was any sleep
disturbance recorded in primary care, identified through Read and SNOMED codes. Association between AD and
sleep disturbance was evaluated using Cox regression, adjusting for age, sex, deprivation score and ethnicity.
Patients were followed until the earliest occurrence of a recorded sleep disturbance, study end, deregistration, or
death. Subgroup analysis included sex, age, socioeconomic deprivation, and ethnicity.
Results:
 823,860 active AD cases were identified and matched evenly to 3,187,462 unaffected controls across age,
sex, socioeconomic deprivation and ethnicity 
(
Table 1
)
. Median follow up time was 5.20 years 
(
Inter quartile range
[IQR] 2.35, 9.29] for cases with active AD and 3.50 years 
(
IQR 1.28, 7.57
)
 for controls. New onset sleep disturbance
was recorded in 53205 
(
6.5%
)
 patients with active AD and 81940 
(
2.6%
)
 controls. Cases with active AD were
identified to have a higher risk of sleep disturbance 
(
adjusted hazard ratio [aHR] 2.05, 95% Confidence Interval
[CI] 2.03-2.07
)
 compared to controls. Adolescents 
(
12 -<18 years
)
 had the highest risk of sleep disturbance
compared to children 
(
2 -<12 years
)
 
(
aHR 2.30, 95%CI 2.20, 2.39
)
. There were marked differences across ethnic
groups, with the highest risk reported in individuals of South Asian ethnicity 
(
aHR 2.50, 95%CI 2.42, 2.59
)
 and the
lowest in those of white ethnicity 
(
aHR 1.94, 95%CI 1.91, 1.96
)
. The risk of AD-associated sleep disturbance was
similar across sexes and deprivation groups 
(
aHR range 1.96 
–
 2.11
)
.

Conclusion:
 A substantial number of children and adolescents with AD experience sleep disturbance, with rates
twice as high as those in unaffected controls. AD related sleep disturbances were highest in adolescents and those
from ethnic groups other than white. These findings underscore the importance of recognizing the impact of sleep
disturbance in paediatric AD patients for optimal patient management and AD treatment.
