Introduction & Objectives:
While randomized controlled trials have investigated the efficacy of upadacitinib for atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
, real-
world data remains limited. We conducted a retrospective multicenter study of upadacitinib for AD as per the
Canadian target-to-treat 
(
T2T
)
 criteria as outlined by Yeung et al 
(
2023
)
.
Materials & Methods:
Our retrospective multicenter study included adult and adolescent patients with atopic dermatitis from three
Canadian institutions who initiated treatment with upadacitinib. The primary outcome was achievement of T2T
metrics as per week 12-16 goals, which included achievement of one clinician-reported outcome 
(
Eczema Area
and Severity Index [EASI] improvement of 
≥
-50% [EASI50] or Investigator Global Assessment [IGA] score 
≤
2
)
 and
achievement of one ; patient-reported outcome 
(
reduction in Worst Pruritus Numeric Rating Scale [WP-NRS] 
≥
3,
reduction in Dermatology Life Quality Index [DLQI] 
≥
4, or reduction in Patient Oriented Eczema Measure [POEM]
≥
3
)
. A nonresponder imputation 
(
NRI
)
 analysis was used to account for missing data. Safety was assessed via
incidence of adverse events 
(
AEs
)
.
Results:
This analysis included a total of 222 patients. The mean age was 45 
(
range: 12-79
)
 years, with 49.1% 
(
109/222
)
being male. At weeks 16±8: T2T metrics were achieved by 79.7% 
(
177/222
)
 of patients in total. Clinician-reported
T2T outcomes 
(
EASI50 and/or IGA 
≤
2
)
 were achieved by 91.4% 
(
203/222
)
 of patients, while patient-reported T2T
outcomes 
(
WP-NRS improvement 
≥
3, DLQI improvement 
≥
4, or POEM improvement 
≥
3
)
 were achieved by
80.6% 
(
179/222
)
.
In total, 40 treatment-emergent adverse events 
(
TEAEs
)
 occurred 
(
18%, 40/222
)
. The most common AEs were
elevated creatine kinase 
(
2.7%, 6/222
)
, elevated triglycerides 
(
2.3%, 5/222
)
, acne 
(
1.4%, 3/222
)
, folliculitis 
(
1.4%,
3/222
)
, herpes simplex 
(
1.4%, 3/222
)
, and myalgia/arthragia 
(
1.4%, 3/222
)
. Six treatment discontinuations 
(
2.7%
)
were noted 
(
AEs; myalgia/arthralgia [n=2], new-onset lung malignancy [n=1], uveitis [n=1], and venous
thromboembolism [n=1]
)
. No serious infections, hypersensitivity reactions, major adverse cardiac events, or
hepatic abnormalities were observed during the treatment period analyzed.
Conclusion:
Our real-world effectiveness outcomes indicate that the majority of patients with AD achieve both clinician- and
patient-reported Canadian T2T goals at weeks 16±8 with upadacitinib treatment. Study limitations include its

retrospective nature and a lack of real-world documentation of POEM scores.
