Introduction & Objectives:
 Absence of flares 
(
worsening of disease requiring escalation of treatment
)
 in patients
with atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a top priority for patients and physicians. Serum CCL17/TARC is a type 2 biomarker
that reflects AD severity. We report the association between baseline serum CCL17 levels and subsequent flares in
patients with AD treated with dupilumab monotherapy for 1 year across multiple dose regimens or those
rerandomized to placebo for 9 months following 16 weeks of dupilumab every 2 weeks 
(
q2w
)
.
Materials & Methods:
 In this post hoc analysis, adults with moderate-to-severe AD who received dupilumab 300
mg q2w in the LIBERTY AD SOLO 1/2 trials 
(
NCT02277743/NCT02277769
)
 and achieved an optimal response of
Investigator
’
s Global Assessment 0/1 and/or 
≥
75% reduction from baseline in Eczema Area and Severity Index at
Week 16, without occurrence of flare 
(
n = 199/428
)
, were rerandomized in SOLO-CONTINUE 
(
NCT02395133
)
 for
an additional 36 weeks to dupilumab 300 mg monotherapy 
(
q2w [n = 80], every 4 weeks [q4w, n = 41], every 8
weeks [q8w, n = 39]
)
 or placebo 
(
n = 39
)
. This analysis reports median baseline serum CCL17 levels prior to
initiating dupilumab treatment in SOLO 1/2. Data are presented as observed for patients who experienced 
≥
1 flare
or no flares during SOLO-CONTINUE.
Results:
 Median baseline CCL17 levels were 1.5
–
3.6 times greater in patients who subsequently experienced 
≥
1
flare during the maintenance phase vs patients who experienced no flares, regardless of maintenance regimen
(
Figure
)
. Safety was consistent with the known dupilumab safety profile.
Conclusion:
 In this analysis, baseline serum CCL17 level was a robust predictive biomarker of flares in patients
with AD.
