Abstract N°: 5707
Healthcare Service Utilization and Medication Use in 128,239 Pediatric Patients with Atopic Dermatitis-
Insights From a Cross-Sectional Case-Control Study in Israel
Naama Cohen
1
, Amit Iton-Schwartz
2
, Daniel Mimouni
2, 3
, Yulia Valdman-Grinshpoun
4, 5
1
Bar-Ilan University, Azrieli Faculty of Medicine, Safed, Israel
2
Rabin Medical Center, Division of Dermatology, Petah Tikva, Israel
3
Tel Aviv University Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences, Tel Aviv-Yafo, Israel
4
Ben Gurion University of the Negev, Department of Dermatology, Be
’
er Sheva, Israel
5
Ben Gurion University of the Negev, Be
’
er Sheva, Israel
Introduction & Objective:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disease, affecting both children and adult patients, causing a
substantial physiological, mental and social burden. Treatment for AD includes topical treatments such as
corticosteroids, calcineurin inhibitors, and phosphodiesterase-4 inhibitors, as well as systemic treatments like
immune-modulating agents and biologics. The introduction of biologics and Janus-kinase 
(
JAK
)
 inhibitors has
been a milestone in AD treatment options, for both adult and pediatric patients. Past research has suggested an
increase in healthcare service uptake among adults, due to the introduction of biologics and JAK inhibitors.
Understanding healthcare service utilization and medication use in children with AD is needed to ensure the
effective integration of these treatments into clinical practice. This study aims to investigate healthcare service
utilization and medication use in children with AD during 2024.
Results:
The study included 128,239 children with AD and 128,239 matched controls. The mean age of children with AD
was 9.7 years; 18% of the participants were 0-4 years old, 30.4% were 5-9 years old, 30.1% were 10-14 years old
and 21.6% were 15-18 years old. 47.4% of patients were female. Children with AD had increased health care
utilization compared to control group, with higher rates of visits to pediatricians, general practitioners 
(
GPs
)
, and
dermatologists. 144 children 
(
0.11%
)
 with AD were treated by immunosuppressive drugs as compared to 78
(
0.06%
)
 of the control group 
(
OR 1.8, 95% CI 1.4- 2.4, p-value <0.001
)
. 410 children 
(
0.32%
)
 were treated with
biologic drugs as compared to 12 
(
0.01%
)
 of the control group 
(
OR 34.3, 95% CI 19.3- 60.9, p-value <0.001
)
. 34
children 
(
0.03%
)
 were treated with JAK inhibitors as compared to 2 children 
(
0.002%
)
 of the control group 
(
OR 17,
95% CI 4.1- 70.8, p-value <0.001
)
. 11,062 children 
(
8.6%
)
 were treated with systemic corticosteroids as compared
to 8,300 
(
6.5%
)
 in the control group 
(
OR 1.4, 95% CI 1.3- 1.4, p-value <0.001
)
.
Conclusion:
During the year 2024, a higher utilization of health care services 
(
e.g. pediatricians, GPs and dermatologists
’
 visits
)
was observed in pediatric patients with AD compared to the control group. As only a small number of children
with AD received immunosuppressants, biologic treatments and JAK inhibitors, we suggest that the use of
systemic medications should be considered in pediatric patients with moderate to severe AD.
