Abstract N°: 5729
Impact of a Novel Topical Cream on Facial Skin Temperature and Redness Appearance Following Infrared
Irradiation: A Split-Face Clinical Study
Ana Du
1
, Lily Jiang
2
1
L
’
Oreal Research and Innovation, Clark, United States
2
SGS Testing & Control Services, Singapore, Singapore
Introduction & Objectives:
Elevated skin temperature can be a significant factor in various inflammatory skin conditions, including eczema,
rosacea, and contact dermatitis, as well as a common consequence of exposure to environmental stressors like
sun, wind, and extreme temperatures. This heightened temperature can contribute to discomfort and redness.
While long-term management of these conditions requires addressing underlying causes, providing temporary
relief from heat-induced discomfort is a crucial aspect of patient care and overall skin comfort1,2. This study
investigates the efficacy of a fresh, lightweight, oil-in-water facial skincare product containing skin soothing
ingredients such as colloidal oatmeal, bisabolol, and panthenol in reducing skin temperature and reducing
temperature related erythema upon application.
Materials & Methods:
This single-center, evaluator-blinded, split-face, randomized, controlled clinical trial enrolled 25 women aged 18-
60 with at least half of the panel with self-reported sensitive skin. Following a baseline assessment of facial skin
temperature using infrared thermography 
(
IRT
)
 and redness appearance captured using VISIA-CR imaging
(
Canfield, Parsippany, NJ, USA
)
, subjects underwent controlled IR irradiation 
(
PHILIPS InfraCare 3000 series
infrared physiotherapy lamps, Koninklijke Philips N.V., Amsterdam, Netherlands
)
 to elevate skin surface
temperature to 42 ± 2°C. Subsequently, the topical cream was applied to one randomized half of the face, while
the other half served as an untreated control. IRT and VISIA-CR imaging were repeated immediately post-IR
irradiation and post-product application. Skin temperature data from 30 locations on each side of the face were
analyzed. Imaging analysis of redness using a* value was analyzed using the VISIA-CR captured images.
Results:
IR irradiation significantly increased bilateral facial skin temperature, maintaining a 
≤
1°C inter-side difference
(
p>0.05
)
. Topical cream application immediately reduced both temperature and redness 
(
a* value
)
 compared to
both post-irradiation and untreated control sides 
(
p<0.001
)
.
Conclusion:
This oil-in-water cream, formulated with colloidal oatmeal, bisabolol, and panthenol, effectively and rapidly
reduced both skin temperature and redness following IR-induced heat. These findings suggest that this topical
cream may offer valuable relief from heat-induced skin discomfort associated with various factors, including post-
aesthetic procedures, and has the potential to be a beneficial adjunct in managing inflammatory skin conditions
and sensitivity exacerbated by elevated skin temperature.
