Introduction & Objectives:
The prevalence of atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 in elderly patients is increasing. However, its management remains
challenging due to atypical clinical phenotypes, treatment resistance, and comorbidities. Janus kinase 
(
JAK
)
inhibitors, abrocitinib, baricitinib, and upadacitinib, have emerged as promising therapeutic options. Nevertheless,
evidence in elderly populations is scarce due to their underrepresentation in clinical trials and safety concerns. This
multicenter, real-life study aimed to evaluate the efficacy and safety of JAK inhibitors in patients aged 
≥
60 years
with moderate-to-severe AD.
Materials & Methods:
A 52-week, multicenter, real-life study was performed enrolling patients aged 
≥
60 years affected by moderate-to-
severe AD undergoing treatment with JAK inhibitors for at least 16 weeks across 16 dermatological centers in Italy.
Disease severity was assessed at baseline, week 
(
W
)
4, 16, 24, and 52 using the Eczema Area and Severity Index
(
EASI
)
, Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
, and Pruritus-Numerical Rating Scale 
(
P-NRS
)
. Adverse events 
(
AEs
)
were recorded at each follow-up.

Results:
 Seventy-two patients 
(
mean age 68.83 ± 6.95 years; 50% male
)
 were included [abrocitinib: n=13
(
18.06%
)
; baricitinib: n=6 
(
8.33%
)
; upadacitinib: n=53 
(
73.61%
)
]. All completed W16 follow-up, with 33 
(
45.83%
)
and 26 
(
36.11%
)
 patients available at W24 and W52, respectively. Baseline scores were: EASI 21.22 ± 10.38, DLQI
18.21 ± 7.33, and P-NRS 7.84 ± 1.94. Statistically significant improvements were observed from W4 onwards 
(
W52:
EASI 0.81 ± 1.27; DLQI 0.31 ± 0.63; P-NRS 0.42 ± 1.03; p<0.0001 for all
)
. No treatment discontinuations or dose
adjustments due to AEs were reported. No significant efficacy or safety differences were observed among
treatment groups.
Conclusion:
 JAK inhibitors demonstrated significant efficacy and an acceptable safety profile in elderly patients
with moderate-to-severe AD. These findings support their use as a valuable therapeutic option in selected elderly
individuals, particularly when other systemic treatments are contraindicated or ineffective.
