Abstract N°: 5820
Efficacy and safety of dupilumab in a case series of transplanted patients with severe atopic dermatitis.
Shared experience from several hospitals in Spain.
Raquel Dominguez-Lopez*
1
, Mónica Munera Campos
2
, Francisco Navarro Triviño
3
, Trinidad Montero Vilchez
4
,
Constanza Riquelme Mc Loughlin
5
, Rubén Barros Tornay
6
, Enrique Gómez de la Fuente
7
1
Hospital Universitario Ramón y Cajal, Madrid, Spain
2
Hospital Germans Trias i Pujol, Badalona, Spain
3
Hospital Universitario Clínico San Cecilio, Granada, Spain
4
Virgen de las Nieves University Hospital, Granada, Spain
5
Hospital Clínic de Barcelona, Barcelona, Spain
6
Hospital Universitario de Cabueñes , Gijón, Spain
7
Ramón y Cajal Hospital, Madrid, Spain
Introduction & Objectives:
New therapies represent a significant advance in the management of atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
. Dupilumab, a
monoclonal antibody targeting IL-4 and IL-13 signaling, was the first approved targeted therapy for AD, inhibiting
Th2-mediated inflammation
However, data on its safety and efficacy in immunosuppressed patients, especially solid organ transplant
recipients, remain limited. We present a multicenter case series of transplanted patients with AD treated with
dupilumab along with a literature review.
Materials & Methods:
We conducted a retrospective, multicenter case series through an email-based survey of dermatologists from the
GEIDAC 
(
Spanish Group for Research on Contact Dermatitis and Cutaneous Allergy
)
. Voluntarily reported cases
were analyzed descriptively, without a standardized protocol.
Results:
Nine patients 
(
8 men, 1 woman
)
 with a median age of 35 years were included. Eight of them had undergone
kidney transplantation and 1 heart transplantation. All were on immunosuppressive therapy to prevent rejection.
Patients exhibited widespread eczematous and intensely pruritic lesions involving nearly the entire body. Three
patients had been diagnosed with AD during childhood, and six during adulthood. Elevated baseline serum IgE
levels were observed in three cases.
Prior therapies included topical corticosteroids 
(
all
)
, systemic steroids, calcineurin inhibitors, or phototherapy.
Baseline scores were recorded before starting treatment, with mean values of EASI 30, pruritus NRS 9, sleep NRS 8,
and DLQI 20.
Treatment with dupilumab was well tolerated. Only one patient developed mild conjunctivitis and facial dermatitis,
managed with topical treatment. Clinical response was excellent in most patients, with significant lesion clearance.
One case was discontinued due to lack of efficacy. Those who did not achieve EASI 0 and IGA 0 still showed
significant improvement, reaching EASI 75 and IGA 1. None showed deterioration of renal function or transplant-
related complications.

Conclusion:
Our findings align with previously reported cases 
(
n=15
)
, showing consistent efficacy and safety of dupilumab in
transplant recipients, regardless of the age, organ transplanted, its duration or immunosuppressive regimen. No
graft rejection occurred, nor was adjustment of immunosuppressive treatment required.
These results support the hypothesis that dupilumab can be safely used in patients with maintained
immunosuppression.
From an immunological perspective, preferential inhibition of the Th1 pathway induced by post-transplant
immunosuppression may promote a shift towards the Th2 pathway, contributing to the development or
exacerbation of AD.
By specifically blocking the Th2 pathway, dupilumab could partially restore this immunological balance and offer a
safe and effective treatment for patients with AD and post-transplant immunosuppression.
EADV Congress 2025, PARIS
17 SEPTEMBER - 20 SEPTEMBER 2025 
POWERED BY M-ANAGE.COM 


Abstract N°: 5850
Global Trends and Modifiable Risk Factors for Atopic Dermatitis Incidence: Insights from GBD 2021
Yi Xiao*
1
, minxue shen
1
, Juan Su
1
, Xiang Chen
1
1
Department of Dermatology, Xiangya Hospital, Central South University, Changsha, China
Introduction & Objectives:
Global patterns in atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 incidence and their associations with modifiable risk factors remain
unclear. This study aims to analyze global trends in AD incidence and identify associated socioeconomic,
environmental, and lifestyle factors contributing to its global disparities and epidemics.
Materials & Methods:
Data on AD in 204 countries and territories from1990 to 2021 were extracted from the Global Burden of Diseases
(
GBD
)
 Study 2021. Age-standardized incidence rate 
(
ASIR
)
 were calculated by sex and region. We used restricted
cubic splines to analyzed the relationship between the ASIR and socioeconomic and modifiable risk factors which
were measured by summary exposure values 
(
SEVs
)
, including high body mass index 
(
BMI
)
, physical inactivity, air
pollution, and poor dietary habits.
Results:
In 2021, 16.0 million new cases of AD were recorded globally, with the highest ASIRs in High-income Asia Pacific
(
474.8 per 100,000 population
)
 and Western Europe 
(
421.7 per 100,000 population
)
 geographically and higher
ASIRs in women. Socioeconomic development showed a strongly positive correlation with AD incidence 
(
TABLE 1;
FIG 1
)
.


The age distribution showed two major peaks: < 5 years and 75-79 years, with a 
“
W
”
 pattern in ASIR across the
lifespan. Among children 
(
0-19 years
)
, high-SDI regions had a higher EAPC, while lower SDI regions showed
higher EAPCs in older age groups 
(
FIG 2
)
.
Among modifiable risk factors, high BMI, low physical activity, and nitrogen dioxide pollution performed positive
associations with AD risk. Diets rich in sugar-sweetened beverages, processed meat, and sodium and diet low in
whole grains further increased risk 
(
FIG 3
)
.
When countries were categorized into high-SDI and low- to middle-SDI groups, we observed that the positive
correlation between BMI and incidence was more stable in low- to middle-income countries 
(
FIG 4
)
.
Conclusion:
Global disparities in AD incidence trends are closely linked to socioeconomic development and modifiable risk
factors, including obesity, air pollution, and unhealthy diets. Addressing these factors may help mitigate the global

burden of AD, particularly in industrialized and rapidly developing regions. Addressing these factors through
targeted public health policies is essential to mitigating the global burden of AD, particularly in industrialized and
rapidly developing regions.
