Abstract N°: 5865
An integrative framework for polygenic prediction of atopic dermatitis in UK Biobank
Charalabos Antonatos
1
, Katerina Grafanaki*
2
, Ashley Budu-Aggrey
3
, Alexandros Pontikas
1
, Adam Akritidis
1
,
Efstathia Pasmatzi
2
, Aikaterini Tsiogka
4
, Stamatis Gregoriou
4
, Lavinia Paternoster
3
, Yiannis Vasilopoulos
1
1
University of Patras, Department of Biology, Laboratory of Genetics, Patras, Greece
2
University Hospital of Patras, School of Health Sciences, Department of Dermatology - Venereology, Patras,
Greece
3
Bristol Medical School, University of Bristol, MRC Integrative Epidemiology Unit 
(
IEU
)
 , Population Health
Sciences, Bristol, United Kingdom
4
Andreas Syggros Hospital, National and Kapodistrian University of Athens, Department of Dermatology -
Venereology, Athens, Greece
Introduction & Objectives:
 Polygenic risk scores 
(
PRS
)
 offer valuable insights into the genetic architecture of
complex diseases such as atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
, but their predictive utility remains limited. Integrating gene
expression data into PRS construction may improve prediction accuracy and deepen understanding of disease
biology.
Materials & Methods:
 In this study, we developed polygenic transcriptome risk scores 
(
PTRSs
)
 derived from AD-
enriched tissues and evaluated their performance against traditional PRS models and a baseline risk model
incorporating eosinophil and lymphocyte counts in the prediction of AD. We conducted transcriptome-wide
association studies 
(
TWAS
)
 using the PrediXcan framework to construct tissue-specific PTRSs. Risk score
performance was assessed in 256888 Europeans 
(
10,816 cases
)
 and validated in an independent cohort of 64152
Europeans 
(
2669 cases
)
 from the UK Biobank.
Results:
 We observed a modest correlation between PRS and PTRS, exerting independent effects on AD risk.
While PRS demonstrated superior predictive performance compared to single-tissue PTRSs, combining both
models significantly enhanced prediction accuracy, yielding a c-statistic of 0.646 
(
95% confidence intervals: 0.634
–
0.656
)
. Notably, tissue-specific PTRSs revealed stronger associations with baseline risk factors, where Eppstein-Bar
virus 
(
EBV
)
-transformed lymphocytes and unexposed skin PTRSs tissues reported positive associations with
lymphocyte counts.
Conclusion:
 To our knowledge, this is the first attempt to compute PRSs in UK Biobank for AD, further enhancing
clinical risk factors and a novel approach for transcriptome-based risk scores. Our integrative approach
underscores the value of incorporating biological information like tissue-specific gene expression to refine
polygenic prediction models and elucidate the complex genetic architecture of AD.
