Abstract N°: 5953
Efficacy and safety of lebrikizumab in combination with topical corticosteroids for moderate-to-severe
atopic dermatitis: A systematic review and meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials
Laura Ghanem*
1
, Jacinthe Khater
1
, Antonia Moubarak
1
, Rama Daoud
1
, Mariam Harb
2
, Fouad El Sayed
2
1
Faculty of Medical Sciences, Lebanese University, Beirut, Lebanon
2
Dermatology, Faculty of Medical Sciences, Lebanese University, Beirut, Lebanon
Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic skin inflammation influenced by genetic,
immunological, and environmental factors. While topical corticosteroids 
(
TCS
)
 are the primary treatment, long-
term use can cause side effects. Lebrikizumab, an anti-IL-13 therapy, has shown promising results as monotherapy.
This meta-analysis evaluates the real-world efficacy and safety of combining lebrikizumab with TCS for AD.
Materials & Methods:
 This systematic review and meta-analysis, registered in PROSPERO 
(
CRD420251017921
)
,
followed the Cochrane and PRISMA guidelines. We searched PubMed, Embase, and Cochrane for randomized
controlled trials 
(
RCTs
)
 comparing lebrikizumab with TCS to placebo with TCS in moderate-to-severe AD. Primary
endpoints: Investigators
’
 Global Assessment 
(
IGA
)
 0/1, Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
75, and 
≥
1
treatment-emergent adverse event 
(
TEAE
)
. Secondary endpoints: a 4-point improvement in pruritus numerical
rating scale 
(
NRS
)
, mean change in NRS, patient-oriented eczema measure 
(
POEM
)
 scores, and sleep-loss scale. A
Der Simonian-Laird random-effects model was used in RStudio.
Results:
 We included 1284 patients from 5 RCTs. Lebrikizumab with TCS group significantly improved the
likelihood of achieving EASI75 compared to placebo with TCS group 
(
RR 1.74; 95% CI 1.50-2.01; p<0.001;
I²=50.5%
)
. It also increased the probability of IGA0/1 
(
RR 1.94; 95% CI 1.56-2.41; p<0.001; I²=38.6%
)
 and a 4-point
improvement in pruritus NRS 
(
TCS 
(
RR 1.68; 95% CI 1.36-2.06; p<0.001; I2=41.0%
)
. Lebrikizumab with TCS showed
greater improvement in pruritus NRS 
(
SMD -0.39; 95% CI -0.53 - -0.25; I²=6.4%
)
, POEM 
(
SMD -5.10; 95% CI -7.06
- -3.14; I²=71.0%
)
, and sleep-loss scale 
(
SMD -0.41; 95% CI -0.61 - -0.20; I²=51.5%
)
. No significant difference in
TEAEs was observed 
(
RR 1.09; 95% CI 0.96-1.23; I²=0.0%
)
. Leave-one-out analysis identified ADhere-J as the main
source of heterogeneity. Subgroup analysis for EASI75, IGA0/1, and TEAEs showed no significant difference by
dosage regimen, indicating the need for further trials.
Conclusion:
 This meta-analysis suggests that lebrikizumab with TCS does significantly improve moderate-to-
severe AD, without a significant increase in adverse events.

