Abstract N°: 6052
Increased Risk of Migraine in Patients with Atopic Dermatitis: A Population-Based Cohort Study Using the
TriNetX US Collaborative Network
Hui-Chin Chang
1
, Shuo-Yan Gau*
1, 2
1
Chung Shan Medical University Hosptial, Evidence-Based Medicine Center, Taichung, Taiwan
2
National Taiwan University, Taipei, Taiwan
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
, a chronic inflammatory skin disease, has been increasingly associated with systemic
comorbidities, including neurological disorders. This study aimed to investigate the risk of developing migraine in
patients with AD using a large real-world dataset.
Materials & Methods:
We conducted a retrospective cohort study using the TriNetX US Collaborative Network, comprising electronic
health records from over 70 million patients. Individuals with a diagnosis of AD were identified and compared to
non-AD controls using multiple analytic models. Propensity score matching was employed to control for potential
confounders, including age, sex, race, BMI, socioeconomic and psychosocial health risks, healthcare utilization,
comorbidities 
(
hypertension, hyperlipidemia, diabetes mellitus
)
, and comedications 
(
acetaminophen and non-
steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs
)
. Various sensitivity analyses were performed to assess the robustness of the
association, including adjustments for different matching covariates, wash-out periods, follow-up durations, and
AD case definitions based on claims algorithms and treatment regimens.
Results:
AD patients exhibited a consistently elevated risk of developing migraine across all models. In crude models, the
hazard ratio 
(
HR
)
 for migraine was 1.946 
(
95% CI: 1.906
–
1.988
)
, increasing to 2.040 
(
95% CI: 1.967
–
2.116
)
 after
adjusting for age and sex. With stricter wash-out periods of 24 and 36 months, the HRs were 2.097 
(
95% CI: 2.008
–
2.190
)
 and 2.123 
(
95% CI: 2.028
–
2.222
)
, respectively. When varying the follow-up time to 5 and 15 years, the HRs
remained elevated at 2.087 
(
95% CI: 1.986
–
2.193
)
 and 1.996 
(
95% CI: 1.920
–
2.074
)
, respectively. Alternative AD
definitions using more stringent diagnostic and treatment criteria also demonstrated increased migraine risks, with
HRs of 2.014 
(
95% CI: 1.770
–
2.292
)
 and 2.253 
(
95% CI: 2.142
–
2.370
)
.
Conclusion:
Patients with atopic dermatitis have a significantly increased risk of developing migraine. These findings highlight
the need for comprehensive care and interdisciplinary management strategies in patients with AD to address
potential neurological comorbidities.

