, Woan-Ruoh Lee
6
,
Nan-Lin Wu
7
, Cheng-Che Lan
8
, Han-Fang Liao
9
1
Department of Dermatology, National Taiwan University Hospital and National Taiwan University College of
Medicine, Taipei, Taiwan
2
Department of Dermatology, Chang Gung Memorial Hospital, Taipei, Taiwan
3
Department of Dermatology, Tri-Service General Hospital, National Defense Medical Center, Taipei, Taiwan
4
Department of Dermatology, Taipei Veterans General Hospital and Department of Dermatology, National Yang
Ming Chiao Tung University, Taipei, Taiwan
5
Department of Dermatology, Kaohsiung Chang Gung Memorial Hospital and Chang Gung University College of
Medicine , Kaohsiung, Taiwan
6
Department of Dermatology, School of Medicine, College of Medicine, Taipei Medical University and Graduate
Institute of Medical Sciences, Taipei Medical University, Taipei, Taiwan
7
Department of Dermatology, MacKay Memorial Hospital Taipei, Taiwan; Department of Medicine, MacKay
Medical College, New Taipei City, Taiwan
8
Department of Dermatology, Kaohsiung Medical University Hospital and College of Medicine, Kaohsiung Medical
University, Kaohsiung, Taiwan
9
Pfizer Ltd., Taipei, Taiwan
Introduction & Objectives:
The ATTRACT 
(
Abrocitinib Taiwan TReatment Pattern and Real-World sTudy in ATopiC Dermatitis
)
 registry was
established to explore the real-world treatment patterns and clinical outcomes of abrocitinib 
(
a selective JAK1
inhibitor
)
 in moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 in Taiwan. We sought to conduct an interim analysis of the
ATTRACT registry, for patients enrolled between June 19, 2024, and October 31, 2024, with a data cut-off on
November 30, 2024.
Materials & Methods:
This prospective, multicenter study is conducted across 8 medical centers in Taiwan. It enrolled patients aged 
≥
12
years with moderate-to-severe AD newly prescribed abrocitinib. Data on demographics, clinical features,
treatment history, and physician/patient-reported outcomes are collected. Adverse events were also recorded at
each participating site through standard AE reporting procedures as the evaluation of safety is not a primary
objective of this registry. Recruitment started in June 2024, and data collection will continue for a minimum of 12
months and up to 24-month observation period per participant.
Results:
As of November 2024, 55 patients were enrolled from 8 sites in Taiwan 
(
70.9% male
)
, with a mean age of 40.8 ±
20.2 years and a mean age at initial AD diagnosis of 31.8 ± 24.7 years. Median follow-up was 72 days 
(
IQR: 43
–
102
)
. At the data cutoff, all patients had completed the baseline visit; and among those eligible for each timepoint,
41.8% had reached week 2, 76.4% had reached week 4, and 27.3% had reached week 12. Prior treatments
comprised topical agents 
(
41.8%
)
, systemic agents 
(
30.9%
)
, dupilumab 
(
10.9%
)
, phototherapy 
(
5.5%
)
, and JAK

inhibitors 
(
upadacitinib: 3.6%; baricitinib: 1.8%
)
. Two of six dupilumab treated patients and two of three JAK
inhibitor treated patients discontinued prior therapy due to lack of efficacy. Majority of the patients 
(
83.6%
)
received a 200 mg dose and self-paid their abrocitinib therapy 
(
83.6%
)
, while 16.4% were covered by national
health insurance. At baseline, the mean Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
 score was 16.0 ± 11.4. By week 2,
the mean EASI had declined to 6.7 ± 5.4, with a further reduction to 4.4 ± 5.9 at week 4, and 4.4 ± 5.7 at week 12
(
72.4% reduction at week 12 from baseline
)
. The mean Peak Pruritus Numerical Rating Scale 
(
PP-NRS
)
 was 6.5 ±
3.0 at baseline. By week 2, PP-NRS decreased to 3.9 ± 3.2, with 27.8% achieving a PPNRS score 0/1. At week 4, the
mean fell further to 3.0 ± 2.8, and 35.7% patients reached a PPNRS 0/1. By week 12, the mean PPNRS was 2.3 ± 2.6
with 64.5% reduction from baseline, and 46.7% patients achieving a score of 0/1 
(
Figure 1
)
. Table 1 summarises
the improvements in various effectiveness measures from Baseline to Week 12. By the data cutoff, three patients
experienced adverse events
—
two reported nausea and one reported upper abdominal pain
—
consistent with
previously documented safety profiles. No new safety signals emerged during this interim analysis.
Conclusion:
These interim findings from the ongoing ATTRACT registry in Taiwan indicate that majority of patients with
moderate-to-severe AD initiating abrocitinib had significant disease improvement. The rapid improvements
observed across multiple clinical and patient-reported outcomes support the effectiveness abrocitinib in routine
clinical practice. Nevertheless, extended follow-up periods and larger patient cohorts are essential to validate the
durability of response and to thoroughly characterize the safety profile of abrocitinib.


 
 
