Introduction & Objectives:
Background
 Maternal gestational weight gain has been associated with childhood atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
, while
the role of maternal pre-pregnancy body mass index 
(
ppBMI
)
, fetal and newborn anthropometric measurements
in AD development is not clear.
Objective
The aims were to determine primarily if maternal ppBMI, secondarily anthropometric measurements in the
newborn and thirdly fetal thoracal and abdominal circumference growth from mid-pregnancy were associated
with offspring AD by three years of age.
Materials & Methods:
The present study comprised 2107 mother-child pairs 
(
54% boys
)
 from the Scandinavian general population-
based prospective Preventing Atopic Dermatitis and Allergy in Children 
(
PreventADALL
)
 cohort study with

available information on maternal weight and AD by three years in the child 
(
Figure 1
)
. Non-selected pregnant
women were enrolled during the general ultrasound examination around gestational age 18 and their babies
included at birth 
(
born from week 35 and onwards
)
. Maternal ppBMI was calculated from electronic questionnaire
information at enrolment, using the WHO classification of BMI 
(
kg/m2
)
 of normal weight 
(
18.5-25; reference
)
,
underweight 
(
<18.5
)
, overweight 
(
25-30
)
 and obese 
(
>30
)
. Anthropometric measures at midpregnancy in the
fetus included thoracal and abdominal circumference as well as calculated weight by Combs formula. Additionally,
we measured left upper arm circumference and length in the newborn. Fetal growth was calculated as fetal weight
gain, as well as thoracal and abdominal circumference growth per day from midpregnancy ultrasound
examination until birth 
(
newborn
)
.
Atopic dermatitis was defined as fulfilling either UK Working Party or Hanfin & Rajka diagnostic criteria at any
time-point based on clinical assessments at 3, 6, 12, 24 and 36 months of age and phone interview at 36 months.
Anthropometric measurements were used as continuous as well as categorical values 
(
10th, 50th and 90th
percentiles
)
. Risk of AD was analyzed by univariable and multivariable logistic regression for ppBMI as continuous
and categorical variable, and as continuous data for anthropometric measures adjusting for relevant confounders
including maternal education, ppBMI and AD.
Results:
Atopic dermatitis was diagnosed in 525/2107 
(
25%
)
 children by three years of age. Maternal ppBMI was within the
normal range in 1637 
(
78%
)
, while 326 
(
2%
)
 were underweight, 326 
(
16%
)
 were overweight and 113 
(
5%
)
 were
obese. Increasing ppBMI was significantly associated with increased risk of offspring AD by three years 
(
adjusted
odds ratio 
(
aOR
)
 
(
95% CI
)
 1.03
(
1.00-1.06
)
, p=0.043
)
. However, BMI categories were not significantly associated
with offspring AD 
(
Figure 2A
)
. Birth length 
(
mean 
(
SD
)
 50.5 
(
2.0
)
 cm.
)
 was associated with AD by 3 years, with low
birth length 
(
<48 cm
)
 conferring a significant decreased risk of offspring AD 
((
aOR
)(
95% CI
)
 0.71
(
0.51-1.0
)
p=0.048
)(
Figure 2B
)
.Birth weight 
(
mean 
(
SD
)
 3.6 
(
0.5
))
 kg was not associated with AD by 3 years of age, nor were
thoracal, abdominal or upper arm circumference 
(
Figure 2C
)
. Neither fetal weight gain nor fetal thoracal and
abdominal growth from midpregnancy were associated with AD by three years.
Conclusion:
Increasing ppBMI conferred a higher risk, while lower birth length reduced the risk of offspring AD by three years
of age.


