Abstract N°: 6175
Kaposi
’
s Varioliform Eruption in Severe Atopic Dermatitis: A Case of Successful Management with a Janus
Kinase Inhibitor
Emanuel Chew
1
, Alicia Lemini
1
, Yazmin Vega
1
, Regina Corona Franco
1
, Miguel Angel Palomar Ortiz
1
1
Instituto Mexicano del Seguro Social, Dermatology, México, Mexico
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis is a chronic inflammatory disease that, until recently, was primarily managed with general
treatments that were often ineffective in severe cases. Recently, several targeted therapies have been developed,
focusing on specific pathways involved in its pathophysiology. This case report describes a patient with severe
atopic dermatitis who developed Kaposi
’
s varioliform eruption, treated successfully with a Janus kinase inhibitor.
Objective:
 To describe the use of a Janus kinase inhibitor in the treatment of severe atopic dermatitis and its
impact on disease management.
Materials & Methods:
We present the case of a 33-year-old female patient with severe atopic dermatitis and Kaposi
’
s varioliform
eruption who attended our clinic within the past year.
Results:
The patient, with a history of allergic rhinitis, atopic dermatitis, hypothyroidism, and Castleman disease, was
undergoing treatment with rituximab and dexamethasone. She consulted the dermatology service due to the
development of a dermatosis over a four-day period. The dermatosis involved all four body segments, with a
predominance on the face. It was characterized by multiple vesicles on an erythematous base, clinically consistent
with Kaposi
’
s varioliform eruption. She was treated with acyclovir, leading to resolution of the lesion.
Despite prior treatments with calcineurin inhibitors, corticosteroids, and folic acid antagonists, the patient
continued to experience poor control of her atopic dermatitis. As a result, treatment with a Janus kinase inhibitor
(
JAK1 and JAK2
)
 was initiated. In the following weeks, she developed a perioral herpes simplex lesion, which was
managed with acyclovir. Notably, the patient showed significant improvement in eczema, pruritus, and a reduction
in quality of life, as measured by the Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
.
Conclusion:
Kaposi
’
s varioliform eruption is a cutaneous manifestation that can occur in patients with atopic dermatitis,
particularly during acute flare-ups. It is characterized by vesicular lesions and erythematous papules, which may
progress to ulcers, typically affecting the face and trunk.
Current treatment strategies for atopic dermatitis focus on alleviating symptoms and reducing flare-ups. While
topical therapies remain the cornerstone of management, systemic treatments are recommended for patients who
do not achieve adequate control with first-line therapies.
Advances in the understanding of molecular mechanisms have facilitated the development of more targeted
therapies for atopic dermatitis. However, the high cost and limited accessibility of these therapies present
significant challenges. Personalized treatments may offer a promising approach to improving disease

management and outcomes in the future.
