Introduction & Objectives:
Chronic Hand Eczema 
(
CHE
)
 is a long-term, multifactorial, inflammatory skin disease of the hands and wrists. Pain
is frequently reported by patients as the most burdensome symptom of CHE, arising due to a combination of
inflammation and cracking/fissuring of the skin. Greater pain severity may be associated with poor sleep, anxiety,
depression, and lower health-related quality of life 
(
HRQoL
)
. This 
post hoc
 analysis aimed to evaluate the effect of
treatment with the pan-Janus kinase inhibitor delgocitinib cream on HRQoL in patients with CHE characterised by
a high severity of baseline pain.
Materials & Methods:
DELTA 1 
(
NCT04871711
)
 and DELTA 2 
(
NCT04872101
)
 were phase 3 trials of identical design. In both trials, adult
patients with moderate to severe CHE were randomised 
(
2:1
)
 to double-blind treatment with delgocitinib cream
20 mg/g or cream vehicle twice daily for 16 weeks. Data were pooled from both trials with the Hand Eczema
Symptom Diary 
(
HESD
)
 used to assess patient-reported baseline pain on a 0
–
10 numeric rating scale. Patients with
severe pain were defined as those with a HESD pain baseline score 
≥
7. The proportions of patients with severe
pain achieving clinically meaningful improvements in HESD, HESD pain, EuroQol 5-dimension 
(
EQ-5D
)
,
Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
, Hand Eczema Impact Scale 
(
HEIS
)
, HEIS Proximal Daily Activity Limitations
(
PDAL
)
, and HEIS Embarrassment scores at 16 weeks were compared across treatments.
Results:
Across the two trials, 50% of patients 
(
n=473/955
)
 randomised to treatment had CHE characterised by severe pain
(
baseline HESD 
≥
7: delgocitinib cream, n=315; cream vehicle, n=158
)
. Compared to patients with baseline HESD
<7, patients with severe pain were more likely to have severe CHE as assessed by Investigators Global Assessment-
CHE 
(
37% vs. 20%
)
 with worse HRQoL scores. At 16 weeks, reductions in HESD and HESD pain scores were
significantly greater 
(
p<0.001
)
 with delgocitinib cream versus cream vehicle in patients with baseline HESD 
≥
7
(
least-squares mean [SE] change 
−
3.8 [0.2] vs. 
−
2.0 [0.2] and 
−
4.1 [0.2] vs. 
−
2.2 [0.3]
)
 and patients with baseline
HESD <7 
(−
2.9 [0.1] vs. 
−
1.1 [0.2] and 
−
2.6 [0.1] vs. 
−
0.9 [0.2]
)
. Improvements in HESD and HESD pain scores
with delgocitinib cream were rapid and significantly greater versus cream vehicle from week 1 onwards. The
proportions of patients with severe pain achieving clinically meaningful improvements in HEIS, HEIS PDAL, HEIS

embarrassment and DLQI at 16 weeks were significantly greater with delgocitinib cream versus cream vehicle
(
Figure
)
. Mean improvements in EQ-5D were also rapid and significantly greater with delgocitinib cream versus
cream vehicle in patients with severe pain at 16 weeks 
(
0.2 vs. 0.9; p<0.001
)
.
Conclusion:
Delgocitinib cream resulted in rapid and significant improvements in pain and HRQoL in patients with CHE
associated with severe pain.
Figure.
 Patients with severe baseline pain achieving clinically meaningful improvements in HEIS, HEIS PDAL, HEIS
embarrassment and DLQI at 16 weeks
