Introduction & Objectives:
Conjunctivitis is a common adverse event 
(
AE
)
 observed in patients with atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 treated with
biologics. However, comparative real-world data regarding incidence, risk factors, and management strategies
remain limited. This study aimed to compare the incidence, clinical features, and management of conjunctivitis in
patients with moderate-to-severe AD treated with dupilumab or tralokinumab in a real-life setting.
Materials & Methods:
A retrospective, multicentre observational study was conducted across 35 Italian dermatology centres. Adult
patients with moderate-to-severe AD who received dupilumab or tralokinumab for at least 16 weeks were
included. Demographic, clinical, and treatment data were collected. For patients who developed conjunctivitis,
detailed information on onset, severity, ophthalmologic referral, and management was analysed.
Results:
A total of 6668 patients were analysed 
(
5899 treated with dupilumab, 769 with tralokinumab
)
. Conjunctivitis
occurred in 10.76% of dupilumab-treated and 12.61% of tralokinumab-treated patients, with no statistically
significant difference in incidence. However, tralokinumab was associated with a significantly earlier onset of
conjunctivitis 
(
15.27 ± 14.47 weeks
)
 compared to dupilumab 
(
35.46 ± 45.18 weeks; p < 0.0001
)
. Ophthalmologic
management predominantly involved lubricating and corticosteroid-based eye drops in both groups. Treatment
discontinuation or switching due to conjunctivitis was more frequent in the dupilumab group 
(
25.35% vs. 14.43%,
p < 0.05
)
. No significant predictors of conjunctivitis severity were identified through logistic regression analysis.
Conclusion:
Conjunctivitis continues to represent a common and clinically significant AE in patients with AD receiving biologic
therapies, with notable differences observed between dupilumab and tralokinumab in terms of onset and clinical
management. Early recognition of ocular symptoms is essential, and dermatologists should promptly initiate
supportive eye care and refer to ophthalmologists when appropriate. Further prospective research is warranted to
elucidate underlying mechanisms, identify predictive risk factors, and establish evidence-based strategies for
prevention and treatment.
