Abstract N°: 6225
Dupilumab Normalizes Type 2 Inflammation and Normalizes/Does Not Perturb Type 1/3 Inflammation in
Atopic Dermatitis
Julie Horowitz*
1
, Kaitlyn Gayvert
1
, Subhashini Srivatsan
1
, Wei Keat Lim
1
, Kerry Casey
1
, Seblewongel Asrat
1
, Emma
Guttman-Yassky
2
, Sara Hamon
1
, Andrea Hooper
1
, Jamie Orengo
1
, Jennifer Hamilton
1
1
Regeneron Pharmaceuticals, Tarrytown, United States
2
Icahn School of Medicine Mount Sinai, New York, United States
Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis is a complex disease predominantly driven by type 2 
(
T2
)
inflammation and a disrupted epithelial barrier that clinically manifests with pruritic skin lesions. Blocking IL-4 and
IL-13 signaling with dupilumab, a monoclonal antibody specific for IL-4R
α
, has shown efficacy in moderate-to-
severe atopic dermatitis and has demonstrated normalization of T2 inflammatory biomarkers. Less is understood
about the molecular effects of dupilumab on the type 1 
(
T1
)
 or type 3 
(
T3
)
 inflammation that is also observed in
patients with atopic dermatitis.
Materials & Methods:
 Unbiased transcriptional analysis was performed using lesional skin isolated from adults
with moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis 
(
N=27
)
 and skin isolated from healthy controls 
(
N=45
)
. In parallel,
unbiased transcriptional analysis was conducted using lesional and non-lesional skin isolated in two studies
including patients with moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis treated with dupilumab 
(
N=47
)
. Normalized
Enrichment Scores 
(
NESs
)
 from Gene Set Enrichment Analysis were used to interrogate expression of T1, T2, and
T3 inflammation pathways in 1
)
 atopic dermatitis patients vs. healthy controls and 2
)
 post-dupilumab vs. pre-
treatment samples.
Results:
 Relative to skin isolated from healthy controls, lesional and non-lesional skin isolated from adults with
moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis are significantly enriched for expression of genes associated with T1, T2, and
T3 inflammation.   
Further, compared with pre-treatment samples, both lesional and non-lesional skin isolated from dupilumab-
treated patients showed a significantly lower T2 inflammation NES. Expression of genes within T1 or T3
inflammation NESs were either unchanged or were normalized by dupilumab treatment.
Conclusion:
 Patients with atopic dermatitis exhibit molecular heterogeneity, with predominant expression of
genes associated with T2 inflammation as well as enriched expression of genes associated with T1 and T3
inflammation. Treatment with dupilumab normalized the expression of genes associated with T2 inflammation
towards expression levels observed in healthy controls. Further, treatment with dupilumab either normalized or did
not perturb the expression of genes associated with both T1 and T3 inflammation. These data indicate that
dupilumab can normalize genes associated with inflammation in atopic dermatitis patients.
