Abstract N°: 6241
Safety, tolerability, and pharmacokinetic 
(
PK
)
 results from the single-ascending dose 
(
SAD
)
 portion of the
Phase 1 study of BBT001
—
a novel bispecific IL-4R
α
/IL-31 antibody
—
conducted in healthy volunteers and
patients with atopic dermatitis
Peter Schrader*
1
, Hariz Hassan
2
, Wei Lin
2
, Lisa Li
2
, Wei Liang
2
, Jingjing Jiang
2
, Thang Ho
2
, Shanshan Xu
2
1
Linear Clinical Research, Perth, Australia
2
Bambusa Therapeutics, Boston, United States
Introduction:
 Type II cytokines 
(
IL-4, IL-13, IL-31
)
 play critical yet distinct roles in Th2 inflammatory
dermatological diseases like atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
. BBT001 is a novel tetravalent 
“
2+2
”
 bispecific antibody
engineered to simultaneously target the IL-4/IL-13 and IL-31 pathways, featuring Fc modifications to extend its
half-life. This first-in-human 
(
FIH
)
 study aims to evaluate the safety, tolerability, pharmacokinetics 
(
PK
)
,
immunogenicity, pharmacodynamics 
(
PD
)
 and exploratory clinical activity of BBT001 following single- and
multiple-ascending doses 
(
SAD and MAD
)
 in healthy volunteers 
(
HVs
)
 and adults with moderate-to-severe atopic
dermatitis 
(
AD
)
. This abstract reports data from the SAD portion; the MAD portion is ongoing and will be reported
separately.
Methods:
 In nonclinical studies, BBT001 demonstrated efficacy in multiple mouse models of AD and pruritus. A
GLP toxicology study in monkeys showed that BBT001 was safe with the no-observed-adverse-effect level
(
NOAEL
)
 up to 200 mg/kg. The nonclinical results were used to enable the FIH study. This FIH study is a
randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, SAD and MAD study 
(
NCT06808477
)
 being conducted in HVs and
in patients with moderate-to-severe AD. The study consists of three parts: Part A 
(
SAD study in HVs
)
, Part B 
(
MAD
study in HVs
)
, and Part C 
(
MAD study in AD patients
)
. In Part A SAD study, up to 38 HVs across 5 cohorts receive a
single intravenous dose of BBT001 ranging from 50 mg to 1200 mg. Safety assessments include adverse events
(
AEs
)
, vital signs, clinical laboratory tests, electrocardiograms 
(
ECGs
)
, physical examinations, and immunogenicity
(
anti-drug antibodies, ADA
)
. Blood samples are collected for PK and PD analyses.
Results:
 The study is ongoing. Subjects have been dosed with either BBT001 or placebo. To date, all dose levels
administered have been well tolerated. Interim data from up to 38 subjects in the SAD portion of the trial,
including preliminary safety, tolerability, and PK results, will be presented.
Conclusion:
 This Phase 1 SAD study characterizes the safety, tolerability, and PK profile of BBT001 in healthy
volunteers. The findings support dose selection and scheduling for subsequent MAD cohorts in healthy individuals
and patients with AD, and contribute to the early clinical development profile of BBT001.
