Abstract N°: 6281
Abrocitinib in combination with dupilumab for refractory atopic chronic hand eczema
Berta Perez Tato
1
, Silvia Marinero Escobedo
1
, Raquel Paz Pérez
1
, Evelina de la Cruz Gomez
1
, Ana María Rodriguez
Martin
1
, Monserrat Franco
1
, Isabel Polimon Olabarrieta
1
1
Hospital Universitario de Móstoles, Dermatology, Móstoles, Spain
Introduction & Objectives:
Targeted immune inhibitors 
(
biologics
)
 and Janus kinase 
(
JAK
)
 inhibitors have revolutionized the
management of moderate- to- severe atopic dermatitis. However, some patients remain insufficiently
controlled with new systemic immunomodulatory treatments 
(
SITs
)
 in monotherapy, especially in
“
difficult to treat areas
”
, as hand eczema.
Materials & Methods:
A-25-year-old man of Asian ethnicity, who works as a warehouse operative, with severe atopic dermatitis
from early infancy, with concomitant severe atopic chronic hand eczema 
(
CHE
)
 for 5 years. He has a
history of moderate/severe asthma and allergic rhinitis. Treatment history included ultra-potent topical
corticosteroids, topical calcineurin inhibitors, prednisone, methotrexate, cyclosporine, azathioprine and
phototherapy. Routine patch testing was performed with positive results to lanolin.
The patient began treatment with dupilumab 300mg every 2 weeks. By week 32, the Eczema Area and
Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
 dropped from 22 to 4, and the Peak Pruritus Numerical Rating Scale 
(
pp-NRS
)
 from
8 to 4, but he maintained severe hand eczema with Investigator global assessment of Chronic Hand Eczema
(
IGA-CH
)
 score of 4 points, with great impact on his quality of life, and causing work-related problems.
We recommended starting oral alitretinoin, but the patient could not afford it.
We associated dupilumab treatment with abrocitinib 200mg per day, but the patient experienced nausea
and dizziness, so dosage was tapered to 100mg per day. After 16 weeks of treatment, the CHE improved to
“
mild eczema
”
, IGA-CH score of 2, and 52 weeks after starting the association, 
“
almost clear
”
, IGA-CH
score of 1. The patient
’
s quality of life improved from 
“
strongly impaired
”
 to 
“
not at all impaired
”
 based
on the Quality of Life in Hand Eczema.
No major adverse events were reported. No remarkable abnormalities were observed during treatment in
complete blood count, lipid panel, coagulation function or comprehensive metabolic panel, except for
creatine kinase elevation 
(
CPK: 977U/L
)
.
Results:
The JAK
–
STAT signaling pathway is involved in the signaling of several cytokines that have been shown to
play a role in the pathophysiology of atopic CHE, such as interleukins: IL-4, IL-13, IL-22 and IL-31. Although
dupilumab is highly effective and used as monotherapy, it might insufficiently treat atopic dermatitis
predominantly involving 
“
difficult to treat areas
”
, necessitating the use of adjunctive therapy. Considering
that JAK-inhibitors target several cytokine pathways instead of one single pathway, are an alternative
option for rescue therapy for refractory CHE.
However, the high cost of combination therapy may hinder its use.

Conclusion:
The combination of biologic 
(
dupilumab
)
 and JAKi 
(
abrocitinb
)
 could be a favorable option for refractory
severe atopic dermatitis, especially in cases with recalcitrant chronic hand eczema.
