Abstract N°: 6284
Dupilumab Safety and Efficacy Up to 3 Years Across Racial Subgroups in Pediatric Patients Aged 6 Months
to 11 Years With Atopic Dermatitis
Elaine Siegfried
1, 2
, Michael Cork
3, 4
, Mercedes Gonzales
5
, Roland Aschoff
6
, Andrew F. Alexis
7
, Lakshi Aldredge
8
,
Zhen Chen
9
, Randy Prescilla
10
, Tien Nguyen
9
1
Saint Louis University, St. Louis, MO, United States
2
Cardinal Glennon Children
’
s Hospital, St. Louis, MO, United States
3
University of Sheffield, Sheffield, United Kingdom
4
Sheffield Children
’
s Hospital, Sheffield, United Kingdom
5
Pediatric Skin Research, Coral Gables, FL, United States
6
University Hospital Carl Gustav Carus Dresden, Dresden, Germany
7
Weill Cornell Medicine, New York, NY, United States
8
VA Portland Health Care System, Portland, OR, United States
9
Regeneron Pharmaceuticals Inc., Tarrytown, NY, United States
10
Sanofi, Cambridge, MA, United States
Introduction & Objectives:
 Racial differences in the presentation, prevalence, and severity of atopic dermatitis
(
AD
)
 are well-documented. Prior studies have shown efficacy and safety of dupilumab treatment across racial
subgroups among adults with AD. Long-term data on disease characteristics and treatment response across
pediatric racial subgroups are lacking. Safety and efficacy of dupilumab were evaluated for up to 3 yr in infants
and children with AD from different racial subgroups.
Materials & Methods:
 Infants and children aged 6 mo
–
5 yr with moderate-to-severe AD and children aged 6
–
11
yr with severe AD enrolled in an open-label extension study 
(
NCT02612454
)
 after prior dupilumab trials
(
NCT03346434, NCT03345914
)
. Patients received weight-based doses of dupilumab 
(
200/300 mg every 4 or 2
weeks
)
. Topical treatments were allowed. For this analysis, patients were categorized into 2 subgroups based on
self-reported race: White vs Asian, Black/African American, and Other. Safety and percentage change from
baseline in body surface area 
(
BSA
)
, Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
, and SCORing Atopic Dermatitis
(
SCORAD
)
 were reported through Week 152. Data were reported as observed.
Results:
 Of 554 patients, 400 
(
72.2%
)
 self-identified as White and 154 
(
27.8%
)
 as Asian, Black/African American,
and Other. Study completion rates for White vs Asian, Black/African American, and Other patients were 88.8% vs
81.8% at Week 52, 77.5% vs 62.3% at Week 104, and 68.0% vs 47.4% at Week 152. Mean 
(
SD
)
 treatment exposure
was 117.9 
(
45.4
)
 weeks for White and 104.2 
(
50.5
)
 weeks for Asian, Black/African American, and Other patients.
Patients from the Asian, Black/African American, and Other subgroup had more severe baseline disease and higher
rates of treatment discontinuation compared to White patients. By Week 152, 22.3% of White and 39.6% of Asian,
Black/African American, and Other patients discontinued treatment, most often due to patient withdrawal 
(
6.3%
and 11.0%
)
 or other reasons 
(
9.0% and 11.0%
)
; differences may reflect a contrast in sample size between
subgroups. Discontinuation due to lack of efficacy or adverse events 
(
AEs
)
 occurred in less than 2.0% of patients in
either group.
Treatment-emergent AEs 
(
TEAEs
)
 occurred in 89.5% of White and 84.4% of Asian, Black/African American, and
Other patients. Common treatment-related TEAEs 
(
White vs Asian, Black/African American, and Other
)
 included
allergic conjunctivitis 
(
2.5% vs 3.2%
)
, nasopharyngitis 
(
2.3% vs 1.3%
)
, and blepharitis 
(
0.5% vs 2.6%
)
. Most TEAEs

causing permanent discontinuation 
(
5/6
)
, including one serious food-related anaphylaxis in a White patient,
resolved or resulted in recovery. Both drug-related serious TEAEs 
(
1 patient per subgroup
)
 also resolved or
resulted in recovery.
At Week 152, consistent decreases in disease signs and symptoms were observed in both subgroups. From parent
study baseline, BSA reduced by over 80% 
(−
85.5% White vs 
−
80.3% Asian, Black/African American, and Other
)
,
SCORAD decreased by approximately 70% 
(−
71.2% vs 
−
69.4%
)
, EASI scores improved by over 85% 
(−
89.6% vs
−
85.5%
)
, and nearly 90% of all patients achieved EASI-75 by Week 152.
Conclusion:
 Dupilumab treatment provided meaningful improvements in clinical outcomes for infants and
children with AD across racial subgroups. The safety profile remained consistent with the established safety data
for dupilumab, supporting its use in diverse pediatric populations with AD.
