Introduction:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronically relapsing inflammatory skin disease with variable age of onset. For
moderate-to-severe AD, treatment guidelines recommend medium or high-potency topical therapies. When these
fail, systemic treatments such as immunomodulators, steroids, and advanced systemics, including monoclonal
antibodies or oral Janus kinase 
(
JAK
)
 inhibitors, are recommended. Abrocitinib, an oral JAK1 inhibitor, was
approved by the FDA in 2022 for adults and in 2023 for adolescents aged 12 -<18. This retrospective, real-world
study evaluated patient characteristics and clinical utilization since abrocitinib received approval in the US.
Methods:
This retrospective study used real-world data from the Komodo Health claims database 
(
1/1/2021 
–
 1/31/2025
)
 to
characterize US adolescents and adults with moderate-to-severe AD treated with abrocitinib. Demographic and
clinical characteristics were assessed descriptively overall and by treatment positioning of abrocitinib among all
approved advanced systemic treatments. Co-prescription with topicals at abrocitinib initiation was evaluated
among patients with 
≥
30 days of follow-up and defined as the presence of a prescription for a topical treatment
within 30 days before or after abrocitinib initiation. Early persistent use of abrocitinib 
(
early persistence
)
 was
defined as the proportion of patients with 
≥
60 days of follow-up with an abrocitinib refill within 30 days of the
first fill end date and was assessed descriptively.
Results:
A total of 3,542 patients met the inclusion criteria; with 315 
(
9%
)
 adolescents 
(
ages 12-<18
)
 and 3,227 
(
91%
)
adults 
(
ages 18+
)
. Median age at treatment initiation was 15 years for adolescents, and 41 years for adults.
Abrocitinib was most commonly prescribed as a second-line 
(
2L
)
 advanced systemic for both adolescent and
adult patients 
(
Table 1
)
. However, 27% of adolescents and 31% of adults received abrocitinib treatment as their
first advanced systemic. Among patients with 
≥
60 days of follow-up 
(
n=309 adolescents; n=3,166 adults
)
, early
persistence was 77% in adolescents and 75% in adults. The proportion of White patients ranged from 21% 
(
1L
)
 to
30% 
(
2L
)
 in adolescents and from 37% 
(
1L/2L
)
 to 33% 
(
3L+
)
 in adults, however there was high missingness in
race/ethnicity data 
(
38% adolescents, 31% adults
)
. The most common prescriber specialties of abrocitinib were
dermatologists 
(
47% adolescents; 55% adults
)
, physician assistants/nurse practitioners 
(
39% adolescents; 32%
adults
)
 and allergists 
(
9% adolescents; 6% adults
)
. Notably, among patients with 
≥
30 days of follow-up 
(
n=313
adolescents; n=3,200 adults
)
, 74% of adolescents and 78% of adults did not have a co-prescription with topicals
at abrocitinib initiation.

Conclusion:
Compared to the JADE TEEN trial, adolescent patients in this real-world study were similar in age but received less
co-prescription with topicals at initiation, consistent with rates seen for adult co-topical use. Early persistence
across age groups suggests that the majority of patients who initiate abrocitinib refill their prescription on
schedule. Adolescent initiators of abrocitinib had similar demographic profiles to those of adult initiators. These
findings suggest that abrocitinib is being used in a broader range of real-world scenarios than clinical trials,
warranting further investigation into treatment outcomes and adherence drivers in adolescent and adult patients
with a longer post-approval follow-up.
