Introduction & Objectives:
 JADE REAL 
(
NCT04564755
)
 was a global, open-label, expanded access protocol study
initiated in 2020 
(
completed September 2024
)
 to provide abrocitinib access to patients with moderate-to-severe
atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 for whom available and approved topical and systemic AD medications were inadequate.
The unique study design simulated a real-world clinical setting. This post hoc analysis aimed to evaluate the
dosing patterns 
(
dose escalation and reduction vs consistent dosing
)
 and efficacy of abrocitinib in adolescent and
adult patients with moderate-to-severe AD in JADE REAL.
Materials & Methods:
 Eligible patients aged 
≥
12 years with moderate-to-severe AD were initiated on once-daily
abrocitinib 100 mg or 200 mg per investigator
’
s discretion. The dose could be changed throughout the treatment
period. Patients were stratified by initial dose 
(
abrocitinib 100 mg or 200 mg
)
 and the number of dose changes
(
continuous dosing or 
≥
1 dose change/s
)
. Dose change groups included patients with a dose escalation
(
abrocitinib 100 mg to 200 mg
)
 and patients with a dose reduction 
(
abrocitinib 200 mg to 100 mg
)
. Efficacy
assessments included the proportion of patients achieving 
≥
75%/
≥
90% improvement from baseline in Eczema
Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI-75/-90
)
, and EASI-90 plus Peak Pruritus Numerical Rating Scale score of 0 or 1 
(
PP-
NRS 0/1
)
 at Week 72; for dose change groups efficacy responses were also assessed at the visit before dose
change 
(
visit 0
)
 and at visits 1 and 2 following dose change.
Results:
 Of 312 patients in JADE REAL, 120 
(
38.5%
)
 and 192 
(
61.5%
)
 were initiated on abrocitinib 100 mg and 200
mg, respectively, with a mean 
(
SD
)
 treatment duration of 379.1 
(
203.3
)
 days. Of patients initiated on abrocitinib
100 mg and 200 mg, 78 
(
65.0%
)
 and 119 
(
62.0%
)
 received continuous dosing, and 42 
(
35.0%
)
 and 73 
(
38.0%
)

patients had 
≥
1 dose change, respectively. A higher proportion of patients aged 
≥
12 to 18 and 
≥
65 years, who
were Black/African American, and who had moderate AD were initiated on abrocitinib 100 mg, while a higher
proportion of patients aged 
≥
18 to <65 years, who were Asian, and who had severe AD were initiated on
abrocitinib 200 mg 
(
Table
)
. Across all treatment subgroups, the proportion of patients achieving efficacy
responses at Week 72 ranged from 75.6% to 92.6%, 53.3% to 75.0%, and 28.9% to 57.9% for EASI-75, EASI-90, and
EASI-90+PP-NRS 0/1, respectively. In the dose escalation subgroup, a comparable or numerically greater
proportion of patients achieved efficacy responses at visit 2 post dose escalation versus visit 0 for EASI-75 
(
59.4%
vs 41.9%
)
, EASI-90 
(
40.6% vs 29.0%
)
, and EASI-90+PP-NRS 0/1 
(
19.4% vs 20.0%
)
; in the dose reduction subgroup,
response rates were comparable at visit 2 post dose reduction versus visit 0 for EASI-75 
(
81.7% vs 80.0%
)
, EASI-90
(
65.0% vs 60.0%
)
, and EASI-90+PP-NRS 0/1 
(
45.0% vs 46.0%
)
.
Conclusion:
 Abrocitinib demonstrated improvements in severity and extent of AD across all treatment subgroups
of patients for whom available and approved topical and systemic AD medications were inadequate. Patients
initiated on abrocitinib 200 mg may decrease dosage to 100 mg with a low risk of losing efficacy, while patients
initiated on abrocitinib 100 mg with lower efficacy may benefit from switching to 200 mg. As the JADE REAL trial
simulates the real-world use of Janus kinase inhibitors, these results may support clinical decision making around
initial dose selection and dosage optimisation based on individual patient needs.
