Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disease that may require long-
term treatment to maintain disease control. Treatment with the oral, once-daily Janus kinase 1
–
selective inhibitor
abrocitinib demonstrated efficacy through 112 weeks in patients 
(
pts
)
 with moderate-to-severe AD. Previous
integrated safety analyses assessed select adverse events of special interest 
(
AESIs
)
 analyzed by age 
(
18 to <65
years [y] and 
≥
65 y
)
 and smoking status in 3802 pts, including data from >5200 pt-y 
(
PY
)
 with up to 4 y of
abrocitinib exposure and a subsequent updated analysis looked at AESIs stratified by refined baseline age
categories and cardiovascular risk factors in 3848 pts, including data from >7000 PY with a safety profile up to 4.5
y of abrocitinib exposure. The current study evaluated safety events stratified by refined age categories and
smoking status using complete data for most pts in the JADE integrated safety analysis up to 6.5 y of abrocitinib
exposure.
Materials & Methods:
 Analysis included pts from 8 phase 2 and 3 parent trials 
(
phase 2b [NCT02780167], JADE
MONO-1 [NCT03349060], MONO-2 [NCT03575871], REGIMEN [NCT03627767], COMPARE [NCT03720470], TEEN
[NCT03796676], MOA [NCT03915496], and DARE [NCT04345367]
)
 and 1 long-term extension trial 
(
JADE EXTEND
[NCT03422822]; data cutoff: December 31, 2024
)
 in the JADE clinical program. Incidence rates 
(
IRs; number of pts
with events/100 PY
)
 of AESIs were assessed in pts by age category 
(
including <18 y, 18 to <40 y, 40 to <50 y, 50
to <65 y, and 
≥
65 y
)
 and smoking status 
(
never and current/former smoker
)
.
Results:
 Of 3850 pts in the pooled safety population, 3052 received the same abrocitinib dose throughout
(
consistent-dose cohort, described herein
)
 and 798 pts comprised the variable-dose cohort; both cohorts
represent a total of 9655.4 PY of abrocitinib exposure. Mean age of the consistent-dose cohort was 33.5 y; 5% of
pts were aged 
≥
65 y and 1% were 
≥
75 y. IRs per 100 PY in the consistent-dose cohort were numerically higher in
older versus younger pts for major adverse cardiovascular events 
(
MACEs; <18 y: 0.07 [0.00-0.42]; 18 to <40 y:
0.14 [0.04-0.32]; 40 to <50 y: 0.09 [0.00-0.51]; 50 to <65 y: 0.79 [0.32-1.63]; and 
≥
65 y: 2.03 [0.74-4.41]
)
 and death
(
<18 y: 0.00 [95% CI, 0.00-0.28]; 18 to <40 y: 0.05 [0.01-0.20]; 40 to <50 y: 0.09 [0.00-0.51]; 50 to <65 y: 0.34
[0.07-0.99]; and 
≥
65 y: 2.02 [0.74-4.40]
)
. Similar trends were observed for other AESIs, including serious infections,

herpes zoster, malignancies 
(
excluding nonmelanoma skin cancer [NMSC]
)
, NMSC, deep vein thrombosis 
(
DVT
)
,
and venous thromboembolism 
(
VTE
)
. IRs for serious infections, malignancies 
(
excluding NMSC
)
, NMSC, MACE,
DVT, VTE, and death were higher or numerically higher in current/former smokers compared with never smokers;
all events of NMSC and DVT occurred in current/former smokers. IRs for herpes zoster were comparable between
groups 
(
current/former smokers: 3.48 [95% CI, 2.70-4.41] and never smokers: 3.56 [3.05-4.13]
)
. Data from the
variable-dose cohort generally showed similar trends.
Conclusions:
 These long-term follow-up abrocitinib safety data with >9600 PY of exposure in pts with moderate-
to-severe AD are consistent with the previously reported risk profile. No new safety signals were reported. IRs
tended to be higher in older pts and current/former smokers, known risk factors for many AESIs included in this
analysis.**
