Abstract N°: 6404
Atopic Dermatitis in a Patient with Severe Autism: Treatment with Dupilumab
mariana cruz
1
, MARIU VALECILLOS ROSAL
1
, Eliana Arnez Bohrt
1
, denisse coloma
1
, karla abril
1
, micaela motzo
1
,
Margarita Larralde
1
, Marias Eugenia Abad
1
1
Ramos Mejía Hospital, Pediatric Dermatology, C1221ADC, Argentina
Introduction & Objectives:
An increase in the global incidence of atopic and neurodevelopmental diseases has been observed. In particular,
Atopic Dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 and Autism Spectrum Disorders 
(
ASD
)
 have gained significant relevance due to their
impact on patients
’
 quality of life. Population studies have proposed the hypothesis that AD may act as an early
precursor of ASD, suggesting that AD in the first years of life implies a higher risk of developing ASD later in
childhood, especially in the presence of other atopic comorbidities such as allergic rhinitis or asthma. AD triggers
an immunological cascade involving mast cell activation, the release of proinflammatory cytokines 
(
mainly IL-6
)
,
and elevated levels of Th2 cytokines, which can cross the blood-brain barrier, leading to neuroinflammation or
direct damage to microglia and neurons. This may play a key role in the etiopathogenesis of ASD and other
neuropsychiatric conditions.
Materials & Methods:
This is a 7-year-old male patient with a prior diagnosis of Autism Spectrum Disorder and severe atopic dermatitis
refractory to topical treatments and systemic therapy such as methotrexate.
Results:
The patient began experiencing generalized xerosis at 2 years of age, accompanied by pruritus and eczematous
lesions. These symptoms were associated with behavioral disturbances and environmental disconnection. Initial
treatment included topical corticosteroids and emollients.
At age 5, his atopic dermatitis became generalized. A punch biopsy was performed, confirming the diagnosis.
Systemic therapy with oral methotrexate was initiated. Although there was an initial clinical improvement, poor
adherence to oral medication necessitated switching to subcutaneous administration after 6 months. During
methotrexate therapy, the patient experienced several bacterial superinfections, requiring hospitalization on one
occasion.
Given that the patient underwent a stepwise treatment approach
—
including topical therapy 
(
topical
corticosteroids, tacrolimus
)
 followed by conventional systemic therapy 
(
oral and subcutaneous methotrexate
)—
without satisfactory clinical response and with recurrent skin infections due to impaired skin barrier function,
treatment with dupilumab was initiated.
After three months of therapy, a significant improvement was observed in clinical symptoms, behavior, and
environmental engagement.
Conclusion:
Studies have shown that, in addition to genetic and environmental factors, Atopic Dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 and Autism
Spectrum Disorders 
(
ASD
)
 may also share common pathways of immune dysregulation and chronic inflammation.

Emerging evidence suggests overlapping immunopathogenic mechanisms, including altered cytokine profiles and
impaired skin and neuroimmune barriers. However, further research is needed to fully elucidate the nature and
extent of this association, with the aim of developing innovative and targeted therapeutic strategies for both
conditions.
