Introduction & Objectives:
The incidence of inflammatory skin diseases is rising in western societies, due to the urbanization, dietary shifts
and reduced microbial diversity. Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 affects 15
–
20
 
% of children and 1
–
3
 
% of adults in Chile,
generating a considerable socio
​
economic burden. In addition, a key factor is the loss of the cutaneous
microbiome equilibrium 
(
dysbiosis
)
, where the bacteria 
Staphylococcus
 
aureus 
(
S. aureus
)
 proliferates and
exacerbates the pathology. In Chile* the microbiome of patients has not been characterized. In this context,
nanoencapsulation offers an opportunity to co
​
deliver antibacterial and anti
​
inflammatory agents directly to the
epidermis while enhancing stability, penetration and patient tolerability. Objectives
 
were: 
(
i
)
 Characterize the skin
microbiome of Chilean AD patients versus healthy controls; 
(
ii
)
 develop sub
​
300
 
nm oil
​
in
​
water nano encapsulates
(
NE
)
 loaded with actives molecules; 
(
iii
)
 validate their physicochemical performance and 
in vitro
 activity.
Materials & Methods:
Twelve mild
​
to
​
moderate AD patients 
(
12 to 56 years olds
)
 and matched to controls 
(
Oct
 
2021
–
Mar
 
2022
)
 were
evaluated. DNA was extracted and full
​
length 16S rRNA sequenced in all samples; taxonomic and diversity indices
were studied using Clustal series of programs. NE were produced by high
​
energy sonication with
pharmaceutic
​
grade surfactants; size, polydispersity, 
ζ​
potential and encapsulation efficiency 
(
EE
)
