Introduction & Objectives:
Around 70% of patients with moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 have head & neck 
(
H&N
)
 area
involvement, associated with embarrassment, stigmatization, and negative impact on QoL and mental health. AD
in the H&N area can be difficult to treat. Tralokinumab, a monoclonal Ab targeting IL-13 indicated for treating
moderate-to-severe AD, was effective and well-tolerated in Phase 3 clinical trials. Here, we present real-world
effectiveness data from 12-months tralokinumab treatment in AD patients with H&N involvement
Materials & Methods:
TRACE is a prospective, non-interventional, international, multi-center, single-cohort study of adults with AD, who
received tralokinumab according to national approved label at treating physician
’
s discretion; concomitant
medications were allowed. Of 835 patients enrolled 
(
Nov-2021 to Jun-2023
)
, we evaluated 654 patients with
baseline AD involvement on the face, scalp, and neck. Assessments included AD localization and disease severity
scores 
(
IGA, EASI, DLQI, PP-NRS, Sleep-NRS
)
, per individual clinical practice. Data are presented as observed.
Results:
The proportion of AD patients with H&N involvement decreased markedly to 68.1% 
(
n=565
)
 and 51.8% 
(
n=367
)
at months 3 and 12. Treatment with tralokinumab reduced AD severity across all endpoints 
(
Tables 1 & 2
)
. Among
those with data, the proportion of patients with clear or almost clear skin 
(
IGA 0/1
)
 increased from 1.5% at
baseline 
(
n=619
)
 to 36.2% 
(
n=509
)
 and 64.7% 
(
n=317
)
 at months 3 and 12. Among patients with baseline IGA
≥
2, 45.0% 
(
n=489
)
 at month 3 and 70.7% 
(
n=304
)
 at month 12 had a 
≥
2-point IGA improvement. The
proportion of patients with severe disease 
(
IGA 4
)
 decreased markedly from 40.1% at baseline 
(
n=619
)
 to 4.3%
(
n=509
)
 and 2.2% 
(
n=317
)
 at months 3 and 12. The proportion of patients with EASI 
≤
7 
(
no or mild eczema
)
increased dramatically from 13.0% 
(
n=525
)
 at baseline to 69.5% 
(
n=426
)
 and 89.3% 
(
n=290
)
 at months 3 and 12.
The mean DLQI score improved from 13.1 at baseline 
(
n=373
)
 to 5.1 
(
n=143
)
 at month 12, while the proportion
of patients with DLQI 
≤
5 
(
no to small QoL impairment
)
 increased from 19.0% at baseline to 67.8% at month 12.
From baseline to month 12: mean PP-NRS decreased from 6.4 
(
n=395
)
 to 3.3 
(
n=140
)
; and proportion with PP-
NRS 
≤
4 increased from 21.0% to 70.0%. Mean sleep NRS decreased from 5.2 
(
n=313
)
 at baseline to 2.2 
(
n=108
)
at month 12. Among patients with baseline Sleep-NRS 
≥
2, 70.7% had a 
≥
2-point improvement at month 12.
Dupilumab-naive 
(
n=510
)
 and dupilumab-experienced 
(
n=144
)
 patients improved similarly, though higher
baseline disease severity was seen in dupilumab-naive patients.

Conclusion:
In adult AD patients with H&N involvement, 12-months treatment with tralokinumab improved H&N involvement,
overall AD severity, and QoL in a real-world setting. Improvements were similar regardless of prior dupilumab use.


