Abstract N°: 6549
“
Waiting well
”
 for Patients: Addressing Barriers to Timely Atopic Dermatitis Care in the UK National
Health Service
Kave Shams*
1
, leila asfour
2
, suzi holland
3
, Stephanie Gallard
4
, Benoit Raymond
5
, Yousaf Aftab
5
, Gulraj Matharu
5
,
Karim Amer
5
1
Trust Headquarters, Leeds Teaching Hospitals NHS Trust, Leeds, United Kingdom
2
Chelsea and Westminster Hospital, London, United Kingdom
3
Eczema Outreach Support, Linlithgow, United Kingdom
4
Royal Liverpool University Hospital, Wirral, United Kingdom
5
Pfizer UK Limited, London, United Kingdom
Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin condition with significant
patient impact. Prolonged waiting times for specialist dermatology referral leave many patients underserved,
leading to frustration and inadequate management. This study investigates key barriers to timely AD care in the
UK National Health service 
(
NHS
)
 and explores solutions to improve interim support before specialist consultation.
Materials & Methods:
 A mixed-methods approach was employed to understand issues around delayed
management of AD. Data were acquired from 3 sources: in-depth one-on-one interviews with 2 patients living
with AD and 7 healthcare professionals 
(
HCPs
)
 involved in AD management; a Delphi survey to achieve expert
consensus 
(≥
70%
)
 of UK HCPs 
(
round 1: N=96
)
 working in primary and secondary care; and an advisory board
meeting with 7 experts and 2 patient representatives to gain advice on the Delphi and interview findings and
identify potential actionable solutions. Thematic analysis was applied to qualitative data, while survey responses
were analysed for consensus.
Results:
 2/2 patients and 5/7 HCPs reported that general practitioners 
(
GPs
)
 need to do more to support their AD
patients. 2/2 patients reported feeling like a 
“
burden
”
 to their GP for seeking help initially and had to strongly
advocate to receive treatment beyond topical steroids.
There was consensus from Delphi respondents 
(
77%
)
 that GPs know the signs and symptoms of AD; however,
consensus was not reached on management of AD in primary care being optimal 
(
50% of Delphi respondents
agreed
)
 or that quality information was provided by GPs to patients 
(
45% of Delphi respondents agreed
)
.
Delphi respondents reported that provision of quick guides 
(
90% agreed
)
 and more comprehensive clinical
guidelines 
(
95% agreed
)
 would be beneficial to primary care AD management. 7/7 expert HCPs and 2/2 patient
representatives agreed that meeting minimum standards in primary care through completion of checklists could
support better management of AD patients.
Waiting times were reported to negatively impact patients 
(
93% of Delphi respondents, 2/2 patient
representatives and 7/7 HCPs agreed
)
. No consensus was reached on referral to secondary care being timely 
(
59%
of Delphi respondents disagreed
)
.
In secondary care dermatology 73% of Delphi respondents reported that there was a high level of understanding
of all AD treatment options among HCPs; however, only 50% agreed that management in secondary care was
comprehensive and satisfactory, and only 27% of Delphi respondents agreed that there was timely access to
specialist care.

Conclusion:
 Interviews with patients and expert HCPs together with Delphi survey findings demonstrated that
whilst GPs are generally knowledgeable, there are critical gaps in AD care, particularly in referral pathways and
primary care management. HCPs agreed on the urgent need for practical tools to optimise treatment before
patients reach specialist care. Implementing checklists and quick guides could improve consistency of care and
ensure minimum standards of AD management are consistently met across the UK. This would improve
confidence amongst less experienced HCPs and help address the identified barriers, which are vital for enhancing
patient outcomes and reducing delays in AD care.
