 = 13%
)
, with consistent results across the monotherapy and topical
corticosteroid subgroups. Dupilumab also led to a significant improvement in sleep quality, as shown by a
reduction in the SCORAD sleep Visual Analogue Scale 
(
VAS
)
 
(
Figure 2
; MD = 
–
2.34; 95% CI: 
–
2.75 to 
–
1.92; 
P
 <
0.00001; 
I²
 = 25%
)
, with slightly greater effects observed in the 300 mg weekly 
(
qw
)
 subgroup. Additional
outcomes favoured dupilumab as well, with a significant decrease in Patient-Oriented Eczema Measure 
(
POEM
)
scores 
(
MD = 
–
7.56; 95% CI: 
–
8.46 to 
–
6.67; 
P
 < 0.00001; 
I²
 = 49%
)
, as well as improvements in Peak Pruritus
Numeric Rating Scale 
(
PP-NRS
)
, and Hospital Anxiety and Depression Scale 
(
HADS
)
 total scores.
Conclusion:
 Dupilumab appears to significantly improve sleep quality and overall quality of life in adults with
atopic dermatitis. Our findings highlight that the benefits of dupilumab extend beyond skin symptom control.
