Abstract N°: 6674
Environmental-Mental health pathways in dermatitis: Insights from an Infodemiological causal Bayesian
network
Marco Scutari
1
, Samir Salah*
2
, Delphine Kerob
2
, Jean Krutmann
3
1
Istituto Dalle Molle di Studi sull
’
Intelligenza Artificiale 
(
IDSIA
)
, Lugano, Switzerland
2
La Roche-Posay Laboratoire Dermatologique, Levallois-Perret, France
3
Leibniz Research Institute for Environmental Medicine, Düsseldorf, Germany
Introduction & Objectives:
 Environmental and mental conditions are known risk factors for dermatitis and
symptoms of skin inflammation, but their interplay is difficult to quantify; epidemiological studies rarely include
both, along with possible confounding factors. Infodemiology leverages large online datasets to address this
issue, but fusing them produces strong patterns of spatial and temporal correlation, missingness, and
heterogeneity, requiring sophisticated modeling approaches to derive valid causal insights about the complex
interactions between environmental factors, mental health conditions, and skin inflammation.
Materials & Methods:
 In this paper, we design a causal network that correctly models these complex structures
in large-scale infodemiological data from Google, EPA, NOAA and US Census 
(
434 US counties, 134 weeks
)
. Our
model accounts for temporal dependencies by duplicating nodes across time points, spatial dependencies
through a covariance matrix as a function of geographical location, group heterogeneity through weights, and
missing data patterns by allowing weights greater than zero only for locally complete observations, with validation
performed using statistical tests for autocorrelation, spatial dependencies, and heterogeneity alongside predictive
accuracy assessment through temporal and spatial cross-validation.
Results:
 Our model successfully captures known causal relationships between weather patterns, pollutants, mental
conditions, and dermatitis. Key findings reveal that anxiety accounts for 57.4% of explained variance in dermatitis,
followed by NO2 
(
33.9%
)
, obesity 
(
7.7%
)
, and PM2.5 
(
0.8%
)
. Environmental factors show significant mediation
effects through mental conditions - the effects of PM2.5, NO2, and SO2 change by factors of 0.54, 0.93, and 0.56
respectively when their impact on mental conditions is blocked. For weather variables, the combined proportion
of variance explained by temperature and thermal excursion changes by 0.11 after removing mental conditions
’
mediation effect. In 2024, the EPA reduced the legal limit on PM2.5 by 25% to 9µg/m³, an action expected to
prevent approximately 4,500 premature deaths annually. Our model predicts this 25% reduction in PM2.5 could
also decrease dermatitis incidence by 18%, while more ambitious reductions to 8
μ
g/m³ could yield a 21%
decrease. Counterfactual analysis reveals cold spells must persist for approximately 4 weeks before producing
meaningful increases 
(
+5%
)
 in dermatitis.
Conclusion:
 Through statistical validation and causal inference, we provide insights into the complex interplay
between environmental and mental health factors affecting dermatitis, offering guidance for policy development.
Our findings show environmental regulation adjustments can significantly impact outcomes, while addressing
both mental health and pollution creates synergistic benefits. While robust, our approach has limitations: the
correspondence between search frequencies and actual disease prevalence remains an assumption; conclusions
about aggregate data may not apply to individuals; and unmeasured confounders may introduce spurious
relationships. Nevertheless, our approach demonstrates the power of infodemiological data fusion for
understanding complex health relationships difficult to investigate through traditional methods.
