Abstract N°: 6772
Efficacy of Ceramide-Containing Moisturizer in Multivesicular Emulsion as a Steroid-Sparing Agent in
Patients with Atopic Dermatitis: A Prospective Randomized Controlled Trial
Supenya Varothai*
1
, Pichanee Limtanakool
1
, Nattha Angkoolpakdeekool
1
, Teerapat Paringkarn
2
, Suthasinee
Pattaravadee
1
, Sasima Eimpunth
1
1
Department of Dermatology, Faculty of Medicine Siriraj Hospital, Mahidol University, Bangkok,
Thailand.Department of Dermatology, Faculty of Medicine Siriraj Hospital, Mahidol University, , Bangkok, Thailand
2
Department of Dermatology, Faculty of Medicine Siriraj Hospital, Mahidol University, Bangkok,
Thailand.Department of Dermatology, Faculty of Medicine Siriraj Hospital, Mahidol University,, Bangkok, Thailand
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disorder characterized by impaired skin barrier function,
including reduced hydration and depletion of key stratum corneum lipids such as ceramides. Although topical
corticosteroids are the mainstay of AD treatment, prolonged use is associated with potential adverse effects.
Barrier repair with ceramide-containing moisturizers offers a potential strategy to reduce steroid reliance.
This study primarily evaluated the efficacy of a ceramide-containing moisturizer in a multivesicular emulsion
(
MVE
)
 versus a simple cream base in reducing corticosteroid use, with secondary outcomes including clinical
efficacy, tolerability, skin biophysical parameters, and patient-reported measures.
Materials & Methods:
This double-blinded, randomized controlled trial enrolled 40 adult participants 
(≥
18 years
)
 with mild-to-moderate
AD, diagnosed per the Hanifin-Rajka criteria. Participants were randomized to apply either a ceramide-containing
cream in MVE or a simple cream base twice daily for 4 weeks, with 0.1% triamcinolone acetonide cream for as-
needed use. The primary outcome was corticosteroid use frequency, recorded daily. Secondary outcomes included
clinical efficacy, comprised of physician-evaluated Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
 scores, Scoring Atopic
Dermatitis 
(
SCORAD
)
, and Global physician assessment 
(
GPA
)
, stratum corneum hydration, and transepidermal
water loss 
(
TEWL
)
. Tolerability was monitored through clinical evaluation. Follow-up occurred at baseline and
weeks 1, 2, and 4.
Results:
As of now 
(
April 2025
)
, only 33 from 40 participants has completed the study with 16 received a ceramide-
containing cream in MVE and 17 received the simple cream base. Baseline demographic and clinical characteristics
did not differ between the two groups. The ceramide-containing cream in MVE group demonstrated an earlier
significant reduction in corticosteroid use by week 2 
(
from 5.56 to 3.69 days/week; 
p = 0.020
)
, whereas the simple
cream base group reached a significant reduction by week 3 
(
from 5.76 to 3.73 days/week; 
p = 0.034
)
. Compared
to baseline, both groups demonstrated significant improvements in EASI, SCORAD, and GPA scores from week 1
onward, with greater percentage reductions across all clinical scores in the ceramide-containing cream in MVE
group. Stratum corneum hydration improved significantly from baseline in the ceramide-containing cream in MVE
group from week 1 and continued to increase throughout the study 
(
p=0.006
)
, whereas the simple cream base
group significantly improved only by week 4 
(
p=0.021
)
. TEWL in lesional site decreased significantly from baseline
in the ceramide-containing cream in MVE group by week one 
(
p=0.016
)
, while significant improvements in the
simple cream base group were found at week two 
(
p=0.027
)
. No adverse events were reported in either groups,

though two cream base participants withdrew due to worsening symptoms at week 2. The full results will be
presented in the conference.
Conclusion:
Addition of topical moisturizers demonstrated efficacy in reducing topical corticosteroid use, improving clinical
outcomes and enhancing skin barrier, with a more rapid onset of these effects from ceramide-containing MVE
cream compared to a simple cream base. This earlier therapeutic response highlights its benefits as a safe and
effective steroid-sparing adjunctive therapy for patients with mild-to-moderate AD.
