Introduction & Objectives:
Insights into the real-world treatment of atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 are key to clinical decision making. The aim of this
analysis was to characterize patients who receive dupilumab for moderate-to-severe AD in a real-world setting
within Canada.
Materials & Methods:
The CANDID phase 4 study is an ongoing, prospective, observational study of patients with moderate-to-severe
AD who receive dupilumab according to Canadian-specific prescribing information. We report baseline
characteristics, and treatment patterns for patients enrolled from October 4, 2023 to March 2, 2025. Analyses are
descriptive; no formal statistical comparisons were performed.
Results:
Data from 305 Canadian patients 
(
249 adults; 34 adolescents [aged 12-17 years]; 22 children [aged 6-11 years]
)
from 8 Canadian provinces were included in this analysis with 60 patients 
(
19.7%
)
 living in urban cities and 245
patients 
(
80.3%
)
 living in rural settings, and females accounting for 51.1% of patients enrolled.
Patients experienced a high disease burden before starting dupilumab at enrollment with mean 
(
± standard
deviation
)
 scores being 30.8% 
(
21.0%
)
 for body surface area, 20.9 
(
11.3
)
 for Eczema Area and Severity Index, 7.6
(
2.4
)
 for Peak Pruritus Numerical Rating Score, 6.6 
(
2.5
)
 for Skin Soreness Numerical Rating Score, 6.2 
(
2.69
)
 for
Sleep Disturbance Numerical Rating Score, 16.1 
(
5.4
)
 for Dermatology Life Quality Index [DLQI] total score, 15.2
(
5.6
)
 for Children
’
s DLQI, and 14.6 
(
7.5
)
 for Dermatitis Family Impact.*** In the previous 12 months, 97.8%, 39.2%,
and 25.4% patients were treated with topical corticosteroids, systemic corticosteroids, and conventional systemic
non-corticosteroid immunosuppressants, respectively.

Conclusion:
Canadian patients enrolled in CANDID demonstrated considerable multidimensional burden of disease across
signs/symptoms despite previous use of topical and systemic AD treatments. This is the first real world evidence
assessment of Canadian patients aged 6 years and older living in both urban and rural areas across the country,
demonstrating the burden of disease for Canadians living with atopic dermatitis.
