Introduction & Objectives:
The advent of targeted therapy has revolutionized the treatment of moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis. We aim
to study the long-term efficacy and safety of tralokinumab.
Materials & Methods:
We conducted a retrospective, multicenter study in the Community of Madrid, describing the clinical and
epidemiological characteristics of 209 patients with moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis, as well as efficacy
measured using the Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
, Investigator
’
s Global Assessment 
(
IGA
)
, and Peak-
Pruritus Numerical Rating Scale 
(
NRS pp
)
 over 52 weeks of treatment according to the approved technical
guidelines.
Results:
A total of 209 patients were included. Of these, 159 reached week 52 
(
30.14% discontinued due to lack of efficacy,

1.91% due to adverse events, and 1.43% for other reasons
)
. Data for 28 patients was available at week 104.
Baseline characteristics showed a predominance of males 
(
57.42%
)
, with a mean age of 37.46 years and an
average disease duration of 20 years. Atopic comorbidities were present in 55.98% of patients, most commonly
asthma and allergic conjunctivitis. Cardiovascular risk history was present in 11%, and 3.43% had a history of prior
tumors. The most common clinical phenotypes were classical flexural 
(
43.06%
)
 and generalized 
(
39.71%
)
. Notably,
80.38% of patients had not received prior advanced therapies.
At baseline, the mean EASI was 25.78, 91.86% had IGA scores of 3 or 4, and the mean NRS pp was 7.6. During
treatment, mean absolute EASI decreased to 9.24 at week 16, 6.03 at week 24, 5.89 at week 52, and 5.31 at week
104. At week 16, 48.45% of patients achieved IGA 0/1, increasing to 66.66% at week 52. Pruritus NRS decreased
from 7.61 at baseline to 4.13 at week 16, 3.63 at week 24, 3.53 at week 52, and 3.25 at week 104.
Better EASI responses at week 52 were observed in patients diagnosed at an older age 
(
p<0.005
)
. Female patients
of older age responded better, although this was not statistically significant.
Our data show that patients who achieve EASI 75 or EASI 90 at week 16 are more likely to maintain response at
week 52. 
(
p<0.005
)
. At week 52, 16.74% of patients were able to space out their treatment regimen.
Adverse events 
(
AEs
)
 occurred in 18% of patients by week 16 and in 6.5% by week 52. Most AEs occurred in the
early weeks of treatment, were mild, and most frequently involved ocular surface disorders.
Conclusion:
Tralokinumab appears to be a safe long-term treatment that maintains efficacy over time, as demonstrated in
clinical trials and supported by real-world evidence. Furthermore, early response at week 16 is predictive of
sustained response at week 52.
