Abstract N°: 7127
Efficacy and Safety of Abrocitinib Versus Dupilumab in Patients Who Self-Identified as Having Skin of
Colour: A Post Hoc Analysis of JADE DARE
Pedro Herranz
1
, Andrew Alexis*
2
, Kenji Kabashima
3
, Fang Wang
4
, Roger Edwards
5
, Gianluca Bonfanti
6
, Andrew
Selfridge
7
, Pinaki Biswas
7
, Brian Esparza
7
, Melissa Watkins
7
1
La Paz University Hospital, Autonomous University, Madrid, Spain
2
Weill Cornell Medical College, New York, United States
3
Graduate School of Medicine, Kyoto University, Kyoto, Japan
4
Dermatology Hospital, Southern Medical University, Guangzhou, China
5
Health Services Consulting Corporation, Boxborough, United States
6
Engineering Ingegneria Informatica, Milan, Italy
7
Pfizer Inc, New York, United States
Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disorder characterised by
recurrent eczematous lesions and intense pruritus. The oral, once-daily, Janus kinase 1-selective inhibitor
abrocitinib is efficacious and well-tolerated in patients 
(
pts
)
 with moderate-to-severe AD. Although AD is present
in pts of all racial backgrounds, clinical presentations of the disease and treatment efficacy vary among racial
subgroups. Variations in pigmentary changes, erythema, and inflammatory response underscore the need for
further contextualisation of treatment efficacy and safety in people with skin of colour 
(
SoC
)
 with AD. This study
aimed to assess short-term efficacy and safety of abrocitinib versus dupilumab in pts with SoC and moderate-to-
severe AD in the head-to-head phase 3 study, JADE DARE 
(
NCT04345367
)
.
Materials & Methods:
 This post hoc analysis included data from pts aged 
≥
18 years with moderate-to-severe AD
who received abrocitinib 200 mg or dupilumab 300 mg with concomitant background topical therapy for 26
weeks in JADE DARE. Pts who self-reported their race as non-White or did not report their race but identified as
Hispanic or Latino were categorised as having SoC, and pts who self-reported their race as White or did not report
their race but did not identify as Hispanic or Latino were categorised as White. Efficacy assessments included the
proportion of pts achieving 
≥
75% improvement from baseline in Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI-75
)
 score,
Investigator
’
s Global Assessment score of 0 
(
clear
)
 or 1 
(
almost clear
)
 
(
IGA 0/1
)
, 
≥
4-point improvement from
baseline in Peak Pruritus Numerical Rating Scale score 
(
PP-NRS4; used with permission from Regeneron
Pharmaceuticals, Inc., and Sanofi
)
, Dermatology Life Quality Index score of 0 or 1 
(
DLQI 0/1
)
, and the composite
endpoint of 
≥
90% improvement from baseline in EASI plus PP-NRS score of 0 or 1 
(
EASI-90 + PP-NRS 0/1
)
 to
Week 26. Safety was assessed via treatment-emergent adverse event 
(
TEAE
)
 monitoring.
Results:
 Of 727 pts in JADE DARE, 205 
(
28.2%
)
 were categorised as having SoC 
(
abrocitinib, n=91; dupilumab,
n=114
)
 and 522 
(
71.8%
)
 were categorised as White 
(
271; 251
)
. At Week 2, greater proportions of pts with SoC
treated with abrocitinib versus dupilumab achieved EASI-75 
(
abrocitinib, 28.6% vs dupilumab, 19.3%
)
, IGA 0/1
(
15.4% vs 6.1%
)
, PP-NRS4 
(
50.0% vs 27.2%
)
, DLQI 0/1 
(
22.5% vs 5.3%
)
, and EASI-90 + PP-NRS 0/1 
(
4.4% vs 0.9%
)
;
similar findings were reported for White pts 
(
EASI-75: 29.6% vs 22.2%; IGA 0/1: 14.1% vs 7.7%; PP-NRS4: 47.6% vs
24.8%; DLQI 0/1: 23.0% vs 6.9%; EASI-90 + PP-NRS 0/1: 6.3% vs 1.2%
)
. The proportions of pts achieving efficacy
responses were higher or comparable in pts treated with abrocitinib versus dupilumab through Week 26 of
treatment and responses were comparable across available skin colour categories 
(
Table
)
. TEAEs in the abrocitinib
and dupilumab groups, respectively, were reported by 71.4% and 61.4% of pts with SoC and 74.9% and 67.3% of
White pts. In both subgroups, acne and herpes zoster were reported more frequently with abrocitinib, while

conjunctivitis was reported more frequently with dupilumab 
(
Table
)
.
Conclusion:
 Regardless of skin colour, greater proportions of pts with moderate-to-severe AD achieved efficacy
responses with abrocitinib compared with dupilumab as early as Week 2 of treatment. The safety profiles of
abrocitinib and dupilumab were consistent with previous analyses of the overall population from JADE DARE.
