Introduction & Objectives:
 Patients with moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 have a longitudinal disease
course with fluctuating AD severity over time, and static assessments may not accurately capture disease severity.
The majority of patients with moderate-to-severe AD treated with systemic AD therapies experience recurrent
flares, with substantial disease burden during flares irrespective of baseline AD severity. Abrocitinib, an oral Janus
kinase 1-selective inhibitor is approved for the treatment of adolescents and adults with moderate-to-severe AD in
multiple regions including Europe. The objective of this study was to assess the frequency of breakthrough
disease flare activity and the severity of any such flare activity in patients receiving continued abrocitinib
treatment.
Materials & Methods:
 Data were included from patients randomly assigned to abrocitinib 
(
200 mg/100 mg
)
 or
placebo as monotherapy or in combination with topical therapy in the phase 3 JADE MONO-1 
(
NCT03349060
)
,
MONO-2 
(
