Abstract N°: 7163
Interpretable Deep Learning Framework for Automated EASI Scoring in Atopic Dermatitis Assessment
Molly Lucas
1
, Sharif Amit Kamran*
1
, Brendon Lutnick
1
, Chaitanya Parmar
1
, Basudha Pal
1, 2
, Asha Patel Shah
1
, David
Apfel
1
, Steve Fakharzadeh
1
, Lloyd Miller
1
, Kristopher Standish
1
, Gabriela Oana Cula
1
1
Johnson & Johnson, New Brunswick, United States
2
Johns Hopkins University, Baltimore, United States
Introduction & Objectives:
 The Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
 is a primary endpoint to assess severity
and response to treatment for Atopic Dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 patients in clinical trials. Traditionally, EASI scoring requires
in-person clinic visits, the scoring process is time-consuming, and scoring has inherent variability between
different raters and even for the same rater over time. This study addresses these challenges by introducing a
method for automated EASI scoring using photographs taken by patients remotely using a smart phone
application.
Materials & Methods:
 We developed a novel AI model based on Vision Transformers, a state-of-the-art deep
learning architecture, to automate EASI scoring using remotely captured photographs taken from smartphone. We
recruited AD patients for this observational study who met inclusion criteria: 1
)
 age 
≥
 18 years, 2
)
 not currently
pregnant, and 3
)
 adhere to taking full body photos. A smart phone application was used to take skin photos and
upload image sets 
(
44 images/set
)
 captured across the full body at up to four different time points. We
augmented the dataset by cropping each image into four quadrants, obtaining 176 images from the 44 images
per set. Data were divided into training/validation/test splits of 739/100/214 visits 
(
from 291/33/81 patients
)
. We
used Inter-class correlation coefficient 
(
ICC
)
 and Mean-absolute-error rate 
(
MAE
)
 to compare EASI scoring
outcomes 
(
range 0-72
)
 between the model and two sets of dermatologists 
(
Rater A and Rater B
)
 who remotely
assessed EASI scores using the image sets. Inter-rater variability was assessed by calculating ICC and MAE
between Rater A and Rater B.
Results:
 The study enrolled 681 AD patients. Each provided 1-4 skin image sets 
(
44 images/set
)
 taken remotely at
different time points, totaling 1390 image sets. After removing patients without active disease, 55,189 images
remained from 416 patients 
(
1088 image sets
)
. After removing incomplete or incorrect sets and blurry images,
46,332 images from 405 patients 
(
1053 image sets
)
 were used for model training and testing. Using the Fitzpatrick
scale, 66.9% of participants had Type I-III skin, and 33.1% had Type IV-Type VI skin. Mean EASI score was 3.25 +/-
4.91. The best model 
(
i.e., max validation ICC
)
 consists of a ViT pre-trained encoder block, followed by an
attention block that calculates the attention scores of each of the features and aggregates the scores. Finally, a
fully connected layer outputs a single EASI value 
(
range: 0-72
)
. We performed a three-way comparison between
our AI model, Rater A, and Rater B. The ICC for each comparison was: AI model vs Rater A: 74.7% [95% CI: 68%-
81%]; AI model vs Rater B: 66.5% [95% CI: 58%-73%]; and Rater A vs Rater B: 70.3% [95% CI: 63%-77%] and is
reported in Fig 1. And MAE is as follows: AI model vs Rater A: 2.28 +/- 3.33; AI model vs Rater B: 2.51 +/- 2.61; and
Rater A vs Rater B: 2.29 +/- 3.60.
Conclusion:
 Remote photography using smart phones could greatly reduce the burden of participating in and
conducting AD trials for both patients and clinicians. Our novel deep learning models, created and validated by
using remotely collected images, were comparable to ratings by board-certified dermatologists, highlighting their
potential utility as tools in clinical trials.

Figure 1. Three-way comparison between Rater A, Rater B, and AI Model. The inter-class correlation coefficient
(
ICC
)
 and mean-absolute error 
(
MAE
)
 of the Raters A vs AI model, the Raters B vs the AI model, and the Raters A
vs Raters B is reported.
