Abstract N°: 7188
Patient Perception of Life Course Impairment With Moderate-to-Severe Atopic Dermatitis
Alexa B. Kimball
1
, Min Yang
2
, Bruno Martins
2
, Ashley Holub
2
, Jingdong Chao
3
, Gaelle Le Bagousse-Bego
4
, Kerry
Noonan
5
, Jason Wang*
3
1
Harvard Medical School, Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center, Boston, MA, United States
2
Analysis Group, Boston, MA, United States
3
Regeneron Pharmaceuticals Inc., Tarrytown, NY, United States
4
Sanofi, Gentilly, France
5
Sanofi, Cambridge, MA, United States
Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic systemic disease associated with significant
clinical and psychological burden, which can lead to cumulative life course impairment 
(
CLCI
)
, defined as an
increasing disease burden over time that leads to irreversible effects such as negative impact on career trajectory,
missed life opportunities, and financial and social impact. However, there are limited data on the extent of CLCI
due to uncontrolled AD from the patient
’
s perspective. A qualitative interview study of adult patients with
moderate-to-severe AD was conducted to investigate AD-related life impact.
Materials & Methods:
 Adult patients with moderate-to-severe AD enrolled in RELIEVE-AD 
(
a prospective, long-
term survey study
)
 who completed the Year 6 survey, were invited to participate in qualitative one-on-one
interviews lasting up to 60 minutes. The semi-structured interview consisted of questions focusing on different
domains of life before and after initiating dupilumab, including disease experience; education, career, and financial
impact; relationships and social life; and psychological impact. Content analysis using ATLAS.ti was conducted to
analyze the qualitative data obtained from the interviews. Here we report the data on the impact of AD before
receiving dupilumab.
Results:
 50 patients participated in the interview study. The mean age was 26.3 years at the time of AD diagnosis
and 45.4 years at the time of dupilumab initiation, and 31 
(
62.0%
)
 were female participants. Among participants
diagnosed with AD before the age of 30 years, 65% of them reported a negative impact of AD on their education,
primarily in reduced concentration 
(
42%
)
 and lowered class attendance 
(
26%
)
. When asked about employment,
72% of all participants noted negative impacts, including reduced work performance 
(
34%
)
 and reduced
concentration 
(
28%
)
. Participants reported a negative impact of AD on their family relationships 
(
62%
)
, dating
(
58%
)
, friendships 
(
54%
)
, and intimacy 
(
32%
)
. AD also impacted patients
’
 daily life and ability to participate in
leisure activities 
(
100%
)
, clothing choices 
(
94%
)
, vacation planning 
(
58%
)
, as well as contributing to dietary
restrictions 
(
48%
)
. All participants discussed the negative impacts of AD on mental and emotional well-being, with
many reporting poor self-image 
(
78%
)
 and stress or worry 
(
76%
)
 often as a result of their AD symptoms.
Participants also reported shame or embarrassment 
(
56%
)
, and even hopelessness 
(
46%
)
 due to living with AD.
Conclusion:
 Participants reported substantial negative impact of AD on various domains of their lives such as
education and career, relationships, ability to live and enjoy a normal life, and mental and emotional well-being.
Due to the irreversible nature of some aspects of CLCI, clinical care of AD should focus on both physical and
psychosocial aspects of the disease and consider effective treatments early in the disease course to help minimize
CLCI.
