Abstract N°: 7201
Ceramide-Containing Moisturiser as Atopic Dermatitis Monotherapy: Clinical Efficacy and Tolerability in
Pediatric and Adult Patients
Tara Friel
1
, Ariana Bitton
1
, Duaa Eisa
1
, Nada Baalbaki
1
1
L
’
Oréal, New York, United States
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is the most common chronic inflammatory skin disease worldwide affecting 103 million
children and 101 million adults globally. Characterized by impaired skin barrier function and altered lipid
composition, AD results in increased transepidermal water loss 
(
TEWL
)
, reduced hydration, and increased
susceptibility to infection and inflammation, resulting in symptoms that can significantly impact patients
’
 quality of
life 
(
QoL
)
. Effective moisturisers play a crucial role in restoring skin barrier function and reducing reliance on more
potent medications such as corticosteroids. This study evaluated the clinical efficacy and tolerability of a
ceramide-containing moisturising cream in a diverse panel of pediatric and adult subjects with mild to moderate
AD.
Materials & Methods:
This before-and-after clinical study enrolled 102 subjects from Brazil with mild to moderate AD, including 48 adults
and 54 children aged 4 months to 63 years. At baseline, 22% of subjects presented with mild AD and 78% had
moderate AD. The study population comprised of Fitzpatrick skin phototypes I-VI. Subjects applied the ceramide-
containing moisturising cream twice daily for four weeks. Efficacy was assessed through instrumental
measurements of transepidermal water loss 
(
TEWL
)
 and skin hydration. Clinical evaluations included AD severity
score 
(
SCORAD
)
, Overall Dryness Score 
(
ODS
)
, Scaling, Roughness, Redness, and Cracks Sum Score 
(
SRRC
)
, and
subject-reported itch assessment. Both clinical and instrumental evaluations were assessed at baseline,
immediately after application, at week 2, week 4, and 3- and 7-day regression. Tolerability was evaluated via
subjective patient reporting and investigator assessment at week 2 and 4. QoL was measured using a validated
questionnaire.
Results:
The ceramide-containing moisturising cream demonstrated statistically significant improvements across all
parameters. SCORAD scores showed significant 38% reduction at 2 weeks and 51% at 4 weeks, with sustained
improvement observed even after discontinuation 
(
36% reduction at 3-day regression and 21% reduction at 1-
week regression
)
 
(
p<0.001
)
. SRRC exhibited similar significant reductions 
(
39% at week 2, 50% at week 4, 31% at
3-day regression, and 19.9% at 1-week regression
)
, consistent across both mild and moderate AD subgroups
(
p<0.001
)
. ODS revealed quick and significant improvement in overall dryness, with a 32% reduction immediately
after application, further improving to 29% at week 4 and maintaining a 12% improvement at the 3 3-day
regression 
(
p<0.001
)
. Visual symptoms as assessed by the investigator, significantly improved after 4 weeks of
treatment. Subject-reported average and worst itch within 24 hours showed significant improvement. The
ceramide-containing moisturising cream resulted in significant improvements in QoL, with a 61% increase at week
2 and a 79% increase at week 4 
(
p<0.001
)
. Excellent tolerability was demonstrated throughout the study.
Conclusion:
This study demonstrates the efficacy and tolerability of a ceramide-containing moisturising cream in managing

mild to moderate AD across a diverse patient population. The significant improvements observed in SCORAD,
SRRC, ODS, and patient-reported itch, coupled with the sustained benefits after discontinuation of use, highlight
the ceramide-containing moisturizer
’
s ability to effectively address AD symptoms and enhance QoL for a diverse
patient population.
