Abstract N°: 7234
Clinical Evaluation of the Efficacy and Tolerance of a Novel Topical Cream for Managing Sensitive,
Weakened and Compromised Skin Barrier
Zoe Draelos
1
, Ana Du
2
, Komal Patel
2
, Deborah Orak
2
1
Dermatology Consulting Services, High Point, United States
2
L
’
Oreal Research and Innovation, Clark, United States
Introduction & Objectives:
Chronic inflammatory skin conditions, characterized by dryness, pruritus, and inflammation, negatively impact
quality of skin and necessitate effective yet well-tolerated topical treatments. Long-term management is often
hampered by poor adherence due to unpleasant product aesthetics, highlighting the need for elegant, efficacious
formulations that seamlessly integrate into daily routines. The present study evaluates a lightweight, non-greasy,
oil-in-water cream combining colloidal oatmeal, bisabolol, panthenol, and squalane in a single formulation for its
efficacy in alleviating dryness, redness, discomfort, and improving skin quality in these conditions.
Materials & Methods:
A four-week, single-center, double-blinded, controlled clinical study was conducted on 62 subjects 
(
26 male, 36
female
)
, aged 15-27, with Fitzpatrick phototypes I-VI and diverse ethnicities, all reporting sensitive, eczema-prone
skin and a compromised skin barrier. Inclusion criteria included mild to moderate visible erythema,
dryness/desquamation, rough iskin texture, and lack of resilience and suppleness.
Subjects applied the investigational topical cream twice daily to facial skin. Expert grading and high resolution
clinical photographic were performed and captured at baseline, immediately post- first application, 8 hours post-
fist application, and at weeks 1, 2, and 4. Subjective assessments via questionnaires were collected concurrently
with clinical evaluations. Transepidermal water loss 
(
TEWL
)
 measurements were obtained from a randomly
assigned cheek at baseline, weeks 1, 2, and 4. Objective and subjective tolerance evaluations were conducted at
baseline and week 4. 
Results:
After 1 week of product use, statistically significant improvements 
(
p<0.01
)
 compared to baseline were observed
for visible redness 
(
-29%
)
, dryness/desquamation 
(
-62%
)
, rough skin texture 
(
-43%
)
, resiliency 
(
+38%
)
,
suppleness 
(
+40%
)
, overall skin quality 
(
+40%
)
. TEWL also decreased significantly compared to baseline after 1
week of use 
(
-17%, p<0.01
)
. These improvements were sustained and further enhanced through week 4. 
Objective and subjective tolerance indicated excellent tolerability of twice daily use. No product related adverse
events were reported.    
Subject self-assessments demonstrated a high level of agreement that the product possesses a melting,
lightweight, non-greasy texture, while providing a soothing and protective sensation on compromised, sensitive
skin.
Conclusion:
This oil-in-water cream containing colloidal oatmeal, bisabolol, panthenol and squalane demonstrated significant
and sustained improvements in dryness, redness, skin texture, resilience, and suppleness in sensitive, eczema-

prone skin, along with reduced TEWL and excellent tolerability, supporting its potential as an effective and
cosmetically elegant solution for managing chronic inflammatory skin conditions.
