Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic systemic disease associated with significant
disease burden, which can lead to cumulative life course impairment 
(
CLCI
)
, a persisting and accumulating disease
burden over time, leading to irreversible effects on various domains of patient life. There are limited data on the
extent of AD on patients
’
 CLCI and how effective treatments may positively affect the lives of these patients. Here
we report the patients
’
 perspective relating to the impact of dupilumab on various aspects of their lives due to
improvement in AD.
Materials & Methods:
 Adult patients with moderate-to-severe AD enrolled in RELIEVE-AD 
(
a prospective, long-
term survey study
)
 who completed the Year 6 survey, were invited to participate in qualitative one-on-one
interviews lasting up to 60 minutes. The semi-structured interview consisted of questions focusing on different
domains of life before and after initiating dupilumab, including disease experience; education, career, and financial
impact; relationships and social life; and psychological impact. Content analysis using ATLAS.ti was conducted to
analyze the qualitative data obtained from the interviews. Here we present the data on the impact of AD following
dupilumab treatment.
Results:
 50 patients participated in the interview study. The mean age was 26.3 years at the time of AD diagnosis
and 45.4 years at the time of dupilumab initiation, and 31 
(
62.0%
)
 were female participants. When asked about
employment, 54% of the participants reported a positive impact after dupilumab initiation, including improved
concentration 
(
28%
)
 and improved work performance 
(
18%
)
. Participants reported improvement in family
relationships 
(
46%
)
, friendships 
(
42%
)
, and their dating life 
(
36%
)
 after dupilumab treatment. Participants also
reported improved daily life/leisure activities 
(
92%
)
, and they experienced improved vacations 
(
52%
)
, fewer
clothing restrictions 
(
74%
)
, and improved diet 
(
32%
)
 after dupilumab treatment. Participants reported improved
mental and emotional well-being after dupilumab, with many reporting less stress/worry 
(
78%
)
, increased
happiness 
(
66%
)
, and improved self-image 
(
66%
)
.
Conclusion:
 Participants reported an improvement in various domains of their life after the initiation of
dupilumab treatment, such as career, relationships, ability to live and enjoy life, and mental and emotional well-
being. The use of effective AD treatment in patients with moderate and severe AD may reduce the effects of CLCI.
