Hospital Alemán, Ciudad Autónoma de Buenos Aires, Argentina
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is the most prevalent chronic inflammatory dermatosis across all age groups. Skin barrier
dysfunction plays a pivotal role in AD pathophysiology. Reflectance confocal microscopy 
(
RCM
)
 offers a non-
invasive, real-time imaging tool to assess epidermal and superficial dermal changes.
The aim of this study is to evaluate the association between skin characteristics assessed by RCM and severity
scores in patients with moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis.
Materials & Methods:
A prospective multicenter cohort study was initiated in April 2024 to evaluate patients with moderate-to-severe
atopic dermatitis, inadequately controlled and with indication for systemic therapy. This long-term project
presents interim results describing the baseline characteristics of currently enrolled participants. Clinimetry,
patient-reported outcomes 
(
PROs
)
, and reflectance confocal microscopy 
(
RCM
)
 images were obtained at baseline.
All RCM imaging was performed at a single hospital.
Results:
Twenty-six consecutives patients with moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis were enrolled. Twenty were male
(
76.9%
)
. The median age at study enrollment was 16 years 
(
IQR 10
–
26
)
. Clinical phenotypes, comorbidities, and
prior treatments are summarized in Table 1. At baseline, the median 
(
IQR
)
 scores were: EASI 29 
(
19
–
33
)
, DLQI 11.5
(
8
–
17
)
, ADCT 13.5 
(
10
–
20
)
, SCORAD 64 
(
51
–
79
)
, and IGA 3 
(
3
–
4
)
.
Main findings on reflectance confocal microscopy 
(
RCM
)
 included disorganized epidermis and spongiosis in all
patients 
(
26, 100%
)
, exocytosis 
(
24, 92.3%
)
, inflammatory infiltrates in the epidermis 
(
24, 92.3%
)
, and
inflammatory infiltrates in the dermis 
(
7, 26.9%
)
. Other findings were loss of papillary edges 
(
22, 84.6%
)
,
acanthosis 
(
14, 53.8%
)
, hyperkeratosis 
(
14, 53.8%
)
, parakeratosis 
(
13, 50%
)
, dilated vessels 
(
26, 100%
)
, dermal
melanophages 
(
1, 3.8%
)
, and vesicles 
(
3, 11.5%
)
.

Conclusion:
Reflectance confocal microscopy emerges as a valuable, non-invasive tool for assessing skin barrier alterations in
atopic dermatitis. Baseline findings
—
including inflammatory infiltrate, spongiosis, and dermoepidermal junction
irregularities
—
highlight its potential for real-time disease monitoring during systemic therapy. These preliminary
results support the integration of RCM into personalized treatment strategies. Further studies are needed to
validate the predictive value of RCM findings for therapeutic outcomes.
