Abstract N°: 7420
Dupilumab Monotherapy Improves Multiple Skin Measures in Skin of Color Patients With Atopic
Dermatitis as Measured by Line-Field Optical Coherence Tomography
Orit Markowitz*
1, 2
, Nicole Shiao
2
, Stephane David Levy
3
, Kwinten Bosman
4
, Brad Shumel
3
1
Columbia University, New York, NY, United States
2
OptiSkin, New York, NY, United States
3
Regeneron Pharmaceuticals Inc., Tarrytown, NY, United States
4
Sanofi, Amsterdam, Netherlands
Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 presents with distinct clinical manifestations in skin of color
(
SoC
)
 patients, which may contribute to diagnostic uncertainty and complicate assessment of disease severity and
treatment response 
(
Kaufman BP, et al. 
Exp Dermatol
. 2018
)
. Detailed evaluation of unique clinical features and
mechanisms of AD progression in SoC in response to dupilumab treatment can provide valuable insight into
disease activity and management in this patient population. In this study, we evaluated AD lesional, perilesional,
and post-inflammatory pigmentary skin structural changes in SoC patients with moderate-to-severe AD before
and after dupilumab treatment using non-invasive line-field confocal optical coherence tomography 
(
LC-OCT
)
.
Materials & Methods:
 10 patients 
(
aged 
≥
18 years, Fitzpatrick skin type IV/V/VI
)
 from the phase 4, open-label
DISCOVER trial 
(
NCT05590585
)
 participated in this investigator-initiated single-center analysis. Patients received
dupilumab 200/300 mg q2w monotherapy for 24 weeks.
Representative areas of AD lesional 
(
Eczema Area and Severity Index [EASI] erythema and papulation severity
subscores > 0
)
, post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation 
(
PIH; Post-Inflammatory Hyperpigmentation Severity Scale
[PHSS] > 1 and EASI erythema and papulation severity subscores = 0
)
, and non-lesional 
(
PHSS 
≤
 1 and EASI
erythema and papulation severity subscores = 0
)
 skin were imaged by LC-OCT 
(
2
–
3 3D stacks per site
)
 at baseline,
and at Weeks 4, 16, and 24.
Changes in skin layer 
(
stratum corneum [SC] thickness, epidermal thickness, and dermal
–
epidermal junction [DEJ]
undulation
)
 and keratinocyte nuclei 
(
volume, shape, atypia
)
 metrics were assessed through segmentation and
measurement analysis 
(
DAMAE Medical
)
.
Results:
 Compared to non-lesional skin at baseline, AD lesional skin had thicker SC 
(
mean 19.49 µm; 35% thicker
)
and thicker total epidermis 
(
mean 172.56 µm; 73% thicker
)
, and had more highly undulated DEJ 
(
mean 97%; 134%
more undulated
)
. AD lesional skin also contained enlarged keratinocyte nuclei in the epidermis 
(
mean 161.75 µm³;
6% larger vs non-lesional skin
)
 and an atypical keratinocyte network 
(
mean 0.316; 47% more atypical vs non-
lesional skin
)
. While no statistically significant differences were observed, all PIH skin metrics were intermediate
between those of AD lesional and non-lesional sites.
At Week 24, AD lesional sites showed a progressive improvement with dupilumab treatment, with metrics
approaching normal skin values. SC thickness differences between AD, PIH, and non-lesional areas were no longer
significant. While values remained significantly higher in AD lesional areas vs non-lesional areas, decreases in total
epidermal thickness and DEJ undulation were observed 
(
31.5% and 32.8%, respectively
)
. There were no significant
differences in keratinocyte metrics in lesional vs non-lesional skin after treatment.
Conclusion:
 In SoC patients with AD, dupilumab monotherapy resulted in progressive improvements in multiple
skin measures, with lesional skin approaching normal skin values, as measured by in vivo 3D skin imaging

techniques.
