Complutense, Madrid, Spain
Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disease characterized by
inflammation, intense itching, and a disrupted skin barrier. Although topical corticosteroids remain the standard
treatment, their long-term use is associated with adverse effects. This meta-analysis assesses the efficacy and
safety of ruxolitinib cream, a topical JAK1/JAK2 inhibitor, compared to vehicle in AD treatment.
Materials & Methods:
 This systematic review and meta-analysis, registered in PROSPERO 
(
CRD420251026000
)
,
followed the Cochrane Handbook and PRISMA guidelines. PubMed, Embase, and Cochrane were searched for
randomized controlled trials 
(
RCTs
)
 up to April 2025. Primary endpoints included Investigator
’
s Global Assessment
- Treatment Success 
(
IGA-TS
)
, and 75% improvement in the Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI75
)
 at weeks 4
and 8. Secondary endpoints included a 4-point improvement in pruritis Numeric Rating Scale 
(
NRS
)
 at week 8,
and the incidence of at least one treatment-emergent adverse event 
(
TEAE
)
. A DerSimonian-Laird random-effects
model was used, with heterogeneity assessed using I2. Statistical analyses were performed using Studio.
Results:
 We included 1,912 patients from 5 RCTs, with ages ranging from 2 to 70 years and 60.25% of them being
female.** Ruxolitinib significantly increased IGA-TS achievement at 4 weeks 
(
RR 4.56; 95% CI 3.01
–
6.92; p<0.001
)
and 8 weeks 
(
RR 4.00; 95% CI 2.97
–
5.38; p<0.001
)
. Subgroup analysis showed no significant difference between
adolescents/adults and children 
(
p=0.35
)
, though both groups benefited 
(
RR 5.07; 95% CI 3.12
–
8.25; p<0.001 and
RR 3.43; 95% CI 1.77
–
6.67; p<0.00001, respectively
)
. No significant dosage-based variation was observed 
(
p=0.08
)
.
Regarding EASI75, outcomes improved at both 4 weeks 
(
RR 3.10; 95% CI 1.79
–
5.38; p<0.001
)
 and 8 weeks 
(
RR
3.16; 95% CI 2.21
–
4.51; p<0.001
)
, with heterogeneity reduced by sensitivity analysis. Benefits were significant in
adolescents/adults 
(
RR 2.30; 95% CI 1.08
–
4.89; p=0.001
)
 and children 
(
RR 4.69; 95% CI 2.43
–
9.05; p<0.00001
)
,
with no difference between groups 
(
p=0.16
)
 or dosage levels 
(
p=0.36
)
. Trial sequential analysis for IGA-TS and
EASI75 confirmed that the required information size was reached and the Z-curve crossed the monitoring
boundary, supporting the robustness of the findings and favoring ruxolitinib. Ruxolitinib also improved pruritus
NRS by 
≥
4 points 
(
RR 2.39; 95% CI 1.62
–
3.53; p<0.001
)
. Finally, the incidence of at least one TEAE did not differ
significantly between groups 
(
RR 0.87; 95% CI 0.74
–
1.03; p=0.10
)
, though adolescents/adults had fewer TEAEs 
(
RR
0.83; 95% CI 0.69
–
1.00; p=0.04
)
, and children showed a nonsignificant increase 
(
RR 1.14; 95% CI 0.74
–
1.75;
p=0.55
)
.

Conclusion:
 Our study suggests that ruxolitinib significantly increases the likelihood of achieving IGA-TS, EASi75,
and a 4-point change in pruritis NRS, without a significant increase in the risk of TEAEs. Further trials are needed
to determine the optimal dosage and assess its safety in children.


