Abstract N°: 7425
evaluation of serum ige levels and peripheral eosinophilia in seborrheic dermatitis: a case-control study
Ezgi Akta
ş
1
, ay
ş
e elif tapkı
1
, seyma gurbuz
1
, mehmet alp matur
2
, elif moustafa
1
, yavuz semiz
3
1
University of Health Sciences Ba
ş
ak
ş
ehir Çam ve Sakura City Hospital, Dermatology and Venereology, Istanbul,
Türkiye
2
University of Health Sciences Gaziosmanpa
ş
a Training and Research Hospital, Dermatology and Venereology,
Istanbul, Türkiye
3
University of Health Sciences, Haseki Training and Research Hospital, Dermatology and Venereology, Istanbul,
Türkiye
Introduction & Objectives:
Seborrheic dermatitis 
(
SD
)
 is a chronic, inflammatory, and recurrent skin disorder in which various factors
contribute to pathogenesis. Immunoglobulin E 
(
IgE
)
 is a molecule that contributes to allergic and inflammatory
responses. Atopy, defined as a genetically determined tendency to develop exaggerated immune responses to
environmental allergens, is characterized by elevated levels of IgE antibodies and can lead to the development of
diseases such as atopic dermatitis, allergic rhinitis, and asthma. Eosinophils are multifunctional leukocytes involved
in the regulation of immune responses, particularly in allergic inflammation. It is thought that some shared
mechanisms may play a role in the pathophysiology of SD and atopic conditions, both of which are chronic,
inflammatory, and recurrent in nature. This study aimed to investigate the presence of atopic diseases such as
allergic rhinitis, conjunctivitis, and asthma in SD patients without atopic dermatitis or other inflammatory skin
disorders, and to assess their serum IgE levels and peripheral eosinophil counts as a marker of atopy.
Materials & Methods:
This prospective, single-center, case-control study included 83 SD patients and 69 age- and sex-matched healthy
controls. Participants with any additional inflammatory dermatosis including atopic dermatitis, systemic
inflammatory disease, or systemic medication use were excluded.
Results:
There were no significant differences in terms of age or sex between the SD and control groups 
(
p = 0.62 and p =
0.76, respectively
)
. No significant differences were observed between two groups regarding history of
conjunctivitis, rhinitis, or asthma 
(
p = 0.487, p = 0.229, p = 0.557; respectively
)
. Total serum IgE levels were
significantly higher in SD patients compared to controls 
(
301.67 ± 935.64 kU/L vs. 70.34 ± 78.46 kU/L; p = 0.018
)
.
Both the serum eosinophil percentage and absolute eosinophil count were significantly higher in SD patients 
(
p =
0.00, p = 0.036; respectively
)
. A significant positive correlation was observed in the patient group between the
facial scores of the Seborrheic Dermatitis Area Severity Index 
(
SASI
)
 and both serum eosinophil count and
percentage 
(
r = 0.360, p = 0.001; r = 0.233, p = 0.034, respectively
)
. A positive and significant correlation was also
found between total SASI scores 
(
face + scalp
)
 and absolute serum eosinophil count 
(
r = 0.265, p = 0.016
)
. Serum
IgE levels were positively correlated with both absolute eosinophil count and eosinophil percentage 
(
p = 0.045, r
= 0.221; p = 0.044, r = 0.221; respectively
)
.
Conclusion:
In this study, total serum IgE levels were significantly higher in SD patients than in healthy controls. The elevated
eosinophil levels and their correlation with disease severity may indicate an eosinophil-driven inflammatory

component in a subset of SD patients. The elevated IgE levels and eosinophil counts, known to be associated with
allergic and atopic processes, suggest that allergens and hypersensitivity responses may play a greater role in the
pathogenesis of SD than previously thought.
