Introduction & Objectives:
While randomized controlled trials have investigated the efficacy and safety of upadacitinib for atopic dermatitis
(
AD
)
, real-world data remains limited. We conducted a retrospective multicenter study of upadacitinib for AD as
per the minimal disease activity 
(
MDA
)
 criteria.
Materials & Methods:
Our retrospective multicenter study included adult and adolescent patients with AD from three Canadian
institutions who were initiated on upadacitinib treatment. The primary outcome was achievement of optimal
targets at week 16±8 as per the MDA criteria, which included achievement of 1 clinician-reported outcome
(
Eczema Area and Severity Index [EASI] improvement of 90% [EASI90], EASI 
≤
3, or IGA 0/1 and body surface area
[BSA] 
≤
2%
)
 and 1 patient-reported outcome Worst Pruritus Numeric Rating Scale [WP-NRS] 0/1 or Dermatology
Life Quality Index [DLQI] 0/1
)
. A nonresponder imputation 
(
NRI
)
 analysis was used to account for missing data.
Safety was assessed via incidence of adverse events 
(
AEs
)
.
Results:
This analysis included a total of 222 patients. The mean age was 45 
(
range: 12-79
)
 years, with 49.1% 
(
109/222
)
being male. At weeks 16±8: optimal targets for MDA were achieved by 70.7% 
(
157/222
)
 of patients in total.
Clinician-reported outcomes 
(
EASI90, EASI 
≤
3, or IGA 0/1 and BSA 
≤
2%
)
 were achieved by 81.1% 
(
180/222
)
,
while patient-reported outcomes 
(
WP-NRS 0/1 or DLQI 0/1
)
 were achieved by 74.8% 
(
161/222
)
.
In total, 40 treatment-emergent adverse events 
(
TEAEs
)
 occurred 
(
18%, 40/222
)
. The most common AEs were
elevated creatine kinase 
(
2.7%, 6/222
)
, elevated triglycerides 
(
2.3%, 5/222
)
, acne 
(
1.4%, 3/222
)
, folliculitis 
(
1.4%,
3/222
)
, herpes simplex 
(
1.4%, 3/222
)
, and myalgia/arthragia 
(
1.4%, 3/222
)
. Six treatment discontinuations 
(
2.7%
)
were noted 
(
AEs; myalgia/arthralgia [n=2], new-onset lung malignancy [n=1], uveitis [n=1], and venous
thromboembolism [n=1]
)
. No serious infections, hypersensitivity reactions, major adverse cardiac events, or
hepatic abnormalities were observed during the treatment period analyzed.
Conclusion:
Our real-world effectiveness outcomes indicate that the majority of patients with AD treated with upadacitinib
achieved at least one clinician- and patient-reported outcome as per the optimal targets for MDA criteria at weeks
16±8. Study limitations include its retrospective nature and a lack of complete real-world documentation for
certain metrics 
(
e.g. WP-NRS
)
.

