Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
, a systemic type 2 inflammatory disease, is characterized by
significant skin barrier dysfunction. Previous reports show that, compared with healthy persons, patients with
moderate-to-severe AD have altered lipid composition in their stratum corneum 
(
SC
)
, which contains significantly
fewer ceramides with very long-chain fatty acids that are critical for lipid matrix assembly and skin barrier integrity.
This analysis reports the impact of dupilumab treatment on SC lipid structure and skin barrier function in patients
aged 6 to 11 years with moderate-to-severe AD.
Materials & Methods:
 PELISTAD 
(
NCT04718870
)
 was an open-label, exploratory study on skin barrier function in
children aged 6 to 11 years with moderate-to-severe AD, who were treated with dupilumab for 16 weeks 
(
follow-
up: 12 weeks
)
 based on baseline patient weight 
(
300 mg every 4 weeks: 
≥
15 kg to <30 kg; 200 mg every 2 weeks:
≥
30 kg to <60 kg
)
 and matched with healthy volunteers. Transepidermal water loss 
(
TEWL, g/m2/h
)
 area under
the curve 
(
AUC
)
 20 was calculated as an integral of TEWL measurements at baseline and after 5, 10, 15, and 20
skin tape strippings and was assessed longitudinally in lesional and non-lesional skin of patients with AD treated
with dupilumab, and in healthy skin. Lipid structure was assessed by lipid chain conformation in the SC using
attenuated total reflectance Fourier transform infrared spectroscopic measurements. This is based on the
frequency of the lipid peak, ranging from low frequency 
(
~2,848 cm-1, associated with tight lipid conformation
)
to high frequency 
(
~2,853 cm-1, associated with loose lipid conformation
)
. Least squares 
(
LS
)
 means were
obtained based on mixed models for repeated measures. All 
P
 values were nominal and there was no adjustment
for multiple testing.
Results:
 At baseline, mean 
(
standard error [SE]
)
 TEWL AUC20 was significantly higher in the lesional skin of
patients with AD 
(
1,440.0 [119.0]
)
 than in healthy skin 
(
431.3 [58.7]; 
P
 < 0.0001
)
. Following 16 weeks of dupilumab
treatment, a significant improvement in mean TEWL AUC20 was observed in lesional skin 
(
903.5 [72.3]; 
P
 < 0.001
)
,
which was sustained through Week 28 
(
797.5 [71.9]; 
P
 < 0.001
)
. At Week 16, there was no significant difference in
LS mean 
(
SE
)
 TEWL AUC20 between lesional 
(
754.2 [76.0]
)
 and healthy skin 
(
648.8 [91.6]; 
P
 = 0.59
)
. By Week 28,
LS mean TEWL AUC20 in lesional skin 
(
650.5 [79.8]
)
 was still comparable to that of healthy skin 
(
611.4 [92.0]; 
P
 =
0.77
)
. Mean 
(
standard deviation [SD]
)
 baseline measurements of lesional SC showed high frequency of the lipid
peak 
(
2,850.1 cm-1 [0.7]
)
, characteristic of a disorganized lipid chain conformation associated with greater SC
permeability, compared with the low frequency 
(
2,848.9 cm-1 [0.5]
)
 observed in healthy SC, indicative of a tightly
organized lipid structure 
(
P
 < 0.0001
)
. By Week 16, the mean peak frequency observed in lesional SC had

significantly reduced 
(
2,849.4 cm-1 [0.8]; 
P
 < 0.01
)
, and persisted to Week 28 
(
2,849.3 cm-1 [0.6]; 
P
 < 0.0001
)
. A
strong positive correlation was shown between lipid chain conformation and TEWL AUC20 
(
Pearson correlation
coefficient = 0.71; 
P
 < 0.001
)
.
Conclusion:
 Dupilumab treatment restores SC lipid structure and improves skin barrier function in patients aged 6
to 11 years with moderate-to-severe AD, highlighting the importance of the lipid structure in establishing proper
skin barrier function. Improvements were sustained for up to 3 months after the end of treatment.
