Abstract N°: 7482
Oral Probiotics as an Adjunctive Therapy in Pediatric Atopic Dermatitis
Sunyoung Woo*
1
1
Tashkent Pediatric Medical Institute, Dermatovenereology, Tashkent
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic skin disease marked by itching, barrier disruption, and frequent flare-ups.
Recent studies point to a link between microbial dysbiosis and worsening symptoms, especially involving the gut-
skin axis. Probiotics are being explored as a safe adjunct to standard care, but clinical data from low-resource
settings remain limited.
To examine whether an 8-week oral probiotic course could reduce disease severity and flare frequency in children
receiving conventional treatment for AD.
Materials & Methods:
We conducted a randomized controlled trial at a medical center, involving 20 children 
(
aged 1
–
18 years
)
diagnosed with moderate to severe atopic dermatitis. Participants were randomly assigned to two groups. The
intervention group received standard care 
(
emollients and topical corticosteroids as needed
)
 plus a daily oral
probiotic containing Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium strains 
(
1×10¹
⁰
 CFU
)
 for 8 weeks. The control group
received standard care alone.
SCORAD scores were assessed at baseline and after 8 weeks. Flare-up episodes were documented throughout the
study, along with caregiver-reported symptoms, including itching and sleep disturbance. Although microbiome
profiling was not available due to resource constraints, the study offers real-world insight into the clinical utility of
probiotics in pediatric AD, particularly in low-resource healthcare settings.
Results:
Eighteen children completed the study 
(
9 in each group
)
. After 8 weeks, the probiotic group saw a greater
reduction in SCORAD scores 
(–
51.8% vs. 
–
27.3%; p<0.05
)
. They also had fewer flare-ups 
(
0.7 vs. 1.6
)
, and more
participants remained flare-free during the study 
(
55.6% vs. 22.2%
)
. Caregivers in the probiotic group more often
reported improvements in itching and sleep. No adverse effects were observed.
Conclusion:
In this study, oral probiotics showed promise as a safe, helpful addition to standard therapy in pediatric AD.
Children receiving probiotics had fewer symptoms and flare-ups. These findings are especially relevant in low-
resource settings, where access to advanced diagnostic tools is limited. Larger trials are needed to confirm these
benefits and better understand the role of microbial balance in AD.
