Abstract N°: 7492
Comparative real-world sociodemographic and severity profiles of atopic dermatitis patientstreated with
lebrikizumab
Clara Ureña-Paniego
1
, Julia Castro Martín
1
, Francisco Javier León Pérez
1
, Salvador Arias-Santiago
1
, Trinidad
Montero-Vilchez
1
1
Virgen de las Nieves, Granada
Comparative real-world sociodemographic and severity profiles of atopic dermatitis patients treated with
lebrikizumab
Introduction & Objectives:
Lebrikizumab, an anti
–
IL-13 monoclonal antibody, has emerged as a promising treatment for moderate-to-severe
atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
. However, real-world data comparing the profiles of patients selected for lebrikizumab
versus other therapies are limited. The aim of this study is to compare the sociodemographic characteristics and
baseline disease severity of AD patients treated with lebrikizumab versus those receiving other treatments
(
including other advanced therapies, systemic or topical corticosteroids
)
 in a real-world setting.
Materials & Methods:
A cross-sectional analysis including patients with AD attending our Atopic Dermatitis Unit was conducted. Patients
receiving lebrikizumab were compared to those on other treatments. Variables analyzed included age, sex, familial
atopy, age at disease onset, and baseline severity scores: POEM, DLQI, ADCT, IGA, EASI, and SCORAD
Results:
A total of 262 patients were included 
(
5 receiving lebrikizumab
)
. Lebrikizumab patients had a mean age of 50.2
(
24.3
)
 years versus 26.5 
(
14.1
)
 years in the non-lebrikizumab group, with later disease onset 
(
35.4 [35.4] vs. 19.9
[24.7] years
)
. Male sex was slightly more frequent among lebrikizumab patients 
(
60%, 3/5
)
 compared to controls
(
45.5%, 117/257
)
. Baseline severity scores were higher in the lebrikizumab group: POEM 18.0 
(
8.7
)
 vs. 14.1 
(
7.9
)
;
DLQI 10.3 
(
11.2
)
 vs. 7.8 
(
6.1
)
; ADCT 17.3 
(
9.9
)
 vs. 10.5 
(
6.2
)
; IGA median 3.0 
(
IQR 3.0
–
3.0
)
 vs. 2.0 
(
IQR 1.0
–
3.0
)
; EASI
21.8 
(
1.4
)
 vs. 12.2 
(
18.5
)
; SCORAD 54.6 
(
11.4
)
 vs. 36.2 
(
23.0
)
. Baseline systemic treatment exposure was higher in
the lebrikizumab group compared to controls: ciclosporin 60% vs. 13.2%; dupilumab 40% vs. 6.2%; tralokinumab
40% vs. 1.2%; upadacitinib 20% vs. 2.7%. No prior methotrexate, baricitinib or abrocitinib use was recorded in the
lebrikizumab group.
Conclusion:
 ** 
 
 
In this real-life cohort, patients selected for lebrikizumab were older, with later disease onset,
higher disease severity, and greater prior exposure to systemic and biologic therapies compared to those on other
treatments. These findings suggest that lebrikizumab is being prescribed in a more treatment-refractory
population in real-world practice, highlighting its potential role in managing difficult-to-treat cases.
