Abstract N°: 7497
Safety of abrocitinib in adolescent patients with moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis in India: Findings
from an MRI sub study of a multicenter, randomized, open-label parallel-group study
Saswati Halder
1
, Ramesh Bhat*
2
, Somesh Gupta
3
, Davinder Parsad
4
, Pradyumna Vaidya
5
, Herwig Koppensteiner
6
,
Andrew Selfridge
7
, Pinaki Biswas
8
, Komal Gaur
9
, Charles Adhav
10
1
Calcutta school of tropical medicine, Kolkata, India
2
Father Muller Medical College , Mangalore, India
3
All India institute of medical sciences, Delhi, India
4
Post graduate institute of medical education and research, Chandigarh, India
5
Jehangir Clinical Development Center, Pune, India
6
5Pfizer Corporation , Vienna, Austria
7
Pfizer Inc, New York, India
8
Pfizer Inc. , New York, United States
9
Pfizer Limited, Mumbai, India
10
Pfizer limited, Mumbai, India
Introduction and Rationale
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin condition characterized by dry skin, pruritus, and defective
skin barrier function. Systemic therapies are often required for moderate-to-severe cases when topical treatments
fail. Current systemic agents in India have limited efficacy and are associated with adverse events 
(
AEs
)
 that
restrict long-term use. Abrocitinib is an oral, once-daily 
(
QD
)
 Janus kinase 1 
(
JAK1
)
 selective inhibitor for treating
moderate-to-severe AD.
Materials and Methods
In this substudy of a randomized, open-label, parallel-group study 
(
NCT05375929
)
 potential effects of abrocitinib
on bone development in adolescents using knee magnetic resonance imaging 
(
MRI
)
 were assessed. All adolescent
participants 12 to <18 years of age continued to receive abrocitinib 
(
100 or 200 mg
)
 as a monotherapy or
combination therapy until 1 year after randomization in the main 
(
NCT05375929
)
 study. This analysis evaluated
safety events and baseline and post-baseline knee MRI results for the adolescent participants in the sub-study.
Results
The substudy analyzed 35 adolescents, 19 participants received abrocitinib 200 mg QD; 16 participants received
abrocitinib 100 mg QD. The median age was 14 years, with a median weight of 45.9 kg and a median body mass
index 
(
BMI
)
 of 20.5 kg/m². All participants received at least one dose of the study intervention, with a median
exposure duration of approximately 50 weeks. All 35 participants were evaluated via MRI scan at baseline for
potential abnormal finding
(
s
)
 in the knee 
(
i.e., any 
‘
potential bone safety finding
’
 or 
‘
other finding
’)
, which were
required to be reviewed by the adjudication committee. The proportion of participants with all-causality
treatment-emergent adverse events 
(
TEAEs
)
 was numerically higher in the abrocitinib 200 mg group 
(
42.1%
)
compared to the 100 mg group 
(
31.1%
)
. Common TEAEs included nausea, pyrexia, nasopharyngitis, and atopic
dermatitis. No severe AEs or serious AEs 
(
SAE
)
 were reported. Knee MRI scans showed no potential bone safety
findings or other findings in any participants at the end of the treatment period identified on independent
adjudication.

Conclusion
The overall safety profile of abrocitinib in adolescents was consistent with the safety profile observed in the entire
study population. No unexpected major safety findings were reported, and no bone safety findings were observed
in knee MRI scans of 35 abrocitinib-treated adolescents enrolled in this substudy indicating no evidence of
clinically relevant bone safety concerns in adolescents treated with abrocitinib. Abrocitinib demonstrated a
favorable safety profile, making it a promising treatment option for adolescents with moderate-to-severe AD in
India.
