Abstract N°: 7510
Real-world use of tralokinumab: monotherapy versus overlapping treatment with conventional
immunosuppressants in atopic dermatitis
Clara-Amanda Ureña-Paniego
1
, Raquel Sanabria-de la Torre
1
, Carmen García Moronta
1
, Salvador Arias-Santiago
1
,
Trinidad Montero-Vilchez
1
1
Virgen de las Nieves, Granada
Introduction & Objectives:
Biologic therapies have transformed the therapeutic arsenal for atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
, offering superior efficacy
and safety compared to conventional immunosuppressants. However, in current practice, the use of a biologic is
generally preceded by a course of immunosuppressive treatment. Tralokinumab, an anti
–
interleukin-13
monoclonal antibody, has been established as an effective and safe long-term treatment option for patients with
moderate-to-severe AD. Nevertheless, transitioning from conventional immunosuppressants to tralokinumab
poses a clinical challenge, as limited evidence exists to guide optimal management during this period. In clinical
practice, an initial overlap between both treatments may be necessary to reduce the risk of flares. The objective of
this study was to compare the effectiveness of tralokinumab monotherapy 
(
TM
)
 versus an initial overlapping
strategy with ciclosporin 
(
OS
)
.
Materials & Methods:
We conducted a prospective observational study including patients with moderate-to-severe AD treated with
tralokinumab for at least 16 weeks. TM was defined as the use of tralokinumab in combination with emollients,
topical corticosteroids, or topical calcineurin inhibitors. OS was defined as the concomitant use of tralokinumab
with a conventional immunosuppressant for up to 12 weeks. Sociodemographic and clinical data, including AD
severity scores, were collected at baseline and weeks 0, 16, 24, and 52.
Results:
A total of 27 patients were included, with a mean age of 40.3 
(
±15.3
)
 years; 59.3% 
(
16/27
)
 were female. TM was
performed in 85.2% 
(
23/27
)
 of patients, while OS was performed in 14.8% 
(
4/27
)
. Both groups showed
improvement in AD severity at week 52. The OS group showed a mean reduction of 15 points in EASI, 2 points in
IGA, and 3.5 points in pruritus NRS, whereas the TM group showed a mean reduction of 18.66 points in EASI, 2.21
points in IGA, and 4.49 points in pruritus NRS. No statistically significant differences were observed between the
groups in EASI 
(
p = 0.52
)
, IGA 
(
p = 0.46
)
, or pruritus NRS 
(
p = 0.49
)
 reduction.
Conclusion:
Both therapeutic strategies were equally effective after 52 weeks of follow-up in patients with AD. Transitioning
from conventional systemic therapy to biologic treatment with tralokinumab can be achieved either through
monotherapy or an overlapping approach.
