Abstract N°: 7514
Nemolizumab treatment did not impair immune response to vaccination in patients with atopic dermatitis
–
 Results from a randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled study.
Vivian Laquer
1
, Stephen Tyring
2
, Jean-Loup Machu
3
, Anna Ryzhkova
4
, Liliana Ulianov
3
, Christophe Piketty
4
1
First OC Dermatology Research, , Fountain Valley, United States
2
Center for Clinical Studies, Houston, United States
3
Galderma R&D, Lausanne, Switzerland
4
Galderma R&D, Zug, Switzerland
Introduction & Objectives:
 Nemolizumab, an interleukin-31 receptor alpha antagonist, improves key signs and
symptoms of moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
. Understanding immunogenicity of vaccines in
nemolizumab-treated patients is crucial to ensure effective immunisation and to optimise long-term AD
treatment. This phase 2, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled study assessed the effect of nemolizumab
on humoral immune responses to tetanus and meningococcal vaccinations in patients with moderate-to-severe
AD.
Materials & Methods:
 Patients 
(≥
12 to 54 years
)
 with moderate-to-severe AD and inadequate response to
topical AD medications were randomised 1:1 to receive either 30 mg nemolizumab 
(
with a 60 mg loading dose at
baseline
)
 or placebo every 4 weeks until week 
(
W
)
 16. At W12, patients also received single doses of tetanus
toxoid, reduced diphtheria toxoid and acellular pertussis 
(
Tdap
)
 and quadrivalent meningococcal conjugate
(
MCV4
)
 vaccines. Immune responses to Tdap and MCV4 were assessed via blood samples collected 4 weeks after
vaccination, i.e. at W16. The primary endpoint was the proportion of patients with positive serum immunoglobulin
G 
(
IgG
)
 response to tetanus toxoid 
(≥
4-fold increase in anti-tetanus IgG concentrations from baseline in patients
with pre-vaccination anti-tetanus IgG concentrations 
≥
0.1 IU/mL or 
≥
0.2 IU/mL anti-tetanus IgG concentrations in
patients with pre-vaccination anti-tetanus IgG concentrations <0.1 IU/mL
)
.
Results:
 A total of 242 patients 
(
123 nemolizumab; 119 placebo
)
 were randomised. The achievement of primary
endpoint was similar between nemolizumab 
(
67.5%
)
 and placebo 
(
65.4%
)
 groups 
(
difference [90% confidence
interval {CI}]: 2.1 [-10.3, 14.5]
)
. Using different criteria, the proportion of patients with a positive serum IgG
response to tetanus toxoid 
(≥
2-fold increase in anti-tetanus IgG concentrations from baseline in patients with
pre-vaccination anti-tetanus IgG concentrations 
≥
0.1 IU/mL or 
≥
0.2 IU/mL anti-tetanus IgG concentrations in
patients with pre-vaccination anti-tetanus IgG concentrations <0.1 IU/mL
)
 at W16 was similar between
nemolizumab 
(
78.8%
)
 and placebo 
(
83.3%
)
 groups 
(
difference [90% CI]: -4.6 [-14.9, 5.7]
)
. At W16, all patients had
a serum anti-tetanus IgG concentration of 
≥
0.1 IU/mL. At W16, the proportion of patients with a serum anti-
tetanus IgG concentration of 
≥
1.0 IU/mL was similar between nemolizumab 
(
97.5%
)
 and placebo 
(
96.2%
)
 groups
(
difference [90% CI]: 1.4 [-3.2; 5.9]
)
. At W16, the proportion of patients with a positive serum bactericidal antibody
response to meningococcal group C capsular 
(
MenC
)
 polysaccharide 
(≥
4-fold increase in serum bactericidal
assay [SBA] reciprocal titer from baseline [using non-imputed values]
)
 was greater in the nemolizumab group
(
77.0%
)
 than in the placebo group 
(
63.6%
)
 
(
difference [90% CI]: 13.3 [0.1, 26.4]
)
; using imputed values, the
proportion was similar between nemolizumab 
(
63.9%
)
 and placebo 
(
57.6%
)
 groups 
(
difference [90% CI]: 6.2 [-8.2,
20.6]
)
. At W16, the proportion of patients with a positive serum bactericidal antibody response 
(
SBA reciprocal
titer 
≥
8
)
 to MenC polysaccharide was similar between nemolizumab 
(
83.3%
)
 and placebo 
(
81.9%
)
 groups
(
difference [90% CI]: 1.3 [-9.3, 11.9]
)
. Dermatitis atopic and headache were most common adverse events.
Conclusion:
 Nemolizumab treatment did not affect immune responses induced by tetanus and meningococcal

vaccinations in patients with moderate-to-severe AD.
