Abstract N°: 7523
Effectiveness of abrocitinib in patients with atopic dermatitis: results of a federated analysis of real-world
evidence in the DREAM TO TREAT AD consortium
David Prieto-Merino
1, 2
, Elizaveta Gribaleva
2
, Thomas Birkner
3
, Niels Krogh
4
, Bolaji Coker
5
, Ahmet Akkoc
4
, Axel
Hertzschuch
3
, Merel Postema
6, 7
, Erman Guler
8
, Benoit Raymond
9
, Anne Grete Froestrup
10
, Abaigeal Jackson
11
,
Con Hennessy
12
, Lara Cutlar
12
, Louise Anna Andrea Gerbens
6
, Alan Irvine, MD, DSc
13
, Maria Oberländer
Christensen
14
, Phyllis I. Spuls
6
, Simon Francis Thomsen
14
, Stephan Weidinger
15
, Thomas Werfel
16
, Jochen Schmitt
3
,
Carsten Flohr*
2
1
University of Alcalá, Faculty of Medicine, Alcalá de Henares, Spain
2
King
’
s College London and Guy
’
s and St Thomas
’
 NHS Foundation Trust, Unit of Peadiatric and Population-
Based Dermatology Research, St John
’
s Institute of Dermatology, London, United Kingdom
3
Technische Universität Dresden, Center for Evidence-Based Healthcare, Faculty of Medicine , Dresden, Germany
4
Zitelab Aps, Copenhagen, Denmark
5
Guy
’
s and St Thomas
’
 NHS Foundation Trust, Research and Development Department, London, United Kingdom
6
Amsterdam UMC, University of Amsterdam, Department of Dermatology, Amsterdam Public Health & Infection
and Immunity, Amsterdam, Netherlands
7
University of Amsterdam, Department of Research Data Management, Amsterdam, Netherlands
8
Pfizer Inc., Istanbul, Türkiye
9
Pfizer Ltd, Tadworth, United Kingdom
10
Pfizer ApS, Ballerup, Denmark
11
Pfizer Healthcare Ireland, Dublin, Ireland
12
UCD Charles Institute of Dermatology, National and International Skin Registries Solutions, Dublin, Ireland
13
Trinity College Dublin, Department of Clinical Medicine, Dublin, Ireland
14
University of Copenhagen, Bispebjerg Hospital, Department of Dermatology, Copenhagen, Denmark
15
University Hospital Schleswig-Holstein, Campus Kiel, Department of Dermatology and Allergy, Kiel, Germany
16
Medical School Hannover, Hannover, Germany
Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a common inflammatory skin disease, with around 10% of
patients requiring systemic therapy. Currently, there is a lack of real-world evidence on treatment outcomes using
systemic medications. DREAM TO TREAT AD 
(
D2T AD
)
 is a pan-European register-based study aimed at evaluating
treatment effectiveness of abrocitinib and conventional systemic therapies in routine clinical practice in Germany
(
GY
)
, the United Kingdom 
(
UK
)
, Denmark 
(
DK
)
, the Netherlands-Belgium 
(
NL/BE
)
 and Ireland 
(
IR
)
.
Materials & Methods:
 Patients who received abrocitinib in four registers 
(
ASTAR UK, TREATgermany, TREAT
NL/BE, SCRATCH Denmark
)
 were included. It is important to note that in GY, novel systemic medications, including
abrocitinib, can be prescribed first-line in patients requiring systemic therapy, unlike DK, NL/BE, and the UK, where
it is required to fail or have contraindications to at least one conventional systemic medication.
Data were pre-processed in each register into an analytical dataset that has a common data structure across all
registers. Federated statistical analysis was conducted in the secure DataSHIELD environment using R.
The effectiveness of abrocitinib was described as the average percentage monthly reduction in Eczema Area and
Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
 from drug initiation to week 16 of follow-up, as well as the percentage of patients reaching

EASI-50, EASI-75, and EASI-90 at that time point. EASI scores at target date 
(
baseline and follow-up
)
 were
calculated as the average of the closest scores before and/or after baseline 
(
within a window of -42 to +7 days
)
and follow-up 
(
+- 42 days
)
, weighted by the inverse of the numbers of days between the EASI measurement and
the target date. Patient baseline characteristics were summarised. Heterogeneity tests between registers were
conducted. Multivariable linear and logistic regression models were run to explain the impact of patient
characteristic on EASI outcomes.
Results:
 Across the four registers 
(
GY, UK, DK, NL/BE
)
 218 patients received abrocitinib, of which 44, 24, 12, and
20, respectively, had baseline and follow-up EASI available for the analysis. The average age of patients ranged
from 30.1 in UK to 39.4 in DK, proportion of women ranged from 30% in NL/BE to 46% in UK. EASI scores at
baseline varied between registers 
(
although not statistically significantly due to sample size
)
, ranging from 17.6 in
DK and GY to 23.4 in the UK. Numbers and types of previous systemic treatments varied significantly, from an
average of one previous systemic treatment in GY to an average of 3.5 in DK 
(
Table 1
)
.
At week 16, almost all measures of EASI changes varied significantly between registers, with GY showing the
strongest and NL/BE showing the smallest improvements 
(
Table 1
)
. The regression models in Table 2
demonstrated that effectiveness increased with higher EASI at baseline, with age and in females, and decreased
with higher number of previous systemic treatments.
Conclusion:
 Using a novel federated analysis approach, we could estimate the effectiveness of abrocitinib in
reducing EASI in the real-world clinical practice in four European registers. The differences in effectiveness
between countries could be linked to differences in healthcare settings and baseline patient characteristics. Further
analyses will focus on comparing treatment effectiveness of novel and conventional systemic medications and
incorporate more follow-up data.

