Abstract N°: 7554
Clinical efficacy of Ophiopogon japonicus oligofructans on children and adults with atopic dermatitis
Maud LE GUILLOU*
1
, Camille Durrieux
1
, Laurie VERZEAUX
1
, Elodie Aymard
1
, Hélène Muchico
1
, Brigitte Closs
1
1
SILAB, Brive, France
Introduction & Objectives:
Topical treatments to fight against atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 include corticosteroids and calcineurin inhibitors,
although they are often associated with adverse events such as skin atrophy, itching and burning sensations. Good
alternatives able to prolong disease relief in between flare-ups are therefore needed. To overcome this issue, a
natural active ingredient from 
Ophiopogon japonicus
, was developed. Oligofructans 
(
OFs
)
 were extracted from
this natural raw material and this study aimed to assess the clinical efficacy of OFs in decreasing the frequency and
intensity of AD flare-ups in AD patients.
Materials & Methods:
For this purpose, a randomized, single-blind, placebo-controlled, multicenter clinical trial was conducted on a
cohort of 90 children and 144 adults with mild-to-moderate AD, that applied tested products twice daily for 60
days.
Results:
The OFs from 
Ophiopogon japonicus
 were successful in significantly reducing the SCORAD, including erythema,
pruritus and body surface area in both cohorts: 3.9 ± 1.3 for children and 6.6 ± 1.1 for adults, compared to
baseline 
(
children: 5.7 ± 5.1; adults: 13.7 ± 0.8
)
. Regarding QoL indexes, OFs improved children
’
s and parents
’
s
IDQOL and DFI, as well as adults DLQI, at both D30 and D60. Finally, OFs reduced the intensity and the frequency
of AD flare-ups in children compared to placebo, as depicted by the favorable Investigator Global Assessment
(
IGA
)
, whereas no difference could be detected between both treatments in adults.
Conclusion:
Altogether, these results prove the beneficial effects of OFs in the course of AD. The mechanism of action of this
natural molecule was also demonstrated in a 3D 
in vitro
 skin model mimicking the molecular characteristics of this
skin disease. Therefore, this natural active ingredient, available in preservative-free powder could be used in skin
care products, as a complementary relay for corticosteroid treatment.
