Abstract N°: 7621
Exploring the language gap in clinical terminology and patient-reported experience in Atopic Dermatitis
Alice Beckley
1
, Hamizah Hamdan
2
, Penny Robinson
1
1
Ipsos, London, United Kingdom
2
Ipsos Sdn Bhd, Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia
Introduction & Objectives:
Clinical severity classifications for Atopic Dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 typically rely on standardized terminology 
(
mild,
moderate, severe
)
 and scoring systems used by healthcare professionals 
(
HCPs
)
1. However, clinical severity
classifications for AD often fail to capture the nuanced way patients experience and describe their condition,
resulting in a potential disconnect in patient-HCP communication which could affect treatment satisfaction and
adherence2. This study aimed to analyze the natural language patients use when describing AD severity compared
to clinical classifications, in order to identify patterns in patient expression and opportunities for improving
patient-centered communication.
Materials & Methods:
A global social intelligence analysis of AD-related conversations was conducted between December 2023-
December 2024. Using Ipsos
’
 Synthesio platform, over 12,300 social media posts containing 
“
atopic dermatitis
”
within 5 words of a pronoun were extracted from multiple sources including Reddit, Instagram, X, Facebook,
YouTube, TikTok, blogs and forums. Although the scope was global, we focused on English-language posts. Text
analytics and qualitative analysis were employed to identify language patterns related to severity descriptions.
Results:
Analysis revealed that the posts rarely use clinical terminology like 
‘
mild,
’
 
‘
moderate,
’
 or 
‘
severe
’
 when describing
AD severity. Instead, they express severity through descriptive and emotive language that combines both physical
symptoms and emotional impact. Patients with mild AD typically described their condition as 
‘
manageable
’
 or
‘
inconvenience
’
, using terms like 
‘
patches of skin
’
 and noting that symptoms are 
‘
persistent
’
 but 
‘
controllable
’
with proper care. Those with moderate AD used escalating language, describing their condition as 
‘
disruptive
’
 that
causes emotional distress. In severe cases, the language became more intense, with terms like 
‘
excruciating
’
,
‘
burning,
’
 
‘
unbearable,
’
 and 
‘
throbbing
’
 being used when describing their condition. Expressions of frustration
were also noted with regards to the disconnect in terminology used between patients and HCPs.
Conclusion:
This study highlights a potential communication gap between clinical severity classifications and patients
’
 lived
experiences with AD. By incorporating patients
’
 natural language into clinical discussions, it could aid HCPs in
improving diagnostic accuracy, treatment adherence and patient satisfaction. Developing assessment tools that
bridge this terminology divide could transform AD management by enabling more meaningful patient-HCP
interactions and more accurate evaluations of treatment efficacy from the patient perspective. Further research is
warranted.
