Galderma R&D, Zug, Switzerland
Introduction & Objectives:
 Long-term clinical data on atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 treatments are crucial to confirm
long-term safety and efficacy of treatment. Nemolizumab treatment has already demonstrated continuous
improvements in itch, skin lesions and sleep and good safety profile until week 
(
W
)
56 in patients with moderate-
to-severe AD. The ARCADIA long-term extension study 
(
NCT03989206; 200-week duration
)
 aimed to evaluate the
safety and efficacy of nemolizumab in adolescents and adults with moderate-to-severe AD.
Materials & Methods:
 This prospective, multicentre, open-label, long-term study enrolled patients who were
either nemolizumab-previously experienced [NPE] or nemolizumab-naïve [NN] from ARCADIA 1&2 phase 3 and
other nemolizumab studies. Patients received nemolizumab 30mg every 4 weeks 
(
Q4W
)
 alongside background
topical corticosteroids 
(
low/medium potency
)
 with or without topical calcineurin inhibitors. The primary objective
of the study was to evaluate the long-term safety of nemolizumab in adults and adolescents with moderate-to-
severe AD. Efficacy endpoints included Investigator
’
s Global assessment 
(
IGA
)
 score of 0/1 
(
clear/almost clear
)
,
≥
75%/
≥
90% improvement from lead-in baseline in the Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI-75/EASI-90
)
,
changes from lead-in baseline in visual analogue scale [VAS] Pruritus and Sleep loss of SCORing Atopic Dermatitis
(
SCORAD
)
 score and Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
. Efficacy results presented here include multiple
imputation 
(
MI
)
 analysis for missing data 
(
missing at random [MAR]
)
 at W56 visit and observed data 
(
OC
)
analysis for patients at W104 visit.
Results:
 At data cut-off, 1062/1901 
(
56%
)
 patients completed W104. The study discontinuation 
(
due to adverse
events
)
 rate was 5% in both groups 
(
NPE: 63/1164 and NN: 37/737
)
. At W56, the proportion of NPE and NN
patients achieving efficacy outcomes 
(
MI analysis for MAR data
)
 was 50% and 48% for IGA 0/1, 65% and 64% for
EASI-75, 46% and 44% for EASI-90, 66% and 61% for a 
≥
4-point improvement from lead-in baseline in SCORAD
VAS Pruritus and 50% and 45% for SCORAD VAS Pruritus<2, respectively. At W104, the proportion of NPE and NN

patients achieving efficacy outcomes 
(
OC analysis
)
 was 63% and 58% for IGA 0/1, 88% and 85% for EASI-75, 68%
and 64% for EASI-90, 87% and 82% for a 
≥
4-point improvement from lead-in baseline in SCORAD VAS Pruritus
and 70% and 67% for SCORAD VAS Pruritus<2, respectively. Improvements in sleep parameters mirrored those
observed in itch parameters. DLQI improved over time. The most frequently reported adverse events were COVID-
19, nasopharyngitis and dermatitis atopic. Exposure-adjusted incidence rates for any treatment emergent adverse
events were 112.8 cases/100 person year and 117.1 cases/100 person year in NPE and NN patients, respectively.
Safety results corroborated those previously reported.
Conclusion:
 Nemolizumab was effective and well tolerated up to W104 of treatment with low study
discontinuation rate due to adverse events. Sustained and continuous improvements with long-term nemolizumab
treatment were observed in skin lesions, itch relief, sleep disturbance and quality of life.
