Abstract N°: 7788
Acceptability and feasibility of patient-reported outcome measures for atopic eczema
Jitske Dijkstra
1
, Eva Knöps*
1
, Jolanda Maaskant
2
, Phyllis I. Spuls
1
, Louise Anna Andrea Gerbens
1
1
Amsterdam University Medical Center, Department of Dermatology, Amsterdam, Netherlands
2
Amsterdam University Medical Center, Department of Internal Medicine, Amsterdam, Netherlands
Introduction & Objectives:
In atopic eczema 
(
AE
)
 research, patient reported outcome measures 
(
PROMs
)
 are used to capture data on
symptoms, quality of life and long-term disease control. To help homogenization of trial data, the Harmonising
Outcome Measures for Eczema 
(
HOME
)
 initiative established a core outcome set 
(
COS
)
 which contains four
PROMs. Previous studies have identified patient-level barriers to completing PROMs, that may lead to inadequate
utilization and hindered implementation of a COS. For the PROMs in the HOME COS for AE, such an exploration
has not been conducted.
This study investigates the feasibility and acceptability of the PROMs in the HOME COS for AE and identifies
potential barriers and facilitators to further implementation, by exploring patient
’
s views and experiences with
completing these PROMs.
Materials & Methods:
A qualitative interview study was conducted, embedded in a prospective cohort study and a randomized
controlled trial that utilize the HOME COS for AE. During study participation, patients completed the PROMs from
the HOME COS. At baseline and at 6 months, patients were interviewed about their views on the PROMs. Audio
captures were transcribed verbatim. A thematic analysis was conducted by two independent researchers.
Results:
In total, 13 patients were included and 25 interviews were conducted. Data saturation was reached. Thematic
analysis yielded five themes regarding feasibility and four themes regarding acceptability. In feasibility, patients
perceived the length and frequency of the PROMs as adequate. Digital surveys were preferred over paper, and in-
hospital administration was preferred over completing the PROMs at home. In acceptability, patients report
neutral affect during survey completion. Disease severity influenced the ease of completion and the perceived
value of the PROMs. The act of answering questions about AE increased disease awareness among patients.
Participants report a presence of 
(
partly
)
 overlapping questions in the PROMs, but do not regard this overlap as
bothersome. Familiarization with the questions after 6 months did not affect perceived feasibility or acceptability.
Conclusion:
From the perspective of AE patients that participate in clinical research, the PROMs from the HOME COS are
feasible and acceptable. No major barriers to 
(
continued
)
 implementation of the HOME COS were reported.
