Abstract N°: 7837
Medication supply for atopic dermatitis 2010 - 2024: Analysis of the national AtopicHealth 1 to 3
healthcare studies in Germany
Anna Langenbruch*
1
, Luka Lomb
1
, Thomas Werfel
2
, Ralph von Kiedrowski
3
, Matthias Augustin
1
1
University Medical Center Hamburg-Eppendorf , Institute for Health Services Research in Dermatology and
Nursing, Hamburg
2
Hannover Medical School, Clinic for Dermatology, Allergology und Venerology, Hannover, Germany
3
Company for Medical Study & Service Selters, Selters
(
Westerwald
)
, Germany
Introduction & Objectives:
The series of studies AtopicHealth1 
(
2010
)
, AtopicHealth2 
(
2017-19
)
 and AtopicHealth3 
(
2024/25
)
 analyses the
healthcare of patients with atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
. There were no improvements between 2010 and 2017-19. As
therapeutic innovations are now available, the healthcare situation will be analysed again as part of the
AtopicHealth3 study.
Materials & Methods:
Like AtopicHealth 1 and 2, the latest Germany-wide multicentre cross-sectional study 
‘
AtopicHealth3
’
 also records
clinical data, the severity of AD 
(
SCORAD
)
, the impairment of quality of life 
(
DLQI, EQ VAS
)
, current therapies as
well as previous therapies and the treatment benefit assessed by the patients. This analysis refers to an interim
status of the AtopicHealth3 study. Data collection is ongoing and enrolment of 1,000 patients with AD in
AtopicHealth 3 is planned.
Results:
Since May 2024, 107 dermatological practices and outpatient clinics throughout Germany have been recruited to
participate in AtopicHealth 3 
(
2024/25
)
. By mid-March 2025, 300 fully completed, valid questionnaire sets from 35
dermatological practices were returned to the study centre. Of the 300 patients, 58% described themselves as
female, 41.7% as male and 0.3% as diverse. The mean age was 43 years. As in 2010 and 2017-19,
glucocorticosteroids 
(
TCS
)
 were the most frequently used topical therapy on the day of the survey 
(
64.0% of
patients, 45.2% in 2010
)
, followed by calcineurin inhibitors 
(
TCI
)
 
(
31.0%, 17% in 2010
)
. At the time of the survey,
52.3% of the patients were being treated with systemic therapies 
(
2017-19: 35.3% ever used according to doctor
)
.
On the day the questionnaire was completed, 38.0% had received biologics and 13.1% non-biological
immunosuppressants 
(
excluding steroids
)
. The proportion of systemic glucocorticosteroids 
(
sGCS
)
 as current
therapy was 9.0% vs. 3.6% in 2017-19 vs. 3.5% in 2010. Among the systemic therapies that patients reported
having received at some point in the past 5 years, biological treatments 
(
52.8% vs. 1.6% in 2017-19 vs. 0% in 2010
)
and sGCS 
(
33.3% vs. 35.3% in 2017-19 vs. 28.0% in 2010
)
 were the most common. Patients currently receiving
biological treatment showed lower burdens according to their DLQI, SCORAD, EQ VAS scores and better
subjective skin health than patients not receiving a biological treatment. In addition, the patient-defined treatment
benefit was higher in those who received a biologic.
Conclusion:
Subject to the still small sample, the quality of care with systemic therapeutics for AD in Germany has improved
significantly compared to 2017-19 and 2010 and the biologics and JAK inhibitors now approved for long-term
therapy are increasingly being used. The patient-reported outcomes show that patients with AD benefit from these

medications in many ways.
The study received financial support from Sanofi.
