Introduction & Objectives:
The stratum corneum, composed of corneocytes and lipids like cholesterol sulfate and glucosylcholesterol, plays a
crucial role in skin barrier function and the pathophysiology of atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
. Natural Moisturizing
Factors 
(
NMF
)
, derived from filaggrin breakdown, are essential for moisture retention and barrier integrity.
Although previous studies have explored NMF and lipid alterations in AD, none have examined how dupilumab
treatment alters these skin barrier biomarkers over time. The objective was to investigate the impact of dupilumab
on disease severity and skin barrier biomarkers 
(
NMF, cholesterol sulfate, and glucosylcholesterol
)
, and the role of
filaggrin mutations.
Materials & Methods:
Sixty patients with moderate-to-severe AD initiating dupilumab were enrolled in the TREatment of ATopic eczema
registry in the Netherlands and Belgium 
(
TREAT NL/BE registry
)
 and included in this substudy. Clinical outcomes
and tape strips from lesional and non-lesional skin were collected at baseline, and after 1, 3, 6, and 12 months of
treatment, with continued clinical follow-up thereafter. NMF and lipid levels were measured in stratum corneum
tape strips, and filaggrin mutations were assessed using blood samples. Long-term clinical response was
categorized using predefined EASI-based criteria.
Results:
Dupilumab improved Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
 and Peak Pruritus Numerical Rating Scale 
(
NRS
)
scores at all follow-up points 
(
P < .001
)
. Lesional NMF levels increased significantly 
(
P < .001 at months 1, 3, 6, and
12
)
, while non-lesional levels remained stable. NMF levels were initially lower in lesional skin compared to non-
lesional skin 
(
P < .001
)
, but increased toward non-lesional levels by month 12 
(
P = .116
)
. FLG mutation carriers
had significantly lower NMF levels in both lesional and non-lesional skin. The strongest NMF reductions were seen
in patients with homozygous or compound heterozygous mutations 
(
P < .001 for both lesional and non-lesional
)
,

followed by heterozygous carriers 
(
P = .004 for lesional and P = .002 for non-lesional
)
, but FLG status did not
affect clinical response. Lesional cholesterol sulfate and glucosylcholesterol levels decreased significantly over time
(
P < .001 at month 12 vs baseline
)
, whereas non-lesional lipid levels remained unchanged. Although lesional lipid
levels remained significantly higher than non-lesional levels at all timepoints 
(
P < .05
)
, the absolute difference
narrowed over time, suggesting partial normalization.
Conclusion:
Dupilumab improves both clinical outcomes and markers of skin barrier function in AD. NMF levels increased and
elevated lipid levels decreased in lesional skin, shifting toward the levels observed in non-lesional skin over time.
