School of Biochemistry and Immunology, Trinity Biomedical Science Institute, Trinity College Dublin, Dublin,
Ireland
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a complex, chronic inflammatory, non-contagious skin disease with high unmet need
and no curative treatment options. Elevation of serine synthesis enzymes and transporters have previously been
described in psoriatic tissues, presenting a potential metabolic vulnerability for therapeutic intervention 
(
Cappello
et al., 2022
)
. This study demonstrates that serine is also limiting in AD skin, accompanied by elevation of serine
synthesis enzymes and transporters. Furthermore, Th2 cells and IgE-expressing B cells play a crucial role in the
pathogenesis of AD and these cell types require serine for proliferation and inflammatory responses 
(
D
’
Avola et
al., 2022; Ma et al., 2017
)
. This study describes a novel therapeutic approach to exploit serine limitation in
inflammatory tissues by small molecule modulation of a key glycolytic enzyme, pyruvate kinase M2 
(
PKM2
)
,
resulting in anti-inflammatory activity in human Th2 cells and IgE-expressing B cells.
Materials & Methods:
Gene expression and metabolite profiles were studied in human skin from AD patients and human cells using RNA
sequencing and metabolomics, respectively. Human Th2 and B cell assays were performed in RPMI media
containing 100 µM serine or 10 µM serine. Proliferation and expression of cell surface markers, CD20, CD27 and
CD38 were assessed using flow cytometry. Release of IL-13, IL-31, IgG or IgE were measured using Homogeneous
Time Resolved Fluorescence 
(
HTRF
)
 or Meso Scale Diagnostics 
(
MSD
)
 assays. The 
in vivo
 pharmacology of PKM2
modulators was determined in the 2,4 dinitrofluorobenzene 
(
DNFB
)
 contact dermatitis model.
Results:
Differentially regulated metabolites and metabolic genes were characterised in AD skin, healthy skin, DNFB skin
and human CD4 T cells. Serine was decreased in AD skin compared to healthy, with a corresponding increase in
serine synthesis enzymes and transporters. When Th2 cells were cultured in serine limited conditions, a PKM2
modulator inhibited Th2 cell proliferation and release of IL-13 and IL-31. Similarly, when B cells were cultured in
serine limited conditions, PKM2 modulation inhibited release of IgE or IgG. These anti-inflammatory effects of
PKM2 modulation were caused by channeling of the glycolytic intermediate, 3-phosphoglycerate towards
pyruvate production, reducing carbon flux through serine biosynthesis.
Conclusion:
Serine limitation in AD is characterised by by high expression of serine biosynthesis genes and transporters. This
presents a potential metabolic vulnerability for therapeutic intervention with a novel PKM2 modulator that inhibits
Th2 and IgE-releasing B cells in serine limited conditions. The tissue-targeted efficacy of PKM2 modulation at AD
inflammatory sites enables a differentiated safety profile. This oral PKM2 modulator has recently entered clinical
trials in healthy volunteers and patients with moderate to severe AD. It could address the major unmet needs of
AD as a safe, convenient and effective therapy where there are limited treatment options under the current

standard of care.
References:
Cappello A, et al. Sci Adv 2022;8: eabm7902
D
’
Avola, A. et al. J Clin Invest 2022; 132
Ma, E.H. et al. Cell Metab 2017; 25: 345-357
