Abstract N°: 7901
Atopic Dermatitis Following Blaschko
’
s Lines: When A Chronic Inflammatory Disease In A Mosaic Pattern Is
A Diagnostic Challenge
Salma Abid
1
, hasnae zerhouni
1
, najoua ammar
1
, syrine hamada
1
, ismaili nadia
1
, meziane mariame
1
, laila benzekri
1
1
University Hospital Ibn Sina, Dermatology, Rabat, Morocco
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
, among the most prevalent chronic inflammatory skin disorders, typically presents with
symmetrical, flexural involvement. The Blaschkolinear pattern - reflecting embryonic pathways of clonal skin cell
expansion - is a well-established hallmark of genetic mosaicism, particularly in monogenic dermatoses. In contrast,
this distinctive segmental presentation remains exceptionally rare in polygenic conditions like AD, with only
isolated cases reported to date.
We present a case of 41-year-old-man, presenting with atopic dermatitis along the lines of Blaschko. The aim of
our case report is to improve recognition of Blaschkolinear presentations in clinical practice to ensure a timely
diagnosis and an effective treatment to these typically severe treatment-resistant variants.
Materials & Methods:
We report a case of a 41-year-old man, who presented to our dermatology department, with erythematous,
pruritic and lichenified plaques. His medical history includes allergic rhinoconjonctivis, chronic cough, and bilateral
incomplete hearing loss since he was 6 years old.
Three months prior to his admission, he presented erythematous, papulo-vesicular, and pruriginous lesions on his
forearms and abdomen, with progressive extension to his trunk and legs. He received numerous local treatments,
including antiobiotics, antihistamines, and topical emollients, with no improvement.
Dermatologic examination revealed erythematous lichenified plaques, with excoriated papules along Blaschko
’
s
lines on the arms, buttocks, multiples excoriated papules on the abdomen, with palmar hyperkeratosis.
A skin biopsy of the lesions showed hyperkeratosis and spongiosis, compatible with an atopic dermatitis.
Results:
The Blaschkoid distribution of skin lesions is common in monogenic disorders but exceptional in polygenic
diseases like atopic dermatitis. The most plausible explanation is loss of heterozygosity 
(
LOH
)
 along Blaschko lines
—
an early postzygotic event causing hemizygosity or homozygosity of disease-predisposing alleles. This results in
pronounced linear lesions superimposed on milder generalized disease.
The presence of lichenified plaques with excoriated papules along Blaschko lines in our patient, with a history of
atopic dermatitis, made us consider a patterned distribution of atopic dermatitis. This superimposed atopic
dermatitis is exceptional, with only isolated reports to date. This could be due to misdiagnosis, as the differentials
for acquired linear dermatoses may be considered, such as lichen striatus and ILVEN 
(
inflammatory linear
verrucous epidermal nevus
)
. Moreover, when systemic atopy is absent, the diagnosis remains uncertain, and the
term Blaschkitis or acquired Blaschko dermatitis is chosen.
Conclusion:

These cases of segmental severe patterns of polygenic diseases provide unique models to investigate mosaicism
and LOH in dermatology. The cutaneous manifestations reflect early postzygotic mutations, offering insights into
disease mechanisms. A deeper understanding could improve diagnostic accuracy in these atypical presentations
that are often treatment-resistant.
