Abstract N°: 7922
Real-life long-term experience on intra-class JAK1 selective inhibitors switch for atopic dermatitis
Gustavo Machado*
1
, Georgio Chidiac
1
, Camille Gautier
1
, Florence HACARD
1
, Marie Tauber
1
, Jean François Nicolas
1
,
Audrey Nosbaum
1
1
INSERM, Lyon, France
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disease for which Janus kinase 
(
JAK
)
 inhibitors 
(
JAKi
)
 have
been approved as an effective treatment option. Patients receiving JAKi often show improvements in clinical
scores such as the Investigator Global Assessment 
(
IGA
)
, Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
, and Eczema Area
and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
. Efficacy comparisons among selective JAK1 inhibitors rely on network meta-analyses, as
head-to-head trials are still lacking. However, JAKi can induce adverse events that lead patients to discontinue
treatment, and about 30% fail to achieve at least 75% reduction in EASI at week 16, prompting a change in
therapy. While intraclass switching among JAKi is not typically considered a first-line strategy after discontinuation
of one drug, we report six cases demonstrating promising outcomes, suggesting that switching between JAK1
inhibitors may be a viable option for non-responders or those experiencing adverse events.
Materials & Methods:
We selected patients diagnosed with AD from a university hospital eczema expert center who underwent a
therapeutic switch between two JAK1 inhibitors 
(
abrocitinib and upadacitinib
)
 as a treatment strategy after
discontinuation either for adverse events or lack of efficacy of the first drug.
Results:
All six patients 
(
100%
)
 had previously received at least one systemic treatment for AD before initiating JAKi
therapy. The follow-up period ranged from 14 to 74 months. The cohort included two males 
(
33%
)
 and four
females 
(
66%
)
, with a mean age of 37 years 
(
range: 29
–
53
)
. Most patients 
(
83%
)
 had been treated with
dupilumab 
(
monoclonal antibody anti-IL4R
)
 prior to starting JAKi therapy. Upadacitinib was the first JAKi used in
66% of cases, followed by abrocitinib after switching. EASI scores were assessed at the time of switching and after
initiation of the second JAKi, showing a general trend toward improvement 
(
Figure 1
)
, although changes were not
statistically significant.
Conclusion:
These findings highlight the variability in clinical response among JAK1 inhibitors used as initial treatment options.
JAKi selectivity across isoforms is influenced by factors such as: dose, cell type, tissue distribution, and patient
genetics. Real-life data may diverge from clinical trial in terms of both tolerability and efficacy. Our long-term
follow-up 
(
up to 74 months
)
 indicates that intraclass switching can be beneficial, although clinical responses often
stabilize after about four weeks and may even decline over time. Further studies are needed to better understand
the long-term efficacy and safety of JAKi therapy, as well as the potential role of intraclass switching.
