Abstract N°: 7943
Investigating the JAK/STAT pathway in pharmacological activities of Broussonetia papyrifera extract using
2D and 3D in vitro models
Fabrice Lestienne
1
, Cecile Viodé
1
, Pascale Bianchi
1
, Karine André
1
, Nathalie Chansard
1
, Camille BOUDET*
2
, Gautier
Doat
2
, Helene Duplan
1
1
Pierre Fabre Dermo-Cosmetics & Personal Care, R&D Department, Toulouse, France
2
Pierre Fabre Dermo-Cosmetics & Personal Care, Laboratoire Dermatologique Eau Thermale Avène, Lavaur, France
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory dermatosis characterized by skin manifestations such as intense
itching and inflammation, leading to a decreased quality of life. The disease is marked by a compromised skin
barrier, an imbalance in skin microbiota, and a type 2 inflammatory response. This study examined the anti-
inflammatory effects of 
Broussonetia papyrifera
 
(
B.p.
)
 plant extract in several in vitro models involving the
JAK/STAT pathway.
Materials & Methods:
The anti-inflammatory properties of B.p. extract, particularly the involvement of the JAK/STAT pathway, were
evaluated using 2D and 3D in vitro models. First, STAT6 and STAT3 phosphorylation assays were performed on
Th2- and IL6-stimulated normal human epidermal keratinocyte 
(
NHEK
)
 models, respectively. Furthermore, to
demonstrate the involvement of cytokines associated with atopic dermatitis, we used a TSLP-stimulated human
mast cell model to assess the inhibition of Th2 cytokine release. To highlight the beneficial effects of B.p. extract
on barrier function restoration, we used a reconstructed human epidermis 
(
RHE
)
 stimulated by a cytokine cocktail
mimicking the atopic environment. Finally, we also used a 3D model to evaluate an emollient containing our active
ingredient as well as a medical benchmark on the modulation of STAT pathway phosphorylation and the
modulation of biomarkers associated with atopic dermatitis.
Results:
Phosphorylation studies showed that the B.p. extract was able to modulate the phosphorylation of STAT6 and
STAT3 after stimulation of NHEK by Th2 and IL-6 cytokines respectively. Correlated with its JAK/STAT pathway
inhibitory activity, the B.p. extract inhibited IL-5 and IL-13 cytokines released in a TSLP stimulated human mast cell
model. In a Th2-stimulated RHE model, B.p. extract prevented the cytokine induced atopic dermatitis phenotype
(
restoration of layer thickness and induction of Filaggrin and Caspase- 14 proteins expression in IHC
)
. Once
formulated in an emollient dedicated to atopic pathophysiology and used topically, the resulting activity of B.
p. extract and associated ingredients showed inhibition of STAT3 phosphorylation in a Th2-stimulated RHE model.
Additionally in this same model, this emollient also showed a positive modulation of defensin and periostin
expression after quantification of IHC staining.
Conclusion:
This comprehensive investigation demonstrated that Broussonetia papyrifera extract exert
potent anti-inflammatory activity in targeting the Th2 axis implicated in AD pathophysiology. Particularly, its
JAK/STAT pathway inhibitory activity was highlight on 2D but also 3D in vitro models. Overall, these results
suggest that the pharmacological activities of Broussonetia papyrifera extract enhance the soothing and barrier-

enhancing capabilities of the emollient formula and provide therapeutic benefit in alleviating the burden of AD.
