Abstract N°: 7954
Targeting SaeRS to disarm S. aureus: An Innovative Emollient with Aquaphilus dolomiae extract-G1 and
dextran sodium sulfate validated in a Reconstructed Human Epidermis Model
Audrey Calvez
1
, Cécile Viodé
1
, Cécile Villette
1
, Muriel Bourrain
1
, Katia Ravard
1
, Pascale Bianchi
1
, Camille BOUDET*
2
,
Gautier Doat
2
, Sandrine Bessou-Touya
1
1
Pierre Fabre Dermo-Cosmetics & Personal Care, R&D Department, Toulouse, France
2
Pierre Fabre Dermo-Cosmetics & Personal Care, Laboratoire Dermatologique Eau Thermale Avène, Lavaur, France
Introduction & Objectives:
Staphylococcus aureus
, upon colonizing the skin, transitions from a commensal organism to a pathogen,
producing virulence factors that exacerbate atopic dermatitis. In this bacterium, virulence is tightly controlled by a
network of two-component system 
(
TCS
)
 signal transduction pathways, which allow the bacteria to activate
virulence programs in response to host defense systems. Targeting the TCSs of 
S. aureus
 offers an innovative
approach for the discovery of antivirulence treatments. A recent 
in-vitro
 study has shown that the combination of
Aquaphilus dolomiae
 extract-G1 
(
ADE-G1
)
 and dextran sodium sulfate 
(
DSS
)
** can inhibit 
S. aureus
 virulence by
targeting SaeRS system, one of the key virulence regulators in 
S. aureus
. This strategy could be a promising way to
counteract 
S. aureus
 proliferation in re-empowering the host
’
s natural immune defenses, while preserving the
commensal community and limiting the risk of raising antimicrobial resistance. The aim of the study was to
validate the anti-SaeRS effect to a more physiologically relevant model.
Materials & Methods:
An experimental model based on Reconstituted Human Epidermis 
(
RHE
)
 co-colonized with 
Staphylococcal
strains* was developed. Digital PCR 
(
dPCR
)
 analysis was then performed to investigate the anti-
SaeRS
 effects of
topical formulas and actives.
Results:
dPCR analysis revealed that the combination of DSS and ADE-G1 significantly repressed 
saeR
, a key regulatory
system for virulence as observed in the previous study. Moreover, this effect was higher when active ingredients
were in formulation underscoring the potential of the emollient as an effective strategy to target 
S. aureus
pathogenicity, without impacting its viability.
Conclusion:
The novel emollient containing DSS and ADE-G1 effectively inhibits 
SaeR
, one of the key genes of the SaeRS
virulence regulator in 
S. aureus
. These new insights, provided by a RHE model study, confirm the association DSS
and ADE-G1 as a promising anti-virulent approach for reducing 
S.aureus
 pathogenicity and thus decreasing its
competitivity inside the microbiota. 
