School of Medicine and Health Sciences, George Washington University, Washington, DC, United States
Introduction & Objectives:
The role of IL-13 and T-helper 
(
Th
)
2 inflammation in the pathophysiology of AD is well established; however,
other T-cell mediated pathways including Th17 may also play a role in disease pathogenesis.1 By targeting both
Th2 and Th17 pathways, combined IL-13, IL-17A, and IL-17F inhibition with a single agent may improve treatment
outcomes in people with AD. Galvokimig is a multispecific antibody
–
based therapeutic that inhibits IL-13, IL-17A,
and IL-17F with an extended half-life through albumin binding. Here we report 12-week efficacy and 18-week
safety results of galvokimig from the UP0089 study in participants with moderate-to-severe AD.
Materials & Methods:
UP0089 
(
NCT04643457
)
 is a two-part, randomised, first-in-human, proof-of-concept, double-blind Phase 1/2a
study of galvokimig monotherapy. Participants with AD were enrolled in Part B of UP0089 and randomised 2:1 to
receive intravenous galvokimig or placebo. The primary endpoints were 
≥
75% improvement from baseline in
Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI75
)
 response rate at week 12 and incidence of treatment-emergent adverse
events 
(
TEAEs
)
 and serious TEAEs from baseline through week 18.
Results:
Participants 
(
N=47
)
 received galvokimig 
(
n=33
)
 or placebo 
(
n=14
)
. At week 12, a median of 64.9% participants
achieved EASI75 with galvokimig 
(
95% credible interval [CrI] 46.0
–
81.0
)
 versus 12.3% with placebo 
(
95% CrI 4.3
–
22.0
)
 
(
posterior probability difference >0.999; all CrI reported using non-responder imputation method
)
. A
median of 46.6% participants achieved EASI90 with galvokimig 
(
95% CrI 28.2
–
65.5
)
 compared with 3.5% 
(
95% CrI
1.3
–
7.9
)
 receiving placebo at week 12 
(
Figure
)
. Differences in combined EASI percent change from baseline
between galvokimig and placebo were observed as early as week 2 and were clinically meaningful at week 12
(
least-square mean difference -55.1%, 95% confidence interval: -70.4% to -39.8%
)
.
TEAEs were reported in 72.7% of participants who received galvokimig and 50.0% of those who received placebo.

The most common TEAEs with galvokimig were rhinitis, nasopharyngitis, headache, dizziness, and oropharyngeal
pain 
(
12.1% each
)
. The most common TEAEs with placebo were rhinitis and dermatitis atopic 
(
21.4% each
)
. One
serious TEAE of haemorrhagic diarrhoea was reported and considered related to galvokimig.
Conclusion:
This first-in-patient study met its primary endpoints, with galvokimig demonstrating a clinically meaningful and
rapid improvement in efficacy measures, and safety outcomes associated with an acceptable benefit
–
risk profile.
Phase 2/3 studies will further determine the benefit
–
risk profile of galvokimig administered subcutaneously in
participants with AD.
