Abstract N°: 7971
Restoring Microbial Balance: A Novel Emollient with Aquaphilus dolomiae extract-G1 and dextran sodium
sulfate reduces S. aureus Virulence and Shifts the Skin Microbiota
Audrey Calvez
1
, Cécile Viodé
1
, Cécile Villette
1
, Muriel Bourrain
1
, Katia Ravard
1
, Pascale Bianchi
1
, Camille BOUDET*
2
,
Gautier Doat
2
, Sandrine Bessou-Touya
1
1
Pierre Fabre Dermo-Cosmetics & Personal Care, R&D Department, Toulouse, France
2
Pierre Fabre Dermo-Cosmetics & Personal Care, Laboratoire Dermatologique Eau Thermale Avène, Lavaur, France
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disorder exacerbated by 
Staphylococcus aureus
 colonization.
As the dysbiosis state set up, the microbiome no longer benefits from the positive role of the commensal
communities, such as 
S. epidermidis.
 Targeted antivirulence strategies offer a promising approach to managing
skin dysbiosis by disarming 
S. aureus
, without killing it. Among these, the SaeRS two-component system 
(
TCS
)
, a
key regulator of 
S. aureus
 virulence, emerges as an ideal target for the development of novel strategies to treat
skin conditions like atopic dermatitis, without disrupting the commensal skin microbiota. A recent study has
shown that a new emollient containing an association of 
Aquaphilus dolomiae
 extract-G1 
(
ADE-G1
)
 with dextran
sodium sulfate 
(
DSS
)
** significantly inhibits the SaeRS system. The aim of the work was to investigate the impact
of the actives prior and after formulation on bacterial growth and on key virulence genes expression governed by
SaeRS.
Materials & Methods:
An experimental model based on Reconstituted Human Epidermis 
(
RHE
)
 co-colonized with 
S. epidermidis
 and 
S.
aureus
 was developed to assess the efficacy of topical formulas and their actives in promoting 
S. epidermidis
adhesion while counteracting 
S. aureus
 proliferation by using viable cell counts on selective media.
Simultaneously, digital PCR 
(
dPCR
)
 analysis was conducted to investigate the anti-virulent effects on 
S. aureus
virulence gene expression.
Results:
The results showed that the adhesion of 
S. epidermidis
 was notably enhanced by the association of ADE-G1 and
DSS alone or in formula, whereas 
S. aureus
 adhesion remained comparatively lower. This highlights the efficacy of
their actives and this new emollient in promoting 
S. epidermidis
 colonization while limiting 
S. aureus
 proliferation.
Furthermore, dPCR analysis shows that the actives significantly repressed virulence genes linked to toxin synthesis
(α
-toxin
)
, and to innate immune evasion factors 
(
sbi
, 
scn
)
. Moreover, this effect was higher when active
ingredients were in formulation reinforcing the potential of the formulas as an effective strategy to reduce 
S.
aureus
 virulence, without impacting its viability.
Conclusion:
By reducing the expression of key virulence genes, including innate immune evasion factors, the new emollient,
containing ADE-G1 and DSS, weakens 
S. aureus
 and increases its vulnerability to the skin
’
s innate immune system.
Additionally, this product helps restore a more balanced skin microbiota. These new findings, provided by a RHE
model study, have confirmed the association ADE-G1 and DSS as a promising antivirulence strategy for improving
skin immune function, reducing inflammation, and preventing 
S. aureus
 flare-ups commonly associated with

atopic dermatitis. 
