Abstract N°: 7979
Digital Patient-Centered Endpoints of Nocturnal Scratch and Sleep in Patients With Moderate-To-Severe
Atopic Dermatitis Treated with Upadacitinib: Results from the Flex Up Wearable Sensor Sub-Study
Shuai Xu*
1
, Toshiya Takahashi
2
, Sang Wook Son
3
, Dylan Richards
1
, Brian Calimlim
4
, Yolanda Armendariz
4
, Namita
Vigna
4
, Matthew D. Czech
4
, Sandra L Goss
4
, Michael Ardern-Jones
5
1
Sibel Health, Chicago, IL, United States
2
Tohoku University Hospital, Sendai, Japan
3
Korea University Ansan Hospital, Ansan, United States
4
AbbVie Inc., North Chicago, IL, United States
5
University of Southampton, Southampton, United Kingdom
Introduction & Objectives:
 Patients with atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 often suffer from substantial sleep impairment,
caused partly by the debilitating itch, with significant impact on quality of life 
(
QoL
)
 and psychosocial well-being.
Objective measurements, such as digital sensor-based approaches, may overcome some of the limitations of
patient-reported outcomes 
(
PROs
)
. In the phase 3b/4 Flex Up 
(
NCT05507580
)
 digital sub-study, patients wore an
inertial sensor to objectively and remotely monitor nocturnal scratch and sleep. Here, we investigate the clinical
validity of sensor-based scratch and sleep measurements using PROs and evaluate treatment efficacy across study
visits.
Materials & Methods:
 In the Flex Up phase 3b/4, randomized, blinded, treat-to-target and dose-flexibility study
of upadacitinib 
(
UPA
)
 15 mg and 30 mg in adult patients with moderate-to-severe AD, patients at select sites
participated in a digital sub-study. Eligible patients wore the ADvanced Acousto-Mechanic 
(
ADAM
)
 hand-worn
inertial sensor on the back of their dominant hand while sleeping for 7 nights prior to the baseline visit, then for 7
nights following visits at Week 4, 12, and 24. The digital parameters collected included total scratch time and sleep
efficiency 
(
SE
)
. These digital parameters were summarized per night, then aggregated by median over the 7-day
visit period. To evaluate clinical validity of the measures, repeated measures correlation was used to assess the
association of digital scratch and sleep measurements with patient-reported measures of itch 
(
Worst Pruritus
Numeric Rating Scale [WP-NRS]
)
, itch while asleep 
(
Atopic Dermatitis Symptom Scale [ADerm-SS] item 1
)
, AD-
related sleep disturbance 
(
Atopic Dermatitis Impact Scale [ADerm-IS] Sleep domain score
)
, and QoL
(
Dermatology Life Quality Index [DLQI]
)
. Furthermore, scratch and sleep features were compared between pre-
and post-treatment periods to evaluate treatment efficacy.
Results:
 Of the 55 total patients enrolled at the digital sub-study target sites,12 patients met the eligibility criteria,
with a mean age of 35.3 years and median time since diagnosis of 26.9 years. Correlations indicate a moderate to
strong association between total scratch time and itch 
(
WP-NRS; r = 0.56
)
, itch while asleep 
(
ADerm-SS Item 1; r =
0.65
)
, and QoL 
(
DLQI; r = 0.76
)
 PROs. Correlations also indicate moderate to strong associations between SE and
AD-related sleep disturbance 
(
ADerm-IS Sleep; r = -0.46
)
 and QoL 
(
DLQI; r = -0.70
)
 PROs. There was improvement
from baseline in total scratch time after 4 weeks of treatment with UPA 
(
Figure 1
)
, with 100% of the patients
showing a decrease in total scratch time at Week 4. Similarly, there was improvement from baseline in SE after 4
weeks of treatment with UPA 
(
Figure 2
)
, with 87.5% of patients showing increased SE at Week 4.
Conclusion:
 Scratch and sleep measurements indicate positive correlations with patient-reported itch, sleep
disturbance, and QoL, suggesting clinical validity of sensor-based measurement of nocturnal scratch and sleep.
Furthermore, at-home scratch and sleep measurements show efficacy of UPA in AD for decreasing nocturnal
scratch and improving SE. The efficacy detected by the sensor at week 4 in a relatively small number of patients

shows the potential utility of objectively measured scratch and sleep as clinical endpoints to inform treatment
benefit for patients using novel and meaningful outcomes with minimal patient burden.
