Abstract N°: 8010
Tolerance and efficacy of an innovative emollient gel containing Aquaphilus Dolomiae and Dextran
sodium sulfate extracts
Marine Rodeghiero
1
, Julie Noharet
1
, Cécile Viodé
1
, Camille BOUDET*
2
, Gautier Doat
2
, Marie-Dominique
Thouvenin
1
1
Pierre Fabre Dermo-Cosmetics & Personal Care, R&D Department, Toulouse, France
2
Pierre Fabre Dermo-Cosmetics & Personal Care, Laboratoire Dermatologique Eau Thermale Avène, Lavaur, France
Introduction & Objectives:
 Daily emollients application is the mainstay of atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 management
and has been shown to improve skin condition and space out flare-ups. Therefore, it is important to provide
patients with safe and effective products with suitable galenic textures to promote adherence, even during
summer season. We present here the safety, efficacy and acceptability assessment of a new emollient gel
containing 
Aquaphilus Dolomiae and Dextran sodium sulfate
 extracts* intended to improve long-term atopic skin
condition while offering an innovative texture.
Materials & Methods:
 First, 31 subjects from 12 to 56 years old were included in a 3-month-long open clinical
study. Main inclusion criteria were mild AD with SCORAD between 10 and 25, xerosis intensity 
≥
 1 
(
scale 0-3
)
 and
pruritus intensity 
≥
 3 
(
scale 0-10
)
, and at least one peak of xerosis and pruritus per month reported by the
subjects/parents during the 3 previous months. Assessments included tolerance, AD severity 
(
SCORAD index
)
,
quality of life 
(
Dermatology Life Quality Index / DLQI
)
, number of peaks of xerosis and pruritus. Then, a consumer
Home Use Test 
(
HUT
)
 was conducted with 68 regular users of emollients products to evaluate the product
’
s
cosmetic acceptability.
Results:
 After 3 months of twice-daily use, the tolerance was deemed good by the dermatologist. AD severity was
significantly improved with a mean SCORAD index decrease by 66% 
(
p<0.0001
)
. Xerosis and pruritus respectively
decreased by 51% and 82% 
(
p<0.0001
)
 and a significant improvement in quality of life was observed 
(
DLQI:
+90%, p<0.0001
)
. Finally, the results from the HUT showed a good perceived efficacy and acceptability thanks to
the product
’
s light texture and ease of application.
Conclusion:
 The efficacy results demonstrate the benefit of this new emollient gel in improving atopic skin
condition in adults and adolescents. Moreover, its good tolerance and acceptability could help to ensure long-
term compliance which is a major challenge in the management of this chronic condition.
