Abstract N°: 8027
Tolerance and efficacy of a new dermo-cosmetic product containing Aquaphilus dolomiae and
Broussonetia extracts dedicated to pruritus associated with dry skin
Marine Rodeghiero
1
, Camille BOUDET*
2
, Gautier Doat
2
, Marie-Dominique Thouvenin
1
1
Pierre Fabre Dermo-Cosmetics & Personal Care, R&D Department, Toulouse, France
2
Pierre Fabre Dermo-Cosmetics & Personal Care, Laboratoire Dermatologique Eau Thermale Avène, Lavaur, France
Introduction & Objectives:
 Dry skin and pruritus are the main features of atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 that can be
encountered in other dermatological conditions such as eyelid eczema and psoriasis. Repeated itchy periods can
significantly impact patients
’
 daily life. We developed a new dermo-cosmetic product, dedicated to pruritus
associated with localized dry skin. It contains two active ingredients 
Aquaphilus dolomiae
 and 
Broussonetia
extracts; this latter having demonstrated anti-inflammatory effects involving the JAK/STAT pathway. We
investigated the tolerance and efficacy of the product in patients with atopic dermatitis and other skin disorders.
Materials & Methods:
 Two monocentric and open studies have been conducted on patients who applied the
product four times a day on localized areas of dry and pruritic skin during 22 days: 26 subjects 
(
aged from 19 to
43 years old
)
 with atopic dermatitis 
(
study 1
)
 and 44 subjects 
(
aged from 24 to 89 years old
)
 with other skin
conditions 
(
as eyelid eczema and psoriasis
)
 
(
study 2
)
. Inclusion criteria included a xerosis score 
≥
 2 
(
moderate
)
on a 0-3 scale and a pruritus score 
≥
 4 
(
over the previous 24 hours
)
 on a 0-10 numerical rating scale 
(
NRS
)
 on at
least one localized area. Primary objective was to assess the tolerance of the product. Secondary objectives
included pruritus evaluation 
(
daily assessment, over the previous 24 hours by NRS at home during the first week,
Chronic Itching Burden Scale
)
, physical and functional signs assessment at each visit, local SCORAD 
(
study 1 only
)
,
cosmetic acceptability questionnaire and standardized photographs.
Results:
 Tolerance was assessed as good in both studies. A significant decrease of the pruritus intensity 
(
NRS
)
was observed during the first week, from D2 
(
study 1: -33%, p<0.0001; study 2: -52%, p<0.0001
)
 to D8 
(
study 1: -
67%, p<0.0001; study 2: -88%, p<0.0001
)
. Also, physical and functional signs assessed by the investigators
significantly decreased during the study, especially itching and dryness 
(
respectively - 86,8% and - 80% at D8 in
study 1 and
-99% and - 91% at D8 in study 2 
(
p<0.0001
))
. AD severity on a target area was significantly improved as Local-
SCORAD decreased by -54,7% at D8 and by -69% at D22 
(
p<0.0001
)
. Moreover, 77% and 95% subjects declared
their skin immediately soothed after application, respectively in study 1 and 2. Finally, the cosmetic acceptability
was evaluated as very good, with subjects reporting the product as efficient in reducing intense itching and their
skin as soothed and more comfortable.
Conclusion:
 Tolerance and efficacy results as well as the cosmetic acceptability of this new dermo-cosmetic
product demonstrated its benefit in the prompt relief of significant pruritus associated with dry skin.
