5
University Medical Center Hamburg, Institute for Health Services Research in Dermatology and Nursing 
(
IVDP
)
,
Hamburg, Germany
Introduction & Objectives:
 PRIDD
™
 is the first fully validated dermatology patient-reported outcome measure
(
PROM
)
 that was developed in partnership with patients 
–
 5600 in total.  PRIDD is a multi-dimensional PROM that
assesses physical, psychological, life responsibilities and social impacts on the lives of patients with any skin
condition. We report the first intervention study evaluating the utility of PRIDD as a quality-of-life assessment in a
clinical trial of a new emollient containing 
Aquaphilus Dolomiae
 in people with atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
.
Materials & Methods:
 33 adults aged 18-56 years presenting with mild to moderate AD 
(
SCORAD index 10 to
25
)
 were consented and enrolled in a 3 months open-label study. Main inclusion criteria were dry to very dry skin
with xerosis intensity 
≥
 2 
(
scale 0-3
)
 and both pruritus and discomforts sensation intensities 
≥
 3 
(
scale 0-10
)
.
Assessments of impact included AD severity 
(
SCORAD index
)
, the Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
 and the
PRIDD.
Results:
 After 3 months of twice-daily use, AD severity significantly improved with a SCORAD index decrease by 
–
70% 
(
p<0.0001
)
. These results were correlated with a statistically significant 
(
p<0.0001
)
 improvement in
participants
’
 quality of life as assessed by both patient-reported outcome measures DLQI 
(
66%
)
 and PRIDD
(
overall reduction of 30%
)
 
(
Figure1
)
. All 4 categories of PRIDD were significantly improved: Physical 
(
-39%
)
responsibilities 
(
-42%
)
, psychological 
(
-51%
)
 and social impacts 
(
-52%
)
; 
(
p<0.0001
)
. 
(
Figure 2
)


Conclusion:
 This first assessment demonstrated that the four impact domains of PRIDD conferred a more holistic
understanding of the impacts of intervention on the lives of people with AD. These results provide further
evidence supporting the use of PRIDD as a shared decision-making tool in dermatology and continued evaluation
of PRIDD as a patient-centered development tool, in this case, within the context of dermo-cosmetic product
development.
