Abstract N°: 8045
Abrocitinib Treatment for Moderate-to-Severe Atopic Dermatitis Observed in Electronic Health Records
and Administrative Claims in a US-Based Real-World Dermatology Setting: Patient Characteristics and
Baseline Disease Severity
Raj Chovatiya*
1, 2
, Amanda Mummert
3
, Stella Chang
3
, Mark Levenberg
4
, Griffith Bell
5
, Melissa Watkins
6
, Amie
Scott
6
1
Rosalind Franklin University of Medicine and Science, Chicago Medical School, North Chicago, IL, United States
2
Center for Medical Dermatology + Immunology Research, Chicago, IL, United States
3
OMNY Health, Atlanta, United States
4
Pfizer Inc., Collegeville, PA, United States
5
Pfizer Inc., Cambridge, MA, United States
6
Pfizer Inc., New York, NY, United States
Introduction & Objectives:
 Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disease with significant
heterogeneity in its clinical presentation and treatment response. Abrocitinib, an oral Janus kinase 1 
(
JAK1
)
inhibitor, was approved in the United States 
(
US
)
 for adults 
(
2022
)
 and adolescents 
(
2023
)
 and clinical trials have
demonstrated efficacy in patients with moderate-to-severe 
(
M2S
)
 AD. Exploration of real-world use of abrocitinib
in M2S AD is limited and this study leverages claims, patient and clinician reported data to characterize M2S AD
patients initiating abrocitinib in outpatient dermatology practices nationwide.
Materials & Methods:
 This retrospective, observational study characterized adolescents 
(
12 - <18
)
 and adults
(≥
18
)
 with M2S AD and a claim for abrocitinib between 01/22 and 02/25 
(
study period
)
 in the OMNY Health
Database. These data are sourced from general and dermatology electronic health records 
(
EHRs
)
 and linked to
open claims. The study included patients with a confirmed AD diagnosis and some patients were excluded per
label. Patient characteristics, Itch Numeric Rating Scale 
(
NRS
)
, and Investigator Global Assessment 
(
IGA
)
 were
characterized based on available data collected on or during the 42 days pre-index. Prior treatments and
comorbidities were characterized using all available EHR or claims data 
(
01/1/17 to index
)
.
Results:
 Overall, 315 patients with M2S AD initiated abrocitinib during the study period [8.3% adolescent, 91.7%
adult], with 111 
(
35%
)
 patients with prior AD biologic exposure. Top prior systemic treatments included oral
prednisone 
(
49.2%
)
 and dupilumab 
(
34.6%
)
. Top comorbidities included overweight or obesity 
(
43.5%
)
, anxiety
(
41.3%
)
, allergic rhinitis 
(
38.7%
)
, contact dermatitis 
(
37.8%
)
 and depression 
(
34.3%
)
. Proportionally, more
adolescents than adults 
(
23.1% vs 13.8%
)
 initiated abrocitinib at the 200 mg dose. Among the 42 
(
13.3%
)
 patients
with Itch NRS and/or IGA documented at index or 42 days prior had, nearly all 
(
95.2%
)
 reported AD in the upper
extremities, most reported a severe Itch NRS 
(
52.8%
)
 but moderate IGA 
(
40.5%
)
.
Conclusion:
 This study is the first to robustly evaluate abrocitinib treatment of AD patients in a real-world setting
with administrative 
(
claims
)
 and clinical 
(
EHR
)
 data. It provides valuable insight into the characteristics of AD
patients treated with abrocitinib in US dermatology practices. These findings characterize the demographics,
treatment, comorbidities, and clinician and patient reported severity of AD patients upon abrocitinib treatment.
Results suggest that most, particularly adolescents, initiated at the higher dose and proportions reporting severe
in Itch NRS and IGA varied Additional longitudinal studies are needed to evaluate the long-term effectiveness for
bio-experienced and -naïve abrocitinib patients, including disease severity at treatment initiation, dose change
patterns, and potential predictors of treatment response.

