Abstract N°: 8070
Experimental and clinical evidence suggests that treatment with IL-37 can improve skin barrier function
Alafate Abudouwanli*
1
, Ge Peng
1
, Mengyao Yang
1, 2
, Quan Sun
1
, Shan Wang
1, 3
, Wanchen Zhao
1
, Yi Tan
1
, Ko
Okumura
1
, Hideoki Ogawa
1
, François Niyonsaba
1, 4
1
Atopy 
(
Allergy
)
 Research Center, Juntendo University Graduate School of Medicine, Tokyo, Japan
2
Department of Dermatology, The First Hospital of China Medical University, Liaoning, China
3
Department of Dermatology, Beijing Children
’
s Hospital, Capital Medical University, National Center for
Children
’
s Health, Beijing, China
4
Faculty of International Liberal Arts, Juntendo University, Tokyo, Japan
Introduction & Objectives:
 Interleukin-37 
(
IL-37
)
 is an anti-inflammatory cytokine that plays a regulatory role in
immune responses; however, its function in atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 remains unclear. In particular, the impact of IL-
37 on skin barrier function, a key pathological feature of AD, has yet to be fully understood. In this study, we
examined IL-37 expression levels in lesional and non-lesional skin of AD patients, analyzed its relationship with
pro-inflammatory cytokines and barrier-related components, and evaluated IL-37 potential as a biomarker for
disease severity and treatment response. The aim of this study is to clarify the role of IL-37 in AD pathogenesis
and its value as a therapeutic target.
Materials & Methods:
 Skin samples from healthy donors, as well as nonlesional and lesional skin from patients
with AD, were obtained from the GEO database. Gene expression analysis was performed to compare 
IL37
expression levels among healthy individuals, nonlesional and lesional skin from AD patients. Further analyses were
conducted to evaluate 
IL37
 expression in relation to SCORAD scores, and pre- and post-biologic treatment status.
Immunohistochemistry was used to assess 
IL37
 expression in skin tissues from healthy donors and AD patients. To
investigate the effect of IL-37, primary normal human keratinocytes were stimulated with recombinant IL-37, and
the expression of key skin barrier components was evaluated at both mRNA and protein levels. An AD-like
phenotype was induced in keratinocytes using IL-4 and IL-13, and the modulatory effects of IL-37 on barrier-
related gene expression were examined. Furthermore, transepithelial electrical resistance 
(
TER
)
 measurements and
a 3D human skin equivalent model were employed to assess the impact of IL-37 on skin barrier function.
Results:
 
IL37
 gene expression was significantly reduced in the lesional skin of AD patients compared to healthy
controls. Moreover, 
IL37
 levels were markedly lower in patients with moderate to severe AD than in patients with
mild AD or healthy skin. Notably, 
IL37
 expression increased following biologic treatment, suggesting its
responsiveness to therapeutic intervention. Receiver operating characteristic analysis demonstrated that 
IL37
 had
high sensitivity and specificity in distinguishing AD lesions from healthy skin. Correlation analyses revealed that
IL37
 expression was negatively associated with pro-inflammatory cytokines and chemokines, while showing a
positive correlation with the barrier-related gene filaggrin. 
In vitro
, treatment of human keratinocytes with
recombinant IL-37 significantly upregulated the expression of filaggrin, loricrin, claudin-1, and 
zonula occludens
-1
at both mRNA and protein levels. Additionally, IL-37 enhanced TER in keratinocyte monolayers. In a 3D skin
equivalent model, IL-37 restored skin barrier integrity compromised by IL-4 and IL-13, indicating the potential of
IL-37 to reverse barrier dysfunction in AD.
Conclusion:
 IL-37 is associated with disease severity and treatment response in AD and promotes skin barrier
restoration by upregulating key structural components. These findings suggest that IL-37 is a promising
biomarker and therapeutic target for AD treatment.
