Abstract N°: 8092
Safety and Efficacy of up to 3 Years of Dupilumab Treatment in Infants and Children With Severe Atopic
Dermatitis
Amy Paller*
1, 2
, Alain Taieb
3
, Stephan Weidinger
4
, Masaki Futamura
5
, Xing-Hua Gao
6
, Lawrence Eichenfield
7, 8
,
Zhen Chen
9
, Randy Prescilla
10
, Tien Nguyen
9
1
Northwestern University Feinberg School of Medicine, Chicago, IL, United States
2
Ann & Robert H. Lurie Children
’
s Hospital, Chicago, IL, United States
3
INSERM U1312, University of Bordeaux, Bordeaux, France
4
University Hospital Schleswig-Holstein, Campus Kiel, Kiel, Germany
5
National Hospital Organization Nagoya Medical Center, Nagoya, Japan
6
The First Hospital of China Medical University, Shenyang, China
7
University of California San Diego School of Medicine, La Jolla, CA, United States
8
Rady Children
’
s Hospital, San Diego, CA, United States
9
Regeneron Pharmaceuticals Inc., Tarrytown, NY, United States
10
Sanofi, Cambridge, MA, United States
Introduction & Objectives:
 Compared to patients with mild or moderate atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
, those with
severe AD have more intense chronic signs and symptoms, higher disease burden, and worse quality of life.
Dupilumab has been previously shown to provide an acceptable safety profile and sustained efficacy for up to 2
years in infants and children with moderate-to-severe AD. We report long-term 
(
up to 3 years
)
 safety and efficacy
outcomes 
(
body surface area [BSA], SCORing Atopic Dermatitis [SCORAD] and its components
)
 of dupilumab
treatment in pediatric patients with severe AD.
Materials & Methods:
 Children 
(
6 months to 11 years
)
 with severe AD 
(
Investigator
’
s Global Assessment score of
4
)
 from prior studies 
(
NCT03346434, NCT03345914
)
 were treated with dupilumab 
(
200 mg every 4 weeks [q4w]
for body weight 5 to <15 kg, 300 mg q4w for 15 to <30 kg, 200 mg every 2 weeks [q2w] for 30 to <60 kg, or 300
mg q2w for 
≥
60 kg
)
 in an open-label extension study 
(
LIBERTY AD PED-OLE [NCT02612454]
)
. Topical treatments
were allowed. Absolute SCORAD score, SCORAD-50 
(
defined as 50% reduction in absolute value from baseline of
parent study
)
, and BSA were assessed at baseline of the parent study through Week 152. Data are reported as
observed.
Results:
 Of the 125 patients with severe AD in this analysis, 62.0% of patients aged 6 months to 5 years 
(
n =
31/50
)
 and 66.7% of patients aged 6 to 11 years 
(
n = 50/75
)
 had completed 152 weeks of treatment. Mean 
(
SD
)
treatment exposure was 124.2 
(
48.6
)
 weeks in children aged 6 months to 5 years and 116.0 
(
48.5
)
 weeks for those
aged 6 to 11 years. Treatment-emergent adverse events 
(
TEAEs
)
 were reported in 86.0% of 6-month to 5-year-old
children and in 89.3% of 6- to 11-year-old children, the majority of which were mild or moderate; treatment-
related TEAEs occurred in 22.0% and 36.0%, respectively. In both groups, TEAEs including conjunctivitis 
(
4.0%,
4.0%
)
, upper respiratory tract infection 
(
2.0%, 4.0%
)
 and blepharitis 
(
2.0%, 2.7%
)
 were most frequently reported.
SCORAD scores decreased from 73.1 to 20.8 in 6-month to 5-year-old children and from 73.9 to 22.7 in 6- to 11-
year-old children. SCORAD-50 was achieved by over 80% of patients aged 6 months to 5 years 
(
n = 27/31
)
 and 6
to 11 years 
(
n = 42/50
)
 by Week 152. Across all SCORAD components 
(
erythema, edema/papulation,
oozing/crusting, excoriation, lichenification, and dryness
)
, the proportion of patients with moderate or severe
scores decreased from parent study baseline 
(
52
–
100%
)
 to Week 152 
(
0
–
25%; 33
–
38% of patients had missing

data at Week 152
)
. Mean affected BSA decreased from 55.6 to 14.2 in 6-month to 5-year-old children and from
62.4 to 8.9 in 6- to 11-year-old children.
Conclusion:
 An acceptable and consistent long-term safety profile was demonstrated for up to 3 years of
dupilumab treatment in infants and children 
(
6 months to 11 years
)
 with severe AD. Moderate and severe scores
for all SCORAD components generally improved to absent or mild, reflecting reduced intensity across all AD signs.
