Abstract N°: 8159
Risk of major adverse cardiovascular events in patients with chronic hand eczema.
Maria Oberländer Christensen*
1
, Johan Sieborg
1
, Ida Vittrup
1
, Caroline Meyer Olesen
1
, Claus Zachariae
2
, Simon
Francis Thomsen
1
, Alexander Egeberg
1
1
Bispebjerg Hospital, Department of Dermatology and Venereology, Copenhagen NV, Denmark
2
Gentofte Hospital, Department of Dermatology and Allergy, Gentofte, Denmark
Introduction & Objectives:
 Chronic hand eczema 
(
CHE
)
 is a common debilitating inflammatory skin disease
linked to several lifestyle factors including tobacco use and being overweight. While moderate-to-severe CHE has
been associated with elevated levels of cardiovascular biomarkers, it is unknown whether adults with CHE have an
increased occurrence of cardiovascular events and whether any increase risk is caused directly by CHE itself or
driven by other comorbidities or lifestyle factors.
Materials & Methods:
 Data were extracted from danish national patient registries, including all individuals aged
18 years or older who received an international classification of diseases, 10th revision diagnostic code for CHE
between January 1st, 1994, and December 31st, 2020. Patients were classified as having mild-to-moderate or
severe CHE based on their treatment history and were matched 1:10 by age- and sex to the general population
without CHE, creating a control cohort for each severity group. Mild-to-moderate CHE patients were reclassified as
severe if their CHE worsened during the study period, thus included in both severity groups. The study period was
from January 1st, 2002, to December 31st, 2020, with the primary end point being major adverse cardiovascular
events 
(
MACEs
)
 defined as myocardial infarctions 
(
MI
)
, ischemic stroke 
(
IS
)
, or cardiovascular death 
(
CD
)
, or a
composite of these. Kaplan-Meier curves illustrated time to MACE. Cox-regression models were used to calculate
unadjusted hazard ratios 
(
HR
)
 and adjusted HR 
(
aHR
)
 with 95% confidence intervals 
(
CI
)
, adjusting for previous
MACE events, smoking history, history of alcohol abuse, diabetes, hypertension, cardiac dysrhythmias,
inflammatory bowel disease, sex, and age but not body mass index as this data was not available.
Results:
 The study comprised 18,253 patients with CHE including 7,545 
(
41.3%
)
 
(
median age: 35.7 years
[interquartile range 
(
IQR
)
 26.4-48.9], 61.8% women
)
 categorized as having mild-to-moderate CHE and 13,107
(
71.8%
)
 
(
median age: 44.5 [IQR 32.4-56.0], 59.9% women
)
 with severe CHE. Patients with mild-to-moderate or
severe CHE were matched with 75,450 and 131,070 individuals without CHE, respectively. Unadjusted analyses
revealed no risk of MACEs when comparing mild-to-moderate CHE with controls 
(
HR: 1.00; 95% CI: 0.97-1.03
)
,
including MI 
(
HR: 1.01; 95% CI: 0.98-1.03
)
, IS 
(
HR: 1.00; 95% CI: 0.97-1.02
)
, and CD 
(
HR: 1.01; 95% CI: 0.99-1.03
)
.
The risk of MACEs remained insignificant after adjusting for potential confounders.
In contrast, patients with severe CHE had significantly higher unadjusted risk of MACEs compared to controls 
(
HR:
1.08; 95% CI: 1.05-1.011
)
, including MI 
(
HR: 1.04; 95% CI: 1.02-1.06
)
 and IS 
(
HR: 1.05; 95% CI: 1.03-1.08
)
. After
adjusting for potential confounders, patients with severe CHE continued to have a slightly increased risk of MACEs
(
aHR: 1.04; 95% CI: 1.01
–
1.06
)
, MI 
(
aHR: 1.02; 95% CI: 1.00
–
1.04
)
 and IS 
(
aHR: 1.03; 95% CI: 1.01
–
1.06
)
, which
increased further 
(
aHR: 1.06; 95% CI: 1.03-1.09, aHR: 1.03; 95% CI: 1.01-1.05, aHR: 1.05; 95% CI: 1.03-1.08,
respectively
)
 when not adjusting for smoking.
Conclusion:
 This study shows a marginally but significantly increased risk of MACEs among patients with severe
CHE. The slightly increased risk was observed prior to adjustment for demographics, comorbidities, and lifestyle
factors, suggesting that these factors may be the primary drivers of such risk. Finally, residual confounding cannot
be excluded.



