Abstract N°: 8168
Cross-regional University Migration Triggering Keratotic Eczema in an Adolescent
—
A Case Report
Lingyu Pan*
1
, Wei Hua
1
1
West China Hospital␀Sichuan University, chengdu, China
Introduction & Objectives:
Keratotic eczema is a chronic inflammatory dermatosis characterized by hyperkeratosis, pigmentation and
lichenification, with a multifactorial aetiology involving genetic, immunological and environmental elements. For
students who relocate to another region for study, abrupt changes in climate, water quality, daily routine and
psychological stress create a typical 
“
study-related migration dilemma
”
, yet their impact on cutaneous barrier
integrity and inflammatory susceptibility has received little attention. By reviewing an adolescent who experienced
several relapses after enrolling at an out-of-province university, we explore how regional conversion and campus-
related stress may precipitate or aggravate keratotic eczema, providing guidance for the clinical management of
students facing this dilemma.
Materials & Methods:
The complete disease course of a 19-year-old male was retrospectively analysed. Campus accommodation
conditions, lifestyle, clinical manifestations, histopathology, treatment regimens and follow-up data were
collected. After other dermatoses and systemic diseases were ruled out, a diagnosis of keratotic eczema was
confirmed by skin-biopsy pathology.
Results:
The first episode occurred after the patient left his hometown and began studying in a hot and arid region. Eight
weeks into the semester, frequent hand-washing with detergents and late-night studying led to cracked, pruritic,
scaly lesions on the dorsa of the hands, followed by grey-brown scaly plaques around the elbows, knees and
ankles. Biopsy revealed basket-weave hyperkeratosis of the epidermis and sparse perivascular lymphocytic
infiltration in the superficial dermis. When the patient took leave and returned to his warm, humid hometown,
topical betamethasone-urea ointment, oral levocetirizine and avoidance of detergents resulted in complete
clearance of lesions. After resuming studies the next term, profuse sweating under high temperatures triggered a
relapse; during the vacation he again improved with intensified moisturising and anti-inflammatory therapy. The
condition is currently stable, but another recurrence is anticipated upon returning to school.
Conclusion:
This case indicates a definite association between keratotic eczema and regional environmental change. Hot, dry
climate, hard water and frequent detergent use may impair the skin barrier, while psychological and lifestyle
alterations caused by academic migration act as additional triggers. For susceptible students, personalised
prevention strategies are recommended: environmental adaptation guidance with enhanced moisturising and
reduced exposure to irritant cleansers before and after returning to campus; behavioural interventions to avoid
mechanical injury such as picking of scales; and dynamic follow-up with timely adjustment of therapy during high-
risk seasons or environmental transitions. Further studies on how environmental change and psychological stress
jointly influence the cutaneous barrier are warranted to establish evidence-based long-term management for
migrating adolescents.

