Abstract N°: 8194
Promising Biomarkers - objective sleep parameters in patients with atopic Dermatitis
Caroline Mann
1
, Katharina Nowak
1
, Patrick Zilleßen
1
, Julia Hinkel
1
, Joanna Wegner
1
, Petra Staubach-Renz
1
1
Johannes Gutenberg University Medical Center , Mainz, Germany
Introduction & Objectives:
Sleep disturbances in patients with AD is a common yet underrated life course impairment. Several studies
focused on assessing the subjective impairment of sleep disturbances, however objective data are lacking. We
aimed to further explore and assess the sleep architecture in these patients using the diagnostic tool of
polygraphy.
Materials & Methods:
27 patients with moderate to severe AD and 6 healthy controls were included in this cross-sectional exploratory
study. 4 patients received additional examination in an overnight sleep lab.
Objective sleep parameters 
(
deep sleep 
(
N3
)
, REM sleep, apnea/hypopnea index 
(
AHI
)
, sleep efficiency, skin
temperature
)
 and patient-rated outcome measures 
(
PROMS
)
 for sleep quality, daytime sleepiness, quality of life
and pruritus were assessed. Associations between sleep parameters and PROMs were analyzed using Pearson or
Spearman correlation. Independent t-tests or Mann-Whitney U tests were used to compare AD patients with
healthy controls
Results:
A marked discrepancy was observed in the sleep quality of healthy controls as compared to patients suffering
from AD. In the AD group, REM and sleep efficiency significantly correlated with the EASI score 
(
p<0.05
)
. The AHI
significantly correlated with EASI 
(
p<0.05
)
 and deep sleep significantly correlated with itch NRS 
(
p<0.05
)
. We
recognized a significant correlation with the skin temperature spread during the night and EASI score 
(
p<0.001
)
.
Conclusion:
We managed to objectify the relevance and extent of sleep impairment in patients with AD. Surprisingly it is
shown, that PROMs alone did not fully reflect the impact. The data suggest that further research and discussion is
required on other potential factors beyond itch, that may affect sleep in AD, such as skin temperature or AHI.
