Abstract N°: 8222
The Effectiveness and Safety of JAK Inhibitors in a Cohort of Real-World patients with Moderate to Severe
Atopic Dermatitis
Kohei Matsumoto
1
, Vincent Yip
1
1
Liverpool University Hospital NHS Foundation Trust, Liverpool, United Kingdom
Introduction & Objectives:
Janus kinase inhibitors 
(
JAKi
)
 offer a targeted mechanism of action to modulate inflammatory pathways in
immune mediated diseases. Abrocitinib, baricitinib, and upadacitinib are licensed in the U.K for the treatment of
atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
. Despite their promise in clinical trials real world data is still emerging and adverse events
such as infections, thromboembolism, major cardiovascular events and malignancy have led to warnings about
their use. We report on the real-world outcomes of a cohort of patients with AD from the U.K. who have received
JAKi therapy.
Materials & Methods:
Patients prescribed JAKi for AD were identified through pharmacy records. Clinical records for each patient were
reviewed. Demographics, treatment history, disease severity, adverse events 
(
AEs
)
 and treatment duration were
recorded. Effectiveness at 16 weeks was defined according to recommendations from the National Institute for
Health and Care Excellence 
(
NICE
)
: at least 50% reduction in Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
 and at least a
4-point reduction in Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
 from when treatment started.**
Results:
There were 85 patients included in the study. Their demographics and clinical characteristics are reported in Table
1. The mean number of treatments for each patient before JAKi was 3.8. Previous systemic treatments included
methotrexate 
(
n=59
)
, azathioprine 
(
n=38
)
, ciclosporin 
(
n=57
)
, MMF 
(
n=11
)
 and phototherapy 
(
n=46
)
. 73
patients 
(
86%
)
 had received a biologic prior to JAK inhibitor therapy 
(
dupilumab = 61, Tralokinumab =12
)
. In
these patients, 46 
(
63%
)
 discontinued biologics because of lack of efficacy and 27 
(
37%
)
 discontinued because of
adverse events. 10 patients were prescribed multiple JAKi.
Mean baseline EASI and DLQI scores for each JAKi are outlined in Table 1.** Efficacy at week 16 as defined by
NICE was achieved by 81% 
(
n=30
)
 of the upadacitinib group, 49% 
(
n=19
)
 of the baricitinib group, and 22% 
(
n=2
)
of the abrocitinib group. The mean treatment duration ranged between 32.8 to 60.4 weeks depending on the JAKi.
95% 
(
n=37
)
 of baricitinib, 73% 
(
n=27
)
 of upadacitinib and all 
(
n=9
)
 abrocitinib patients received the higher dose
of treatment.
Treatment was discontinued due to lack of efficacy in 29% 
(
n=25
)
 of patients and due to adverse events in 11% of
patients 
(
n=9
)
 
(
Table 1
)
. Adverse effects leading to discontinuation were nausea 
(
n=2
)
, chest infection 
(
n=2
)
,
chest pain 
(
n=1
)
, generally unwell 
(
n=1
)
, abdominal pain 
(
n=1
)
, seborrheic dermatitis 
(
n=1
)
 and HSV reactivation
(
n=1
)
. Two deaths occurred in the baricitinib group 
(
ages 96 and 66
)
 due to staphylococcus aureus bacteraemia
and pneumonia. Other adverse events reported include acne 
(
n=5
)
, HSV reactivation 
(
n=2
)
 and dyslipidaemia
(
n=1
)
.
Conclusion:
In our real-world cohort of patients with moderate-to-severe AD, who have already been extensively treated with

systemics and biologics, JAK inhibitors achieved NICE criteria for effectiveness in 60% of patients. Upadacitinib
was the most effective JAKi in our cohort. Limitations include variability in baseline disease severity and previous
treatment exposure between different JAKi groups and low numbers of patients who were prescribed abrocitinib.
JAKi were generally well tolerated with adverse events consistent with those reported in clinical trials. New
treatments for AD with novel mechanisms of action beyond biologics and JAK inhibition are required for patients
who do not respond to existing therapies.
