Abstract N°: 8263
Evaluation of the efficacy of a medical device repairing emollient cream in adults with mild to moderate
head and neck atopic dermatitis
Marie-Dominique Thouvenin
1
, Marine Babin
1
, Valérie MENGEAUD*
2
, Andreas Wollenberg
3, 4, 5
1
Pierre Fabre Dermo-Cosmetics & Personal Care, R&D Department, Toulouse, France
2
Pierre Fabre Dermo-Cosmetics & Personal Care, Laboratoire Dermatologique Ducray, Lavaur, France
3
University Hospital Augsburg, Dept. of Dermatology and Allergy, Augsburg, Germany
4
University of Luebeck, Comprehensive Center for Inflammation Medicine, Luebeck, Germany
5
Ludwig-Maximilian University, Dept. of Dermatology and Allergy, Munich, Germany
Introduction & Objectives:
Head and neck atopic dermatitis 
(
HNAD
)
 is probably the most characteristic form of adult AD, with a strong
impact on quality of life. The management of these sensitive areas, often exposed to environmental triggers is
particularly challenging and specific datas on this location are sparse. The objective of the study was to evaluate
the efficacy of a medical device repairing emollient cream in this indication.
Materials & Methods:
Adults with mild to moderate HNAD 
(
3 
≤
 Local SCORAD 
≤
 10
)
 were included in a multicentric exploratory clinical
trial in Germany and applied the product twice daily for 4 weeks. Main assessments included local SCORAD and
PO-SCORAD, HNAD extent, local investigators and patients global assessment 
(
IGA and PGA
)
, pruritus and skin
pain intensity 
(
average over the past 24 hours; numerating rating scale
)
, quality of life 
(
Dermatology Life Quality
Index / DLQI, Chronic Itch Burden Scale
)
 and biometric evaluations 
(
Hydration Index /HI, Trans-Epidermal Water
Loss/TEWL
)
. Safety was also recorded.
Results:
Nineteen patients 
(
from 22 to 47 years old
)
 were included in the study which took place during the COVID
pandemic. 37 % of them reported more than 6 HNAD flare-up per year. At the end of the study 
(
D29
)
, we
observed a significant decrease of: Local PO-SCORAD 
(
p<0.001; -46%
)
, extent of HNAD 
(
p<0.001; -33%
)
, mean
pruritus intensity 
(
p=0.037; -32%
)
, mean skin pain intensity 
(
p=0.038; -40%
)
. An improvement of HNAD was
assessed by investigators for 58 % of the patients and by 53 % of patients themselves. A significant decrease in
DLQI score was also observed 
(
p<0.005; -38%
)
. Local SCORAD, HI and TEWL also improved 
(
p>0.05
)
. Nine
subjects had dermatological adverse events 
(
DAEs
)
 suspected to be related to the product 
(
mostly functional
signs as pruritus, skin pain
)
. For 7 of them, DAEs were also suspected to be related to HNAD worsening.
Conclusion:
The results of this study highlight the benefit of a medical device repairing emollient cream in head and neck
atopic dermatitis.** Despite the pandemic context during the study, including potential aggravating factors of
HNAD 
(
stress,** wearing of masks, 
…)
, a significant effect of the product on clinical parameters and quality of life
was demonstrated.
