Abstract N°: 8277
Integrated Single-Cell Atlas of Atopic Dermatitis Enables Cell-Type-Resolved Deconvolution of Bulk RNA-
Seq During Dupilumab Treatment
Lena Jacqueline Steinmeier
1
, Yask Gupta
1
, Jasmin Giebe
1
, Tatiana Sezin
1
, Diamant Thaçi
1
1
Lübeck, Institute for Inflammation Medicine, University of Lübeck, Lübeck, Germany
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disease with complex pathogenesis. Single-cell RNA
sequencing 
(
scRNA-seq
)
 has recently emerged as a powerful tool to dissect cellular heterogeneity and immune
dysregulation in multifactorial diseases. Although it provides high-resolution insights into individual cell
populations, its statistical power is often limited by data sparsity due to dropout events.
Herein, we aimed to 
(
1
)
 increase single-cell data power by integrating multiple datasets into the most
comprehensive single-cell atlas of atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 generated to date, 
(
2
)
 use this atlas to deconvolve bulk
RNA-seq data from dupilumab-treated patients, and 
(
3
)
 validate findings by analysing gene expression in tape
strips collected longitudinally from an independent cohort of AD patients we prospectively recruited.
Materials & Methods:
We integrated four public scRNA-seq datasets from human AD-affected and non-affected skin, as well as healthy
control skin using Seurat pipeline. Quality control excluded cells with <500 or >10,000 counts, >10%
mitochondrial reads, and potential doublets 
(
UMIcounts >3000
)
. We then applied the ENIGMA framework to
deconvolve publicly available bulk RNA-seq profiles before and after dupilumab treatment and predicted cell-
type-specific gene expression changes using the sc-RNAseq atlas. To validate our findings, we collected tape strips
from an independent prospective cohort of 19 moderate-to-severe AD patients before and after 16 weeks of
dupilumab therapy. Multiplex qPCR was used to assess the relative expression of the identified candidate genes in
response to therapy.
Results:
Our final atlas comprised 53 skin biopsies 
(
lesional and non-lesional
)
 from 31 AD patients and 22 healthy controls,
profiling a total of 189,806 high-quality cells. Louvain clustering revealed 18 distinct cell populations, with
keratinocytes 
(
27%
)
, T cells 
(
17%
)
, and fibroblasts 
(
16%
)
 being the most abundant in AD skin. Compared to
healthy controls, AD patients exhibited significantly increased proportions of monocytes/macrophages 
(
P=0.01
)
,
proliferating T cells 
(
P=0.001
)
, proliferating keratinocytes 
(
P=0.006
)
, and activated dendritic cells 
(
P=1×10
⁻⁵)
,
while melanocytes 
(
P=0.04
)
 and lymphatic endothelial cells were reduced in AD.
We next leveraged this comprehensive atlas to deconvolve bulk RNA-seq data from a longitudinal dupilumab
treatment study in 24 AD patients, enabling cell type
–
specific gene expression profiling in response to therapy
and prediction of treatment outcomes. Focusing on suprabasal keratinocytes, we identified 2,435 differentially
expressed genes over the course of treatment. To validate these findings, we performed multiplex qPCR on tape-
strip samples collected longitudinally from 19 prospectively recruited AD patients. This confirmed the predicted
upregulation of candidate genes such as 
SBSN
 and 
DMKN,
 and downregulation of 
S100A9
 and 
SERPINB3/4
 in
response to dupilumab treatment.
Conclusion:

We present a comprehensive single-cell atlas of AD skin, providing a valuable resource for dissecting cell-type
–
specific gene expression patterns. Integrated with a deconvolution approach, this atlas enables robust
interpretation of bulk RNA-seq data, even in the absence of matched single-cell profiles, and facilitates insights
into treatment responses in AD.
