5
Aarhus University Hospital, Department of Dermatology, Aarhus, Denmark
Introduction & Objectives:
While the efficacy and safety of oral Janus kinase inhibitors 
(
JAKi
)
 is well-demonstrated in clinical trials, there
remains continued concern for the risk of rare but potentially severe adverse events. Real-world evidence of JAKi
use in atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is accumulating and provides important insight into safety and effectiveness for
routine clinical practice. This systematic review and meta-analysis assessed the real-world effectiveness and safety
of abrocitinib, baricitinib and upadacitinib in the treatment of AD based on real-world evidence.
Materials & Methods:
PubMed and EMBASE were systematically searched from inception until 3rd January 2025 for observational
studies investigating effectiveness and safety of abrocitinib, baricitinib and upadacitinib for the treatment of AD.
The primary outcomes were the proportion of patients achieving a 
≥
75% improvement in the Eczema Area and
Severity Index 
(
EASI-75
)
 after 12 and 16 weeks, respectively. Secondary outcomes included the proportion of
patients achieving EASI-90 and the proportion of patients experiencing adverse events 
(
AE
)
. As most studies
reported outcomes combined for all doses of JAKi and only few studies reported exclusively for the different
dosages, all outcomes are reported for all dosages combined.
Results:
A total of 58 real-world studies with 336 patients treated with abrocitinib, 586 with baricitinib, and 2,599 with
upadacitinib were included. The proportion of patients achieving EASI-75 was 62% 
(
95% confidence interval [CI]:
34-84%
)
 and 62% 
(
95% CI: 48-74%
)
 for abrocitinib, 53% 
(
95% CI: 39-67%
)
 and 51% 
(
95% CI: 31-70%
)
 for
baricitinib, and 70% 
(
95% CI: 65-75%
)
 and 82% 
(
95% CI: 73-88%
)
 for upadacitinib after 12 weeks and 16 weeks,
respectively. The proportion of patients achieving EASI-90 was 46% 
(
95% CI: 24-70%
)
 and 23% 
(
95% CI: 15-34%
)
for abrocitinib, 29% 
(
95% CI: 21-37%
)
 and 24% 
(
95% CI: 16-34%
)
 for baricitinib, and 59% 
(
95% CI: 47-71%
)
 and
60% 
(
95% CI: 43-68%
)
 for upadacitinib after 12 weeks and 16 weeks, respectively. The most frequently reported
AEs across all JAKi were acne, HSV infection, nausea, and headache occurring in 22.0% 
(
95% CI: 15.7-30.0%
)
, 5.2%
(
95% CI: 1.5-16.8%
)
, 9.8% 
(
95% CI: 4.9-18.7%
)
, and 5.5% 
(
95% CI: 2.1-13.7
)
 of patients treated with abrocitinib,
6.2% 
(
95% CI: 2.8-13.4%
)
, 6.7% 
(
95% CI: 3.7-11.7%
)
, 7.1% 
(
95% CI: 3.8-12.7%
)
, and 3.7% 
(
95% CI: 1.8-7.7%
)
 of
patients treated with baricitinib, and 14.4% 
(
95% CI: 10.6-19.3%
)
, 6.8% 
(
95% CI: 4.2-11.0%
)
, 3.1% 
(
95% CI: 1.4-
7.05
)
, and 5.1% 
(
95% CI: 3.2-8.7%
)
 of patients treated with upadacitinib, respectively.

Conclusion:
JAKis demonstrate high effectiveness and safety in real-world as observed in trials. Importantly, HSV and acne
appear to be the most common adverse events reported in published real-world studies. However, lack of
reporting of outcomes according to dosages complicates conclusion and the risk of channeling bias 
(
healthy user
effect
)
 cannot be refuted.
