Abstract N°: 8306
Tralokinumab therapy in head and neck atopic dermatitis: a case series of 18 patients
Paola Merlo Gómez*
1
, Lucía Pinto Pulido
1
, Elena García Verdú
1
, Laura Martínez Alcalde
1
, Alicia Urbano Bueno
1
,
Ana Rodríguez-Villa Lario
1
, Elena Sánchez Gómez-Aparici
1
1
Hospital Universitario Príncipe de Asturias, Dermatology, Alcalá de Henares 
(
Madrid
)
, Spain
Introduction & Objectives:
The head and neck area is frequently affected in atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
. Lesions in visible areas such as in the head
and neck AD subtype are associated with significant impact in the quality of life of these patients. Treatment of
this specific location can be challenging given the limitations of treatment with topical corticosteroids and the
scarcity of literature on this matter. In our study we describe the efficacy of tralokinumab therapy in 18 patients
with head and neck AD.
Materials & Methods:
Retrospective observational single-center study including patients with atopic dermatitis affecting the head and
neck area treated with tralokinumab.
Results:
A total of 18 patients were identified. The median age was 38 years. All of them had already received systemic
therapy and 33.3% of the patients in our group, prior biologic therapy. About 83.3% of patients had at least
another comorbidity. In our study, a mean basal EASI score of 22.7 and mean NRS of 6.4 were found. After three
months of treatment, 63.6% of the patients with head and neck dermatitis showed complete remission of lesions
in this area, with a mean NRS of 3.6.
Conclusion:
Head and neck dermatitis is a difficult-to-treat area in atopic dermatitis. Tralokinumab may represent a suitable
therapeutic option in this group of patients.
