Abstract N°: 8355
A Case of Phosphoglucomutase 3 Deficiency Presenting with Severe Atopic Dermatitis and Recurrent
Infections: Response to Dupilumab Therapy
Muazzez Cigdem Oba
1
, Yusuf Demir
1
, Muhammed Aydın
2
, Zeynep Meric
2
, Dilan Demir Gumus
2
, Esra Yucel
2
, Ayca
Kıyım
2
1
Cerrahpasa Medical Faculty, Department of Dermatology, Istanbul University-Cerrahpasa, Istanbul, Turkey.,
Istanbul, Türkiye
2
Division of Allergy and Immunology, Department of Pediatrics, 
İ
stanbul University-Cerrahpa
ş
a, 
İ
stanbul, Türkiye.,
Istanbul, Türkiye
Introduction & Objectives:
Phosphoglucomutase 3 
(
PGM3
)
 deficiency is a rare autosomal recessive disorder characterized by immune
dysregulation and abnormal glycosylation. The clinical spectrum ranges from hyper-IgE syndrome-like phenotypes
to combined immunodeficiency, with features including severe atopic manifestations, recurrent infections,
elevated IgE, eosinophilia, and variable neurocognitive and skeletal abnormalities.
We describe a 3-year-old boy with homozygous PGM3 variant presenting with severe atopic dermatitis, recurrent
infections, markedly elevated IgE, and eosinophilia who responded to dupilumab therapy.
Materials & Methods:
A 3-year-old boy born to consanguineous parents presented with a 2-year history of progressively worsening skin
lesions. The patient presented with multiple, erythematous, eroded, hypertrophic lesions over extremities along
with eczematization and serous drainage in head and neck region. Bilateral conjunctivitis with crusting and
hyperemia was noted. His medical history included bronchiolitis at 5 months of age, anaphylaxis following
chickpea ingestion at age 2, and multiple food allergies to lentils, beans, and eggs.
Growth parameters including weight, height and head circumference were within normal percentiles. However, the
patient exhibited neurodevelopmental delay with absence of sentence formation.
Laboratory studies included CBC, immunoglobulin levels, complement studies, and genetic testing for primary
immunodeficiency disorders.
Results:
Laboratory investigations revealed persistently elevated eosinophilia with percentages ranging from 1.5% to 51.2%
(
normal 0-7%
)
 and absolute counts of 0.31-4.77 × 10³/
μ
L 
(
normal 0-0.8 × 10³/
μ
L
)
. Total IgE was markedly
elevated at >2500 IU/mL 
(
normal <100 IU/mL
)
. Complement studies showed consistently low C4 levels of 0.03-
0.04 g/L 
(
normal 0.1-0.4 g/L
)
.
Initial management with topical corticosteroids, emollients, and antimicrobial wash therapy provided minimal
benefit. The patient was subsequently hospitalized with fever 
(
38.7°C
)
 and vesicular eruptions consistent with
eczema herpeticum. Treatment with intravenous acyclovir, intravenous immunoglobulin, wet wrap therapy, and
intensified topical steroids yielded limited improvement.
Genetic testing identified a homozygous missense variant in the PGM3 gene 
(
NM_015599.3:c.145A>T
(
p.Arg49Trp
))
, classified as a variant of uncertain significance associated with autosomal recessive

Immunodeficiency 23. Skeletal and neurologic findings of the patient were in line with the diagnosis of PGM3
deficiency.
Following diagnosis, dupilumab therapy 
(
200 mg sc
)
 was initiated, resulting in marked improvement in the
patient
’
s skin condition starting after the first dose of dupilumab. Eosinophil counts decreased from 45-51% to
7.5% after treatment. Total IgE levels also showed a decreasing trend. He had no further episodes of skin
infections during the 4 month follow-up period.
Conclusion:
This case emphasizes the importance of considering monogenic immune disorders in patients with early-onset,
severe atopic dermatitis accompanied by recurrent infections and elevated IgE. Genetic testing is essential for
accurate diagnosis, and dupilumab therapy represents a promising targeted approach for managing the
cutaneous manifestations of PGM3 deficiency. In addition, this case illustrates the efficacy of dupilumab in
managing the cutaneous manifestations of PGM3 deficiency.
