Abstract N°: 8385
Lipid and Hepatic Changes in Patients Treated with Upadacitinib: A 52-Week Follow-Up Study
Carolina Montero-Vilchez
1
, Trinidad Montero-Vilchez
2
, Salvador Arias-Santiago
2
1
Virgen de las Nieves University Hospital, Pharmacy, Granada
2
Virgen de las Nieves University Hospital, Dermatology, Granada
Introduction & Objectives:
Upadacitinib, a selective JAK inhibitor, has been associated with changes in lipid, and hepatic parameters in clinical
trials. Reported alterations include dose-dependent increases in lipid parameters, and elevations in liver enzymes
(
Gamma-glutamyl transferase, GGT, and alanine aminotransferase ALT
)
. However, the long-term real-world impact
of these changes remains uncertain. This study aims to evaluate the analytical changes in patients treated with
upadacitinib over 52 weeks.
Materials & Methods:
We conducted a prospective observational study including AD patients who initiated upadacitinib for the first
time. Lipid, and hepatic parameters were monitored at baseline and throughout the 52-week follow-up period.
Changes in lipid profile and liver enzymes were analyzed.
Results:
After 52 weeks of treatment, 37 AD patients were included. Mean age of the patients was 29.38 
(
12.02 SD
)
 years,
being 62.2% 
(
23/37
)
 of them female. No significant changes were observed in liver enzyme levels compared to
baseline, ALT 
(
20.00 vs 23.51, p=0.13
)
, GGT 
(
19.67 vs 23.52, p=0.07
)
. Changes in total cholesterol were observed,
(
169.93 vs 189.13 p=0.015
)
, however no other significant changes were reported in any other lipid parameters;
LDL 
(
112.50 vs 120.80, p=0.18
)
, HDL 60.30 vs 62.70 p=0.32
)
. No cases of significant lipid alterations requiring
intervention, or drug-induced liver injury were detected.
Conclusion:
In this real-world cohort, upadacitinib treatment did not lead to significant lipid or hepatitc alterations over 52
weeks. These findings suggest that routine laboratory monitoring, while recommended, may not frequently detect
clinically relevant alterations. Further studies with larger populations are warranted to confirm these results.
