Abstract N°: 8404
Patients with Atopic Dermatitis in visible Areas have a high Disease Burden and respond well to Treatment
with Biologics and JAK Inhibitors: Results from the TREATgermany Registry
Susanne Abraham*
1, 2
, Barbara Kind
2
, Jörg Welker
2
, Katharina Loennecke
2
, Stephan Weidinger
3
, Thomas Werfel
4
,
Jochen Schmitt
2
1
Department of Dermatology, University Allergy Center, Medical Faculty Carl Gustav Carus, TU Dresden, Dresden,
Germany
2
Center of Evidence-based Healthcare, University Hospital and Medical Faculty Carl Gustav Carus, TU Dresden,
Dresden, Dresden, Germany
3
Center for Inflammatory Skin Diseases, Department of Dermatology and Allergy, University Hospital Schleswig-
Holstein, Campus Kiel, Kiel, Germany
4
Department of Dermatology and Allergy, Hannover Medical School, Hannover, Hannover, Germany
Introduction & Objectives:
TREATgermany is a prospective registry for moderate to severe atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 in adult patients, in which
currently 2,369 patients are followed up. Patients with AD often suffer from a high disease burden, especially when
visible parts of the body such as the face and arms are affected.
Materials & Methods:
Here we analyzed a subgroup of 1169 patients who received a new systemic therapy with a follow-up visit 
(
FUV
)
no earlier than 28 days after the start of systemic therapy. The target population included 786 patients with visible
involvement 
(
Eczema area severity index 
(
EASI
)
 head >=2 or EASI arms >=4
)
, of whom n=586 received a biologic
(
dupilumab, tralokinumab or lebrikizumab
)
 and n=119 a JAK inhibitor 
(
upadacitinib, baricitinib or abrocitinib
)
.
Results:
The Dermatology life Quality Index 
(
DLQI
)
 of patients with visible skin lesions treated with biologics decreased
from 14.5 to 5.4 
(
minus 9.1
)
 at the first follow-up visit. In patients treated with JAK inhibitors, the treatment
response was similar, with an improvement from 14.9 to 4.3 
(
minus 10.6
)
. In comparison, patients who did not
have facial and arm involvement showed an improvement of the DLQI from 11.9 to 5.0 
(
minus 6.9
)
 when treated
with biologics 
(
n= 318
)
 and from 10.8 to 4.7 
(
minus 6.1
)
 when treated with JAK inhibitors 
(
n= 49
)
.
Sleeplessness in the last 3 nights and pruritus in the last 3 days of patients with visible area involvement were also
significantly improved both with biologic therapy 
(
improvement of 4.0 and 3.7, respectively, on the numeric rating
Scale 
(
NRS
)
 from 0-10
)
 and with JAK inhibitor therapy 
(
improvement of 4.3 and 4.4, respectively, on the NRS
)
.
When visible areas were involved, the initial POEM 
(
Patient Oriented Eczema Measure
)
 was elevated and a clear
improvement could be achieved with both, biologics 
(
20.1 at the initial visit and reduction of the 1st FUV to 9.0,
improvement of 11.1
)
 and JAK inhibitors 
(
from 20.6 to 7.7, improvement of 12.9
)
. The initial POEM score was
lower in patients when visible areas were not affected: an improvement from 17.5 to 8.3 
(
9.2 point improvement
)
was seen in patients treated with biologics, and from 16.1 to 7.9 
(
8.2 point improvement
)
 in patients treated with
JAK inhibitors.
Conclusion:

In summary, patients with visible area involvement reported a significant improvement in their quality of life and
their symptoms with both biologic and JAK inhibitor therapy.
