Abstract N°: 8426
Treatment with an abrocitinib surrogate molecule alleviates disease phenotype and prevents skin barrier
impairment during flare-up in a humanized mouse model of atopic dermatitis
Sofoklis Koudounas
1
, Lisa Zondler
1
, Markus Fehrholz
1
, Ralf Ludwig
1, 2
, Kristian Reich
3
, Melissa Watkins
4
, Karin
Pappelbaum
1
, Amos Gilhar
5
, Ilaria Piccini
1
, Marta Bertolini*
1
1
QIMA Life Sciences, QIMA Monasterium GmbH, Münster, Germany
2
University of Schleswig Holstein, Lübeck, Germany
3
University Medical Center Hamburg-Eppendorf, Hamburg, Germany
4
Pfizer Inc, New York, NY, United States
5
Technion 
–
 Israel, Institute of Technology, Haifa, Israel
Introduction & Objectives:
Treatment with the JAK1 inhibitor Abrocitinib 
(
Abro
)
 alleviates clinical signs of atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 and inhibits
flare activity in patients. Here, we aimed to explore mechanisms whereby Abro functions to reduce AD disease
recurrence using a humanized AD mouse model where disease relapse can be observed following exposure to
sonic stress.
Materials & Methods:
Abdominal skin from 3 healthy donors was transplanted onto 60 beige-SCID mice. 30 days after transplantation,
grafted skin was injected intradermally with autologous 
in vitro
 Th2-polarized peripheral blood mononuclear cells
(
PBMCs
)
. After xenograft development of an AD phenotype, mice received a daily oral administration of vehicle or
PF-06667291 
(
PF; abro surrogate molecule
)
 for 14 days. One day after treatment discontinuation, mice were
exposed to sonic stress for 24h. 14 days following sonic stress exposure, macroscopic imaging confirmed the
sonic-stress-induced flare. Xenografts were also analysed by histochemistry, quantitative 
(
immuno-
)
histomorphometry or RNA-Seq.
Results:
Treatment with PF in the AD xenografts was found to significantly reduce epidermal thickness, and tendentially
increased filaggrin and claudin-1 expression. PF also slightly decreased the number of CD3 T-cells , including
those expressing the resident marker CD45RO or the skin homing molecule CLA. Thus, in line with clinical data for
Abro, therapeutic treatment with PF led to alleviation of AD signs in our humanized mouse model.
RNASeq analysis after disease amelioration and induction of lesion recurrence revealed upregulation of genes
associated with skin barrier 
(
IVL, HRNR, CLDN8
)
 and lower expression of genes associated to inflammation,
i.e. alarmins 
(
S100A7, S100A8
)
, Th1 
(
CXCL9, CXCL10
)
, Th17 
(
CXCL1, DEFB2
)
, and Th2 
(
CCL13, -17, -18, -22,
)
 in
grafted skin from at least two donors in the PF treated group
.
The number of CD3 T-cells were just slightly lower in xenotranplants exposed to PF before flare up induction, as
compared to those deriving from vehicle treated mice. Similarly, epidermal thickness and the expression of
involucrin did not differ between treated and untreated xenotransplants after sonic stress. However, higher level
of filaggrin, keratin-1 and in particular claudin-1 were detected with the latter reaching significance, in PF treated-
samples compared to vehicle xenotransplants after lesion reappearance.
Conclusion:

Thus, PF was found to reduce the severity of sonic stress-induced inflammatory relapses and enhance barrier
integrity in AD-induced xenografts. These data suggest that keratinocyte responses to Abro may contribute to its
ability to inhibit AD flare reappearance.
