6
, Aleksandra Rak-Za
ł
uska
7
, Monika Ró
ż
ewicka-Czaba
ń
ska
7
,
Dominik Samotij
1
, Adam Reich
1
, Jacek Szepietowski
5
, Natalia Zdanowska
6
, Justyna Szcz
ę
ch
1
, Aleksandra Lesiak
3
,
Joanna Narbutt
3
1
Department of Dermatology , Institute of Medical Sciences, Medical College of Rzeszow University, Rzeszow,
Poland
2
Department of Dermatology, Venerology and Paediatric Dermatology, Medical University of Lublin, Lublin,
Poland
3
Department of Dermatology, Pediatric Dermatology and Dermatological Oncology,, Medical University of Lodz,
Lodz, Poland
4
Department of Dermatology and Venereology , Medical University of Bialystok, Bialystok, Poland
5
Department of Dermatology, Venereology and Allergology , Wroclaw Medical University, Wroclaw, Poland
6
Department of Dermatology, Sexually Transmitted Diseases and Clinical Immunology, , University of Warmia and
Mazury in Olsztyn, Olsztyn, Poland
7
Department of Skin Diseases and Venerology PUM, , Pomeranian Medical University, Police, Poland
Introduction & Objectives:
TADPol 
(
Treatment of Atopic Dermatitis in Poland
)
 study is a prospective multicenter project aimed to compare
the effectiveness and safety of dupilumab 
(
DUPI
)
 and upadacitinib 
(
UPA
)
 in the treatment of moderate to severe
atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 over a 52-week period among AD patients in Poland.
Materials & Methods:
This real-world observational study included 118 patients diagnosed with moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis
(
AD
)
. The cohort consisted of 53.4% males and 46.6% females, with a mean age of 25.4
 
±
 
15.5 years. Patients
received either dupilumab 
(
DUPI; 52.6%
)
 or upadacitinib 
(
UPA; 47.4%
)
. Most patients 
(
71.2%
)
 were biologically
naïve at the start of therapy. The follow-up period was 52 weeks.
Baseline characteristics varied slightly between treatment arms. In the DUPI group 
(
n=62
)
: mean age 23.5 years,
Eczema Area and Severity Index 
(
EASI
)
 26.7, SCORing of Atopic Dermatitis 
(
SCORAD
)
 67.5, itch Visual Analogue
Scale 
(
VAS
)
 7.5, Dermatology Life Quality Index/Children
’
s Dermatology Life Quality Index 
(
DLQI/CDLQI
)
 18.2,
and 19.3% with prior biologic use. In the UPA group 
(
n=56
)
: mean age 27.5 years, EASI 29.7, SCORAD 60.2, itch
VAS 7.3, DLQI/CDLQI 18.1, and 39.2% with prior biologic exposure.
Results:
Both DUPI and UPA significantly improved EASI scores. UPA demonstrated a faster onset of action, with early
response evident by Week 4 and a slight edge in achieving full clearance. However, DUPI showed progressive
improvement, with comparable or superior outcomes by Week 52.
Long-term symptom control favored DUPI. At Week 52, itch VAS was 1.7 in the DUPI group compared to 3.8 in the
UPA group. DUPI also outperformed UPA in pain reduction starting at Week 16, with final values of 0.6 vs. 1.9,

respectively. Treatment persistence beyond Week 24 was higher in the DUPI group, suggesting improved
tolerability and long-term adherence.
Conclusion:
Preliminary real-world findings highlight distinct response dynamics between DUPI and UPA. While UPA provides
faster initial improvement in moderate-to-severe AD, DUPI offers superior long-term control of itch and pain,
along with better treatment retention. These results support a personalized approach to treatment selection based
on clinical presentation, patient expectations, and therapeutic goals.
