King Faisal Specialist Hospital and Research Center, Montreal, Canada
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 affects 5
–
20% globally, with severe cases requiring systemic therapies. Advancements like
Janus kinase inhibitors highlight the need to explore the evolving research landscape for better treatments.
Materials & Methods:
The databases used to search for publications were Web of Science Core Collection with the search strategy that
included the search terms 
“(
Janus kinase inhibitors OR JAK
)
 AND 
(
atopic dermatitis
)
.
”
 A total of 797 publications
were identified, of which 776 were included in the study after exclusions. The following tools were employed to
analyze the publication output, citations, keyword co-occurrence, and international collaborations of the included
studies: VOSviewer, Excel, and Draw. io.
Results:
The USA had the highest number of publications 
(
34%
)
, followed by Germany 
(
12.62%
)
, Japan 
(
11.87%
)
, and
China 
(
8.5%
)
. Pfizer funded 38 studies, AbbVie supported 35, and Eli Lilly contributed to 32. Emma Guttman-
Yassky authored 25 publications, while Eric Simpson contributed 22. Among journals, The Journal of Allergy and
Clinical Immunology and The Journal of Dermatological Treatment were notable, with 20 and 26 publications,
respectively. Highly cited references included 
“
Oetjen 
(
2017
)”
 with 674 citations and 
“
Wollenberg 
(
2018
)”
 with
393.
Conclusion:
This study highlights global research trends and collaboration networks, offering insights to guide future research
on JAK inhibitors for atopic dermatitis.
Keywords: The following keywords were frequently used; these include 
“
atopic dermatitis
”
 and 
“
JAK inhibitors
”
.
