Abstract N°: 8472
An animated educational video is effective for improving treatment outcomes in pediatric patients with
atopic dermatitis:  A prospective  trial.
eve finkelstein
1
, ayelet ollech
2
, michal neumark
1
, Vered Molho-Pessach
2
1
hadassah medical center, dermatology, jerusalem, Israel
2
shaare zedek medical center, jerusalem, Israel
Introduction & Objectives:
  Compliance with treatment of pediatric atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is low, despite
parents
’
 distress over their children
’
s suffering.
We aimed to assess whether viewing an educational video that explains the pathophysiology of AD and the
rationale behind the treatment protocol improves disease outcomes in children with atopic dermatitis.
Materials & Methods:
 We scripted an educational video which was animated by a professional studio. 100
pediatric patients with AD were enrolled and treated according to the standard treatment protocol for AD, based
on the 
Consensus
‐
based European guidelines for treatment of AD in adults and children
.
The patients were divided into two groups, matched by age and disease severity. The intervention group received
the animated video by email as well an individualized printed treatment plan, while the control group received
only the individualized printed treatment plan. Disease severity and quality of life were assessed at 1and 3 months
after the initial visit.
Results:
 Eighty-two patients completed the requirements and were included in the statistical analysis. The
intervention group showed statistically significant improvement in all outcomes, with EASI SCORAD, pPNRS, and
CDQLI scores improving significantly at both at 1 month and at 3 months compared to the control group.
Conclusion:
 An animated educational video can improve patient outcomes in pediatric AD.
