Abstract N°: 8496
Drug survival of systemic off-label therapies for treatment of hand eczema
Maria Oberländer Christensen*
1
, David Thein
1
, Caroline Meyer Olesen
1
1
Bispebjerg Hospital, Department of Dermatology and Venereology, Copenhagen NV, Denmark
Introduction & Objectives:
 Hand eczema 
(
HE
)
 is a common and often chronic inflammatory skin disease. First-
line treatment includes trigger avoidance, emollients, and topical corticosteroids 
(
TCS
)
 or topical calcineurin
inhibitors 
(
TCI
)
, and recently delgocitinib was approved as an additional topical treatment option. Alitretinoin is
the only systemic treatment approved for HE, however a large proportion of patients receive off-label therapies
when initiating systemic therapy. Prior studies, investigating drug survival of off-label treatments in HE, have often
been limited by small sample sizes and not included biologics. This study aimed to assess drug survival of off-label
treatments, including biologics, in a nationwide HE cohort.
Materials & Methods:
 All Danish patients diagnosed with HE who were treated with off-label systemic therapy
between 2008 and 2024 were included in the study. Off-label treatment included acitretin, azathioprine,
cyclosporine, dupilumab, methotrexate and mycophenolate mofetil. Data was extracted from Danish national
patient registries. Treatment series started at initiation and terminated at the end of drug supply, migration, death
or initiation of another HE treatment. A period of 120 and 180 days was considered an allowable gap in drug
supply. Patients could be included in multiple groups, but only the first treatment series with each drug was
included. Unadjusted survival curves were presented using Kaplan-Meier curves and a frailty model 
(
Cox-
regression models
)
 was used for risk assessment. The frailty model was adjusted for sex, age, asthma, atopic
dermatitis, calendar year, socioeconomic status, and time since hospital-confirmed diagnosis.
Results:
 This study comprised 2,251 patients with HE [mean age: 50.3, standard deviation 
(
SD
)
 14.7, 57.0% female]
and 2,783 treatment series. A total of 1,236 
(
54.9%
)
 patients received methotrexate, 594 
(
26.4%
)
 acitretin, 648
(
28.8%
)
 azathioprine, 160 
(
7.1%
)
 cyclosporine, 91 
(
4.0%
)
 dupilumab and 54 
(
2.4%
)
 mycophenolate mofetil.
Majority of patients had been treated with either potent and/or very potent TCS 
(
95.8% and 76.7%, respectively
)
prior to systemic treatment. When comparing off-label therapies using Cox proportional hazard regression model
with acitretin as reference, dupilumab and cyclosporine showed significantly higher drug survival compared to
acitretin 
(
HR: 0.43, 95% CI: 0.32-0.58 and HR: 0.81, 95% CI: 0.67-0.98, respectively
)
 with dupilumab having the
highest drug survival of all treatments both for HE patients with 
(
n=41
)
 and patients without AD 
(
n=55
)
, although
drug survival was highest in HE patients with AD treated with dupilumab. In contrast, azathioprine 
(
HR: 1.02, 95%
CI: 0.91
–
1.16
)
, methotrexate 
(
HR: 0.99, 95% CI: 0.89
–
1.10
)
, and mycophenolate mofetil 
(
HR: 0.84, 95% CI: 0.63
–
1.13
)
 did not show a significant difference in drug survival when compared to acitretin. Finally, 869 
(
38.6%
)
patients had been treated with >1 off-label systemic therapy.
Conclusion:
 Drug survival of traditional systemic therapies was comparable with cyclosporine showing slightly
higher drug survival compared to acitretin, but comparable to most other systemic therapies. Dupilumab
demonstrated the highest drug survival among off-label treatments, potentially reflecting higher efficacy and
tolerability. This may indicate that most off-label treatment options in HE are inadequate in achieving disease
control.

