1
, Dagmar Simon
2
1
Inselspital, Bern University Hospital, University of Bern, Dermatology, Bern, Switzerland
2
Inselspital, Bern University Hospital, University of Bern, Department of Dermatology, Bern, Switzerland
Introduction & Objectives:
 Targeted therapies with monoclonal antibodies, biologics, as well as Janus kinase
inhibitors 
(
JAKi
)
 have become important tools in the clinical management of patients with moderate to severe
atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
. Upon therapy with biologics, the development of a 
de novo
 or worsening of a pre-existing
immune-mediated disorder have been reported. We aimed at investigating the spectrum of adverse events 
(
AE
)
observed in AD patients on systemic therapies and at identifying possible interferences with the immune system.
Materials & Methods:
 This single-center retrospective study provides a descriptive analysis of AE observed in 145
patients on systemic therapies 
(
n=288
)
 for AD.
Results:
 Overall, 269 AE had been reported in AD patients treated with oral immunosuppressants, biologics
(
dupilumab, tralokinumab
)
 and/or JAKinh, of which most were mild 
(
67.3%
)
, but in 27.1% the culprit drug had to
be stopped. AE arising from immune or cytokine imbalance upon biologics were ocular surface disease 
(
67/75
events
)
, the onset or worsening of autoimmune/-inflammatory diseases, including Crohn
’
s disease, sarcoidosis,
vitiligo, alopecia areata, and psoriasis, as well as cutaneous herpes virus infections. AE upon immunosuppressant
and JAKi therapy were mainly related to infections and abnormal laboratory results, e.g. increased creatinine and
liver enzyme levels.
Conclusion:
 Novel therapies interfering with the immune system may cause dysbalances followed by unexpected,
paradoxical AE. Assessing the spectrum of AE to biologics and JAKi is important for further patient management,
but also for understanding immunologic mechanisms and interactions 
in vivo
.
