Abstract N°: 8504
Diagnostic and Treatment Challenges in Atopic Dermatitis Associated with Food Allergies
Raluca Miulescu
1
, Alina Turenschi
2
1
Pediatric Hospital Ploie
ș
ti , Ploie
ș
ti , Romania
2
Pediatric hospital Ploie
ș
ti , Ploie
ș
ti , Romania
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis is a chronic inflammatory skin condition frequently associated with food allergies, particularly
among children. The complexity of this interaction leads to significant diagnostic and therapeutic challenges, with
a considerable impact on patients
’
 quality of life.
Materials & Methods:
This paper aims to analyze the difficulties in diagnosing and managing atopic dermatitis associated with food
allergies and to provide solutions based on the latest scientific evidence.
Results:
Accurate diagnosis is often hindered by the overlapping symptoms of atopic dermatitis and food allergies.
Sensitization tests, such as skin prick tests and specific IgE measurements, can be useful but have limitations.
Therapeutic interventions, including elimination diets and topical or systemic treatments, require careful
individualization to avoid adverse effects and nutritional deficiencies.
Conclusion:
Atopic dermatitis associated with food allergies represents a multidimensional challenge in family medicine and
pediatrics. Diagnosis and treatment should be approached in an integrated manner, involving a multidisciplinary
team, in order to optimize clinical outcomes and improve patients
’
 quality of life.
