Abstract N°: 8519
CD1a-targeted PD-1 agonist bispecifics achieve localized inhibition of inflammatory T cells in Atopic
Dermatitis
Adam Curnock
1
, David Overton
1
, Randeep Singh
1
, Charlotte Viant
1
, Ohannes Mazigi
1
, Susan Aungier
1
, Onur
Egriboz
2
, Ilaria Piccini
2
, Janin Edelkamp
2
, Marta Bertolini
2
, Hussein Al-Mossawi
1
1
Immunocore Ltd., 92 Park Drive, Abingdon OX14 4RY, United Kingdom
2
QIMA Life Sciences, QIMA Monasterium GmbH, Münster, Germany
Introduction & Objectives:
T cells are central in the pathogenesis of atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
, reacting to environmental allergens, driving the
inflammatory response, thereby causing a compromised skin barrier. In the regulation of T cell activation, the PD-
1 receptor serves as a crucial immune checkpoint. By delivering inhibitory signals, PD-1 helps maintain the
threshold for T cell activation, thereby limiting the responses of effector T cells, promoting the resolution of
inflammation, and establishing T cell tolerance.
Materials & Methods:
In an effort to address inflammatory skin diseases, such as AD, we developed a skin-targeted PD-1 agonist as a
novel therapeutic strategy. This approach utilizes a PD-1 agonist attached to cell-bridging bispecific antibodies
containing an affinity enhanced targeting domain, which selectively binds to CD1a. CD1a is a non-restricted, HLA-
related molecule that is prominently expressed on skin antigen-presenting cells 
(
APCs
)
. The PD-1 agonist
component is designed to inhibit T cell activity at the immune synapse when the bispecifics attach to CD1a during
antigen presentation.
Results:
Binding specificity of the bispecifics to APCs was shown in co-culture assays where IL-2 secretion from CD4 T cells
was inhibited in APCs positive for CD1a but not in CD1a negative APCs, confirming no systemic activity.
Furthermore, we could confirm that the PD-1 binding domain of the bispecifics does not compete with the binding
of natural PD-1 ligands 
(
PD-L1 and PD-L2
)
 and instead acts additively with endogenous PD-L1 to inhibit T cell
activation, demonstrated in a co-culture assay of primary monocyte derived Langerhans cells 
(
MoLCs
)
 and T cells.
Finally, treatment with the bispecifics reduced house dust mite 
(
HDM
)
-stimulated IL-13 release in autologous
MoLC - T cell assays. Thus, the 
in vitro
 results validate that the bispecifics specifically and effectively inhibit MoLC-
stimulated T cell activation.
Target engagement experiments conducted on healthy human skin 
ex vivo
 revealed co-localization of the
bispecifics and CD1a, confirming the specific binding of fluorescently labeled molecules to APC. Additionally, in AD
lesional skin, bispecific-labelled APCs were found in proximity to T cells, further highlighting their potential to
target T cells in the affected tissue. Quantitative 
(
immuno-
)
histomorphometry demonstrated that the CD1a-
targeted PD-1 bispecific effectively suppress T cell proliferation and cytokine array analyses confirmed reduced
release of pro-inflammatory mediators in AD lesional and peri-lesional skin of 3 patients.
Conclusion:
These findings underscore the potential of CD1a-targeted PD-1 agonists to achieve localized inhibition of
inflammatory T cells within the skin. This localized approach offers the significant advantage of circumventing

systemic immunosuppression, making it a promising therapeutic strategy for managing inflammatory skin
conditions such as AD.
