Abstract N°: 8592
Which primary endpoint should I choose for an AD study to best discern a difference from placebo?
Giuseppina D
’
Angelo*
1
1
Indero, Montreal, Canada
Introduction & Objectives:
Choosing a primary endpoint that will discern a difference in active treatment versus placebo in early phase atopic
dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 studies is key to a successful study. We examined several continuous and binary endpoints that
are commonly used in AD studies and using statistical methodologies, we present our recommendations. We also
examined the trend over time to assess the potential impact of higher placebo rates being observed on the choice
of endpoint.
Materials & Methods:
Using data from 10 
(
ten
)
 in-house studies in atopic dermatitis conducted over the last five years, we compare
power and variability for the following endpoints: Change from baseline in EASI, Percent change from baseline in
EASI, clinical success rate 
(
defined as a 2-point decrease from baseline in IGA and a score of 0 or 1 at the primary
endpoint visit
)
, 2-point decrease from baseline in IGA, EASI50, EASI75. Linear mixed models and Chi-squared
based tests were used for continuous and binary primary endpoints, respectively.
Results:
Results show that using binary variables such as clinical success or EASI50/EASI75 are less robust than continuous
ones when trying to discern a difference from placebo in earlier phase studies. Through the assessment of 
p
-
values and point estimates, we see that power is greater when using continuous endpoints to examine a
separation from placebo.
Conclusion:
Statistical theory generally supports the choice of a continuous endpoint versus a binary one and this work
supports this recommendation.
