Abstract N°: 8611
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 in patients with skin of colour: A retrospective analysis from a tertiary dermatology
centre
Vasiliki Pikoula*
1
, manaal bharadia
1
, Anthony Bewley
2
, Maria-Angeliki Gkini
2
1
Barts and the London School of Medicine and Dentistry , London, United Kingdom
2
Department of Dermatology Barts Health NHS Trust , London, United Kingdom
Introduction & Objectives:
Despite recent advances in management of atopic dermatitis, patients with skin of colour remain
underrepresented in clinical trials and real-world data studies. Variations in genetics, phenotypes, comorbidities
and tolerability may influence treatment choise and real-world outcomes. This study assessed the effectiveness,
tolerability, and patient experience of EMA-approved advanced therapies 
(
biologics and JAK inhibitors
)
 in patients
with skin of colour diagnosed with moderate-to-severe AD.
Materials & Methods:
Fifty consecutive adult patients with skin of colour diagnosed with moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis seen
from 10/24 to 05/25 in our specialist clinic were included. Baseline characteristics, such as age, sex, ethnicity, and
Fitzpatrick skin type were noted. Disease history, comorbidities, treatment course, clinical outcomes 
(
EASI, DLQI
)
,
and adverse events were extracted and analysed retrospectively using SPSS. At baseline patients were already on
a conventional systemic treatment or on an advanced treatment.
Results:
Mean age was 29.6 years; 56% were male. Most were of Bangladeshi 
(
64%
)
, Chinese 
(
14%
)
, or Pakistani 
(
6%
)
background. Commonest Fitzpatrick skin types were IV and V. Disease onset was typically in childhood. Asthma
(
48%
)
, hay fever 
(
42%
)
, and food/environmental allergies 
(
38%
)
 were common comorbidities. Ocular disease
(
24%
)
, eczema herpeticum 
(
20%
)
, and psychological comorbidities 
(
12%
)
 were also reported. Most patients had
≥
2 comorbidities, highlighting the complex clinical profile of this cohort.
Dupilumab was most used 
(
58%
)
, followed by upadacitinib 
(
22%
)
, tralokinumab 
(
8%
)
, lebrikizumab 
(
8%
)
, and
abrocitinib 
(
4%
)
. Most were on their first advanced therapy 
(
70%
)
, 24% on second, and 6% to their third.
Switching to another therapy was mainly due to suboptimal disease control. Side effects such as conjuctivitis, were
another reason for switching, while some patients opted for oral treatments due to being unable to self-inject.
Baseline EASI and DLQI were 21.2 and 16.9. At last visit, these improved to 5.0 and 6.0, respectively. For biologics
(
n=37
)
, baseline mean EASI was 22.7 
(
SD 13.2
)
 and DLQI 17.6 
(
SD 7.1
)
; at last follow-up EASI 6.1 
(
SD 8.5
)
 and
DLQI 6.8 
(
SD 7.4
)
. For JAK inhibitors 
(
n=13
)
, baseline mean EASI was 16.7 
(
SD 10.4
)
 and DLQI 14.8 
(
SD 7.6
)
;
follow-up EASI 1.8 
(
SD 4.0
)
 and DLQI 3.5 
(
SD 4.9
)
, suggesting a stronger response. However, several patients
remained on biologics despite suboptimal control due to some improvement or concerns around JAK inhibitors
’
safety.
Adverse effects in biologics included ocular surface disease in 32.4% 
(
e.g. conjuctivitis, dry eyes
)
, and facial
involvement 
(
24.3%
)
, mostly with dupilumab. JAK inhibitors side effects included acneiform rash 
(
15.4%
)
 and
infections 
(
7.7%
)
. Real-world barriers such as medication delays, needle aversion, and topical non-adherence also
impacted outcomes.

Conclusion:
In this skin of colour cohort, patients tended to have severe disease and multiple comorbidities. Advanced
therapies improved disease severity and quality of life, though responses varied by drug class. Treatment
decisions were often influenced by comorbidities, patient preferences, and practical factors, supporting a
personalized care approach. Finally, further research is required to better understand the disease and treatment
options in this underrepresented group.
