Abstract N°: 8702
Long-term use and negative impact of systemic glucocorticosteroids in individuals with atopic dermatitis 
–
analysis of German claims data
Matthias Augustin*
1
, Theresa Klinger
1
, Charlotte Willers
1
, Katharina Müller
1
, Kristina Hagenström
1
1
IVDP/UKE, Hamburg
Introduction & Objectives:
Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a chronic inflammatory skin disease often associated with severe pruritus and a
significant reduction in quality of life. While topical therapies are often the first line of treatment, systemic
treatment options are needed in most moderate to severe cases. To date, n=7 systemic drugs are approved for
longerm use in Germany. By contrast, systemic glucocorticosteroids 
(
sGCS
)
 are considered as a short-term
treatment option only due to their side effect profile.
Materials & Methods:
To investigate the extend of use of sGCS in patients with AD and the incidence of adverse effects using routine
claims data from the German Statutory Health Insurance 
(
SHI
)
 system.
A retrospective cross-sectional and longitudinal analysis was performed using anonymised claims data from DAK-
Gesundheit 
(
2016-2020
)
, a nationwide statutory health insurance. Individuals with AD were identified using ICD-
10 codes 
(
L20
)
. Frequency of sGCS prescription was defined as: 1
)
 the number of quarters with at least one sGCS
prescription and 2
)
 the defined daily doses 
(
DDD
)
. Propensity score matching 
(
PSM
)
 was used to control for
confounding. Possible side effects 
(
e.g. osteoporosis, diabetes
)
 were modelled in an adjusted logistic regression.
Results:
The prevalence of AD in 2020 was 4.07% 
(
4.12% women, 3.71% men
)
. In the same year, 9.91% of people with AD
received sGCS compared to 5.54% of people without AD 
(
p<0.01
)
. The prescription rate was significantly higher in
women 
(
10.20% vs. 9.42% in men, p=<0.01
)
 and in older people. The prevalence of AD and sGCS varied
regionally. During 3 years of follow-up, 51% of people with AD who received sGCS were prescribed sGCS for > 1
quarter and 15% for > 6 quarters. The odds of developing osteoporosis 
(
odds ratio 3.95 [definition 1] and 1.80
[definition 2]
)
 and diabetes 
(
odds ratio 1.90 [definition 1] and 1.38 [definition 2]
)
 were significantly higher in
people with AD receiving sGCS.
Conclusion:
A significant proportion of people with AD in Germany receive long-term sGCS therapy, contrary to guideline
recommendations. The incidence of conditions known to be adverse effects of steroids was significantly higher in
people receiving long-term sGCS for AD compared to short-term users and patients without any sGCS use,
suggesting the need for optimised care.
