Abstract N°: 8706
Dropout rate in digital strategies for the management of atopic dermatitis: Systematic Review and Meta-
analysis
Juan Carlos Hernández Rodríguez
1
, David Morón-Mas
2
, Cristina García-Muñoz
3
, Rocío Bueno-Molina
1
, Mercedes
Sendín-Martín
1
, Jose Juan Pereyra Rodriguez
1
1
Virgen del Rocio University Hospital, Dermatology, Seville, Spain
2
University of Seville, Medicine, Seville, Spain
3
Universidad Loyola Andalucía, Ciencias de la Salud y Biomédicas, Seville, Spain
Introduction & Objectives:
Digital interventions offer promising tools for the management of atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
, a chronic inflammatory
skin condition with significant impact on patients
’
 quality of life. However, high dropout rates in randomised
controlled trials 
(
RCTs
)
 may compromise the validity of outcomes and hinder implementation in clinical practice.
Materials & Methods:
A systematic review with meta-analysis and meta-regression was conducted following PRISMA 2020 guidelines.
Five databases 
(
PubMed, Scopus, Web of Science, Cochrane Library, CINAHL
)
 were searched from inception until
April 2025. RCTs reporting dropout rates in digital interventions for AD were included. The Joanna Briggs Institute
tool assessed risk of bias. A meta-analysis of proportions estimated overall and subgroup dropout rates. Odds
ratio 
(
OR
)
 meta-analyses compared dropout likelihood between digital and control groups. Meta-regression
explored the influence of sex, type of digital strategy, follow-up duration, and sample size.
Results:
From 11 RCTs comprising 13 study arms and 2,089 participants, the overall pooled dropout rate was 12.4%. Digital
strategies showed a dropout rate of 13.2% versus 11.4% in control arms. No statistically significant difference was
found 
(
OR = 1.06; p = 0.67
)
 between groups of study. Meta-regression revealed no significant moderators of
attrition.
Conclusion:
Digital interventions for AD present slightly higher dropout rates compared to controls, although differences are
not statistically significant. These findings support the inclusion of a 12.4% attrition rate when calculating sample
sizes for future trials. Transparent reporting of dropouts is essential to improve the reliability and implementation
of digital health research.
