Abstract N°: 8875
Two Sides of the Same Inflammation: Clinical Coexistence of Hidradenitis Suppurativa and Atopic
Dermatitis
Betina Freitas Françoso
1
, Maria Eduarda Rotta
1
, Gabriela Alves de Souza
1
, Giovana Sorensen
1
, Helena da Silva
Martucci
1
, Carolina Soraya Proença Dantas
1
1
Centro Universitário das Faculdades Associadas de Ensino - UNIFAE, São João da Boa Vista, São Paulo, Brazil
Introduction & Objectives:
Hidradenitis suppurativa 
(
HS
)
 is a chronic, inflammatory, and recurrent skin disease, primarily affecting post-
pubertal women. It presents with painful nodules, abscesses, and fistulas in intertriginous areas such as the axillae,
inframammary folds, groin, and buttocks. These regions contain hair follicles and apocrine glands, central to
disease pathogenesis. The impact on quality of life is significant due to chronic pain, drainage, odor, and
functional limitations. Management is based on severity and includes topical agents, antibiotics, hormonal
therapies, biologics, and surgery. Atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 is a common chronic inflammatory skin condition with a
genetic basis. It typically presents with pruritus, xerosis, and characteristic distribution, often beginning in
childhood. AD is associated with other atopic disorders such as asthma and allergic rhinitis. Both HS and AD
significantly affect quality of life and may share underlying immune dysregulation.
Materials & Methods:
A 19-year-old woman has been followed for HS since 2018, with lesions in the genital area, buttocks, and
inframammary folds. These presented as nodules, abscesses, cysts, and fibrotic bands. Her condition worsened
after weight gain. In April 2023, isotretinoin was started without significant response. Adalimumab was introduced
in August 2023 as a biologic treatment. By March 2024, semaglutide was added, resulting in 12 kg of weight loss
(
current weight: 102 kg
)
. She also had a history of atopic dermastitis.
Results:
This case report explores the clinical association between hidradenitis suppurativa and atopic dermatitis, and the
therapeutic strategies employed for disease management. Biologic therapies for HS have been discussed in the
literature; however, treatment options remain limited and often include hormonal therapies or antibiotics. In this
case, the patient
’
s atopic background overlaps with known HS risk factors such as female sex, African descent, and
smoking. There appears to be a meaningful connection between the two diseases, suggesting a shared
etiopathogenic basis, including impaired signaling, defective epidermal barrier, and dysregulation of antimicrobial
peptides 
(
AMPs
)
. This implies the possible involvement of common genetic factors. Studies indicate that HS
patients may develop AD, and the most significant clinical link lies in the reduced therapeutic response to HS
treatments in atopic patients, and worsening of HS in the presence of AD. This is thought to result from a
continuous inflammatory response due to epidermal barrier dysfunction, potentially linked to deficient Notch
signaling. Although few studies have directly addressed this comorbidity, clinical experience supports a correlation
based on shared pathophysiological traits.
Conclusion:
This case highlights the clinical complexity in managing patients with coexisting chronic dermatologic diseases
such as hidradenitis suppurativa and atopic dermatitis. Both conditions significantly impair quality of life and share
immunological pathways, supporting the rationale for integrated treatment strategies. While their coexistence is

rarely detailed in the literature, it underscores the importance of an individualized, multidisciplinary approach, with
emphasis on early diagnosis and improved access to immunomodulatory therapies in severe or refractory cases.
