Introduction & Objectives:
Insights into the real-world treatment of atopic dermatitis 
(
AD
)
 are key to clinical decision making. The aim of this
analysis was to characterize skin of colour patients who receive dupilumab for moderate-to-severe AD in a real-
world setting within Canada.
Materials & Methods:
The CANDID phase 4 study is an ongoing, prospective, observational study of patients with moderate-to-severe
AD who receive dupilumab according to Canadian-specific prescribing information. We report baseline
characteristics and treatment patterns for patients enrolled from October 4, 2023 to March 2, 2025. Analyses are
descriptive; no formal statistical comparisons were performed. Of the 305 patients enrolled into CANDID, 46%
(
n=139
)
 were from diverse backgrounds with skin of colour; this also included Indigenous patients 
(
First Nations,
Inuit, and Metis
)
.
Results:
Data from 139 pediatric, adolescent, and adult patients with skin of colour 
(
Asian [19.3%]; Indigenous [11%]; South
Asian [8%]; Black/African American [4.3%]; Arab [0.7%]; and Latin [0.7%]
)
 were enrolled from the total study

population of 305 patients 
(
46% of enrolled patients
)
, with 75%, 15%, and 10% being adults, adolescents, and
pediatric patients, respectively.
Patients had a high disease burden prior to dupilumab start at enrollment with mean 
(
± standard deviation
)
scores being 30.7% 
(
21.4%
)
 for body surface area, 20.4 
(
11.8
)
 for Eczema Area and Severity Index, 7.6 
(
2.2
)
 for
Peak Pruritus Numerical Rating Score, 6.0 
(
3.0
)
 for Skin Soreness Numerical Rating Score, 4.0 
(
3.3
)
 for Sleep
Disturbance Numerical Rating Score, 17.3 
(
5.0
)
 for Dermatology Life Quality Index [DLQI] total score, 16.7 
(
5.1
)
 for
Children
’
s DLQI, and 15.4 
(
7.3
)
 for Dermatitis Family Impact. In the previous 12 months, 97.3%, 37.4%, and 27.9%
patients were treated with topical corticosteroids, systemic corticosteroids, and conventional systemic non-
corticosteroid immunosuppressants respectively.
Conclusion:
Canadian patients with skin of colour enrolled in CANDID demonstrated considerable multidimensional burden of
disease across signs/symptoms despite extensive previous use of topical, systemic and non-systemic AD
treatments. This is the first real world evidence assessment of Canadian patients from diverse backgrounds and
skin of colour including the Indigenous population, demonstrating the burden of disease for diverse Canadians
living with atopic dermatitis.
